{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm","id":1314066,"title":"The Realm","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-05-24T07:31:53-07:00","id":1314066,"title":"The Realm","content":"It's out. I haven't had time to read much, I just looked at a few bits.<br /> The Realm isn't the book I'm most looking forward to (partly because I'm running a game in the distant North-East, partly because the Blessed Isle was such a dull book), but it does look decent so far.<br /> <br /> I like some bits of art, I'm not so keen on others, but that's personal taste, they all seem good quality. And I really like how consistently they portray the Realm's style and architecture, which is difficult with different artists. For example, you can definitely visualise what Realm soldiers look like, as they're always portrayed with the same uniform.<br /> <br /> The art for the satrapies is very nice.<br /> <br /> The timeline looks much better than the timeline in 2nd ed's Blessed Isle.<br /> <br /> The satrapies are arranged geographically around the world, like the Lunar Domains.<br /> Fajad<br /> Pneuma<br /> Cherak<br /> Greyfalls<br /> Prasad<br /> The Lap<br /> Zhaojun (really an area of satrapies, including Goldenseal)<br /> Wu-Jian<br /> Utahi (a city-mountain in the West that used to have an empire)<br /> <br /> So mostly new ones for 3rd ed. Cherak, Greyfalls and the Lap were in 1st and 2nd ed, but Cherak never really got a proper write-up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T08:08:44-07:00","id":1314080,"title":"","content":"Is it only out for backers right now?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T08:27:02-07:00","id":1314084,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314080#post1314080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it only out for backers right now?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Only for backers. It's the errata/editing pass.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T09:02:38-07:00","id":1314094,"title":"","content":"Any ETA on when the public release will be(based on past releases)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T09:52:46-07:00","id":1314112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314094#post1314094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Any ETA on when the public release will be(based on past releases)?</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what Rich Thomas said in this week's Monday Meeting comments (which was they were thinking of releasing it to backers at the same time it went public, or a little earlier), then soon, next Wednesday at earliest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T10:09:22-07:00","id":1314115,"title":"","content":"I would be surprised to see it only go through 6 days of errata/edit passing. I'd personally guess closer to a month.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T10:18:19-07:00","id":1314118,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Unsung Hero</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314115#post1314115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would be surprised to see it only go through 6 days of errata/edit passing. I'd personally guess closer to a month.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. We need to let backers finish reporting issues; go through the compiled issues to see what needs implementing; send a corrected PDF to Onyx Path; wait for a new version of the PDF to come back to us; go back and forth with confirming corrections until the proof is clean; and then Onyx Path needs to approve the revised PDF and send it out. This takes a while.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T12:13:45-07:00","id":1314131,"title":"","content":"Suddenly, I'm an happier person. I've really waited for this book, so I let's see what we got :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T12:26:39-07:00","id":1314136,"title":"","content":"Weird observation but I could have sworn in past editions that the Blessed Isle didn't have slaves in an official capacity. While they existed, they were few and sort of &quot;not talked about&quot; culturally. This one seems to state outright that they exist and are a part of society. Am I simply mis-remembering?<br /> <br /> (<b>Please don't make Slavery the new subject of the thread, we've had enough threads on the topic and I don't want to derail</b>)<br /> <br /> Other observation, there's some really phenominal writing in here. An example of a line that really stood out to me: &quot;The Sword of Creation swung but once, and as the aftershocks of Creation\u2019s rescue died away, the world fell quiet.&quot; Exceedingly evocative to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T12:57:59-07:00","id":1314148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Unsung Hero</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314136#post1314136\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Weird observation but I could have sworn in past editions that the Blessed Isle didn't have slaves in an official capacity. While they existed, they were few and sort of &quot;not talked about&quot; culturally. This one seems to state outright that they exist and are a part of society. Am I simply mis-remembering?<br /> <br /> (<b>Please don't make Slavery the new subject of the thread, we've had enough threads on the topic and I don't want to derail</b>)<br /> <br /> Other observation, there's some really phenominal writing in here. An example of a line that really stood out to me: &quot;The Sword of Creation swung but once, and as the aftershocks of Creation\u2019s rescue died away, the world fell quiet.&quot; Exceedingly evocative to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Slavery's always been there, just limitted to Dynasts. There's a huge chunk on how it is carreid on on th Blessed Isle all the way back in <b>Manacle &amp; Coin.</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T13:13:23-07:00","id":1314151,"title":"","content":"Was Fokuf ever explicitly mortal before? I seem to recall it was left ambiguous in previous editions. But here he's just flat mortal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3487-jetstream","name":"Jetstream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3487_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T13:14:54-07:00","id":1314152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jetstream</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314151#post1314151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Was Fokuf ever explicitly mortal before? I seem to recall it was left ambiguous in previous editions. But here he's just flat mortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>I always understood him to be mortal, but I don't have my books around to verify.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T13:17:39-07:00","id":1314153,"title":"","content":"Pretty sure he was a mortal. That was kind of the point, having him be a scapegoat from the worst-faring House the Empress could put the blame on, not that it was really gonna work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23684-alistair","name":"Alistair","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23684_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T13:46:39-07:00","id":1314157,"title":"","content":"He was always mortal. In <i>Return of the Scarlet Empress</i> he was mortal enough to get Exalted as a secret Infernal, I believe. I think there\u2019s one spot in 2e\u2019s Compass: Blessed Isle where he\u2019s implied to be a DB because that writer thought it was implausible the Dynasty would make a mortal Regent, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T14:04:12-07:00","id":1314162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Minton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314118#post1314118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah. We need to let backers finish reporting issues; go through the compiled issues to see what needs implementing; send a corrected PDF to Onyx Path; wait for a new version of the PDF to come back to us; go back and forth with confirming corrections until the proof is clean; and then Onyx Path needs to approve the revised PDF and send it out. This takes a while.</div> </div> </div> </div>Er. Rich reported in the latest Ex3 KS update that the advance PDF for The Realm would be going public on Wednesday. So, er, not sure what's going on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T14:21:44-07:00","id":1314169,"title":"","content":"It just means everyone will get a chance to go after typos starting Wednesday, and anyone who purchases the advanced PDF via KS or DTRPG will get the final one when it\u2019s released. I mean, I can\u2019t guarantee that\u2019s how it\u2019ll work, but it\u2019s how it\u2019s always worked before.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T14:41:04-07:00","id":1314172,"title":"","content":"Neat book overall. Really goes the distance to make the Blessed Isle more interesting than it was in prior editions where it was weirdly rather static considering there's about to be a Civil War. Here, it really nails the feeling that the Scarlet Empire is teetering and on the verge of collapse into chaos. Also, hey, the Jadeborn don't look like generic fantasy elves/dwarves but look like weird rock/gem people! That's way more interesting.<br /> <br /> Did note an error though; Sesus is referred to as Ragara's &quot;half-brother&quot; in one part of The Realm but in What Fire Has Wrought, Sesus is referred to as &quot;she&quot;. I suppose it could be that Sesus is a trans woman but I don't think this would be the best way to demonstrate that, so this seems more like someone just was working off an old draft or something where Sesus was a dude rather than a lady.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12698,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12698-sunandspring","user":"SunAndSpring","date":". 05-24-2019, 05:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12698-sunandspring","name":"SunAndSpring","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12698_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T14:52:13-07:00","id":1314174,"title":"","content":"Love the clothing section especially, although the entire section about living standards by class is cool, especially how grand and elaborate Patricians are only to arrive at the Dynasts and find that they prefer simple but high-quality wear. Sort of draws a cool 'old money' versus 'new money' parallel.<br /> <br /> Also, regarding pets, who in their right mind wants to rob a house guarded by giant spiders? I am honestly more intimidated by the idea of a pack of giant spiders that want to eat me than their cranky Exalted owner<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11320,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","user":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","date":". 05-24-2019, 07:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T16:01:08-07:00","id":1314191,"title":"","content":"It is going to be an agonizing wait. I was not active in the Exalted-sphere, due to having lost interest/focus because of the incredibly slow pace of source book releases (that was a big problem with Exalted 3ed until very recently, though I am glad to see it is looking like it moves a bit faster now), so I never realised that there was a Dragon Blooded Kick starter before it was long over.And now that I am back and about to start a new campaign, I feel I need it now at once, haha. So yeah, here is hoping it gets released pronto so I can actually buy it, because knowing other Game Masters are actually reading it now kind of blows hard <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T16:02:05-07:00","id":1314192,"title":"","content":"Okay, going to have a nice look through the Realm.<br /> <br /> First off, the cover. I really like this one, because it captures a good cross section of what the Realm is about; peasants working the fields while a finely dressed and haughty noble rides past on a well-decorated horse, accompanied by soldiers and officials. All of them in the shadow of a grand city surrounded by vast white walls carved with elaborate traceries. It\u2019s colourful and inviting, and I think it makes life in the domain of the Dragon Blooded have a certain feel of\u2026 exposure to power, even while there\u2019s a wall of social separation. I will however slightly criticise the silhouettes in the corner, as they seem a bit out of place and distracting.<br /> <br /> Quick check of the credits\u2026 I\u2019m rather excited about the credit given for Lea for \u201cthinking through the mechanisms of empire\u201d, looking forward to seeing a follow up on that.<br /> <br /> Aaaaand I\u2019m going to skip straight past the table of contents, as I want to know all of that stuff as I encounter it firsthand.<br /> <br /> Right, that will do to start. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T17:04:34-07:00","id":1314201,"title":"","content":"I'll be posting first impressions as I go through the book. <br /> <br /> The cover:<br /> <br /> - Jesus Christ, the art is on point. That is absolutely beautiful and it immediately communicates the class disparity between Dynasts and peasants. Absolutely amazing.<br /> - Calling it now, that peasant with the sickle is totally a Lunar and that Dynast is not waking up tomorrow morning.<br /> - The way the title and the product line are formatted is really cool as well.<br /> <br /> Introduction:<br /> <br /> - Major points for quoting Said.<br /> - Very clear statement that the Realm subjugates and exploits everything it can conquer, and this is not a good thing. <br /> - Then we get the conflict between the houses.<br /> - Thinking about it, I like this presentation a lot. To the Dragon-Blooded, the civil war is their immediate concern (unless a Lunar shows up, obviously). To the rest of Creation, the Realm is the problem, and that's what gives the Great Houses something to temporarily unite against until they're secure enough to get back to fighting each other again. It sets the conflicts up nicely.<br /> - Kinda curious about when satrapies are or aren't considered barbaric. Is it as soon as they swear loyalty to the Realm, or do they need to work to become civilized?<br /> - Oh God, all that shade at Hero.<br /> <br /> Fiction:<br /> - Banditry on the Blessed Island seems really fucking stupid or really fucking bad? That's (supposed to be) basically suicide for mortals, isn't it? Are they being led by a Dragon-Blooded?<br /> - Or maybe an Abyssal? This lady seems to like blood a lot.<br /> - Okay, so no respect for the Dragon-Blooded anymore.<br /> - I'm guessing this is how Sadaal Exalted?<br /> <br /> Chapter 1:<br /> - I like how the book frames how easy it is to accept the Empress as an alternative to all the other shitty things happening in Creation.<br /> - It amuses me that the Imperial City was constructed for the convenience of the Empress' waiting petitioners.<br /> - Oh, hai there p. XX.<br /> - I would like to see more of the One-Mind Plague.<br /> - You have to appreciate how much effort the Empress put into crippling the Realm so no one would ever be able to challenge her.<br /> - Huh. So two years left before the Empress is officially considered dead and everything goes even more nuts? I like that.<br /> - Hey, there's a mention of how bandits are able to survive on the Blessed Isle. Sounds really sucky.<br /> - Thank you for pointing out that slavery is shit.<br /> - Interesting that the merchants have a lower social standing than the patricians. Says a lot about the Empress' priorities.<br /> - The political wrangling between patricians and Dynasts is very interesting.<br /> - Is there a criminal organization that's not backed by one of the Great Houses?<br /> - Akaya Moda is strangely terrifying.<br /> - I really like the attention to detail paid to daily life on the Blessed Isle.<br /> - I have a hard time reconciling House Ledaal zealotry with Ledaal Kes' whoring and cocaine habit. Also, I love to talk about Ledaal Kes' whoring and cocaine habit. He's the best.<br /> - I like how prevalent the troubles caused by the upcoming civil war are in the Great Houses' writeups. Really shows how desperate for resources they are.<br /> <br /> Chapter 2:<br /> - Archons are especially interesting. There's a lot of potential there for character concepts. Archon who Exalts after their magistrate is killed treacherously by one of the Great Houses? Yiiiiiisssssssssss.<br /> - Love all the gatekeeping on the Thousand Scales.<br /> - There's a sort of Dark Souls feel to how all the Realm's institutions are falling part.<br /> - On to the All-Seeing Eye!<br /> - Oh, the All-Seeing Eye has contacts in the Threshold? That is interesting.<br /> - Burn Notice. Burn Notice everywhere. At least the person in charge is gone, so it's not like the Eye is allowing random old guys to subvert all their operatives and assets for shits and giggles.<br /> - Love how the Deliberative was established to make democracy not happen.<br /> - You ever wondered what would happen if a brawl broke out in the Deliberative? Imagine all the kung fu fighting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T20:37:24-07:00","id":1314227,"title":"","content":"Please. I have to know who wrote the section on the Salt Rate.<br /> I love you. It\u2019s possibly my favorite bit of the book, and certainly stands testament to the overall goal of a well-thought out, reasonable and believable setting.<br /> <br /> I never thought I\u2019d be able to explain the difference between responsible/irresponsible banking in Creation but it turns out I actually can.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s so simple! The Realm needs Salt to expand, So periods of growth will be associated with a high draw on Salt Harvests. When the harvest is big, the Rate can be lower. Then if the Realm Isn\u2019t expanding, the Salt yield doesn\u2019t need to be as high, so the rate goes up to appease the Salt Gods.<br /> <br /> Which actually makes it a very reliable canary for the overall economy and rate of growth! So on commercial loans you can reasonably set your standard interest rate to the Salt Rate, because it\u2019s already acting as an indicator as to the likelihood of Default, because the Realm ultimately runs on profit, particularly profit through conquest!<br /> Arg I love it so much!<br /> And then the additional detail of the gods and banks setting their own rates in the absence of an Imperial Regulator? Genius!<br /> I am immediately making a Ragara character and giving them \u201cMajor: Current Salt Rate (Anxiety)\u201d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1247-maseiken","name":"Maseiken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1247_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T21:19:58-07:00","id":1314229,"title":"","content":"The Salt Rate dates back to Manacle &amp; Coin in 1e, so if you're thanking whoever came up with it, that'd be discussions between Geoff Grabowski and <i>I believe</i> Andrew Wyatt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T21:37:11-07:00","id":1314230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314201#post1314201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Interesting that the merchants have a lower social standing than the patricians. Says a lot about the Empress' priorities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tbf, they are part China and the Immaculates are a bit Confucian. It would super weird if they didnt think little and less of Merchants.<br /> <br /> They are also pretty Roman, so glory and blood being admirable while wealth is just envied is pretty for the course.<br /> <br /> Can't actually think of that many historical civs that didnt hold merchants somewhere between ambivalence and contempt actually.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T22:06:38-07:00","id":1314235,"title":"","content":"Wait a minute...<br /> <br /> Oh gods!<br /> <br /> Is Ragara Marcus Licinius Crassus?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11320,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","user":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","date":". 05-25-2019, 01:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T22:18:48-07:00","id":1314238,"title":"","content":"I love this book so much. How the hell did you guys write a section that makes me go &quot;Oh my god, I need to run a short campaign based solely on the party working for the Imperial Post Office, delivering urgent messages through vicious hordes of bandits and along dangerous storm-swept mountain passes.'?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T23:25:59-07:00","id":1314243,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stephen Lea Sheppard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314229#post1314229\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Salt Rate dates back to Manacle &amp; Coin in 1e, so if you're thanking whoever came up with it, that'd be discussions between Geoff Grabowski and <i>I believe</i> Andrew Wyatt.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh dang! Fair enough. Well it\u2019s still great, and unless it was also somewhere in 2e I don\u2019t know about, it\u2019s good to have it back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1247-maseiken","name":"Maseiken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1247_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-24T23:30:28-07:00","id":1314244,"title":"","content":"Love the sidebar on discrimination, a quick and dirty guide to how to RP it in the Realm along with a hefty warning to go over it with players beforehand and make sure everyone is cool. Its especially cool that it goes out of its way to state that it can be as periphery or essential to a given character as the player wants.<br /> <br /> These are obvious things but it is always great to see them put <i>starkly </i>into books.<br /> <br /> Its also really great that its portrayed as an encompassing thing that creates harsh expectations as well as real limitations, I appreciate the subtlety.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11320,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","user":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","date":". 05-25-2019, 02:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T01:33:56-07:00","id":1314255,"title":"","content":"What do people think of the radically redesigned Jadeborn? I like them. Mostly because they're new and different. I'm pretty bored of the old ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T02:03:58-07:00","id":1314262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314230#post1314230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can't actually think of that many historical civs that didnt hold merchants somewhere between ambivalence and contempt actually.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, IMO I would characterize slightly (not wholly) differently; it seems fairly typical to view merchants as better than peasants (and the vast mass of the population, including craftsman and so on in times and places where this sector expanded) but not as good as landlords, warriors, religious leaders and philosophers. From a certain perspective since most of the writing across history is done for an aristocratic or at any right highest status audience that looks like contempt in what see; however it is worth distinguishing from low social standing.<br /> <br /> China seems like a bit of an exception where the literati-landlord class of bureaucrats (closest in analogy perhaps to what is today our class of civil servants, media and university complex) expended a lot of energy on writing tracts and developing a heavily articulated ideology in which everyone who wasn't either the emperor or part of the literati-landlord class (including, largely, the army, merchants, Buddhist and Taoist priests and so on) and who could present an alternative power base sort of sucked and should know their place, preferably lower and more distant from influence on power than the peasants. Though the extent of this in actual implementation was sharply variant! (Because they needed armies, trade, etc. there's a limit to how much &quot;<i>Good iron doesn't make nails; good men don't make soldiers</i>&quot;, and the same for merchants, can actually be practiced or believed).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T04:27:00-07:00","id":1314268,"title":"","content":"Historically, aristocracy has looked down on merchants pretty heavily. Since their wealth was classically tied to land incomes, aristocrats lived in an economic environment fundamentally different than that of a merchant, for one, that meant their income was usually fixed while merchant income fluctuated(this became a serious problem for them as trade became more profitable and land incomes depreciated in value). Participating in trade meant actually working to achieve a living, something aristocrats viewed as crass and lowbrow, since the expectation was that their own fief based incomes would be adequate to fund their lavish lifestyles. Merchants upset this worldview by being able to acquire enviable wealth by interacting with the working class, thus jeopardizing the aristocracy's sense of superiority. The way the two groups handled money was different too, merchants tended towards frugality(and displays of opulence were generally those that their funds could handle) while nobles were constantly socially encouraged to spend without regard for cost(worrying about costs being a thing for peasants). This had the effect of leaving many nobles drowning in debt (you can see this with the Great Houses still spending money on lavish parties and taking out compromising loans from House Ragara even when they're approaching civil war, most Dynasts just don't know how to dial back spending).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T05:31:57-07:00","id":1314274,"title":"","content":"I'm currently in Kyoto, Japan for a few days. There's something quite cool in reading The Realm one paragraph at a time, in between meditating in boudhist temples and visiting the Shogun's Palace. It feels... appropriate.<br /> <br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.imgur.com/EWgOrrm.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Ultimate Jade-Screened Exalted Scholar, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1304239-my-next-campaign-tdhr-peach\" target=\"_blank\">THDR : Future Dragon-Blooded campaign</a>, and other links.<br /> If you like Ex3, you can support <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/holdenshearer\" target=\"_blank\">Holden</a> on Patreon!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":15}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/541-letipex","name":"LeTipex","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar541_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"902"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T06:10:13-07:00","id":1314276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314255#post1314255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do people think of the radically redesigned Jadeborn? I like them. Mostly because they're new and different. I'm pretty bored of the old ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Redesign? There is not nearly enough detail to say they play any different, and the art seems to show Workers, Warriors, and an Artisan. But, well, I surely do <i>love </i>the <i>look </i>in that one piece of artwork. It presents them as so much more &quot;unhuman-people-carved-from-jade&quot; rather than &quot;no-really-they-are-not-dwarves&quot;. Hopefully that sets the new standard for future depictions.<br /> <br /> There's also the exiled Jadeborn in Werck that promise some intriguing plothooks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T06:14:19-07:00","id":1314277,"title":"","content":"Anyone have a pic of the new Jadeborn?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T06:33:02-07:00","id":1314280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greyman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314276#post1314276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Redesign? There is not nearly enough detail to say they play any different,</div> </div> </div> </div>I meant aesthetically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T07:21:44-07:00","id":1314289,"title":"","content":"I like how the book presents Fokuf. His previous portrayal came across as lacking basic self awareness (espeically in that comic) and the only interesting thing to do with it was to go &quot;suprise! he's been fooling you all along!&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T07:23:59-07:00","id":1314290,"title":"","content":"All right, time to get into this in earnest. First off, I\u2019d say the quotation used to open the book is quite fitting and sets the appropriate mood; I\u2019m sure that every single person who sees it will take its meaning and its essential truth to heart, and have no defensive arguments or nitpicks whatsoever.<br /> <br /> That said, I do think there\u2019s something worthwhile for the starting paragraph declaring that peasants of the Blessed Isle have typically had better lives than their fellows across the sea; it speaks to the security of the heart of the Realm, if nothing else. I think that the opening sets a good picture for how the Realm is so deeply rooted and the focus of so many agendas within and without that its downfall would not be a smooth transition. After Fangs, I like how Lunars are given a place of prominence here, at the same time as I find the phrasing for putting Solars last to make them not so much an afterthought as an unknown quantity with way too much potential to influence the situation.<br /> <br /> Just going to skip over the chapter descriptions and have a glance at the lexicon\u2026 it\u2019s interesting to me how barbarian is now inducted as formal terminology for the Realm, which also ties in nicely to Fangs. I\u2019d been wondering how the terminology for social strata works now, so it\u2019s interesting that citizen is a term encompassing peasant, patrician and Dynast together, rather than being its own middle level; I take this as final confirmation of my long supposition that peasant is not a term that applies even to, say, urban merchants and manufacturers, unless the implication is supposed to be that such people do not exist and those ranks are filled with patricians. Either possibility is curious to me, but I\u2019m ready to let the book make its case. <br /> Let\u2019s see, other new terminology\u2026 martial orders and their oblates are new, though I have an inkling that they\u2019re primarily concerned with the Caul. Nice to see the new significance of the Merchant Fleet reiterated. I had been wondering about attitudes towards the Old Realm for\u2026 reasons that I cannot recall, but that question seems reasonably resolved by a statement that a local term for the First Age is the Realm Before, so that\u2019s fine. The rest is all familiar territory.<br /> <br /> The inspirations seem about what one would expect, even if the specific works aren\u2019t all familiar; a Chinese classic on intrigues among a sizable ruling clan (although the idea of it including the relationships of servants intrigues me), fantasy fiction about an empire amidst civil war of supernatural powers (whose name I\u2019m sure will prompt no acrimony), non-fiction about colonialism and imperial relations, aaaand\u2026 Heh, of course that old chestnut of Grabowski\u2019s (fairly accurate) critique of Hero would find its way in; I like the way it\u2019s referenced as inspiration for the Realm and Dynast outlook on the Empress (as likely cultivated by her) rather than using its stylized elements straight up. They look like a good selection to me, and I meant want to sample a few of them if I can.<br /> <br /> Now, a fiction piece concerning a small cross-section of the Realm\u2019s citizens being accosted by bandits; they kill all the guards, take the point of view character hostage and even profane a sacred statue, and no comeuppance comes upon them. It\u2019s an interesting way to start, establishing a tone not of the empire\u2019s power, but of the vacuum left as it\u2019s beginning to fall apart. I like how the ways in which it places women first reinforces the culture of the Realm, as well as the little bit ascribing some vague but enormous dimensions to the Imperial Mountain at the start, both for setting the scene and giving an idea for how to portray it. I\u2019m liking the bandit leader\u2019s design in the picture, with how she\u2019s practically dressed and has a rough face, but still a bit of ornamentation in things like her braid and lipstick.<br /> <br /> So, chapter one, History and Life in the Realm; this is something that I\u2019ve been particularly looking forward to, to get a full sense of what has been adjusted for the changes in What Fire Has Wrought, what has remained the same because it was good, and maybe consolidating a few favoured details scattered through old source books.<br /> <br /> The choice to start the description of the Realm\u2019s beginning from the point of view of somebody on the ground level is really good, for a number of reasons. For one, it\u2019s an avenue for some really evocative language of living through the eschaton\u2019s onset and abrupt cancellation. It sets a strong emotional tone for the Realm\u2019s beginning, a sense of the terrified, confused and cathartic feelings of the moment in which it would be easy to capitalise and establish such a polity; the kind of thing that cuts through a clinical detachment and scepticism. And it\u2019s a reminder that there are people subjected to events who aren\u2019t getting the full picture, and that there\u2019s significance to their outlook.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m glad that when it starts getting more into the history of things, it maintains brevity (no sense in devoting too much attention for something so removed) and a dash of mythic tone, as that both keeps the scenario from feeling mundane and would logically be how things are experienced in a setting such as this. It\u2019s also a good opportunity to provide some flowery description for the Fair Folk, which I\u2019m generally for; I like calling them the gentry of imaginary worlds in particular.<br /> <br /> The origin story of the Scarlet Empress remains the same in general outline, but the details are adjusted mildly; the tone is more literary than biographical. The thing that stands out to me the most is not going into detail of her journey into the manse, particularly not adding anything so specific as her having needed to personally sacrifice some Hearth member. I suspect that some people might view this as sanitising a bit, but my recollections place such a backstory as having been too rigid and provocative, and I think that it\u2019s better for the overall mystique of the character for the details of her quest to be mysterious both in and out of character. I also like things such as the sense that her drive towards the weapons was overcoming a certain scepticism of her era, as well as language giving a sense of it more as an interconnected arsenal than a singular device.<br /> <br /> The Seven Tigers story is still here, and I appreciate how concise it is; it gets across the point of how the immediate aftermath had some opportunists whose ambitions could have been disastrous, and that the Empress demonstrated a willingness to turn her power upon fellows and Creation in general, without needing to devote too many words to it. That\u2019s not to say that it lacks flair, just that it\u2019s efficient and has a good sense of proportion.<br /> <br /> Ohh, now <i>this</i> is something particularly clever; Chejop Kejak is still present in this backstory, but with the twist of presenting him in the terms that he would be recognised in popular legend and official biography of the Realm, as an anonymous monk who followed astrology to meet with her and declare that she had been divinely selected to revive the Realm and rule the world, pledging the support of the Immaculate Order to give some structure to her nascent government. I like describing him in these terms for how it gives the more widespread perspective and keeps more of the prominence to her, and think it has just enough hints for a newcomer to put together that he\u2019s a Sidereal Exalt. Overall, I find this way of writing the history to be engaging, getting across the important details in short order and having a kind of smooth flavour to it; you get a feel for tone and emotion in the events more than if they were recounted at length.<br /> <br /> Oh dang; yeah, the image of the Scarlet Empress proclaiming her sovereignty is definitely something that always ought to have been presented in full colour. I also like how the frame is oriented upwards from the perspective of the peasants, increasing her sense of scope and dominance, and reinforcing how that gesture would have impacted its witnesses.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve generally enjoyed the backstory to the Imperial City, but I\u2019ll admit to a few details that were a little bit incongruous; the version in Exalted: the Dragon Blooded being slightly at odds with describing it as coming from a thorough ruined city, and the description of the Empress\u2019 early activities as having a certain fickle quality which seemed a bit out of sorts with the gravitas suggested only shortly before. I think this part keeps the essentials of that story while tidying up some of the details. I even find the manner in which it describes her seemingly unknowable standards for whom she would admit entry to lend itself both to her mystery, and convey a sense of practicality. The adjustment in motives for those not called upon to \u201cleaving will be a grave insult\u201d to \u201cwhat if we\u2019re called upon right after we leave\u201d is one I like, purely because it speaks to me directly. The image of encampments moving to occupy and reconstruct ruins is compelling to me, and the description of the sequence of dependent industries that develops it from a boom town to a fully operational city captures a quality I find attractive in how systems and communities form connections. Overall, I like how this section conveys the ad hoc nature of the Imperial City, right down to the lack of official name, and how it sets a contrast between that and it having become a centre for wealth and governance across Creation.<br /> <br /> Araka Jeresh\u2019s story is still here, but makes a few slight adjustments; the description of his original overture conveyed an implicit misogyny to the character that never fully made sense to me, so I\u2019m glad that\u2019s out while retaining his general presumption of offering to share the burden of her authority. Likewise, I\u2019ve often kind of thought that her so quickly taking him as a lover was a bit misogynistic on the part of the writing, so I like it stating here that he was taken on as a messenger first, and a relationship came after. This would also be a spot where I find it okay to display the more playful quality of the Empress in the idea that extreme backtracking amused her enough to recruit somebody. This section in general, of the games of trying to impress and bedazzle the Empress and the manner in which she would respond, reminds me a lot of a time that Lea made a point of how the character needs to walk a fine line between having genuine sexual agency and the sexual agency of powerful women just winding up as something to titillate a male audience. In my opinion, it manages to walk that line, as I find the descriptions of the elaborate gestures made to win her favour as placing a lot of the authority with her. And with her motives to take on lovers\u2026 I can\u2019t quite nail it down, but my immediate emotional reaction is that it\u2019s doing a good job. It feels\u2026 like a combination of actual considerations in dynastic politics with pulp fantasy stylings (in which things are a bit more lurid) and some things that have become baked into Exalted (a general sense of meaningful intimacy not needing to come from fully committed relationships, and that there can be political exchanges in such things due to that sense). And it\u2019s not making too big a deal out of it, so it can pass by as mundane in the eyes of the setting; in the absence of overall misogyny in the surrounding culture, there wouldn\u2019t be any inherent slut shaming that comes from implying political support by openly entering a relationship, never mind the attitudes to the mere rumour of such faced by the likes of Elizabeth I and Catherine the Great. In contrast to the prior sections building up her mystique, I also find it to be rather humanising for the Scarlet Empress. I think it\u2019s a good job.<br /> <br /> Oh, there actually is a sidebar clarifying that the emerald-clad monk was Chejop Kejak; this is the kind of thing I get for processing the books in this manner. Normally I\u2019d be a bit critical of a move such as this, but the sidebar wins me over with its choice of language. Things like the contrast between the official story of a humble monk proclaiming and entreating her with a shrewd sage who functions as a full founding partner in the empire, and that the Empress proved a lot more firm than he expected or liked. I also get a good sense out of the description of one of Chejop\u2019s benefits giving him an established government in which to give Sidereals plausible covers in travelling and anonymous officials. Yyeesss, being clever ultimately seems less valuable than providing details on what the Empress and Sidereals get out of their partnership, and it thankfully still does so in a manner that is to the point and a bit sharp-witted.<br /> <br /> All right, the establishment of the imperial service, a premise of which I\u2019ve always been fond. For a start, I like that idea that she was initially only willing to personally meet with military commanders, presenting a challenge for having agents to enact her will without relocating officers from areas in which they were needed and suited; it not only gives me a picture of having to adapt her style on the fly and without a proper apparatus to call upon, but a starting bias towards familiar colleagues that she needed to grow out of in order to actually govern. In this way, we seem to get not only the origins of the All-Seeing Eye, but the general policy of governing by continuously keeping subordinate powers off-balance and in competition, striking me as not so much an elaborate calculation as being initially her only means and understanding of how to manage the powers that have clustered themselves beneath her. That idea that, lacking a formal apparatus to exercise power, her means of keeping all of these wealthy and experienced subordinates in line is to collect and distribute information that ensures they\u2019re constantly churning against one another. As somebody with a fondness of grand institutions having ignoble origins, I like the All-Seeing Eye emerging from the informants keeping that mess going, and I enjoy the image of her participation being offset by a public persona of holding herself loftily above their intrigues.<br /> <br /> The ministries strike me as comparatively prosaic, which is honestly what you\u2019d want in a bureaucracy (or \u201cpaperwork junta\u201d as the book colourfully describes it). What I like here is maintaining the point that these early days are still pretty wild, as the lack of formality makes things like tax collection amount to sending people out to seize property, and offsetting those already in possession of power and connections by giving them responsibilities that occupy their attention and drain their resources. I\u2019ve always trying to keep an eye out for the writing giving ideas for areas to apply the Bureaucracy Ability, and I think this is one that would serve as inspiration for games in which players are establishing their own services. My tastes also go to the description of how she could secure service and loyalty from mortal leaders by means of their desire to have official cover to interfere with Exalted superiors. And it\u2019s good to have another sense of the transition in governing style and structure, not only from early Realm to familiar Realm (i.e. extracting the military from civil governance), but away from the Shogunate in things such as subordinating powerful landlords to a centralised bureaucracy. So it comes out as a display of the Realm\u2019s origin that is less abrupt, has more sense of history, while also having some practical play benefits.<br /> <br /> Similar goes with the origins given to the magistrates, who an initial dependence on personally trusted individuals became not only an opportunity to cultivate the legend and popularity of her enforcers being grand and charismatic figures of justice, thus allowing her to spread her own cult of personality, but a means of quietly eliminating challenges to her autocracy and concentrating vacant powers in herself, further giving the Shogunate a sense of presence by its deliberated absence.<br /> <br /> Thus we\u2019re given an elaborate background for what the Realm\u2019s law and custom is, and why these are seen to derive solely from the Scarlet Empress. It at once makes the Blessed Isle feel a lot more diverse and messy, and gives a sense of credibility to the weight of her presence and the amount of power she\u2019s managed to personally exercise.<br /> <br /> The legions appear to be the branch of the imperial service whose origins have changed the least from prior Editions; the basic outline of a pre-existing military that she didn\u2019t want to make anxious with threats of a purge, and revived with things like benefits given to private enterprises that would finance and furnish them is all still there. If there\u2019s any difference, I would find it to be things that make the early legions seem a lot stronger, give more of a sense that they came through the Contagion in better shape, and that part of her process for both projecting her own power and directing their attention externally was through things like grand parades. It covers a few of the points that would interest me such as how the military is being paid for, gives it a basis for some impressive pageantry, and lays the groundwork for the Realm\u2019s militarism and expansionism. It also conveys an area in which the authority of the Empress might have become less absolute than it was in other areas, having to deal more carefully with something that might have had the power to destroy her.<br /> <br /> So we\u2019ve got the establishment for all of the parts of the Imperial Service, including the practical considerations for how and why they operate and both the accuracies and perceptions of the power held over them by the Scarlet Empress. What Fire Has Wrought gave us the picture of the power distributed through the Great Houses, but all of this is the structure of the imperial apparatus that clearly and meaningfully operates apart from and above them.<br /> <br /> This section has all been <b>very</b> good. It breathes life and personality and history into the origins of the Realm. It gives meaningful information about how these governing institutions might originate and operate, but keeps it from being too dry and excessively detailed; it is densely packed in the best way. The best part is the way that it continuously gives the Empress a sense of presence, a few strategically placed peeks behind the curtain, providing an origin for her stature over her empire that feels organically developed as well as not being as absolute as it would appear. And, as I kept saying before, being presented in this lean, tight, personable manner really feels like it could serve as a blueprint for nation-building activities undertaken in play, so it has practical value in addition to enhancing the Realm by giving a sense of reality to its beginning. I feel as though it would be a difficult balance to provide information like this in a manner both educating and entertaining (while also not being overwritten), and it\u2019s managed beautifully here.<br /> <br /> This seems like a natural spot to take a break, but I do not think it shall be a very long one, as I\u2019m eager for more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T07:39:03-07:00","id":1314295,"title":"","content":"I too liked the Empress's visitor being presented as simply an important and slightly mysterious monk; I think 2nd ed presented Sidereals in a too matter-of-fact way, so that while players technically knew that the Sidereals were unknown, it didn't<i> feel </i>like that, and so they'd quickly become prosaic.<br /> <br /> Overall, 3rd ed's been much better about making it quite clear that Sidereals are unknown to most. It doesn't go quite as far as I would, but I can understand why.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T07:39:11-07:00","id":1314296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314289#post1314289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like how the book presents Fokuf. His previous portrayal came across as lacking basic self awareness (espeically in that comic) and the only interesting thing to do with it was to go &quot;suprise! he's been fooling you all along!&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Bringing a touch of humanity to Fokuf, while still very much presenting him as First Edition's Assiduous Reader of the Immaculate Texts, was one of the things I was really happy about getting to write about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T09:00:33-07:00","id":1314306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314255#post1314255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do people think of the radically redesigned Jadeborn? I like them. Mostly because they're new and different. I'm pretty bored of the old ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like it in idea, I'm personally not so big on the specific illustration of them though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T16:30:13-07:00","id":1314398,"title":"","content":"At last I have The Realm, I was expecting this book with a lot of interest. I will comment on things as they occur to me and I am reading:<br /> The banditry in the initial story I suppose comes to represent the decline and weakness of Realm in the context of the impending civil war. Five years ago it would have been unthinkable, not anymore.<br /> I like that Chejop Kejak. be the one who finally convinces the Empress to become the Empress. Interesting, and new detail, that the Empress did not decide to take power initially, and was convinced of it, adds an interesting nuance to the character.<br /> Peasant rebellions, I like the detail, although it is a pity that only two are treated in detail, I would have liked to see more of it.<br /> It is good to finally point out (it is obvious but I think it is the first time that is published in the official book) that the policy of the Empress of infrequent bowels was one of the greatest weaknesses of Realm during his government limiting its scope and efficiency.<br /> A dozen Great houses eliminated? I always thought they were less, half a dozen at the most, but it gives a good idea of \u200b\u200bhow dangerous Realm's politics are and how no house should take its position for granted.<br /> I like the mention that there are still many uninhabited places, unknown and with ruins of the past in the Blessed Island, it is worth remembering that not all this town and are clear places for PC adventures.<br /> The section of the social structure is fine, things that were known before but better organized. I am surprised that the natives of the Blessed Island can be enslaved, in earlier editions it was mentioned that it was completely forbidden no matter the crime to maintain the separation between commoners and slaves, because a native never was.<br /> So far it's pretty good and interesting, but there's still a lot to read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25189-dagurasu","name":"Dagurasu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T16:52:15-07:00","id":1314401,"title":"","content":"Now, with the establishment of the government out of the way, it\u2019s on to the subject of the masses. Beginning with the point that, while the Empress was concerned with establishing authority over the elites, the countryside was still tumultuous in a manner that local authorities found difficult to manage. We\u2019re given an early picture of legions inefficiently deployed to put down full uprisings, around the early patrician class needing to curtail them with shows of force or the occasional payoff. All of this to give a picture of why the Immaculate Order was integral to the development of the Realm; their system of matching exhortation that Dragon Blooded are rightful rulers with insistence that this is conditional on them displaying proper virtue and nobility is framed as a means of not merely reinforcing the desired hierarchies, but giving peasants a mechanism to protest. So we\u2019re given a picture of the state religion and class relations that is a bit more reciprocal and gives the workers a bit more agency, even if it\u2019s possibly a bit nominal for being filtered through authorities that serve the interests of the state. What\u2019s particularly interesting to me is the idea that the Empress finds the occasional peasant revolt to be an acceptable outlet for their frustrations, being generally neither a real nor rhetorical danger to her; I guess that\u2019s one of the benefits of being the literal saviour of the world (even if capitalised on by other development for her cult). The Unbroken Rushes Movement is still here, but in this adjusted milieu it stands out with the idea that the unknown monk leading it took the step of questioning the legitimacy of the Empress herself.<br /> <br /> I like how there\u2019s a description of the Order\u2019s role in widespread education, both for the means of projecting imperial propaganda and for presenting a beneficial amenity of the Blessed Isle. What\u2019s particularly interesting is that idea that there are time when the deft hand of the Empress deals with rebellion by pardoning and incorporating the good will of the rebel leaders, which not only maintains her majesty among the peasants but provides a means for her to reassign territories to the detriment of Dynasts. I find this true to life on a couple of counts, both of the monarchy that finds it advantageous to provide some level of support to the workers over the nobility (as the latter is often a more direct threat), and having read some accounts of nascent rebels against Qing emperors who ended up being won over as ardent supporters.<br /> <br /> Right, good, a sidebar on the subject of exactly how land ownership works; this is something I\u2019ve been particularly waiting for some word on. Yes, maintains the basic line from the original book still holds, that all land belongs to the Empress and its distribution is based on a series of leases connected to her. I will admit that I would have liked a bit more clarity on how the properties of Dynast and peasant intersect, but presuming that doesn\u2019t come up further in the book that\u2019s a question for later. The key point remains that Houses are increasing rents to acquire more revenue in the short term.<br /> <br /> For relations with the Threshold, I think some of the events have been adjusted slightly, placing the earliest drive for conquest a bit earlier than in prior Editions. I think the motives are changed a bit as well, with the focus on a perceived need for foreign resources. And it puts the satrapy system into the context of needing to govern despite limited staffing or infrastructure.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, it states that the earliest to acquiesce were those harmed by the Seven Tigers; I was thinking that meant concentrated in the South, but looking back those generals aren\u2019t explicitly tied to that Direction this time. I like the description of the Empress inviting sovereigns to take audience with her, as it recalls an image I\u2019ve always found attractive, of Napoleon demonstrating his power by conducting audience surrounded by the subdued princes of old European domains. The impression I\u2019m getting here is that the familiar satrapy system was something initially established subtly; of special trade incentives that evolved into full tribute, and defence of vassals transitioning into conquest of neighbours. I like that the employment of diplomats is referenced here, as I\u2019ve often felt that they were an under-realised quality in portraying Realm foreign policy, and I think it does a good job of tying things like Immaculate proselytizing to serving practical local needs (and vice versa with regards to reconstruction efforts to better facilitate extraction of resources for the Blessed Isle).<br /> <br /> The whole thing comes off in a very sinister light which I\u2019m enjoying, which adds to the credibility of the Empress becoming very good at this kind of management. I particularly like that idea that, in times when the Realm had neighbours who were better suited to warfare on even terms, the preference was to conquer neighbours and leave them isolated against the natural dangers of this setting rather than engage in costly direct war. Her approach to imperialism puts me in mind of the Simpsons\u2019 depiction of Bill Gates.<br /> <br /> Before going to the subject of the Anathema, I want to say that I find all of this to complement the picture given in Fangs at the Gate; they\u2019re both written with a slant inclined towards their respective subjects, and between the two one can probably get a more complete and complex picture. On that note, it\u2019s interesting how the small number of free Solars are presented as a comparatively more manageable threat than the Silver Pact. It certainly makes sense, given how the latter more uniformly had capabilities that would be suited for combatting such a foe, and were vastly more numerous. Fangs already did an exceptional job for Lunars, but it\u2019s helped along by presenting them from the perspective of their enemies, this idea that their dominions are hellish places to try and campaign in and that the Empress has needed to personally survive the occasional assassin come in the guise of a petitioner. It also reinforces the value that the Order and the Realm draw from one another, the former providing the more mobile and expert Wyld Hunts and the latter enabling them with provisions and official sanction. Description of the borders of the Threshold being defined by a game of cat and mouse in particular strengthen my perception that, historically, the greatest success of the Wyld Hunt would be in presenting a threat Lunars would be obligated to withdraw from.<br /> <br /> All right, going to give the timeline a quick perusal; I\u2019ll start with an appreciation for it being presented contiguously, rather than divided with sections of text. Let\u2019s see\u2026 I like being able to put numbers to descriptions of the process of establishing the magistracy and bring the military under the throne\u2019s control. Tepet\u2019s failed siege would be expected, but it\u2019s interesting to include the exile of Cevis Ghandarva. Scavenger Lands invasions are about the same\u2026 as is founding of the Deliberative, but for once we\u2019re getting more solid timelines for the development of Great Houses (interesting that it took more than a century). I\u2019ve always been very fond of House Jerah and Eyem (oracle-engine is an effective term in this altered milieu) and their understated role in the backstory of Ragara, so it\u2019s nice to see them placed here; interesting that Ragara was only 29 when he acquired his House. The conquest of Prasad is much earlier than I would have expected. Like how Bagrash Kol\u2019s achievements are attributed to use of a \u201cprimeval relic\u201d.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, what else\u2026 finally given a firm date for the reappearance of the Caul. Oh, <i>and</i> the beginning of current trade in the West; the fact that it\u2019s qualified as mortal presents an intriguing picture of what the previous situation might have been. Ah, and House Iselsi\u2019s assassination attempt is still preceded by a spectacular failure to invade the East, it\u2019s just that both events have been moved forward significantly rather than the House languishing for some four hundred years.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s a leaner timeline than in First Edition, and in most respects I\u2019d say it benefits from that. It drops a few extraneous details in favour of ones that provide a stronger sense for relevant history i.e. the relative ages of different Great Houses, and it effectively incorporates new details such as the dissolution of Shogunate gentes. The one area in which I\u2019d say the First Edition version has it beat would be in how that one used the opportunity to reference a few strange and intriguing events, but I\u2019ll grant that the approach to designing the setting in this Edition lends itself to doing the same thing elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Moving on, there\u2019s a bit more detailing for those enemies that have presented a meaningful challenge to the Realm as a whole. I like the placement of Bagrash Kol\u2019s empire as something that cemented the imperialism by presenting a threat that could overcome isolationist sentiments, and it\u2019s good to include more subtle and weird dangers such as a heresy promoted by Lunar infiltrator (again building heat for them by giving them operations on the Blessed Isle itself) and a sorcerous plague. Given how it\u2019s been discussed in the past, I\u2019d find that giving the Realm more vulnerabilities actually strengthens its presentation, because it can cultivate the idea that it\u2019s continuously proven the strengths of the Dragon Blooded rather than them resting on their laurels.<br /> Speaking of Dragon Blooded achievements, there\u2019s then the matter of Lookshy. I\u2019m a bit uncertain about returning with the idea that using the Defence Grid inflicts backlash on the Isle, but that\u2019s offset by the neat idea that reluctance to fire it at the Scavenger Lands was for fear of relics buried throughout rather than just the arsenals of Lookshy. I\u2019m kind of imagining that the framing of the three invasions as a battle with the city is meant to capture the Realm perspective specifically; that they would frame it as warfare against a defiant Shogunate legion and downplay the rest of the River Province, whereas the other side might still have more to say for the range of heroes and contributions provided by the likes of Sijan and Nexus. In any case, I think it presents a fun new dynamic to their relationship to have the idea that they both get more capital from facing off across the Inland Sea and spooking those behind them than they do from actually resolving the conflict.<br /> <br /> Now, the section introducing the Dynasty as part of the greater scope of the Realm begins with the point that despite innumerable lovers over the centuries (including the fact that they were women when they weren\u2019t well-bred Exalted), the Scarlet Empress has had few official marriages, Rawar being the first. It\u2019s interesting to me how this is folded into a bit of background for Ragara\u2019s ambitions; he\u2019s probably no less of a dick, but there\u2019s at least some motive behind it when there\u2019s a whole thing of the Empress having several children via lovers who held equal status to one born from her official consort. Hmm, the description of Sesus as a brother seems to be in error, probably ought to be corrected. In any case, it looks to me like a good way of establishing the Realm\u2019s concept of a line of succession (and the measures somebody might go to trying to secure it in earlier centuries) by way of anecdote.<br /> <br /> Description for the Thousand Mazy Paths ties nicely into all of the historical setup, with an especially good contrast against the original attempts to prevent outright civil war. The particulars given sound pretty good, that idea in which nobody has a clear shot at the top because there are too many obstacles and rivals, and I find it to give a reasonable basis for why Ragara was unimpeded before being suddenly stopped. It\u2019s also good for it to state right out that this was all something that made the state less effective even while it ensured the security of the autocrat. I think prior Editions were always a bit too coy concerning the hows and whys of Houses being raised up and cast down, so good on them for including a reference to that procedure; I believe that twelve fallen Houses might be a larger number than before, but it fits with making the Realm\u2019s history a bit more dynamic and giving the Houses more distinction and variety. I\u2019m glad to see the idea of her announcing a date for selecting an official successor being given a bit more focus, and think it\u2019s cute to point out that her disappearance is still very far off of her projection.<br /> <br /> I like the way that the description of the prior disappearances of the Empress aren\u2019t phrased in terms that offer a truth to precisely what she did as much as different perceptions shaped by the level of society the speculator comes from; it makes possibilities for what became of her a bit more open ended. The Council of the Empty Throne has a nice bit of grit to its membership and motives; I get a slight impression that the only people who could be considered trustworthy for participating in that decision were some of the oddities, somewhat on the periphery of the factions that they represented. Likewise the description of the manner in which the empire is breaking down manages to be both informative of the finer details and evocative with some nasty, grinding imagery. The sense I get from this is not quite that the Realm is ignominiously collapsing as it being a repository of vast power that is turning in on itself, and that would carry both the promise of interesting times for the participants and the threat that even in its death throes it might inflict disaster on the surroundings.<br /> <br /> Yes, I think this final section on the organization and distribution of power in the\u2026 the Scarlet Realm, that was the term that raised a query from me about accepted terms for the First Age. Anyway, this is a sensible place to stop. The whole subject has been well-handled in my eyes, with a real sense of terrible strength and far too much complexity and a feel for the presence of mighty, decadent, scheming mandarins even when it\u2019s not addressing them all by name. It\u2019s all sex and blood and money, painted in the picture of a historical treatise for an overly bureaucratised state. It\u2019s good stuff there.<br /> <br /> Mind, I would be interested to see if the lifestyle section winds it down a bit, focuses a bit more on the personable side.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T17:05:03-07:00","id":1314404,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314306#post1314306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I like it in idea, I'm personally not so big on the specific illustration of them though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can\u2019t help but wonder from the picture if they\u2019ll have kind of a Steven Universe Homeworld Gems dynamic where the type of mineral they\u2019re visibly composed of corresponds to their function in Jadeborn society, with the gemstones as the ambassadors, granite as laborers, presumably the soldiers might be iron, etc etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T20:19:40-07:00","id":1314442,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314404#post1314404\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I can\u2019t help but wonder from the picture if they\u2019ll have kind of a Steven Universe Homeworld Gems dynamic where the type of mineral they\u2019re visibly composed of corresponds to their function in Jadeborn society, with the gemstones as the ambassadors, granite as laborers, presumably the soldiers might be iron, etc etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not against it, although I'd hope there's still at least the option for Artisans to be very pretty statue-people, just with touches of inhumanity. To go with the Gems, it's like comparing the Pebbles or the very focused-purpose Gems to &quot;basically weird colored humans&quot; like the main cast.<br /> <br /> But making the other castes less dwarf and more ''weird earth elemental thing&quot; to people who don't know the details is perfectly in keeping and not even that far from the description used in previous editions!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2334-aliasi","name":"Aliasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2334_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-25T20:25:50-07:00","id":1314445,"title":"","content":"Chapter 3:<br /> - Like that, as with everything else, the Great Houses couldn't help but assign officers to the Legions for political reasons rather than merit. They absolutely do know better, but having a Legion that's loyal to the House is more important to them than having a Legion staffed by the best soldiers they could find.<br /> - Interesting that the state apparatus was afraid an Outcaste would take control of the Legions. Explains a lot, actually. At least the Great Houses can keep each other in check, so I guess it's an effective compromise.<br /> - Oh, and it turns out a few Outcastes did take their soldiers and conquer a kingdom for themselves.<br /> - More explanation for the banditry. Disaffected soldiers will do that to you.<br /> - I have actually been wondering why Tepet had Legions if all Legions were supposed to serve the Empress directly, and here's the explanation.<br /> - Service guarantees citizenship!<br /> <br /> Chapter 4:<br /> - And now we have the Immaculate Order.<br /> - I'd be interested in seeing a more detailed comparison of the heretical worship of the Immaculate Dragons and the worship of the community's local gods and spirits. How does the physical presence or absence of a god change people's beliefs and worship?<br /> - Few know the name of Malfeas? Clearly, this must be rectified. #PartyTime #HookersAndBlowForEveryone #MakeSureTheHookerIsn'tMara<br /> - I'm loving these Exigents.<br /> <br /> Chapter 5:<br /> - Now we have the Blessed Isle.<br /> - How much bad shit is going to happen when the Great Houses inevitably try to mine the Imperial Mountain?<br /> - I want to visit the haunted ruins of Meru.<br /> - &quot;Places to come from&quot; is a surprisingly compelling title for a sidebar.<br /> - That may be the weirdest Dead Poet Society reference ever.<br /> - &quot;Eccentric&quot; is one way to describe Ragara Bhagwei.<br /> - Goddamn it, Mnemon. Banish your demons once you're done playing with them.<br /> - Chanos Tsunbal makes me want the Getimian book yesterday.<br /> - So the Valley of the Ancients is multiple horror movies just waiting to happen.<br /> - Yet more places to come from!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T01:12:53-07:00","id":1314482,"title":"","content":"Something else I really appreciate. All the details on board and card games, I sense that someone involved in this was annoyed as I was about the lack of information on Dynastic past times besides Gateway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T08:01:46-07:00","id":1314542,"title":"","content":"So, I shall start the section describing life on the Blessed Isle with the sidebar that goes into life in the not-Blessed Isle. It\u2019s a good thing to have, seeing how perspectives on and relations with the satrapies has often seemed like a neglected subject, and contributed to the sense that the Realm\u2019s actual power abroad was limited.<br /> <br /> Hmm, it appears that Dynastic takes on foreign ideas are somewhere between haute couture and appropriation, adopted in exaggerated form and quickly forgotten while the lower levels of the upper crust go to significant expense to emulate them. For the time being, I\u2019m going to assume that a stasis among the peasantry is something that owes a lot to actual policies and sumptuary laws that curtail social mobility.<br /> <br /> The satrapy side being less influenced on the whole by cultural projection from the Realm is a bit less than what I expected, but makes sense with how there\u2019s a level of separation. I feel that it also speaks a bit to how the Blessed Isle doesn\u2019t exactly cultivate a national culture that could be projected in the first place, seeing how the elites very deliberately hold themselves apart. Thinking about the description of the Immaculate Order\u2019s presence, I do think there\u2019s something attractive in picturing the Blessed Isle as relatively diverse culturally, but locked into a form of uniformity by centrally controlled administration and established religious organisation. Hence, the Order\u2019s missionaries probably becomes the main way in which the Realm projects soft power, and some form of bringing them in line culturally.<br /> <br /> Okay, going on to the Blessed Isle proper. Speaking of diversity, I think this introduction does a good job of setting up the point that it\u2019s quite large, so in addition to being able to hold a massive number of cities and enormous agrarian countryside in which to generate and transmit wealth, it\u2019s still capable of holding a lot of nooks and crannies that go unobserved by the state and in which marginal people can take refuge. I\u2019ve sometimes wondered about how the distribution of the Dragon Blooded can function on the Isle, so if it\u2019s going for an idea that they\u2019re primarily urban then I\u2019m all for it (I imagine that many still maintain lavish country holiday homes, and that a rather sizable number of these contribute to the mentioned ruins when abandoned by the death or disinterest of their owners).<br /> <br /> Before continuing with the text, my eye is caught by the large splash art piece. I like the shading and colouring on this one; I find that it has something of a comic book style, but with a slight nod for the photorealism that has been popular with a few pieces in this Edition. I like how expressive and colourfully dressed and diverse in appearance the peasants are, and the inclusion of a few different character models for participating women (with the particular focus on one who looks like she was a normal farm worker prior to attempting to smash in the heads of soldiers with a mallet). The city makes for a nice backdrop with its towers; having recently read over the Making Of art book and its description of Realm architecture, I wonder if there will be an emphasis on how the overcrowded cities tend to build upwards.<br /> <br /> Speaking of building upwards, starting with the lowliest class in the Realm. I don\u2019t think there\u2019s very much new to the dispossessed (besides being called that rather than disenfranchised\u2026 which I\u2019d say makes sense, as there wasn\u2019t exactly a franchise to remove from them), just providing the circumstances in which they attain that status and the basically non-existent rights, although it does give a bit more weight to the cultural background of the practice (such as the strength of Dynast testimony). The detail on how dispossession is not a hereditary status stands out to me, as does the idea that in a lot of cases that can end up being a distinction without a difference; I can imagine a lot of interesting backstories or dramatic scenarios revolving around subjects such as having gotten adopted into a peasant family or needing to grow up with the limits imposed by your parents stigma. I want to say that this is a new detail, but can\u2019t quite find a direct First Edition description of the disenfranchised; Exalted: the Dragon Blooded merely refers to them as a matter of course, the core book gives a picture that is very different to what I\u2019m familiar with, and the idea of it as a punishment for failing to meet tax obligations seems to be introduced in Manacle &amp; Coin (which in context now looks like a recent practice). It would appear that the version I\u2019m most familiar with, resembling this one, originated in the Second Edition core\u2019s (excellent) description of the Realm. Anyway, I also find it interesting to have a sidebar referring to the matter of enslaving them, how there are propositions to make doing so legal, and opposition from the morally and commercially inclined alike.<br /> <br /> On that note, it goes into the matter of actual slaves, being viewed as actually a rank above. I get the impression that slavery is somewhat harsher in some respects now, with the idea that they have no rights whatsoever (I take it that the point about owning property refers to a thing in practice, rather than something that they\u2019re entitled to). I like the way that a sense of history and financial savvy is given to House Cynis (being now firmly established as less than three hundred years old) in the idea that they made even slavery non-hereditary to ensure that their peers would always need to purchase a steady stream of replacements; I suppose that such children are typically destined for Immaculate orphanages (between this and the dispossessed, the book does seem to have a bit of a priority of creating a significant number of parentless children). There\u2019s a compelling contrast between the point that there can be Dynasts who view slaves as friends and confidantes, at the same time as there is liable to be abuse as a matter of course, and the state itself is inherently traumatic. It kind of puts me in mind of stories in which a point-of-view character becomes trapped in the orbit of some charismatic but extremely dangerous and volatile figure, like The Last King of Scotland. The sidebar on abolitionism is a curiosity for having such an out-of-character reference, but makes its point on how the sentiment exists enough (not entirely for moral reasons) that there are legal limitations imposed.<br /> <br /> Now, the peasantry, a particular subject of interest to me, and right off the bat it does indeed clarify how it includes everybody between the labourer and the wealthy merchant. Honestly, I question this shift in terminology, as it strikes me as being ripe for confusion and rather out of sorts with the colloquial and historical use of the term \u201cpeasant\u201d. In discussion, it also seems to be something that is going to need to endlessly require qualifiers on whether or not one is speaking of the expected agrarian working class or people of significant means. I\u2019ve been suspicious of this since the Third Edition core, and now that it\u2019s laid down unambiguously I just do not understand it.<br /> <br /> That aside, it\u2019s an interesting bit of nastiness that Dynasts are empowered to some degree to inflict summary executions, and I do like it making the point that the richest merchants can overshadow some patricians, and thus be afforded a form of mobility.<br /> <br /> Now, the patricians. I\u2019ve often thought that the patricians occupied a bit of an awkward spot in the game, lying at an intersection of ideas and presumptions that never quite communicated with one another, ending with them being left in a spot envisioned as generic wealthy aristocrats (despite the idea that land ownership is all deferred to the Empress), and otherwise kind of ignored. So it\u2019s good here to clearly and unambiguously lay out precisely who they are in the very first line: the administrative class of the Realm. In an empire where rent-seeking is curtailed and aristocrats need to work for a living, your lower aristocracy becomes strictly defined as \u201cthe people who are drawn upon for all of the middle management and general bureaucratic processing work\u201d. It\u2019s clear, direct, and effectively plays off of both the established structure of the Realm as a whole and gives context to their relationships with the Great Houses.<br /> <br /> What\u2019s especially interesting is the idea that this leaves them in a position where Dynasts actually compete to secure <i>their</i> favour. I suppose that the luminaries of the Houses are practical enough to recognise that they need staff to manage their holdings and agendas, and that it costs less to make appeals to them than it would be to hold oneself loftily and find all of the good patricians scooped up by rivals; seeing as Dynasts need to work for a living, they don\u2019t quite have the luxury of making patricians crawl over one another in seeking their patronage. And it gives another concreate thing for Dynasts to actually intrigue over, as well as people to fill out their social scene beyond their own somewhat limited numbers. It\u2019s a good adjustment that provides a means for patricians to be a more engaging part of the Realm. The only thing I think it really sacrifices is my notion of Dynasts competing over control of wealthy heirs and heiresses, and really, who else cares about that?<br /> <br /> \u2026Or it could just be transferred to aristocracies of the satrapies.<br /> <br /> And in the interests of making the Blessed Isle more engaging across all levels, there\u2019s the point about how patricians end up in a closer proximity to the peasantry. For the sake of my sanity, I\u2019m going to combine that reference to patrician landowners with a few other details placed around the book and read it as an intended shorthand for \u201cpatricians assigned to administer peasant-worked estates on behalf of the Empress or Dynasts\u201d rather than literally being in possession of land from which they can live off the rents. Oy.<br /> <br /> And a little reference to the value of begetting Dragon Blooded from the perspective of patricians. I\u2019m picturing an intriguing chain of interests and power plays, in which you have Dynasts plying patricians with vast wealth, who then employ that to attract marriage from outcastes, and work that towards a situation in which one of their own Exalts and they get to have higher negotiations with the Dynasts.<br /> <br /> What Fire Has Wrought of course does the bulk of the work describing the Dragon Blooded in the Realm, but a summary is not amiss, and I think it\u2019s a prime opportunity to clarify the exact place of the unExalted Dynast; namely, still ranked far above the patricians and objects of significant wealth and privilege, at the same time as being on the lowest rung of their family ladders and knowing it. Outcastes have some of the same benefits due to Dynasts, but lack the proper status unless they can actually join a Great House, and otherwise end up being the top dogs in the lower patrician class. Cadet house members are technically Dynasts but are viewed as lacking the social graces, and are simply less wealthy, aaaand the true members of the Scarlet Dynasty are placed at the top of the pyramid, this being where it reintroduces the point that at any given time, about half are employed and the rest are engaged in grandiose personal pursuits.<br /> <br /> The description on Dynast naming conventions was something I quite appreciated in What Fire Has Wrought, since I think it\u2019s helpful to get a feel for the community, actually guide the creation of characters, and offsets a bit of prior books putting out names without much apparent consistency. Hence, I\u2019m glad that the same standard is being applied across the whole Realm society. Let\u2019s see\u2026 most peasants having no family names and tend to go for short names referencing nature, makes sense; I\u2019ll be interested to see if any of those modified immigrant cultures show up. I like the idea that patricians can often end up with family names that, in using natural imagery, speak to peasant origins; I\u2019m imagining some cutting Dynast wordplay that draws attention to that in a manner intended to be derisive. I take it that their given names are similarly made up and euphonic. Dynast conventions are already known, and I like how outcastes include a point of people who\u2019ll often change their name to reflect their newly achieved status; their relationship to family names is also quite distinct. And I\u2019m amused by the idea that the Realm might occasionally press down on satrapies to remove the names of noteworthy rebels from circulation.<br /> <br /> Right, getting into the gritty details of daily life now. Interesting choice to begin with marriage and family; not much to say on that beyond liking how the matriarchal values are promulgated through patricians and wealthier peasants emulating the matchmaking of the Dynasty. It makes sense that the Realm is not actually sexually liberated (amounting to liaisons being kept discrete), and having recently started reading an autobiography from a Chinese writer, I\u2019m intrigued by the notion that women of the Blessed Isle might engage in a system of concubinage similar to Chinese men. You know, thinking about that might put the matter of wealthy merchants being classed as peasants into perspective to me\u2026 setting that aside, it strikes me as another thing that might reinforce matriarchal qualities, as well as perhaps a tendency towards smaller families; in a system where a man can have multiple women bearing legal children, the size of his family reflects his status, but with a woman with multiple men I suppose it becomes a system where the identity of the father is given less significance and the family values become more concentrated. Perhaps that would lend itself to why the lower classes end up with closer relationships to their children than Dynasts have.<br /> <br /> And then you have slaves and the dispossessed engaging in the age-old practices of conducting marriage among themselves regardless of legal sanction, as an assertion of their basic humanity.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve always had a fondness for the description of contemporary trends in Dragon Blooded armour, so I\u2019m glad that there\u2019s a sidebar (again effectively abridged) here referring to the prevailing styles, while also making the point of how they fit into Immaculate aniconism; I\u2019d also say it\u2019s good to rename the somewhat generic Elemental style to Dragonscale. Festivals being such a significant aspect of pre-modern life makes them something I think the game ought to have given more attention to, so I\u2019m glad that there\u2019s a sidebar addressing the subject and laying down some standards, particularly for it going beyond the Immaculate calendar and into various local practices and state proscriptions. The idea of the Realm having a feast of fools during Calibration, that even Dynasts engage in, is especially intriguing.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m finding that the approach to peasant clothing (even within the bounds of the definition being broadened) to be a bit more sophisticated; the working class being not presumed to wear ragged material speaks a bit better for them, as well as the notion that they might have some different wear for retiring. I like how clothing styles in general are given quite a bit more elaboration and variation, since I do think the First Edition rendering of \u201ceverybody in white body wraps\u201d was a little bit\u2026 uninspired. Also the way that they\u2019re attributed jewellery, which even if not made of precious material probably has a fine artistry.<br /> <br /> I find something amusing in the idea that patrician fashions include togas, as it gives me the impression of trying very hard to look ostentatious; similar goes for how they employ especially large, eye-grabbing hats. I like how there\u2019s an idea that male fashions are designed to be slinky, for the sake of being attractive to Dynasts.<br /> <br /> After all of that, it\u2019s fun how the Dynasts themselves revert to structurally simpler and more practical forms of clothing, while also being lavishly cut and coloured to ensure that they stand out. It gives me this impression that the status of Dynasts is intended to be denoted with fine materials and expert tailoring, but also a capacity to rapidly engage in action as needed; you wouldn\u2019t want to go around in clothing that you could end up being entangled in, or that heavy action would lead to being torn up. It makes them stand out a bit amidst historical aristocracy, where the intention was to specifically flaunt one not needing to engage in physical strains with voluminous clothing and delicate features; I see some of that in the idea that their unExalted members like sturdy boots and gloves.<br /> <br /> Now, let\u2019s see about this art and architecture. My memory of the Dragon Blooded relationship to aniconism was shaky, but I\u2019m glad to see that they\u2019re afforded an exception for the sake of their mansions and estates being decorated with portrait and sculpture, even if the general attitude has given the Realm a sophisticated tradition of calligraphy, illumination and filigree that I\u2019d imagine even the less pious Dynasts would want to get in on. It\u2019s also good that there\u2019s a reminder that when sculptures come up, they\u2019re liable to be painted. The description of architectural styles and priorities is less elaborate than I would have looked, but I\u2019m happy it conveys a clear aesthetic, and it\u2019s nice to have a detailed standard for the format of Dynast houses considering they\u2019re likely to be a central feature of many games. It also speaks to their distinct luxury and wealth that they don\u2019t merely go in for singular, if enormous buildings; that also lends itself to my presumption that their homes are frequently abandoned and left to ruin, owed to things like the probable expense of maintaining them and the likelihood that they\u2019re so tailored to the needs and preferences of the individual that it\u2019s often hard to find somebody else that would want them.<br /> <br /> Furniture and house decorations would be a major element of conspicuous consumption in such an environment, so good to focus a bit on the trends there. I\u2019d say that it strikes a good balance of folding in the supernatural qualities of the setting by referencing the use of geomancy in construction of actual manses, while also grounding it in the idea that such patterns are valued even in buildings that wouldn\u2019t overtly harness Essence.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m going to take a break there, as I\u2019ve got some thing to do. As I was hoping, thus far the cultural sections are more down-to-earth and humanising, while still playing service to the Realm\u2019s particular stratification and varying oppression throughout.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:07:56-07:00","id":1314577,"title":"","content":"So Chanos Tsunbal... Getimian?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:13:49-07:00","id":1314579,"title":"","content":"Top of page 180: &quot;It doesn't heave into view over the horizon, as land ought.&quot;<br /> <br /> In Creation, shouldn't land heaving into view over the horizon be a shocking and alien experience? Should &quot;horizon&quot; even be a word?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25206-evil-midnight-lurker","name":"Evil Midnight Lurker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25206_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:16:21-07:00","id":1314580,"title":"","content":"Creation's horizon and most of the complicated physics things work like irl does for the sake of familiarity and not having to pack even more information into the setting in order to play the game","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23684-alistair","name":"Alistair","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23684_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:22:28-07:00","id":1314582,"title":"","content":"Well that's just weird.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25206-evil-midnight-lurker","name":"Evil Midnight Lurker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25206_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:27:43-07:00","id":1314583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Evil Midnight Lurker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314582#post1314582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well that's just weird.</div> </div> </div> </div>The alternative is everyone constantly having to manually adjust their expectations of how the world looks and behaves all the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:28:22-07:00","id":1314584,"title":"","content":"There's always a horizon even on a flat surface. The vanishing line is a principle most folks who have taken any art are familiar with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:28:59-07:00","id":1314586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314577#post1314577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Chanos Tsunbal... Getimian?</div> </div> </div> </div>I really was worried everything we saw about them prior to their book being released was going to be about how Rakan Thulio is a powerful renegade Sidereal.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:35:50-07:00","id":1314588,"title":"","content":"Fun bit on the horizon thing:<br /> <a href=\"https://www.askamathematician.com/2012/08/q-if-earth-was-flat-would-there-be-the-horizon-if-so-what-would-it-look-like-if-the-earth-was-flat-and-had-infinite-area-would-that-change-the-answer/\" target=\"_blank\">Q: If Earth was flat, would there be a horizon? If so, what would it look like? If the Earth was flat and had infinite area, would that change the answer?</a><br /> <br /> Basically, for human purposes, the Earth is technically flat just by dint of it's total curve being much smaller than you can perceive. A flat world like Creation would not only have a horizon, it'd basically be in teh same spot. The aritcle goes on a bit about curved light from gravity and infiite planes, but Creation don't have to worry about those really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:37:54-07:00","id":1314590,"title":"","content":"I also noticed that the Imperial Mountain is just a small blur to people on the coast now (possibly it always was, but it's clear).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:41:26-07:00","id":1314591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stephen Lea Sheppard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314583#post1314583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The alternative is everyone constantly having to manually adjust their expectations of how the world looks and behaves all the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>...this works just fine in settings like Hollow Earth Expedition or Mystara's Hollow World, which are considerably more alien-feeling than a mere flat ocean.<br /> <br /> And the unaided human eye can't make out anything past about twelve miles of sea-level atmosphere, which is only about four times as far as Earth's horizon anyway.<br /> <br /> About the only good reason I can think of to force an apparent curvature of the world is that it would invalidate Creation-Slaying Oblivion Kick. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25206-evil-midnight-lurker","name":"Evil Midnight Lurker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25206_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T13:54:10-07:00","id":1314593,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314590#post1314590\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also noticed that the Imperial Mountain is just a small blur to people on the coast now (possibly it always was, but it's clear).</div> </div> </div> </div>The Imperial Mountain's only size descriptor so far in 3e is that it's described described &quot;...rises miles high into the sky...&quot; Note that this could be like, say, 10 miles high and almost twice as tall as Everest. If you plugged that into a calculator, that would mean at the imperial city, it'd be about 19 arc minutes above the horizon, about 2/3 the size of sun and moon here on Earth. Being that size, and blocked by things like the air (which is likely not transparent), clouds, buildings, and all that, seems about right to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T14:43:36-07:00","id":1314599,"title":"","content":"It just seems to me that this is a missed opportunity for Creation to be <i>entertainingly</i> <i>weird</i>, and it would be nice to see descriptions like &quot;The Caul does not fade into view in the far azure distance, as islands ought.&quot;<br /> <br /> I do concede the argument on the concept of horizons, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25206-evil-midnight-lurker","name":"Evil Midnight Lurker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25206_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-26T16:45:57-07:00","id":1314606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314586#post1314586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really was worried everything we saw about them prior to their book being released was going to be about how Rakan Thulio is a powerful renegade Sidereal.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're getting a full write-up - factions, QCs, powerset explanation, basically everything you need for an ST to run one - in Crucible of Legend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Neall Raemonn Price</span><br /> Beleaguered Scion Developer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","name":"Neall","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar137_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1204"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T04:05:51-07:00","id":1314679,"title":"","content":"Oh wow. Didn't expect that. Is it basically intended to be the equivalent of the Liminal and Exigent (and Lunar, and Sidereal, and DB) parts of the Antagonist section?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T04:13:18-07:00","id":1314682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neall</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314606#post1314606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They're getting a full write-up - factions, QCs, powerset explanation, basically everything you need for an ST to run one - in Crucible of Legend.</div> </div> </div> </div>I saw in the Ask thread and I'm glad that we're abandoning the previous team's Fog of War approach to new content.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T05:42:58-07:00","id":1314692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314679#post1314679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh wow. Didn't expect that. Is it basically intended to be the equivalent of the Liminal and Exigent (and Lunar, and Sidereal, and DB) parts of the Antagonist section?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's going to go into greater depth than that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T08:31:20-07:00","id":1314708,"title":"","content":"So, the subject of pets and similar animals is just given a sidebar this time around. It\u2019s adding a few touches of the exotic and fantastical throughout, such as the idea of snakes as common for pets and disposal of vermin. Giant wolf spiders as guard animals has been a notion that I had, although I would often have pictured them being exclusive to Dynasts, while I\u2019m curious about the use of llamas in that capacity; swans are self-explanatory of course. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The kinds of exotic pets that actually are kept by the Dragon Blooded make me think about the likely extreme additions to their houses that would be needed to accommodate them, the hippopotamus in particular.<br /> <br /> Not much to say about the cuisine, the foodstuffs at each level seem fairly standard. I\u2019ve always liked how Dynasts have a fondness for things like curries, and I find the range of meats to effectively convey their significant disparity in wealth (that alone seems like something that would compel many patricians to get invited to feasts), and the inclusion of distilled spirits adds a touch of the anachronistic as a further illustration of those privileges. No, the bigger thing is not what the people eat, but how; the contrast between how people even up to the patricians eat communally to reinforce the closeness of their families with the greater reservation of Dynasts. The idea that a lot of these people eat alone in their private rooms, it conveys a certain loneliness to me that I find compelling, at the same time as being another instance of them appearing to drop some of the ostentations of real life aristocracies. The premise of Dynastic parents using dining with their children as the process for inspecting them sounds like it could make for some very tense scenes, and I\u2019m interested in the idea that among the things they gather to dine for are honouring servants. Finally there\u2019s the matter of commercial food industries, which I think both benefits clarifying on some of the differences of modern day businesses (particularly the idea that inns for travellers are places where you eat what\u2019s made available, rather than having a custom choice) and that urban life often relies on the local equivalent of fast food.<br /> <br /> Temples seem a lot more reserved than in prior Editions (where there was talk of large cities being home to thousands of them), and I personally need to remind myself that they wouldn\u2019t be distributed in the manner of something like Christian churches servicing parishes of a few hundred or thousand people for weekly congregations. It gives me the impression of the temples as more distant, impressive structures, held apart from regular life. The description of how the wealthy might fund higher quality shrines in the occasional village is a nice way of giving them a sense of presence in areas where they\u2019re unlikely to be personally present ever.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s a good idea to make a point of how the existence of the actual supernatural informs spirituality and folk beliefs, for people who can\u2019t quite be sure what to attribute to the powers of spirit or Exalt. I like the idea that folk heresies for the Immaculate Philosophy can be focused on distinctive landscape features and memorable unlikely events, and the basis for their numerology sounds clever; I especially like the way that it works in associating the number four with death and discord. I also think that it makes good use out of the traditional idea that ghosts have had little actual presence on the Isle to actually create more elaborate details, rather than write it off in a straightforward manner, especially the way that this is tied in to some of the greater historical presence of Great House founders. It seems like a slightly missed opportunity to not have a reference to ideas about the Lunars here, but introducing awareness of the Mountain Folk by way of changeling myths makes up for it a bit. I\u2019m also finding this to make a good contribution to a sense of some cultural diversity across the Isle, by attributing particular folk beliefs to specific zones.<br /> <br /> Ah yes, the developers had said about the Immaculate perspective on mortal thaumaturgy and sorcery, how it\u2019s frowned upon but acceptable if properly trained and employed well (especially in the service of authorities). I\u2019m not sure what it is, but I like something in the wording used to describe that process, although it gets a lot juicier with the ideas of how more malevolent powers are perceived, utilised and punished. I particularly like how that includes those who don\u2019t have intrinsic powers, but have made bargains with ghosts and gods to harm their enemies. Same goes with the idea of charlatans and how they\u2019re particularly liable to claim congress with the ghosts of treacherous fallen Houses to give credibility to their offers. And I\u2019m glad that it devotes some attention to the existence of folk magics employed by the populace at large without direct invocation of supernatural power, since it makes the people more real with acknowledgement of culture separate from such things. This is the kind of thing that made me glad for the shift away from Second Editon thaumaturgy.<br /> <br /> The representation of the Black-Helms makes them look very distinct and outstanding in a way that I can imagine makes law enforcement a very visible presence on the Blessed Isle. Heh, I was actually wondering if such dress might impose a particular burdensome expense on the state, so it\u2019s nice that the question is actually addressed in their description, with how such a thing is reserved for the prefectures that can afford it, and otherwise you just get the headgear, belt and buckle and sword, and anything else needs to be purchased individually. It\u2019s interesting to me that it operates as a police force sufficient to actually investigate crimes, possibly even in a preventative manner, although something in the description of them collecting rumours leaves me thinking that in a lot of cases the investigations aren\u2019t precisely rigorous. It gives me an impression that the investigative quality of the Guardians is slightly more focused on presenting the image of the Empress\u2019 justice rooting out criminals, rather than being too concerned with the accuracy of it.<br /> <br /> That description of how rural Guardians are appointed in pairs gives me a picture of a lot of misfit duos in the backwater. Also like the way it sets up Guardians as possible forces for magistrates to requisition at need. That the Imperial Force is not only appointed by the Greater Chamber of the Deliberative, but is now described as answerable only to them, gives me an impression that for all of the prestige of being appointed to them they might ultimately wind up as more of a token force (barring Dynast crimes that are harmful to the interests of the state and Empress in particular); this is reinforced a bit by describing Dynasts as generally only lightly touched by the law. The image that I\u2019m getting is that the Imperial Force isn\u2019t exactly there to enforce the law on Dynasts as it is to collate reports passed along by witnessing Guardians, and then deployed to detain such Dynasts in the event that Empress or Deliberative has some political gain from actually prosecuting. Otherwise, it doesn\u2019t quite seem to be open season, but I suspect that Dynasts who never give the authorities reason to dislike them and keep an appropriate level of discretion can get away with a fair amount. Still, that idea that even when a Dynast does something extreme enough to oblige the Black-Helms to step in they\u2019re required to be supremely deferential amuses me a bit.<br /> <br /> Ohh dear, my initial read on the title of one of those sidebars was that \u201cShun\u201d was actually the name of a lawyer that we were being given an anecdote about. So, the Realm does not permit the inclusion of trained legal professionals in judicial proceedings? Not even for civil matters? That\u2019s very curious; even Medieval Europe had lawyers. For criminal law, this would be another thing that would make me think that the concern of the Realm is the appearance of speedily detaining and convicting a plausible subject of the crime rather than being overly concerned with actually getting the correct person (with due process perhaps being close to a foreign concept). Not quite sure what to make of excluding advocates from civil proceedings; it would seem to stack the deck heavily against the citizenry on <i>either</i> side of any given case, and place enormous power in the hands of the judges. The sidebar on exile looks mostly tailored for creating interesting backstories to Dynasts abroad from the Blessed Isle, and the particularly fun scenario of their children born after the fact being transported back to participate in Dynastic life.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve got to say, on the whole law and order in the Realm seems like the kind of thing that a majority of people would want to avoid participating in if they can manage it. One thing I\u2019m curious about is how, for all the mention of the power of Guardians to hand out summary punishments, what the substance of those punishments might be. This would seem like the place to put them, but I\u2019ll reserve full judgement until I see the section detailing the Imperial judges themselves.<br /> <br /> Putting criminal activity on the Blessed Isle into perspective is a good move, as well as how it provides ideas for what the operations of such groups might be. I like the way that the loss of the Merchant Fleet\u2019s revenues has made one of the fleets of the so heavily lauded House Peleps to become a lot more amenable to accepting bribes to permit contraband to sneak past them. It\u2019s nice to utilise the point of the Isle still having unexplored and exploited locales here for the sake of adding a form of criminal activity unique to the setting, as well as the sense that it can be a particular way for the lower classes to reap enormous profit if they\u2019re daring or clever enough; it ties into my thoughts of long abandoned Dynast homes, and how these might have been subject to the occasional scavengers rooting around in the hopes of finding something valuable and powerful left behind, while needing to evade the authorities. And it all adds effectively to the point that the modern Realm is beginning to break down as greater taxation pressure and more lax enforcement leave much criminal activity with room to expand.<br /> <br /> For the example criminal organisations, I wonder if the association with certain Great Houses is meant to be a more recent development, or inform the lax attitude that Dynasts generally have towards the law; perhaps a layer of separation that can be employed to make it harder for the Empress or Deliberative to have effective pretences against them. Perhaps it simply varies; a group like the Jade Hand mostly operating in its own right but finding itself benefiting from the assistance of some Great Houses who see the value of such a group existing in the overall weave of their elaborate schemes, where something like the Shansin Six essentially operates as a branch of a House\u2019s operations (while probably still making its own decisions on a few of its sidelines). It\u2019s enough to make me think that crime syndicates are more than just groups established and run by the Great Houses, even if the power of those groups pervades enough that it can be hard to avoid not being under some level of their influence. As for the Gallows Dogs, I enjoy the descriptions of some of the people they have on hand to carry out hits on the Exalted, such as an Iselsi scion (natural place for one to end up) and Exigent.<br /> <br /> Okay, after all of that heavy subject matter, I\u2019ll be happy to wind it down a bit with what the people do for fun\u2026 which, like the clothing, is going to appropriately challenge the level of my vocabulary\u2026 oh, it\u2019s a ring toss game. It would appear to me that the preference among most classes is for straightforward games that employ easily acquired or no equipment, while the upper classes go for ones that require items of particular expense and expertise, such as weapons and land vehicles. I\u2019d suppose that this might be a factor in why ball games tend to be transitional (perhaps in addition to not quite having a climate to support any given one in the long term). Really, it\u2019s interesting on the whole to present some games as having passed out of fashion or newly starting to come into it; attributing card games as something being newly taken up by the upper classes lends a mild veneer of, I\u2019d say somewhere around 17th or 18th century European aristocracy. Also good to make a point about various board games being popular among the peasantry (I find the name \u201chunt-the -Anathema\u201d to be particularly amusing), as a contrast to the inclusion of animal and human blood sports; gladiatorial fights are an unexpected inclusion, but I\u2019d say they help contribute to some of the underlying brutality of the Realm, even if they probably end up more like how the worked in real life than the lurid popular perception of them. And I like the Immaculate Order\u2019s nuanced views on sports gambling (at the same time as their reservation is of the classist \u201cit\u2019s a way for the poors to get more money than they deserve\u201d rather than the consequences it can have for destroying people). Nothing to say on the sidebar for playing cards beyond appreciating them giving a visual guideline that places them in the setting\u2019s context.<br /> <br /> All right, laying out clearly that the Blessed Isle makes use of printing and has a literary industry; I\u2019d been wondering if books might be a good that they can produce for export. In the Time of Tumult, can probably provide a means of spreading subversive ideas through the distribution of subversive tracts. I think the plays and storytelling are fairly standard (although its good to have a reminder of them across class lines), but the paragraph on music does a nice job of giving a sense of its form and variety, as well as some of the terminology employed in character and locations in which to find such; the Jade Cockerel genre in particular intrigues me, while I\u2019m wondering if Eight Winds might be regarded as a sort of uncouth form. While Dynasts maintaining privately and lavishly housed collections of artwork and various curiosities (or looted treasures) is something that I both expected and can readily picture, having visited assemblies of such places in some old English manors relatively recently. More unexpected would be the sense that Immaculate assemblies of relics and religious artwork would probably be the closest equivalent to a public museum, although it makes sense and can probably be very attractive and edifying to people in the setting.<br /> <br /> Hmm, travel looks like a particularly invested topic, I\u2019ll save that for a bit later.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T08:37:20-07:00","id":1314710,"title":"","content":"Something else I noticed that has been alluded to a lot, but I don't think not specified with exact numbers, is that the West (Coral, Wavecrest, etc) was only discovered by the Realm 100 years ago.<br /> I think that works well, there's a lot that can be done with that; presumably there's still lots of the West that's uncharted or undiscovered. And it also means there's probably a lot of areas that know little of the Realm. It makes the Pelleps-V'neef struggle for the West a bit more interesting. And I assume more will be said in the Western book.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T09:21:09-07:00","id":1314715,"title":"","content":"Another thing I liked is that, like Lunars' dominions, both the Prefectures and Satrapies are described from north clockwise around. So north, then north-east, then east, then south-east, etc. It just makes it much easier to follow without having to keep looking back at the map to see where things are. Whereas the old Compass books tended to just put things in a random order, or put the biggest things first (which isn't terrible, but I think it makes them seem more isolated, whereas if you write about one place and then the place next to it, and then the place next to that, it gives a better idea of how they interact).<br /> <br /> I hope they keep this for Across the Eight Directions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T09:40:05-07:00","id":1314719,"title":"","content":"Really love the Valley of the Ancients, it shows both of how clever and cunning Big Red was. She had to keep the ghosts of a valley filled with ancient Solar Tombs happy, so she turns it into an expensive settlement where habitation is a rare and prized reward but fills it with a ton of expensive regulations to essentially use the people to maintain and keep the ghosts happy.<br /> <br /> Its also cunning because there is no way she does not know that the second she is gone they will crack open the tombs and get all of their dumb butts slaughtered by dozens of angry Solar Ghosts and their minions (only a Ragara would think robbing the tomb of a guy that seems to have invented necromancy is a good idea).<br /> <br /> Although its got me curious for how they are going to build ghosts this edition, since it seems quite difficult to believe the claim that each ghost is a match for a veteran Hearth (and feats like destroying an entire household in one night seems well outside of what anything short of a Deathlord could do in past editions).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T09:44:34-07:00","id":1314723,"title":"","content":"If anything, I would think from how it sounds, the Ancients are very much stronger then the likes of Nephwracks, though that also brings into question how strong are the Deathlords this edition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T09:52:52-07:00","id":1314724,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iceblade44</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314723#post1314723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If anything, I would think from how it sounds, the Ancients are very much stronger then the likes of Nephwracks, though that also brings into question how strong are the Deathlords this edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've always been skeptical of Deathlords as written tbh, one of those things where I tend to not like to use stats for them in my games. Sort of feels like it always falls short of the point, but 3rd is pretty good with scale so I am cautiously optimistic.<br /> <br /> The Ancients as a whole are a very cool bit of worldbuilding, even if the Realm Before was very diffuse it still adds up that Exalted would have dedicated burial grounds or have fallen together as a Circle instead of the usual and pretty bizarre tendency to have their tombs be so isolated despite the importance of teamwork to Exalts.<br /> <br /> Frankly, I also like that it creates an obvious but logical threat on the Blessed Isle. Its a problem that was always either too costly to fix or had very useful consequences, so it adds up that the Emp wouldn't have bothered to fix it. A crusade would have potentially hurt the Grid's power flow, cost her lives and have potentially added artifacts and magic into circulation that she probably didnt want in the hands of her scions. Leaving it alone gives her something useful to distract ambitious people with, puts many of them in the same place and almost guarantees that they will get themselves killed if they are reckless enough to potentially challenge her.<br /> <br /> As I said, it makes the Red Emp a clever ruler to take a potentially apocalyptic threat and make it into a regulator check against ambition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T10:18:33-07:00","id":1314729,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314715#post1314715\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another thing I liked is that, like Lunars' dominions, both the Prefectures and Satrapies are described from north clockwise around. So north, then north-east, then east, then south-east, etc. It just makes it much easier to follow without having to keep looking back at the map to see where things are. Whereas the old Compass books tended to just put things in a random order, or put the biggest things first (which isn't terrible, but I think it makes them seem more isolated, whereas if you write about one place and then the place next to it, and then the place next to that, it gives a better idea of how they interact).<br /> <br /> I hope they keep this for Across the Eight Directions.</div> </div> </div> </div>The chapter schema for <i>Across the Eight Directions</i> is clockwise by Direction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T11:32:15-07:00","id":1314742,"title":"","content":"The parts about the trip I liked a lot, they reminded me a bit of Rokugan with their travel documents and bandits who commit crimes in one territory and go to another to avoid punishment. The Great Coast Road I guess it's a Sorcery Working, I wonder what Circle exactly.<br /> The section of games I liked a lot, I only wish that they could have been treated in more detail.<br /> My favorite part is that of the homes, especially the Dynastics, really highlights with an admirable economy of words the separation and isolation between family members. Especially progenitors and descendants.<br /> The sidebar of discrimination I think treats the matter correctly.<br /> The details of the political operation are very useful for any GM.<br /> The excellent section of the Great Houses, a good reminder of how the Kingdom is being torn deeply even before the civil war begins.<br /> Very satisfied with the first chapter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25189-dagurasu","name":"Dagurasu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T12:34:28-07:00","id":1314759,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314710#post1314710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something else I noticed that has been alluded to a lot, but I don't think not specified with exact numbers, is that the West (Coral, Wavecrest, etc) was only discovered by the Realm 100 years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it was full-on <i>discovered</i> only 100 years ago. Fajad's whole backstory involves it being the gateway to the West before the Wu-Jian route was discovered, so clearly there was <i>some</i> trade before that. And I'll note that the Caul isn't that much further from the southern tip of the Western archipelago than Fajad is from the north-eastern tip, so I'd think that in the times when the Realm controlled the majority of the Caul, they could have used that as a gateway too. So I think the West was known, but not easily accessible, up until the Wu-Jian route was opened up. <br /> <br /> All that said, I definitely agree with the idea that the West was only recently opened up to the Realm is a good approach. It means that the West is either freshly conquered, or still available for conquest, giving lots of opportunities for Dragon-Blooded PCs to either participate in that, or possibly try to stop it or make it less bad, and for non-DBs to fight against it. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T12:38:59-07:00","id":1314764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dagurasu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314742#post1314742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Great Coast Road I guess it's a Sorcery Working, I wonder what Circle exactly.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we assume the whole Coast Road is a singular working, it's pretty much <i>gotta</i> be Solar, and I'd say Ambition 3 - Solar Ambition 2 is &quot;Alter major metaphysical properties of a city-sized region&quot;, and the Coast Road is by far bigger than a city-sized region. It <i>could</i> be done as a series of smaller workings, of course, but the way it's described sure seems to me to be all once piece. So, chalk it up to some ambitious sorcerer of the 1st Age. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T13:54:59-07:00","id":1314788,"title":"","content":"I'd dont think it be that high, at least not 3. Mainly because I wouldn't describe the properties of the Road as major metaphysical changes. It self heals and it cuts travel time by a fifth. Not that big. It is likely Solar yeah because of the scale, I just don't buy those properties being the result of a high ambition","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T14:41:57-07:00","id":1314799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314759#post1314759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fajad's whole backstory involves it being the gateway to the West before the Wu-Jian route was discovered, so clearly there was <i>some</i> trade before that.</div> </div> </div> </div>A good point, and interestingly I see that Fajad was only conquered by the Realm a century ago. So I guess that it is, as you say, that it opened up to the Realm 100 years ago, rather than being entirely undiscovered.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T14:47:56-07:00","id":1314803,"title":"","content":"One thing I like a lot about the way 3rd ed locations, satrapies especially, are written, is that the writers now seem to actually consider a)distance, and b)the development of history since the Contagion.<br /> <br /> In 2nd ed, it often seemed like the Realm conquered stuff all over the place in the first two hundred years, and then everything basically stayed the same. And it often seemed like the satrapies somehow teleported tribute and troops between the Realm with no thought for the difficulties of distance.<br /> <br /> Whereas in 3rd ed you've got a Realm that expanded slowly across the world, slowly across more and more, but in the last hundred years it's expanded so far that it's become over-extended, unable to easily reach it's far-flung domains, making it vulnerable to the Lunars. And now, in this time of decline, a sudden confluence of disasters has hit, which could very well lead to its collapse, or at least massive reduction in power/size.<br /> <br /> It seems much more realistic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T15:10:08-07:00","id":1314813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314588#post1314588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fun bit on the horizon thing:</div> </div> </div> </div>Found that a while ago myself. TL;DR: the difference between Creation\u2019s horizon and Earth\u2019s is that in Creation things won\u2019t go <i>over</i> the horizon, they\u2019ll just get smaller and smaller until you can\u2019t see them any more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T16:54:58-07:00","id":1314829,"title":"","content":"Wonder what that means for the sea routes to the Caul and from An-Teng, the Realm only &quot;discovering&quot; routes to the West that is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-27T17:28:38-07:00","id":1314840,"title":"","content":"I feel as though historically there have been somewhat mixed messages about banditry on the Blessed Isle; it\u2019s been referenced sure, even designated as a responsibility of Realm officials, but other parts struck me as asserting that, in its heyday, authority within the Realm was absolute enough to keep such things to a minimum. I like this statement that the sheer size of the Isle affords its lengthy roads enough space for bandit gangs to operate both for its clarity, and in its own right, a detail of the Isle and area in which to set stories. The same goes for the other side of that, of the idea that one of the activities of imperial legions is to simply march the highways of the Realm and drive the bandits before them, or that travel tends to require convoys or armed escort. This is the thing, it doesn\u2019t let imperial power there be so absolute as to be static and boring at the same time as it holds that whenever it comes around, the only viable option is to retreat. Heh, come to think of it, this handily resolves my wonder of where one might set the characters of Ronja rovardotter (having given the anime version my attention recently); twould be a good use for some of those long abandoned forts or mansions. Other than that, that\u2019s a nice breakdown of class distinctions for road and vehicles.<br /> <br /> You know, I\u2019ve always been a little bit uncertain about the Great Coast Road\u2019s self-repair capabilities, so that medicine goes down easier with the notion that it\u2019s been gradually becoming less efficient. I wonder if it always had that capacity to shorten journeys; in any event, it\u2019s an understated additional bit of magic that makes it feel a bit more worthwhile. Also nice to see that it\u2019s been generously stocked with waypoints for travellers and an instance of the Isle\u2019s famous heliograph network; I like the idea that some of the latter have been held by bandits for the potential it might have for instances in which an outlaw group might be incorporated into the state apparatus, that some official might turn up with offers of clemency so long as they transmit the Empress\u2019 messages. And it\u2019s great to start veering back into the subject of lines being drawn for the civil war with such highways as the priority target and some of the initial skirmishes being employed clandestinely, in addition to the opportunities for criminals. The accompanying art makes the road look especially stark (although I wonder if it\u2019s a bit narrow), and I enjoy seeing one of the heliographs represented like this.<br /> <br /> Nothing to say about the waterway travel, it just sounds very colourful; it\u2019s got just enough for me to imagine that there are communities of river people. The real meat is in the descriptions of travel regulations. I can\u2019t quite remember if peasants were more restricted then prefectural boundaries in prior Editions, so I\u2019ll leave aside comments on whether or not it makes them less serf-like. The important thing to me is how it makes such a big deal out of being able to make such travel, the bureaucracy or black markets that one is obliged to engage with, as well as the potential infrastructure to enforce it. I\u2019ll say one thing for designating the mercantile classes as peasants, the idea that they become stuck in this morass, and can have things like permits delayed or denied held over them or become required to deal with wealthy Dynasts to actually pay for their permits, it carries a lot of potential. Even apart from scenes concerned with playing out or negotiating such interactions, I\u2019m thinking of it as another layer to dividing loyalties across the Isle; something like Houses promising supporters a moratorium on permit fees for ten years. There\u2019s similar fun to the idea that just being along with an Exalt means that no permit is required, which puts me in mind of scenarios where Dynasts or outcastes engage in smuggling favoured peasants across prefectural lines in plain sight, or the occasional audacious individual tries to insert themselves into a retinue. Also fun to imagine how coastal inhabitants take a lot of advantage of the fact that they can travel the periphery of the Isle a lot more easily than they could head inland.<br /> <br /> That sidebar on scholarship makes for another nice addition to the point that the Dynasts and those around them have not been sitting idle for seven hundred years. I always find lists of professions, technical or otherwise, to be helpful for getting a feel of both day-to-day life and larger governing and economic policies to a place, so I\u2019m happy that one of those is included, and it\u2019s a very good idea to lay out the general technologies employed across the Realm. That extends into the discussion of communication and the Imperial Post. I\u2019m actually kind of moved by the idea that it\u2019s one system that ends up being more accommodating across class lines with that idea that couriers tend to be willing to carry convenient correspondences at affordable prices (makes me think that a not inconsiderable number of even lower status people maintain some distantly removed pen pals), and I enjoy the notion that the areas they have to travel through has given them something of a sterling reputation. Hmm, I need to read up again on how fast services such as the Pony Express or Mongol post relays could carry messages, just to have a sense of the regularity that could be maintained. Following on with the matter of heliographs, I\u2019m a bit amused by how the personal correspondences of Dynasts are given such a low priority. It\u2019s odd, I don\u2019t think I\u2019ve ever thought of the heliograph network as something that would circumnavigate the Isle before, but it makes a lot of sense; it might technically by a longer distance that straight from end to end, but you don\u2019t need to deal with the mountains and a lot of the point is probably to send messages to the Imperial City in the event of invaders anyway. All in all, the Post looks like one of the most compelling organs of the imperial service so far.<br /> <br /> (This all makes me want to watch Xam'd again, faulty English subtitles and all)<br /> <br /> Aaaand the description of life on the Blessed Isle ends on a high note with details on the general quality of that life, and how the traditional practices and provided infrastructure of the Isle tends to keep it high across the board. Underground sewage lines and indoor lavatories alone have to be an inconceivable boon for the urban working classes, and healthcare sounds adequate if not comprehensive. The idea that villages contain bathhouses as a matter of course lends a nice element to the appearance of such places if they should be visited, as well as what it says for the communal experience of inhabitants. The range of medical professions catering to the wealthy conveys a nice sense of care that is not only good, but luxuriant, and I\u2019m really happy that it adds a note on how this all extends to the Exalted. Or rather, how the systems don\u2019t, even though the concerns do, because I really like this idea that healthcare in the Dynasty is almost the exclusive province of the Dragon Blooded, on a few counts. For one, the implication that a lot of what they might need to recover from could be beyond the capabilities of mortals to handle, and that even when it isn\u2019t that\u2019s likely not a state of vulnerability that any Dynasts want to be exposed to the peasantry in. The idea that Exalted physicians (which as we\u2019ll recall, are prominent in House Cynis) are the caretakers for the unExalted on grounds that they need to be kept in the very best shape for the sake of the bloodline helps ensure that such a profession maintains steady work. And the notion that the few elderly Exalted start to have their immaculate health break down and benefit from regular nursing care from private slaves is in line with how I\u2019ve always wanted to conceive of their later years, should they reach them. That they sometimes merely go so far as to employ god or demon to aid in restoration adds a nice uncanny quality to the subject of preserving their lives.<br /> <br /> And it ends on a sidebar concerned with the point of discriminations in the Realm, both laying out where the lines tend to be drawn (sexism and slaves, \u201cbarbarians\u201d and beastfolk) and advising on how Storytellers should handle it with deft and sensitivity. I think the substance of the advice is fairly boilerplate, but that\u2019s all it needs to be, to just lay it out in black and white so that it can be continuously reminded and is the standard for a common agreement. As for some of the description of the form of the Realm\u2019s sexism, I\u2019d been wondering from What Fire Has Wrought if anything would remain of the idea that it\u2019s a thing that hurts and pressures women as well, with the confining expectations placed on them. I\u2019m all for removing the specific example of \u201cwomen are disdained for pregnancy out of marriage\u201d, but I still find a value to the essence of such a double standard applying to other areas of their lives. It\u2019s a reminder that the Realm is not exactly an empowering structure even to the people that it is less disdainful of in its prejudice; it\u2019s authoritarian, and that imposes demands and uniformity even on the people that it would place above. This, and a whole bunch of other things dotted throughout, conveys to me that while the Realm enriches the members of the Scarlet Dynasty, it isn\u2019t exactly nice to them.<br /> <br /> So yeah, life on the Blessed Isle sounds pretty good. Narratively speaking of course, the described experience is decidedly a mixed bag in a lot of effective, engaging ways, many of which feel as though they have practical value in gameplay whether playing one of its princes, a visitor, or somebody with other agendas insinuating themselves or drifting along the peripheries. I find it to be written in a manner that doesn\u2019t simply describe how they live (although when it does, it makes it sound vibrant), but leaves a lot of hooks in which to <i>do things</i>, and I think that\u2019s very good for a game setting book. And it has been particularly good with keeping those elements while being very tight and focused, almost never using more words than it needs to make its points or paint its picture. This chapter isn\u2019t over yet, and I expect the standard will be maintained throughout, but I would find that the lifestyle topic would be the one where doing so would be the most challenging, so kudos for that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T06:28:19-07:00","id":1314932,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314174#post1314174\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Love the clothing section especially, although the entire section about living standards by class is cool, especially how grand and elaborate Patricians are only to arrive at the Dynasts and find that they prefer simple but high-quality wear. Sort of draws a cool 'old money' versus 'new money' parallel.<br /> <br /> Also, regarding pets, who in their right mind wants to rob a house guarded by giant spiders? I am honestly more intimidated by the idea of a pack of giant spiders that want to eat me than their cranky Exalted owner<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Dynasts with giant spiders for sentinels/pets? That's relevant to my interests.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T11:37:23-07:00","id":1314977,"title":"","content":"So, was anyone else confused by House Sesus being a merchant house and wealthy enough to rival Ragara? Because I thought the merchant houses were Ragara, Nellens, and V\u2019Neef and that Sesus was the Military and Dirty Tricks house? Am I just confused?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T11:43:01-07:00","id":1314979,"title":"","content":"Vance mentioned this, but in WFHW it was mentioned in that Sesus rivals Nellens in wealth. Also I think that section is not only referring to Ragara but a whole lot of other factors as well. The reason V'neff is not indebted to Ragara is because of its age, it hasn't been around long enough for that to happen, over all though its still a very new House and is facing all types of troubles. Sesus while they are indebted to Ragara, they have enough surplus wealth and savvy thanks to their spy network that they can work around that. Also I dont think any houses rials Ragara in wealth, that section is just referring to the Houses who don't have to deal with all types of factors.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T11:43:30-07:00","id":1314980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BrilliantRain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314977#post1314977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, was anyone else confused by House Sesus being a merchant house and wealthy enough to rival Ragara? Because I thought the merchant houses were Ragara, Nellens, and V\u2019Neef and that Sesus was the Military and Dirty Tricks house? Am I just confused?</div> </div> </div> </div>I recommend reading the &quot;House Economics&quot; section for Sesus in <i>What Fire Has Wrought</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T11:48:35-07:00","id":1314981,"title":"","content":"I want to run a Heist where a bunch of Exalted criminals break into one of the currently inaccessible Treasure Manses, steal a good chunk of the money and.... no one notices, because the money is just numbers on paper and whether they can actually get at the jade or not doesn\u2019t really matter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T11:57:34-07:00","id":1314984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BrilliantRain</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1314981#post1314981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I want to run a Heist where a bunch of Exalted criminals break into one of the currently inaccessible Treasure Manses, steal a good chunk of the money and.... no one notices, because the money is just numbers on paper and whether they can actually get at the jade or not doesn\u2019t really matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>It might matter eventually if they launder enough of the treasure that it has an economic impact on the price of available jade.<br /> <br /> But that would take a bit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T14:33:18-07:00","id":1315019,"title":"","content":"So, administrative divisions. Number and structure of the prefectures is about the same, though we\u2019ve of course got an extra dominion; I\u2019ve often thought in terms of prefectures being sized in a manner similar to divisions in China or America, where you get less populous western ones being larger, but I take it from this initial description that they\u2019re more about taxable commercial output. I think that the demographics called upon to appoint as prefects is about the same as it ever was, but the idea that they were always appointed by the Deliberative (with a veto for the Empress) is a bit of a shift; apart from being a bit more sensible to me (it seemed at times like a position numerous and spread out enough to be a bit taxing and tedious for the Empress to appoint each) and makes me wonder if there are some overall adjustments to that body. I also think a stronger emphasis is being placed here on the value of prefectural production. While the description of governors supplements a straightforward administrative posts with a few useful hooks, such as the idea of a new prefect coming in to deal with governors loyal to a previous House, and that a Dynastic scheme to extend power over the countryside is to consolidate villages to a large enough status to have loyal governors appointed. The point about forced relocation in particular has me wondering if some of the schemes of the Great Houses to reduce the peasantry to serfdom have been modified.<br /> <br /> Okay, so for a while now I\u2019ve not really been able to make sense of descriptions of taxation policies in previous Editions; that whole rigamarole of sending officials around to convert scrip into jade and then ceremonially be granted it\u2026 I couldn\u2019t exactly see the point. And I think you even got some places in the books saying that direct taxation was an insignificant and almost perfunctory form of revenue compared to satrapial tribute. For the time being, the idea reads to me as having been simplified and made more direct. It\u2019s interesting to me how prefects are centrally appointed officials, but still function partially as House tax farmers and thus a source of their revenue; it\u2019s another thing that clears up exactly how the Houses work in Realm finances, where before it could be a bit shaky. As always, I appreciate attention being given to how there\u2019s a bit of graft, and I think the attitude towards how there can be a bit of back and forth with citizens concealing assets from tax collectors is new, and I like the notion that there was always a bit of leniency in collecting in full on the understanding that this could be disruptive. Likewise the reference to people more boldly concealing their full worth among the usual increase in misappropriated funds in the absence of the Empress; it\u2019s another thing that lends the commoners a bit more agency.<br /> <br /> Right, with the contrast given between new prefects and life-long governors, I was wondering what their term lengths were, so glad to see that provided. I find the description of the politics underlying prefectural appointments to further clarify the position of the Great Houses in territorial distributions of the Blessed Isle. I like how the idea of appointing prefects to areas containing the interests of different Houses captures the spirit of the imperial Chinese policy of keeping officials from becoming too familiar with their purviews, in a system where the priorities would be quite different, and it\u2019s interesting to imagine the tension inherent to a House trying to push for a scion being granted a valuable prefecture so they can get a cut of the taxes, even if doing so might forfeit a bit of other influence in the area as the scion becomes obligated to serve imperial interests more. On the other hand there\u2019s the matter of local patricians actually being ones who have become more deeply rooted in the administrative apparatus, and there are some good ideas for how they can leverage that for advantage over the prefects. The plot hook about numerous prefects\u2019 terms coming to an end soon and working out deals for new appointments is very good, and can read nicely as an effective prelude for Great Houses turning large sections of the Isle into family fiefs (alternatively, somebody trying to abate war might look to negotiate to retain a system of diverse appointments serving a central interest). It does a good job in any case of making it clear how most prefectures do not and cannot function as such already, which also makes the contrast with the homelands of each House more striking in comparison. All of those points come together nicely with the description of Dynasts finding prefectures to be places to vacation to continue the point that their residences are usually more concentrated, even if they might often tour the Isle for recreation or to attend to far-flung business interests. And I like the impression of the Immaculate Order functioning as a kind of parallel administrative institution, ministering to the needs of the people while also passing judgement on the officials, continuing the image of one of their functions being to provide the peasantry with an outlet; it\u2019s an interesting position for an established religion, to occasionally incite uprisings as a matter of course. All of this goes nicely with the illustration, which I take to represent the idea of local administrators serving multiple different interests even while sharing the proverbial table (in any case the costuming and diegetic lighting and shading is nice, and the depiction of each of them in a different colour is striking).<br /> <br /> \u2026 Unless it\u2019s just supposed to be a somewhat late depiction of Dynastic dining practices, with the contrast between Exalted at the big table and mortals at the minor one? Still looks nice, although the technicoloured people seem to be wearing patrician-style hats.<br /> <br /> Now, that sidebar on money is very good. First off, it\u2019s nice to have a clear list of the various expenses that the Great Houses need to grapple with as they face the onset of civil war, as well as their various revenue streams. The main thing that draws me is the attitude being presented; I feel as though in some spots in the past, the Houses were attributed practices that were unsustainable while not being entirely mindful of the fact, coming across as largely an exercise in greed and excess. The impression that I get here is that they\u2019re a bit more considerate of the long term, but need to balance that against the requirements of the near future. It lends a slightly more desperate air, and sets up an environment in which opportunists might find ways to exploit the Exalted masters of the great empire. The line in particular about how peasant taxes are viewed as limited by the prospect of revolt suggests to me that the policy of conning them into debt to the Dynasty where they weren\u2019t simply driven into disenfranchisement has been abandoned altogether; strategizing for conflict against the other Houses probably makes the question of authority over the rural populations a low priority at best. All together, it makes a very strong practical and emotional picture, a firm idea of what the current schemes and needs of the Houses are and the sense that many scions will be struggling with the limits of their assets.<br /> <br /> And speaking of, the chapter finishes with a discussion of the Great Houses directly. It\u2019s been fun inferring a bunch of details sure, but I\u2019m glad to be given a few direct words on how they fit into the overall structure (such as a summary of their general assets and contributions, which particularly helps to clear up their relationship with land), particularly when delineated along lines of their particular characters and interests. It\u2019s a small note, but I also think it\u2019s helpful for getting a feel of them to clearly state the basis for their origins as pre-existing family groups consolidated under the authority of upraised children.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, if House Cathak is anything to go by, it would appear that the structure for each of these write-ups goes\u2026 brief summary of House culture and how that influences practices (as a permutation of info earlier in the book, limiting redundancy with What Fire Has Wrought), general strategic goals and economic needs, the manner in which they influence areas under their authority, and external relations. Looks good to me, effectively distinguishes them and gives a solid basis for playing out their long-term plans, as well as adding to the social diversity of the Blessed Isle. For the specifics of Cathak, I like that idea that they tie their legions to things like grain shipments as a form of propaganda, while it also provides a reminder of the brutality in dealing with unruly satrapies. Their long-time militarism is leant a bit more savvy with the idea that the necessary expenses imposed on their prefectural holdings are offset by cultivating the good reputation of their forces, and I like the way that retiring soldiers bringing some legion values to the general peasantry in the form of sport and competition can constitute a form of influence even without deliberate policy. In a similar vein to patrolling imperial legions playing off of bandits, I think the idea that the traditional Cathak legions having worked against heretical movements is a solid basis for some activity on the Blessed Isle itself (preventing them from feeling idle), and the manner in which various services cater to them adds some nuance to the military presence. It\u2019s also fun to see the financial benefits that their adopting the role of queenmaker has afforded to them.<br /> <br /> For Cynis, I think the mission of rounding them out a bit is helped a lot by the contrasts given to how they\u2019re viewed in different quarters; the scandal among the other Houses, the allure presented to patricians (which nicely complements the point of how they\u2019re courted for the sake of Dynastic operations) and the less glamourous image perceived by the commoner majority from their monopolies in the slave trade and large-scale agricultural concerns. But even that is given some contrast with how the Cynis aesthetic values express themselves in their holdings; I like how the priority there is not to present them as predatory, but instead as patrons of public works that can greatly beatify a prefecture, even down to the level of rural villages, as well as providing an environment in which more liberal attitudes can survive by way of their own indifference. It amuses me to consider that when a Cynis commissions some fine statuary or attractive garden to be placed in some minor village, it might be driven not so much out of altruism as it is to give their own travelling a more scenic view, even if the peasantry probably benefit all the same. With all of the previous consideration for Dynastic involvement in criminal enterprise, I\u2019m glad to see that be a direct point of discussion, and a reminder of how one of the House\u2019s primary interests is in gathering and strategically distributing sordid information about their fellows (as well as a means of further enriching themselves by having a hand in the distribution of their narcotics to classes that I suspect are typically forbidden them). All of that luxury is nicely offset by the notion that they\u2019re among the Houses most finding their usual revenue streams disrupted by the current crisis, and I think the matter of how they\u2019re making overtures to the Guild is a strong plot hook for both sides of that exchange.<br /> <br /> Ledaal\u2019s Shadow Crusade is being used to give them a very vivid presence in the perceptions of the common populace; their protection from the very real threats of the Fair Folk, subversive spirits and their adherents and the Celestial Exalted being offset a bit by their intrusion and interference. The measures they go to seem to create a potential for them to be among the least popular Houses when encountered up close, as opposed to being admired from a distance, and I would find that to punctuate the difficulties they\u2019ve been given in facing the Time of Tumult. Ah, that point is made even clearer with the idea that they maintain the strongest and harshest distance from even the patricians, and I can imagine some fun scenarios in which many people of the lower classes are making them pay for it a bit at a time when they could do with some more popularity (as well as the occasional scion struggling to try and adopt a friendlier demeanour). One of the most interesting things to me here is how Arjuf, which I\u2019ve often viewed as the Realm\u2019s second financial capital, is being presented as starting to repel merchants how don\u2019t want to be squeezed to fund Ledaal\u2019s numerous concerns and agendas. I actually found that House to be one of the more sympathetic for how pressed it was, so I like getting a much harsher image in seeing it from this angle.<br /> <br /> A lot of what is attributed to Ledaal might have once been associated with Mnemon, but I can readily see how their emphasized closeness to the Immaculate Order and presence in architecture and construction gives it a much better (if still somewhat austere) standing among the common populace (even while it reminds us that the matriarch herself ends up not quite being popular). I\u2019m guessing that their interests in beatifying even lower level communities actually is more altruistic than that of Cynis, and I might see compelling scenarios in which some economic policy was undertaken for the sake of being able to pay for such a thing out of pocket; I can imagine that more than one village owes its bath house in particular to Mnemon charity. Also nice to see their diverse spiritual and artistic inclinations contribute to their acclaim. It\u2019s like Cathak in its helpful reminder that satrapies can be harshly treated (albeit the focus is probably different, and I imagine that this House is more widely disliked by foreign gods), and has the clearest avenue for making the point about how imperialist infrastructure developments have a high priority for more easily projecting force and extracting wealth. With the emphasis on the Order\u2019s place in peasant risings, it\u2019s amusing to me to consider how this House might actually end up cultivating some of the strongest environments for protest (if not dissidence), while the idea that the organization is supporting the House through lean times lends itself to the hook of a rise in Mnemon\u2019s authority might be commensurate with a more strictly enforced faith. This House was one of my favourites in the Dragon Blooded book, and I find that compounded in this picture of their compelling austerity coupled with being hard pressed.<br /> <br /> A more thoroughly rounded look at the Blessed Isle puts Nellens\u2019 personal and financial closeness to the peasants and patricians into perspective, and its interesting how they\u2019re able to enhance this popularity by way of their more diverse and lucrative assets enabling them to ease off on taxation. It\u2019s a funny thing how peasants in other prefectures ape the prejudices of the Dynasty; I\u2019m imagining that\u2019s not quite as much about being influenced by the views of the powerful as it is taking advantage of the catharsis of being able to direct some disdain at a House that other Houses will not respond harshly to. I like how the spirit of propping the House up is used to make their lack of satrapies something that they turn into a strength, and the idea that they would cultivate influence by using a combination of administrative position and wealth to help pay off tributary shortcomings is very clever. It\u2019s good to make the point that its money is based on less sturdy foundations, lest it come across too well (even if that is supplemented by the point that attacks on its holdings could cause a cascade that might offend the lower classes too deeply for them to keep their heads down). It\u2019s also fun how needing to spend to secure itself in the event of war might well tip it off as they potentially give away their overlooked revenue streams and savings.<br /> <br /> Of course there\u2019s going to be a strong emphasis on Peleps\u2019 romantic popular perception, since the Exalted book made that foundational to their House identity. It\u2019s also an interesting thing how their meritocratic attitudes have to twin edges of making prefectural government around them feel more honest at the same time as it becomes harder to get into. Ages ago I imagined Cynis as the House with the strongest showings of foreign goods and fashion, but I\u2019m good with the idea of that being transferred to Peleps as an extrapolation of its naval and maritime interests and giving its reputation as grand adventurers a means of disseminating throughout the Isle\u2019s communities, especially with them being at the forefront of trade to the relatively recently opened West. It all helps to set up the House as the local equivalent to 16th and 17th century adventurers of the Caribbean and the Spanish Main (things that I felt compelled to read more about recently); I also enjoy the understated way that they\u2019re attributed a form of cultural appropriation that is likely to be highly offensive in-character. All of that goes nicely with the ways in which the still extreme expenses of the navy are forcing them to take actions that are drastic when they aren\u2019t outright treason. It all works as a nice set up for the possibility of going off to conquer the West outright, including the possibility that pushing too far could end up with them being all but driven out. The one thing that I would be critical of is a feeling that it could have given a bit more perspective to the actions of Peleps scions as imperial judges, but perhaps that will be helped out later in the book.<br /> <br /> Ragara\u2019s quality of having extreme financial power might have been one-note, but I find that it\u2019s effectively varied with points such as them having an interest in maintaining good relations with business partners and being less culturally interfering in satrapies than other Houses. Also how the traditional disdain for such a profession can colour the views even of people used to the dominance of the Exalted, while the existence of groups like the Guild can provide an example that makes even Ragara look attractive and heroic to some. I find the particulars of their ostentatious lifestyle to be reasonably distinct from those of Cynis, with the preference for extravagant meals being particularly reminiscent of the most grotesque excesses of the 19th century British, and it helps to give them more presence and personality. It makes sense how they would cultivate environments of emulating their excess, by which they can further profit, and it\u2019s fun how they would be so strongly opposed by the Order (no doubt ardently, if covertly, encouraged by Mnemon herself). There were questions raised last year about how Ragara\u2019s wealth being so closely tied to debt would survive a major war, and I think those are effectively addressed by the point that they wouldn\u2019t, and thus the House would want to ensure that a civil war was resolved quickly (which it\u2019s probably in a position to influence with the way it\u2019s using its current assets to take control of divested resources and provide or withhold supplies). That and the idea that they\u2019re placing a much stronger emphasis on repayment in hard currency. All of that is offset well with the point that the House is in a more precarious position than might be expected, at the risk of enemies seeking destruction if it pushes too far.<br /> <br /> So much of Sesus in What Fire Has Wrought was concerned with activities that were clandestine, so I\u2019ve been wondering a lot how their functions in the light of day might come across. It\u2019s interesting how it goes for an approach of them being wildly unpopular, but with such a combination of military, mercantile and political power, as well as an impeccable Exalted pedigree, to ensure that it\u2019s hard for contemptuous peers or subordinates to make too much of it. It\u2019s funny to me how the House descended from Sesus feels reminiscent of the practices of elder brother Ragara in terms of not playing the game established by the Empress fairly, with the degree to which their intelligence networks deny the lower classes the opportunity to rebel and protest; the idea that criminal organizations thrive in areas under their authority probably contributes to their inauspicious reputation. The relations with the Guild are especially interesting, between the idea that sabotaging Guild activities creates an environment in which business independent from both can grow and the especially compelling addition to the battle lines of more aggressive opposition on the Guild\u2019s part presenting risks to the close ties Sesus has with Cynis (that House being made further interesting in retrospect with the idea that it is allied with two fierce rivals).<br /> <br /> House Tepet has had so much time being emphasized as the falling star among the Scarlet Dynasty that it\u2019s nice to get an external perspective on them which is more supportive, and I particularly like the way that their status as preservers of Shogunate traditions is tied into this. In keeping with the idea that the House might be in too dire straits to preserve what could be righteous about it, the point about how magnanimity might need to be foregone for the sake of acquiring the resources to keep themselves going works well. It\u2019s also nice how their distinction is used to give them and their associates even more overt cultural variations; I\u2019m all for the Houses as a whole preferring more practical garb, but I\u2019ve always found the junihitoe to be such a striking form of garment that I\u2019m glad that Tepet\u2019s Shogunate stylings are used as a means of giving it presence in parts of the Realm and its satrapies. As for their wealth, I think it helps to prevent their losses from seeming to make them too toothless with the point that they could end up going straight to outright plunder of satrapies or prefectures under enemy Houses, and I find the idea that inhabitants of their own homeland hold them in such esteem (or at least preferable to alternatives) that they\u2019re willing to help reduce shortfalls with sizable donations.<br /> <br /> Huh; on reading the timeline, I\u2019m sure I would have done the numbers pointing out that House V\u2019neef is only fourteen, but somehow it didn\u2019t quite land so solidly as it does when it\u2019s put out here in a description of the House\u2019s standing. It\u2019s very fitting that the alphabetical order places this House at the end here, because it gives one perspective on the cultures and interests that are likely to be found in the many different prefectures (and maybe some satrapies) that V\u2019neef has found herself with, for which she has not yet had the opportunity or structure to leave her own mark on. In What Fire Has Wrought, the point of how she\u2019s been remanded so many outcastes, many quite experienced, made the House look stronger than ever to me, but here the point on how they don\u2019t quite have a unitary culture illustrates the liabilities of it. As such, I\u2019m also picturing the House a bit more through what might be the matriarch\u2019s eyes; where before I thought there was something amusing (if endearing) about her being in a maternal position with respect to grizzled old veteran outcastes, the idea that she continuously tries to throw grand parties to cultivate a House identity and create a form of familial solidarity makes me imagine her as being a bit intimidated by many of the daughters and sons that she\u2019s been saddled with, and hard-pressed in the highly unexpected modern climate to bind them together into a functional whole. On a similar note, I\u2019d say that a bit of reality is brought down on the otherwise high-riding image of them being granted the lucrative Merchant Fleet and thumbing their nose in Western competition with Peleps; that a more holistic view illustrates the fact that they\u2019re in a rather precarious position, saddled with significant expenses and sharply limited emergency resources, alliances, reputation or firmly established revenue streams. I particularly like the idea that trying to make up for these limits in their territories is making a very poor first impression, and is creating an environment in which people might rebel against them in general or even try to invite back former overlords. V\u2019neef still has a lot going for it, and if this write-up emphasizes just how young they are as a House, it does so in a manner that makes clear some potential to grow up admirably (or at least be very interesting in the precise manner that they disastrously fall apart).<br /> <br /> On a similar note, House Iselsi\u2019s highly sinister write-up is touched with the prosaic in the points made here about how decades of dismantling has left it largely forgotten among the populace at large, and maintaining the elements of that sinister agenda have mundane necessities that they\u2019re in an awkward position to maintain. I don\u2019t find it to be a diminishing look; really, it helps provide them with some focus (and explanation for why execution of their vengeance might be slow-going) by pointing to the necessary maintenance requirements. It humanises them a bit. And I think it does well for them how some of the examples include adapting their disadvantage to further subvert and disrupt their enemies, sneaking around among their intrigues and skimming money where they can in a manner that leaves behind a lot of scapegoats.<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t really have anything more to say about chapter one. I should think that my comments throughout should illustrate how it has effectively covered the queries and wishes I raised towards the start, and my general regard for its utility and entertainment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-28T20:33:14-07:00","id":1315075,"title":"","content":"So, it occurred to me that 3e did something interestingly subtle with Eyem in this edition. Given that they're playing up the assholishness of Ragara and the untrustworthy nature of the house he created....how exactly can we trust that Eyem was a bad influence and the whole scandal wasn't manufactured to hide Ragara's research into unholy matters? Especially since the account of the event is a line in a timeline, not an actual blurb.<br /> <br /> Edit: Thinking on it further, the two entries about Eyem in the timeline, a total of three sentences, mange to do several things at once. They establish what Eyem is, that House Jerah exists, when and how House Ragara came into existence, and, because of the context of who Ragara is, create a whole unreliable narrator feeling about the event (to the point that I wouldn't be surprised if Ragara has Eyem in his basement, and that's why the House has done so well in finance). I honestly don't remember how much of that was pulled from previous editions, but whoever wrote that has my compliments.<br /> <br /> This is a very good book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":226,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","user":"BrilliantRain","date":". 05-29-2019, 12:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T06:44:37-07:00","id":1315150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315019#post1315019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh; on reading the timeline, I\u2019m sure I would have done the numbers pointing out that House V\u2019neef is only fourteen, but somehow it didn\u2019t quite land so solidly as it does when it\u2019s put out here in a description of the House\u2019s standing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, the house is so fresh that one of the aspectbooks (wood) had the hilariously messed up tale of how some young student (Peleps i think but i could be wrong), resentful of house losses caused by the foundation of house V'neef, decided to assassinate one of them as petty revenge. Except there were no actual V'neef at his school, so he choose to make do with a young Cynis (both houses are wood, right?) Belar Norren. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> If memory tricks me not, V'neef herself is about 60 years old, what is somewhat young for a dragon-blood and about the same age as senator Nellens Poramo.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315019#post1315019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It\u2019s very fitting that the alphabetical order places this House at the end here, because it gives one perspective on the cultures and interests that are likely to be found in the many different prefectures (and maybe some satrapies) that V\u2019neef has found herself with, for which she has not yet had the opportunity or structure to leave her own mark on. In What Fire Has Wrought, the point of how she\u2019s been remanded so many outcastes, many quite experienced, made the House look stronger than ever to me, but here the point on how they don\u2019t quite have a unitary culture illustrates the liabilities of it. As such, I\u2019m also picturing the House a bit more through what might be the matriarch\u2019s eyes; where before I thought there was something amusing (if endearing) about her being in a maternal position with respect to grizzled old veteran outcastes, the idea that she continuously tries to throw grand parties to cultivate a House identity and create a form of familial solidarity makes me imagine her as being a bit intimidated by many of the daughters and sons that she\u2019s been saddled with, and hard-pressed in the highly unexpected modern climate to bind them together into a functional whole. On a similar note, I\u2019d say that a bit of reality is brought down on the otherwise high-riding image of them being granted the lucrative Merchant Fleet and thumbing their nose in Western competition with Peleps; that a more holistic view illustrates the fact that they\u2019re in a rather precarious position, saddled with significant expenses and sharply limited emergency resources, alliances, reputation or firmly established revenue streams. I particularly like the idea that trying to make up for these limits in their territories is making a very poor first impression, and is creating an environment in which people might rebel against them in general or even try to invite back former overlords. V\u2019neef still has a lot going for it, and if this write-up emphasizes just how young they are as a House, it does so in a manner that makes clear some potential to grow up admirably (or at least be very interesting in the precise manner that they disastrously fall apart).</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of this has been mentioned in previous editions but the overall image given this time around with WFHW and the Realm is definitely far clearer and definite. Certainly relevant to my interests as one of my most perenial dynast characters is the young scion of a group between cadet house and outcastes with deep family ties to House V'neef (and the Iselsi &amp; Forest Witches, but not the kind of thing to admit among polite company <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) and a liking for giant sapient spiders and other exotic mounts/familiars.<br /> <br /> Little aside, what do either of the two books tells us about fallen or cadet houses, households and other minor or peripheral brances of dynastic society?<br /> Have toyed with <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/193586-households-cadet-houses-and-fallen-houses-bits-of-color-in-the-edge-of-the-dynasty\">those </a>subjects in the past, though it has been a few years since i last discussed such stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 05-29-2019, 10:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T07:34:02-07:00","id":1315163,"title":"","content":"Before I move on, something that I\u2019ve found particularly helpful with the latter section of the previous chapter is some groundwork for coming up with the distribution of the Dragon Blooded around the Blessed Isle, which I\u2019ve always felt to be one of the more difficult areas of making their presence sensible with their numbers. The key thing would be to regard them as not being deployed in a static manner, helped by the way that their power over prefectures is a more indirect thing; the only ones who might be expected to never really travel at all are the few elderly retirees.<br /> <br /> The model I\u2019ve got at the moment is that (of those who aren\u2019t committed in work for the Imperial Service) at any given time, about a third of a given Great House will be concentrated in the home prefecture; most in the capital, with others distributed around a few of the lesser cities and towns and in country estates (those being either ones who are a bit on the outs or have a wish to be away from the bustle of the family at large, while still being only a day or so\u2019s travel from them). Around a fifth will be in the Imperial City; those serving in the Deliberative are prominent there, as well as others serving House interests in the national capital. Maybe around one tenth have permanent settlements dotted around the Blessed Isle, often those who have retired or are otherwise inclined to a bit of isolation, such as a House\u2019s sorcerers. The rest are in continuous circulation around the prefectures and satrapies, on assignment from House elders or overseeing their own branches of the business interests; for the prefectures, that also includes a lot of times visiting places where the interests of other Houses dominate, in which they\u2019re looking for new opportunities (those will often be prefectures in which a cousin has been appointed prefect).<br /> <br /> So you\u2019ve got a situation in which only ten prefectures and the capital are ones in which there are sizable numbers of Dynasts altogether, but you\u2019ll often have a few Dragon Blooded spread across a number of the others on a temporary basis. For maximum drama and intrigue, arrangements are often made to ensure that Exalts from multiple Houses are attending to the same prefecture at the same time, as their Houses often want them to keep tabs on one another; that\u2019s liable to be the frontier in which a lot of deals are made.<br /> <br /> I say temporary, but I\u2019m picturing a situation in which assignment to interests in any given prefecture (or cluster of them) can last several years. Younger Dragon Blooded will be the most frequently on the move, at the same time as they\u2019ll be experiencing the freshest pressure to have children, so a thing most likely to result in a couple laying down roots for a while far from their homeland will be falling pregnant in the middle of such excursions. Hence, Dynasts raising their children are often scattered all over, and in closer proximity to members of other Houses doing the same than they are to their own.<br /> <br /> A key thing in this picture is that it\u2019s a distribution of Exalted business rather than demographics, meaning that any given one can transition between the same; younger ones can end up among the ones spending extensive time in the home prefecture, while matriarchs and other established figures can be personally overseeing and advancing House interests, or engaged in other business (not to mention contributing to the upbringing of their youngest scions). I\u2019m picturing Mnemon in particular as a matriarch who most evenly divides time between her domain and concerns across the prefectures, between her personal architectural work, ties to the Immaculate Order, and especially hands-on attitude to the House\u2019s youth.<br /> <br /> I think this works as a basis for how they can have presence and interactions without being spread too thin, as well as a basis for some dynamic activity on the Blessed Isle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T08:35:45-07:00","id":1315183,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315163#post1315163\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A key thing in this picture is that it\u2019s a distribution of Exalted business rather than demographics, meaning that any given one can transition between the same; younger ones can end up among the ones spending extensive time in the home prefecture, while matriarchs and other established figures can be personally overseeing and advancing House interests, or engaged in other business (not to mention contributing to the upbringing of their youngest scions). I\u2019m picturing Mnemon in particular as a matriarch who most evenly divides time between her domain and concerns across the prefectures, between her personal architectural work, ties to the Immaculate Order, and especially hands-on attitude to the House\u2019s youth.<br /> <br /> I think this works as a basis for how they can have presence and interactions without being spread too thin, as well as a basis for some dynamic activity on the Blessed Isle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seems like a good breakdown. One last element to bear in mind that would be more demographically distributed rather than strictly on the basis of activity is the distribution of demenses and manses. Since most of those would probably merit a relatively permanent Dynastic presence around the Isle, even in the otherwise 'empty' prefactures.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Storyteller Vault releases!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/269728/Sorcerous-Ways-Mystic-Martial-Arts\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerous Ways: Mystic Martial Arts</a> - Punch people for magic power!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/252892/These-Mean-Streets\" target=\"_blank\">These Mean Streets</a> - Advise and systems for street-level punk-noir Vampire larp","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/169-wastevens","name":"wastevens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"939"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T11:39:47-07:00","id":1315233,"title":"","content":"So I notice that they've added a small bit to the section on the Wood Shrine in the Realm, that was very contraversial before. Immaculates believe that when the five people vanish when a pilgrim has completed the shrine-quest, the dead five will be reborn as the pilgrim's Dragonblood children. I think that works, because to an Immacualte true believer it's sad but ultimately good (you get to be a Dragonblood in your next life, the aim of most Immaculate worshipper), to the Dragonblood pilgrim they've helped people, and to the non-Immaculate (Solar, Lunar, beastmen, etc) it's the killing of five innocents just so a Dragonblood can have Dragonblood kids.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T15:03:45-07:00","id":1315279,"title":"","content":"I wonder what the theological explanation was in the First Age","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T15:19:19-07:00","id":1315284,"title":"","content":"The next chapter fiction is fairly straightforward. I like getting a look at a magistrate\u2019s operations separate from the Exalt and a sense of how the archons can operate independently, as well as a sense for the methods and concerns of their investigations. The accompanying art doesn\u2019t have much in the way of a scene, but the pastel style is nice and I like how distinct the three characters look. I\u2019m not sure what to make of the eyes in the background; are they supposed to represent the bravos attacking them, or imply the presence of a shadowy mastermind?<br /> <br /> Right, chapter two, The Machinery of Empire. I\u2019ll be interested to see how this distinguishes itself from the related portions in the prior chapter, as well as how some older information might pop in the new style. It will also be nice if dedicating a chapter for this lets a few things that were a bit overlooked in the past get more attention.<br /> <br /> Intro paragraphs are good for the basic information of what the thesis of the chapter is. I like the way that each branch of the service is given a little point about how the Time of Tumult is changing or disrupting it.<br /> <br /> This opening for the magistrates gives a good sense for them. I find beginning on the point of how their operations and exploits make them figures of popular legend is a good thing to start with, to make them exciting and inviting. It further helps to characterise the Blessed Isle to imagine it as the staging ground for such adventures. The matter of how they\u2019re forbidden to possess wealth is nothing new, but is made well to give the picture of both how their requisition powers can be utilized in the execution of their duties, and in how their lifestyles can be privileged but transitory. And we\u2019re given their chief adversaries in the form of the ministries, with the setup for their current conflict. I enjoy the little reminder of their origins among the colleagues of the Empress from before her rise.<br /> <br /> Often I\u2019d question devoting a lot of attention to a state that has been transitioned away from, but for the magistrates it\u2019s distinctly apt, since their change in circumstances is so drastic and understanding the background would be useful for developing stories about the grievances of their enemies. I also think it\u2019s helpful to attribute them some authority over the Dragon Blooded when the conventional police came across as a lot more restrained; given the power that magistrates exercise, it gives me a sense that they\u2019re both held above the usual political considerations and themselves feel confident to pursue charges against the Realm\u2019s elite (although it\u2019s interesting that a privilege of matriarchs is being immune from their judgements).<br /> <br /> I\u2019m finding this book to give a stronger emphasis to the point that the magistrates were the most visible presence of the Scarlet Empress throughout the Realm, which I think gives new life to the way she personally selected and appointed them. I\u2019m imagining that her own presence could render being offered the position an especially powerful experience, and one that could strongly inform the backstories of characters. I think it\u2019s good to emphasize how, although drawn from criminals, it\u2019s of the type who might find themselves abused or pushed aside by the activities of the elite, to make such a pool of candidates feel more sensible and create motivated individuals. I also find it fun for there to be a line about selecting people who weren\u2019t necessarily the best marriage material to ensure that such plans in the Dynasty aren\u2019t disrupted, considering some of the stories in the fiction of previous Editions. Yes, it\u2019s overtly making the point out of the Empress\u2019 own charisma, in combination with going for such marginalized figures, is a basis for their particular loyalty, it gives the thing more punch than it had before, when it would merely be stated that she appointed them; in addition to being presented as part of what makes their own legend, I can imagine how magistrates impressively going about their duties helped promote the narratives of the Scarlet Empress and contribute to the sense among commoners that administrative grievances fall to figures obscuring her attention. Also good is developing the idea that new recruits receive semi-official training from the veterans gives a sense of camaraderie to what previously felt like a fairly isolated profession. I\u2019d say that it gives them stronger prospects to whether the current crisis, and there\u2019s something especially intriguing about the idea of many magistrates banding together to effect change rather than being scattered to be picked off one by one; it adds another layer to the upcoming civil war (and plays nicely with how V\u2019neef is offering them refuge).<br /> <br /> The regular reports back to the Empress benefit from the character added by the recruitment process, and I\u2019m thinking that this emphasis on them as her agents and heroes of the Realm might help make their associated scribes into more prominent figures. Like the magistrates themselves, I think the description of archons has been improved a bit with a stronger emphasis on the benefits that breed personal loyalties, with the added detail of how it can constitute a path of social mobility otherwise unheard of in the Realm; I like the point made about them being selected for high potential left unrealised by their station in particular (both for the broader sense of regular people on the Blessed Isle and for the potential of having disputes for magistrates with Immaculate monks that they\u2019re otherwise untouchable to). Also nice for the point of how magistrates are individuals, will not excel at everything that might be required of them, and there are more mortals than other Exalted to help cover their shortcomings. The matter of how the decree that archons may not outlive their magistrates is, while motivating, not a high priority to enforce for those archons who discretely disappear makes the idea a bit more grounded to me. I also like the attention given to the prospect of romantic liaisons, and how these add to the iconoclastic nature of those enforcers.<br /> <br /> I take the point about the magistrates being attributed a rather freestyle approach to their duties, but think that it\u2019s helpful to state that they had some particular priorities, even official assignments, to guide the image of where they might end up. There\u2019s something amusing in the image of how Peleps, with its new reputation would often intersect with magistrates as a matter of course owing to their domains being particular hotspots for activity that the magistrates would be charged with working against. In addition to some clarity about how they divide jurisdiction, I like the description furthering their camaraderie with them tending to have dinner and hold discussion together in the event of crossing paths. The way that much of their day-to-day life consists of merely travelling and waiting to come across something to investigate adds a nice touch of the prosaic, in keeping with the game\u2019s usual milieu, and the examples of the varied ways in which they might actually handle issues is both a fun read and looks useful for giving a clear image of what they do practically.<br /> <br /> With all of that background dealt with, it gets to the subject of their current prospects. I think this may have often been an underserved topic, sometimes seemingly forgotten, and even when addressed in a slightly perfunctory manner. The approach here looks stronger to me; first there\u2019s a better emphasis on exactly why the ministries are directing such hostility, and what they have to gain (as well as lose; I like the multi-faceted picture it gives of the Realm\u2019s power players in making the point that undercutting the magistrates leaves the ministries themselves with more difficulty handling the Great Houses). It also makes the magistrates themselves feel a bit more proactive in the face of it, with the ideas such as how they\u2019re assigning themselves out of reach in the satrapies or working to exercise their remaining official power to try and remove ministerial enemies, rather than trying to continue business as usual and hoping for the best. The idea of it as a kind of shadow war going on in parallel to the battle lines of the Scarlet Dynasty makes the future of the Realm feel more unstable and uncertain, as well as simply a more engaging play arena. And the stronger emphasis on their reputation does a lot to lend credibility to the idea that, should persecution of them cross a line, the commoners of the Isle might revolt of their own accord.<br /> <br /> Ah, it\u2019s nice to see that even in a section such as this we\u2019re given a few example characters to inspire and make the subject less abstract. I think it also gives a good basis for understanding how Dynasts might end up in this position, that would otherwise seem very far removed from them. I like how several of them have a consistent theme of trying to seek some kind of redemption, or at least have first-hand insights into what they\u2019re now charged with investigating. I think that it also helps to provide clarity to why criminals of the Realm might be the sorts who can be entrusted with such authority; that the values of this society might regard a number of things as deeply appalling that still don\u2019t ultimately reflect too badly on one\u2019s character. Sure an Immaculate abbot who violated chastity vows for an affair with a married Dynast would be a subject of significant disgrace, but probably not morally bankrupt or fundamentally untrustworthy enough that charging them with the duty of a wandering inquisitor is a terrible idea, especially when one might appeal to pre-existing ideals and frame it in terms of redoubling commitment to them. That and the odd time that the circumstances that make you technically a criminal can also leave you as somebody with a real axe to grind against the elites.<br /> <br /> Another art piece that I don\u2019t have much to say about, it\u2019s just a nicely drawn and coloured picture of a typical investigation scene.<br /> <br /> Hmm, that wasn\u2019t too long\u2026 I think I can fit the ministries in here as well.<br /> <br /> {About halfway through going through them}<br /> <br /> Nope, there are a fuckton of ministries detailed. I\u2019m honestly quite happy with that, for reasons that should be apparent when I get to them, but that\u2019s going to have to wait until tomorrow and be a whole post to itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T18:06:45-07:00","id":1315320,"title":"","content":"FYI Isator, I keep buying Exalted books and it's ALL YOUR FAULT.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-29T18:32:17-07:00","id":1315328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315320#post1315320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">FYI Isator, I keep buying Exalted books and it's ALL YOUR FAULT.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, I think Robert Vance and Eric Minton should get some of the credit for that. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T06:21:57-07:00","id":1315427,"title":"","content":"<b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/415-equitable-remedy","name":"Equitable Remedy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar415_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T06:47:57-07:00","id":1315432,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &amp;quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&amp;quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ummm... did you happen to read the paragraph after the one you quoted? The whole point of that story is setting up a situation for Dynast player characters to intervene showing that Dragons DO have teeth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T06:55:10-07:00","id":1315434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.</div> </div> </div> </div>Spoiler: those bandits are there because Dragon-Blooded of three houses are manipulating the whole thing behind the scenes as part of a complex plan to kick House Nellens in the teeth and secure holdings in the Threshold. There's a lot of layers to it beyond bandits rolling up and killing a bunch of patricians and mortal monks. That bandit leader thinks she's stronger than the pampered Dynasts who are blessed with elemental divinity, but she's just their pawn.<br /> <br /> Dragons are highly competent, but they're not unified, and that's what is driving the dissolution of the Realm. When a vast entity like the Realm goes into chaos mode, lots of people are inclined to see that chaos as a ladder, and they will work to accelerate the destruction for their personal gain. And they will do so, like all the Exalted do, with excellence.<br /> <br /> On a personal note, I've witnessed Dragon-Blooded get the short shrift across two editions, and nobody I know of on the writing team wants that to be the case for a third.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Neall Raemonn Price</span><br /> Beleaguered Scion Developer</b>","edited":{"userid":137,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","user":"Neall","date":". 05-30-2019, 10:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/137-neall","name":"Neall","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar137_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1204"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T06:57:24-07:00","id":1315435,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh no, the canonical answer to &quot;what happens when an immortal god-queen and absolute ruler vanishes before she can iron out who the heir is&quot; is &quot;bad things to her country.&quot; How...deprotagonizing?<br /> <br /> Seriously, the Realm ain't weak. It's got a mortal wound, but characters are unreliable sources; up until the exact moment the Empress vanished, it was quite happily chugging along, plopping satrapies everywhere. Yeah, the Lunars were wearing them down, but &quot;Silver Pact victory in a few centuries&quot; isn't exactly &quot;Realm dying.&quot; More like &quot;entropy is a thing that happens.&quot; Really, the fact that pretty much Her Redness' entire special operations apparatus and intelligence agency more or less rolled with it is two strikes against them being incompetent. Really, the Realm's problem is that the Empress didn't see herself vanishing; it's even canonical that she felt she needed to select an heir before she vanished, she just thought she had a century to think about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T06:58:09-07:00","id":1315436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If you are going to judge the whole book based on your slanted take of an intro fiction, honestly your loss and that of your group.<br /> <br /> Trying to get a clearer image somewhere - like, i don't know, the 7 pages of a thread you apparently did not read before posting - might help with that a little, i guess.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T07:04:06-07:00","id":1315439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Realm is overstretched, it cannot sustain itself in its current state. It's not a matter of DBs being weak or incompetent, it's an abnormal state of affairs due to a very unique set of circumstances.<br /> <br /> You're free to like the new content or not, but it seems contradictory that you hold the Realm as the most interesting society and yet consider that society's flaws as an instant turn off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","name":"Epitome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25285_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T07:37:48-07:00","id":1315445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I\u2019m the one who outlined </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>The Realm</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">\u2019s opening fiction with bandits attacking a band of mortal travelers, so I figure my perspective may be of interest.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">First, the Dragon-Blooded are my favorite Exalted. I think they\u2019re cool, in large part because of how involved they are with mortal matters. And this is an edition which posits that mortals matter; that they\u2019re more than scenery, that they can be meaningful challenges for PCs to confront and overcome.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Blessed Isle is the heart of Dynastic power, but if it\u2019s a walled garden where nothing bad ever happens, there\u2019s not much scope to tell exciting stories there. Conveniently, the scale of the Isle and the Empress\u2019 disappearance provide strong justifications for disorder and conflict. There are thousands of Dragon-Blooded in the Dynasty, but most of them are busy with bureaucratic, commercial, military, or personal pursuits; and the Isle is too vast for mortals or Exalts to effectively police. This gives scope for scenarios like, yes, rooting out overbold bandits.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Second, while </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>The Realm</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> is a book that\u2019s primarily useful in Dragon-Blooded games, it\u2019s not meant exclusively for that purpose. It\u2019s a setting book in a place where the Dragon-Blooded Dynasty is a significant player, but it isn\u2019t exclusively a Dragon-Blooded book. The Blessed Isle and the satrapies are home to ten thousand Dragon-Blooded, yes, but they live amid countless millions of mortals \u2015 not to mention occasional non-Dragon-Blooded Exalted.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Blessed Isle is home to patricians, peasants, and yes, bandits. I wanted to emphasize that up front; that even when you\u2019re playing Dragon-Blooded on the Isle, there\u2019s a whole world of mortal activity going on. And when you\u2019re playing a Solar, Lunar, Exigent, or the like on the Isle, there\u2019s stuff to get involved with that doesn\u2019t involve confronting the Dragon-Blooded directly.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The chapter fiction isn\u2019t meant to be the end of a joke where the Dragon-Blooded are the punchline. It\u2019s the start of a story; it\u2019s the part that happens before your PCs \u2015 whether Dragon-Blooded or not \u2015 have become involved.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T07:44:36-07:00","id":1315447,"title":"","content":"I'll be honest, with the Blessed Isle book, I felt it was okay as just somewhere to come from; as a setting to play in, it was deathly dull, except for the Deliberative.<br /> <br /> This book seems better; there's ghost-haunted ruins, bandits, ancient anathema tombs, first age stuff gone mad, Dragonblood plotting against each other, etc.<br /> <br /> <br /> For myself, while I think the Realm is a good book, it's not super-interesting because my game is in the far north-east, as far from the Realm as you can be. But were I running a game in the Realm, it's much better than the Blessed Isle book.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T07:45:09-07:00","id":1315448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315432#post1315432\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ummm... did you happen to read the paragraph after the one you quoted? The whole point of that story is setting up a situation for Dynast player characters to intervene showing that Dragons DO have teeth.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was actually thinking that that intro fiction is the absolute perfect starting scene for a game where the PCs are all Immaculate monks. Like maybe they\u2019re having a friendly debate about whether it is more important to emulate all the dragons imperfectly, or one dragon perfectly, and then they happen upon this scene. Now, as they are humble monks in friendly territory they\u2019re not bedecked in jade, so the bandits just think they\u2019ve snagged another catch for that day. <br /> <br /> Until, of course, five of those supposedly mortal monks spin/drop/leap into their various dragon martial arts forms to a collective \u201cOhhhh shiiiiiiit\u201d from the bandits. Or maybe a smirk and a shrug from the thieves followed quickly by a lesson in Immaculate doctrine called \u201cthe unmindful fox\u201d delivered by way of a roundhouse kick that could shatter a stone pillar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T08:38:55-07:00","id":1315458,"title":"","content":"Just finished my first read-through of the books, I am sure that I missed some stuff or didnt catch everything.<br /> <br /> But wow, it was absolutely awesome and if I am honest it created a whole new bar for quality in setting books for me. I usually have a gripe or two about any given thing but there was absolutely nothing that irked or bothered me with this book.<br /> <br /> Actually, there is one thing, now I'm impatient for more setting stuff!<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:20:59-07:00","id":1315514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &amp;quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&amp;quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mate. It\u2019s the intro fiction. Maybe, just maybe, the speaking character has some BIAS.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3487-jetstream","name":"Jetstream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3487_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:48:46-07:00","id":1315522,"title":"","content":"Dude\u2019s allowed to be unhappy with the tone of what ought to be a tone-setting line, and wary of what it portends. I don\u2019t agree with his assessment but let\u2019s not go all public pillory on this; it\u2019s a bad look.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T11:55:07-07:00","id":1315525,"title":"","content":"I'm not super far into the book, but I just wanna throw mad props at the writing team. They did a fantastic job providing a rich, detailed look at life and culture on the Blessed Isle that's still enjoyable to read, unlike the bone dry textbook writing of 2E.<br /> One more tally in the &quot;Third Edition Best Edition&quot; column.<br /> <br /> And kinda jumping on the bandwagon, that intro fiction absolutely screams wuxia/chanbara opening scene to me. Absolutely in line with your Yojimbo, Seven Samurai, Dragon Inn. I can picture the coolie hat wearing wanderer stopping at the scene, the bandit telling them to mind their own business.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":882,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","user":"SamuraiMujuru","date":". 05-30-2019, 02:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T13:18:36-07:00","id":1315558,"title":"","content":"Thanks to y'all for the good vibes! &lt;3<br /> <br /> If anyone spots an error in the current PDF of <i>The Realm</i>, please let us know via this online form: <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfFsC6mRrRwDkWAYlUgSO4EyG7OifqCdl3vsqK64dLopUQx-g/viewform\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfFsC6mRrRwDkWAYlUgSO4EyG7OifqCdl3vsqK64dLopUQx-g/viewform\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfFsC6mRrRwDkWAYlUgSO4EyG7OifqCdl3vsqK64dLopUQx-g/viewform\" target=\"_blank\"> https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfFsC6mRrRwDkWAYlUgSO4EyG7OifqCdl3vsqK64dLopUQx-g/viewform </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T13:43:51-07:00","id":1315566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Equitable Remedy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315427#post1315427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Warning: Contains Heterodox Takes</b><br /> <br /> I was equal parts excited and anxious about The Realm as I think that the Realm is, by far, the most interesting society in Creation. I took that ambivalence to drivethrurpg to take a look at The Realm preview to see if a wanted to buy it.<br /> <br /> I read the opening chapter fiction, and the excitement died.<br /> <br /> I was already resigned to the fact that a lot of word count would be dedicated to DBs/the Dynasty being weak and incompetent, but the opening chapter fiction? &amp;quot;Dragons don't have teeth. If they did... I wouldn't be here.&amp;quot; You and me both, sister.<br /> <br /> Exalted seems to be working overtime to get me not to buy any more books in the line. Of course, I'm just one guy of no particular consequence; I'm just sharing my reaction to the new product.<br /> <br /> Maybe I'll pick up the line again when Sidereals drops.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it's not the best preview in that context. The book is okay; it doesn't continue in that fashion. *shrug*<br /> <br /> I'm not really a fan of the new canon, and I have enough old books to run a game set in the Realm. But if you're new to Exalted then it's a pretty comprehensive resource. It's not all Dragons getting owned in their own back-yard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T17:33:19-07:00","id":1315605,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m not sure if this is a typo or a deeper meaning or something, but the sidebar \u201cA Footsoldier Wakes\u201d makes mention of \u201cthe five other men in his tent\u201d which is presumably the rest of his Fang and his Fanglord. Did I misunderstand again, and he\u2019s just bunking with five other random male soldiers? Or is this Fang being made up entirely of men just a random quirk? Or are Fangs segregated by gender?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T18:17:03-07:00","id":1315609,"title":"","content":"Okay, drawing a direct comparison first off, I\u2019m extremely glad that the section on these does not begin with an <i>interminably</i> long description of the procedures for resolving jurisdictional disputes. I\u2019ve always wanted to like the Thousand Scales as an organ of the Realm, but somehow I\u2019ve never quite felt that the books were interested in giving a strong sense of what they were and where they were located. You got scattered references to their concerns in the succession crisis, but never really something as direct and consolidated as introducing them with a description of struggling fiercely to hold their departments together in governing the Realm while the Exalted elites are focused on dividing territories and possessions up. It illustrates them as an institution that functions in its own right, rather than just another arena for the Dynasty; more on that in a bit.<br /> <br /> First off, it helps me to actually picture their bureaucratic functioning to be given a description of not just the sizes of ministries, but the kinds of offices that they operate within (as well as where they\u2019re located in the Imperial City and around the Isle, and what reason there is for some devolution). I find the more dynamic character of the Blessed Isle to be reflected in the matter of how rural offices for ministries might be assigned, as it implies to my that they\u2019re not infrequently moved around (and I chuckle at the image of some recently relocated bureaucrats needing to settle in to an appropriated country manor). Similar goes for how the Empress\u2019 process of checking the power of individual bureaus creates scenarios such as division, absorption and some needing to temporarily take on additional duties when an especially offensive one has to be dissolved and reconstituted from scratch.<br /> <br /> So, for the matter of the Thousand Scales as something that stands largely separate from the authority of the Scarlet Dynasty, I think a lot if helped by them really doubling down on the prominence of the patricians in them; for one thing, it provides a clear picture of exactly who the bureaucrats are. It also gives a basis for some patrician families to have a standing of mortal gentry of long history, even if they lack things like direct land holdings, rather than coming across solely as the lackeys of the Dynasty. The matter of unExalted Dynasts entering the Scales only to find that they\u2019re suddenly the smaller fish who need to make more subservient appeals to patrician families (helpfully given a number of names to latch onto) isn\u2019t just a fun reversal, but lends mortals more agency and authority even in the Realm itself. And setting it up as a prize for Dynasts to fight for control over helps to give it a stake in the conflict and make it more of a player on its own standing.<br /> <br /> The description of entering the Thousand Scales is also a step up, foregoing a lot of words about how attractive it is across class lines and instead presenting the actual procedure, and the practical obstacles for people of lesser means managing to so much as take the exams, let alone present a respectable image of themselves should they do well enough to be given an opportunity. It makes the matter seem less like deliberately dangling peasant applicants on the end of a string, in a way that made the government of the Realm look a bit too underhanded and duplicitous. No, I prefer this idea that it generally amounts to a way of giving the wealthy a means of forging connections in the bureaucracy by sponsoring their way through the doors. I could actually see it as covering the First Edition point of giving the ministries a large pool of potentially talented candidates to select from at the same time as it encourages the higher patricians and even the Great Houses to foot some of the bill.<br /> <br /> The description of advancement through the ranks reads a lot more deft and nuanced to me; assassination to create openings is still there, but there\u2019s none of this talk of it being treated in the cavalier manner of \u201cpersonally killing a superior is considered tacky\u201d. Yes, I\u2019m recalling the main things that I found frustrating about the original write-up; between the amount of words devoted to how hard it was to peruse the regulations defining everybody\u2019s job (with those gigantic three thousand page books) and the idea that they\u2019re constantly embroiled in scheming to murder one another (there\u2019s a reference to treating it like a spectator sport), it was hard to envision them ever actually getting any <i>work</i> done. No, keep it short and sweet; the idea that they\u2019ll often target people a few steps up the ladder to create an opening immediately above while making it harder to trace is clever enough. That and the attention given to all of the forms of leverage that can be applied against a superior or colleague who remains alive. There\u2019s a fun subversion to the idea that, in the current uncertainties, assassination actually becomes a less surefire way to seek promotions as the Deliberative appropriates the power to fill emergency vacancies and the whole thing becomes deadlocked with Dynastic scheming and simple paperwork inertia. It\u2019s a nice touch of humour to end on the image of somebody successfully murdering a superior, just to find that the only way they can try and secure advancement is through taking the exams.<br /> <br /> The ministries themselves have firmer ground to stand on. The Treasury in particular always struck me as one of the stronger written organs of the Realm\u2019s government, and Bal Keraz (now given a fancy title of Empress\u2019 Exchequer, and now the successor of a mother instead of father) was somebody I always wanted to know more about. As such I\u2019d say it needed little adjustment; what changes are made, the ministers who started misappropriating funds, were purged by way of Keraz\u2019s alliance with the All-Seeing Eye, and have thrown in their lot with the Dynasty where they can, looks to me to be all for the good of increasing the Treasury\u2019s presence in the oncoming conflict. The Edition\u2019s greater considerations for financial practices having an expression in the form of the value of the treasure-manses (another fine old element) being something transferable despite lacking the means of penetrating their defences to access the hard currency is another fun dimension to how canny the institute\u2019s managers are.<br /> <br /> The Humble and Honest Assessors of the Imperial Tax is obviously a good place to reiterate how everybody is engaged in the game of trying to conceal their assets from the government, and it gives a little something extra to the surveyors and collectors with that idea that they might often make their rounds in disguise and could do with a grounding in self-defence (something about that reads strangely familiar to me, and I don\u2019t think it\u2019s how they were written in the past\u2026 I have the oddest sense that a similar idea came up in forum discussion a long time ago). I like the tension underlying how the person in charge retains integrity while his department becomes rife with corruption, and find the newly defined attitude that Cathaks bring to business to help envision Curuk\u2019s personality and actions a bit more. He also comes across as a figure more willing to fight for the future of his charge.<br /> <br /> Of the three financial ministries, the Foreign Office appears to have made the transition with the least alteration, hardly any at all really; it\u2019s still the body concerned both with coordinating assessment of the satrapies\u2019 worth and advising on how to exploit them without causing too much damage. The major change seems to primarily emerge from the overall Edition\u2019s modification of how the satrapy system works; Ledaal Arnis\u2019 main frustration remains that the tribute is not flowing correctly, and she\u2019s even still presented as unable to see a way out of her position, but the concern has gone from the satrapies asserting increasing independence to the manner in which the Houses are both not remanding the state its share of the tribute and heedlessly sucking their holdings dry. It\u2019s still a fine example of a more prominent department of the government, and I\u2019d say it\u2019s okay to present at least one of the people in charge as being a bit crushed under the pressure of the state collapsing. And it\u2019s particularly fitting to this one, since the question of how the Houses are implementing sustainable foreign policy would be the most out of her hands, compared to the other two grappling with their own subordinates who are present on the Isle with them.<br /> <br /> Oh, okay; the enormous Imperial Registry is still around. I still maintain that the approach, even the placement, here prevents it from giving the Thousand Scales an image of being tremendously ponderous. Everything about the interpreters remains the same, but expressing it with this brevity helps contribute to the sense of efficiency. I\u2019d also say that this is a ministry in both a tangential and important enough position to be afforded some of that extreme violence in its punishments. Nothing much to say about Wave Akeru, but I like how her duty is both given some activity and used as the means to convey how the Registry is distributed.<br /> <br /> (This would be where I realised I needed to break it up)<br /> <br /> There are a few things I like about this art piece. The lighting is very nice and it\u2019s good to be given a scene of what the bureaucratic activity actually consists of. I like the way the whole thing is centred around a single colourful individual who is implicitly Exalted, which can also give a sense of the prominence they hold in a given office. And the fact that most of the people featured are women (although that does make me notice how there hasn\u2019t yet been a statement on them being more prominent in administrative positions).<br /> <br /> Errrr\u2026 okay, I\u2019m going to handle the main ministries first, and then go back to cover the ones in sidebars.<br /> <br /> Now, the imperial judges. This would be another one where I often felt as though there were a lot of mentions on them that never quite covered basic information like where they\u2019re based out of or how cases are brought before them, so I\u2019m happy to see that addressed here. Given the description of how the Black-Helms often have the authority to punish wrongdoers or resolve disputes, the ideas for the forms of pressure that have them remand cases to the courts sound interesting, both in terms of what convinces them to not exercise their own authority and instances in which it\u2019s impressed upon them to not let an offence slide with a warning. The judges themselves still have the issues inherent to the matter of pulling double duty as prosecutor while there\u2019s no involvement of legal counsel, and the law code sounds sufficiently complex that it\u2019s not the thing that can be inscribed on public monoliths and made readily comprehensible. But the matters of how they employ investigators and are ascribed skills such as forensics and interrogation at least implies that they\u2019re expected to be comprehensive in judging cases, and the power of magistrates to unilaterally overturn verdicts sounds like an incentive to do due diligence; I can imagine that it was often an embarrassment to have one of the enforcers of the Empress impugn your honour and authority like that. My queries about the form of punishments is handily addressed here; I imagine that fines are by far the most common form of punishment (and a handy source of prefectural revenue), and that beatings (which I presume includes things like lashing) are typically for those who couldn\u2019t afford them. If it\u2019s one judge per prefecture, I can readily see how House Peleps Exalted or mortal scions could be prominent among them without straining their numbers (the Exalted in particular seeming well suited for the level of activity ascribed), and I like a bit of intrigue entailed by the upper classes getting a special appeals court; the whole thing also gives a nice picture of how the Houses would be looking to appoint judges firmly in their pockets. Last of all, I find the sidebar on their role in Dynastic marriage to be amusing, that notion of being favoured for eloquence and often being a sinecure for older ones.<br /> <br /> The Honourable and Humble Caretakers of the Common Folk would be the last major one detailed in First Edition. I find that nothing in their remit has been particularly changed, rather elaborated upon to give a clearer picture of the daily practicalities (which is <i>precisely</i> the kind of thing that I would want from a write-up such as this); how they monitor crop yields and designate food stock assignments, assess infrastructure and war manse upkeep, determine which prefectures have the greatest need for resources for the Guardians and audit prefectural finances to ensure that local officials aren\u2019t squeezing their charges or not giving the state its cut. It strikes me as a very effective meeting point between the Realm\u2019s devolved government and central control, and something liable to generate a massive quantity of paperwork. I particularly like being given a clear instance of the deliberate overlap in purviews with how their assessment of taxation clashes with that of the Humble Assessors. The matter of the replacement for the murdered Custodian being blocked by the Deliberate feels stronger in the context of their wider practice, and I like how we\u2019re given particular examples of the consequences of that lack of leadership, giving a clearer picture of the Realm\u2019s gradual diminishment; it\u2019s also interesting to see a bureau being brought down not by unchecked corruption of its officials or being divided up among the Houses, but the simple disarray coming from how their command structure is being gutted and there are private interests that would merely ignore them. Bal Keraz and Cathak Kuruk taking responsibility for trying to give some direction for the flailing ministry is the sort of thing I always liked about them.<br /> <br /> The Stewards of Imperial Assets are new, and appear quite fitting to the focus elsewhere on the Blessed Isle\u2019s sophisticated infrastructure (and this nice new emphasis on the Realm Defence Grid as a more distributed system). I find that I can easily reconcile the statement that there\u2019s fierce competition for construction and maintenance bids with how What Fire Has Wrought ascribes House Mnemon the right of first refusal for contracts from the Throne with an idea that the Mnemons simply\u2026 refuse a sizable number of projects, either because they\u2019re committed elsewhere or a given project isn\u2019t lucrative enough (either that or it was meant to be even more literal and refer to personal commissions from the Empress); in any case, the idea of firms violently clashing or sabotaging one another enriches the character of the Isle a bit, as well as being very true to what is typical of the history and conduct of construction workers. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It\u2019s interesting to me how the current situation represents a likely hitherto unrealised implication of the disruptive policies of the Empress; there might have been circumstances in which all of the Stewards needed to be disbanded and the Caretakers temporarily took up their duties, but I\u2019d say there\u2019s never before been a case in which one ministry technically survived but had been so compromised that it crashed into a closely related other one executing its duties. The connection to the Realm Defence Grid in particular probably gives the ministry a particularly strong commitment, if their institutional culture is strongly indoctrinated with the idea that the key defensive weapon of the empire, the one that literally saved the world, was in their charge, and that lends a compelling touch of desperation to the idea that they prioritize maintaining the war manses above all else. That ministry head Rose Adal might have insight into its functioning from a lengthy career makes the Sword a more engaging and versatile target than the inscrutable single manse that has been focused on before, and it strongly appeals to me that a figure of such focus for the agendas and appeals of the Great Houses is a mortal and patrician.<br /> <br /> The Sagacious and Scrupulous Registrars of Sorcerous Puissance are something technically new, although they cover a familiar element of the setting: the obligations of sorcerers to the Scarlet Empress. Really, with their broader array of powers in the form of workings and the manner in which sorcery might be a bit more widespread among the populace and tolerated from the lower classes, keeping a comprehensive registry of them would seem more important than ever; I think it gives some interesting activities to ascribe them consultant sorcerers who help to assess the activities or presence of others. I find something particularly fun about the idea that the Great Houses value unregistered sorcerers for being able to come at one another from unforeseen angles, and the idea that there would be ones who find the prospect so unappealing that they go out of their way to become part of the official record. Not much to say about the Obligations themselves, beyond an appreciation for some of the guidelines given behind the history of how the Empress exercised that authority (particularly waiting quite a while for them to become powerful enough to have a greater breadth and depth of services to call upon). I find Rein Melana to constitute a fun contrast with the wider perceptions of sorcerers; that while they themselves carry a certain stigma, there\u2019s something seen as exciting and mysterious to an official who is charged with management of them. Along the same lines as sorcerers looking to register, I quite like the idea that they\u2019re doing as much as the Houses are to appeal to Melana with the hopes that she\u2019ll make them sound less desirable to exploit toward the ends of the Realm\u2019s elite.<br /> <br /> As I said when it was brought up in What Fire Has Wrought, the Righteous and Accountable Ministry of Weights and Measures struck me as a bureau that previous Editions treated a bit humorously with its flowery title and mundane portfolio, being redeemed with a more serious attitude to the implications of enforcing standardisation. I see that as being effectively built upon and expanded here, with points such as how standard measurements of length are not only employed in things such as land assessment and boundary resolution, but determining the purity of coinage being offered in tribute (tying nicely into the updated description of the importance of silver to the Realm\u2019s economy). Even the most direct applications of measurement standards sound more important here, whether it be ensuring the proper construction of buildings or how keeping merchants weighing goods keeps the economy flowing smoothly. Hence, it builds a lot of credibility for it being an institute whose ministers are ripe for corruption and traditionally a strong target for the magistracy. I like the idea that an outcaste brought up on Peleps principles might be liable to have gone far enough in trying to keep the ministry honest to invite an assassination attempt, and think there\u2019s something interesting to the implication that the Dynasty attempting to interfere with this branch of the bureaucracy would be especially crossing the line; that it\u2019s one thing to not be sending the government its due, and another entirely to try and obfuscate exactly how much is owed or being sent in the first place.<br /> <br /> The Splendid and Just Arbiters of Purpose are basically identical here to their presentation in What Fire Has Wrought, even if a description of the ministries would be incomplete without them. The main important thing I\u2019m getting is the Master of Orphans and the agendas of the Scarlet Dynasty concerning the ministry; I think Agama Orir having personal ties to House Tepet and directing outcastes in their direction is both another good addition to giving that floundering House a bit more of a fighting chance, and a reminder that corruption isn\u2019t always an unsympathetic thing. And I have a particular liking for the idea that the Empress\u2019 policies towards outcastes, at the expense of the Dynasts, would be something they\u2019d resent enough to want to eliminate the bureau altogether and finally have direct access to the found eggs; the idea that Mnemon\u2019s austerity and high concern for its pedigree would leave them so concerned with not being saddled with the thin-blooded or leave them to be snatched up by rivals that they would advocate for the Arbiters is just gravy.<br /> <br /> My but this is lengthy. I\u2019ll be a bit more reserved in assessing the sidebar ones, unless any jump out.<br /> <br /> Okay, we\u2019ve got a couple of additional finance ministries to probably establish that area of government as the most intentionally convoluted in the Realm. The Empress\u2019 Private Purse reminds me that there has thus far been no reference to the old rituals entailed in taxation, so between that and referring to the cash as integral to the Realm\u2019s economy and future (particularly somebody acknowledging that it\u2019s what they\u2019d need to pay the common soldiers in) I\u2019m happy to declare them dead, and the scrip itself raised a bit from the idea of it being a kind of elaborate con to have the populace deal in worthless money*. Meanwhile the Bursars of Barbarian Tribute help to further place the matter of collecting silver from the satrapies into context, especially with the manner in which the Realm employed it; I particularly like the idea that it was mostly stored for the sake of backing instruments used in dealing with merchants and foreign princes, not only for how sensible that would be in place of dealing in large quantities of coinage but the implication that an indirect means for the Empress to exert control in the Threshold, even beyond the borders of satrapies, was by controlling the currency supply (not to mention tying those who would deal in Realm bonds more closely to its trade, as such documents were probably harder to enforce among other merchants than they would be back on the Blessed Isle). I like how the Dynasty would prize the reserves for their potential in hiring foreign soldiers to supplement their divided legions and exert more direct power over their Threshold possessions, and enjoy the ingenuity of the patrician head trying to appropriate the money towards raising a private army to assert her house\u2019s own position should the worst happen.<br /> <br /> Not much to say on the Compilers of the Curriculum beyond liking that it\u2019s there to showcase centralised authority over education, while the Devoted Wardens of the Empressi Wilderness are a good reminder that bureaucracy isn\u2019t all about administration of the inhabitants. In a place with the size and diverse ecology of the Blessed Isle, I would imagine that overseeing the wilderness and safeguarding against wildfires is a particularly invested responsibility, and I get a kick out of the idea that dealing with a group as privileged as the Scarlet Dynasty would provide an especially tense motivating factor to ensuring that the forests are kept populous with game (it\u2019s also nice to have a name for the people that poachers would be concerned with dodging). I can even see it as having an unstated overlapping responsibility with a few other ministries; I have to imagine that being responsible for safeguarding against, say, earthquakes or floods results in jurisdictional disputes with the Caretakers and Stewards.<br /> <br /> Huh, I wasn\u2019t expecting genealogies to be something that the Realm would place under government control, although its importance to tracking and assessing the Dragon\u2019s Blood makes sense as something that the state would want to keep track of and keep honest. I\u2019ll have to think about how this might complicate my ideas of the forgery of such records as a fraught subject for intrigue\u2026 okay I\u2019ve thought about it; it adds an extra layer of complication to such activities in the form of things like coordinating heists or bribes to have such records placed in official storage (adding even more motive to cover-ups or fighting investigation as it can go beyond undermining marriage negotiations and into outright concealing criminal activity), and I would see the charges for access to records as being the kind of thing that might incentivize those trying to determine somebody\u2019s lineage to seek alternative avenues (as well as a clever additional means for the Empress to have bilked money from the Houses). I also like the infighting inherent to Sesus working to prevent Nellens from accessing the data that would assist its quest to improve their pedigree.<br /> <br /> I enjoy the idea that the ministry behind issuing permits for crossing prefecture lines has limited prestige or meaningful power, but is a big enough deal in the lives of even people who can be quite wealthy as to make positions therein lucrative. And while the Imperial Post was elaborately detailed elsewhere, I find this chapter\u2019s context to give stronger weight to the point that, for all the power of the Great Houses, maintaining the lines of communication with the human assets that even they are limited to (let alone the operations of the Blessed Isle as a whole) is so integral to the function of the land that it\u2019s the one ministry that can be relied upon to be allowed to function and keep its operating budget in this Time of Tumult (despite the efforts of its Master\u2019s House). Last is the ministry dedicated to collecting dubious relics; I\u2019m guessing that their being so poorly fitted would be a combination of such an area being ultimately a low priority in the Realm\u2019s administration for the limited practical value, as well as how much of that purview is already handled by private interests. It\u2019s another one where it\u2019s good to have a name for the people behind preserving sanctioned historical accounts. I also like being given another reference to the clandestine research of Ledaal and Ragara.<br /> <br /> Finally there\u2019s the sidebar on sorcerous societies. The basic principle behind them, that stigma in the Realm creates an environment for them to band together and have their own network to coordinate or simply socialize without apprehension, that always seemed like a good extrapolation from the characteristics of sorcerers in this place and basis for drama. Yet in practice they\u2019ve never quite grabbed me, for reasons of often seeming to be overlooked and a few of the examples being a bit banal (it also wasn\u2019t helped by first placing them at the tail end of the chapter about the life of Dynasts). I\u2019ll admit that I don\u2019t think this rendition gets to the best start with the Sorcerers of the Scarlet Throne, who I find to just be a tad too Magicians\u2019 Alliance in their demands to be taken seriously (although there is something fun to the idea of the Empress amusing herself by exploiting their conceits to put their powers to menial tasks); there is at least something helpful to throw into the buildup to war in some of them making overtures to the Scarlet prefect, although none of this is quite new. Similar with the Watchers, although the original premise being replicated here was at least a bit more interesting, employed to police their compatriots against letting their weirder powers get to their heads (especially as regards demons) and sinisterly eliminating them. The one original addition, the Cabal of Righteous Midwives, has a lot more going for it in how they fold into this intriguing new dimension to Dynastic society, and I do like the descriptions of how binding and commanding the relevant demons to fulfil the task satisfactorily would entail specialised knowledge.<br /> <br /> Hhhhowever\u2026 I do feel a need to be a bit critical of this sidebar on the grounds that it feels a bit at odds with itself. Societies are introduced largely as something like subcultures to allow sorcerers outlets despite the manner in which they\u2019re marginalized in Dynastic society, but then the examples we get make them seem a lot more like trade guilds trying to ply specialized services to that society for prestige or profit. It\u2019s not helped by having two out of three of them dedicated in particular to serving the Scarlet Empress. The idea of trying to organise themselves to coordinate or more effectively advertise making their powers available as is the typical remit of sorcerers in the Realm, that would be fine but I would want it to be how they were summarized (and maybe one more example given that wasn\u2019t dedicated to the Empress). If you\u2019re going to introduce them as subcultures, I feel as though it would be much stronger to convey them in terms of outlooks and practices that arise from them sharing their particular unusual insights and personal experiences. Heck, even the ones given could fit into that with a few adjustments in the wording; I would find the Scarlet Throne one a bit more compelling if there was a better sense that their conceits and ambitions were a direct response to the usual marginal status, or if the Watchers were described not as a result of official commission but emerging from a few sorcerers who got together and concluded from shared anecdotes that, hey, sorcery represents a unique spiritual danger to its practitioners and people of the Realm at large, and maybe we should keep an eye on our fellows.<br /> <br /> But, leaving all of that aside, the description of the Ministries is quite to my satisfaction. I think it does a lot to convey the mechanisms of the bureaucracy both in terms of how they\u2019re engaged by the paper pushers and interacted with and impacting the populace at large. It strengthens their sense of importance to the operation of the Realm, effectively sets them apart from the Great Houses in a manner that makes them both target and rival (even when Dynastic scions are incorporated into their structure), and there are a reasonable number of hooks into the incipient conflict strewn throughout. It has overt and covert references to the in-built structural weaknesses that feel credible, and incorporates the idea of killing one\u2019s way up the ladder without extending it to the level of caricature (and worse, eating up a majority of the word count). In short, this is a description of the Thousand Scales of the kind that I\u2019ve always wanted.<br /> <br /> (As should be apparent from this particular entry having been <i>so</i> long. Like, analysing Charms long, it\u2019s ridiculous. But informative.)<br /> <br /> <br /> *Okay; having looked back at the credits gave me the slightest glance at the word \u201cmoney\u201d in the table of contents, reminding me that there\u2019s still a lot of dedicated information to provide about the flow and handling of currency in the Realm and the policies therein, so I understand that I\u2019m not quite out of the woods yet. Optimistic outlook: such will be an opportunity to provide a direct layout of the desired changes, or at least frame the old models in a manner that reads more sensibly or comprehensibly to myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T19:49:23-07:00","id":1315616,"title":"","content":"As a side note, I like the implication that a peasant minister has still overcome the largest hurdle once certified despite all the costs of being a minister, because the Great Houses and patricians see a highly competent individual who isn't bound by previous familial alliances and tend to lend them a hand up, getting a huge favor owed to them in the process. It's a realistic reaction, and it shows why peasants still try to become ministers; it's a quick route to the middle and upper classes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-30T21:03:46-07:00","id":1315629,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stephen Lea Sheppard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315522#post1315522\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dude\u2019s allowed to be unhappy with the tone of what ought to be a tone-setting line, and wary of what it portends. I don\u2019t agree with his assessment but let\u2019s not go all public pillory on this; it\u2019s a bad look.</div> </div> </div> </div>Normally I wouldn't, but making a judgment of a game line based upon a single snippet of fiction that doesn't even really qualify as a short story is just... What?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3487-jetstream","name":"Jetstream","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3487_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T07:59:35-07:00","id":1315691,"title":"","content":"The All-Seeing Eye would be another element of the Realm that I thought was always pretty solid, so I wouldn\u2019t require much beyond invigorating the language and a bit of tidying up. The introduction is nice, conveying the point about the organisation serving as a national panopticon with the added novelty of being a subject of adventure literature (which also helps to characterise the Realm\u2019s culture a bit more), and it uses just enough colourful language without hyping them up too much. I again like the reference back to their origins in the Realm\u2019s tumultuous beginning.<br /> <br /> I find the idea that the organisation officially doesn\u2019t exist, being written off as the subject of an overactive public imagination, to be cute, and I find that image to be helped a lot by the idea that it operates in a decentralized enough manner as to not have bureaucratic operation or oversight, which I suspect has often historically been true of secret police and state intelligence. I like seeing that point made about the range of backgrounds that its operatives and informants can be drawn from, and the humbler origins it might have, which matches up nicely with a point made about how only a minority are career Dragon Blooded; this part does read like a departure from First Edition to me, where it seemed to be saying that most of the agents were Exalted, and this version is something I find both preferable and sensible. That works well with providing a clear picture of how the Eye is structured, that pyramid of assets to agents to handlers to intelligencers: that the primary activity of agents is likely to be the recruitment of and communication with assets who do the bulk of the literal spying heavywork, the agents reporting to and being coordinated, directed and assessed by handlers, who process it into key information given to their own superiors for report to the Empress. I think it\u2019s helpful to give this description, since the obvious clandestine nature of intelligence work means that the actual practicalities of how it functions are not necessarily familiar to people. With all of that in combination with the point of how a particular focus for the Eye is the River Province, where they\u2019re in competition with Lookshy\u2019s rangers, I\u2019d say that we have all the necessary makings for an Exalted John LeCarre novel (while there are still the Bond-esque dedicated and well-furnished Exalted assassins). I would imagine that, in the absence of a formal support network to do analysis work, the upper levels of the pyramid find a lot of exercise for their supernatural talents to make up for it in sheer data processing.<br /> <br /> It would seem from that sidebar that the role of the All-Seeing Eye in the Wyld Hunt is being downplayed a bit. The biggest thing standing out to me there is that the utility of astrologers in identifying the incarnation of Celestial Exalted appears to have been scaled back significantly, which I find to be both in keeping with the Edition\u2019s general approach to such magic, and something to make the Realm historically a bit less omniscient when it comes to the identification of Anathema, which I would say helps a lot with being able to present the Lunars as operating more in the Threshold. I like how it ties into the Realm\u2019s diminishment with the idea that, in the absence of a well-funded Wyld Hunt to concentrate significant force on Anathema, it\u2019s falling more and more on Eye operatives to try and come at those enemies sideways; it also gives a nice picture for how Lunars in play might respond to facing an unfamiliar distribution of force.<br /> <br /> The artwork there is very good, possibly one of the best in the book so far (maybe even out of all of these books); the colours, lighting and resolution convey a very sinister tone, which complements how well the man\u2019s expression is drawn to depict the terrifying uncertainty of receiving a token from the Eye. The way that the background consists of his view of the object in his hand is an especially nice touch, since it both symbolically conveys to the reader the manner in which he\u2019s being figuratively observed by something unidentifiable and dominating and conveys the character\u2019s own perspective on what he\u2019s looking at; that to see an object printed with that insignia is to feel as though you\u2019re holding an eye that is literally watching you. The manner in which the object and background are aligned also gives this nice impression that the token is being projected out and above him. It\u2019s a very good accompaniment.<br /> <br /> On that note, there\u2019s a repeat of the familiar recruitment process in which promising agents are left the symbol of the Eye and judged based on their capacity to keep quiet about it, murdering those who fail. I like how the association with assassination is one in which the group needs to police outsiders leaving false calling cards, both for the logical extrapolation from their practice and how they\u2019d have a concern with people getting the wrong impression about who ends up targeted. The stronger focus on how the group is structured lends itself to this being another one where the top ranks were personally promoted by the Empress (probably implying that this would be a bit like the magistrates in terms of the value of cultivating a more direct form of loyalty), and it\u2019s useful for continuing to fit them into interesting times with how the system of promotions is something that they\u2019ve had to start taking internal responsibility for.<br /> <br /> Okay, so before I look at the final section that fully summarizes their position in the Time of Tumult, I want to go over the things that have most drastically changed from their original presentation and why I like them, because there are actually quite a few more than I expected.<br /> <br /> First, the way that their being less formally instituted means that they aren\u2019t given a narrative of facing the issues of long-time domestic enemies who resented their intrusions gutting the structure through eliminating agents and cutting their funding. Looking back, I really think that was something that made them not feel distinct enough from the likes of the legions, the ministries and especially the magistracy; that whole point of \u201cwhat they\u2019ve been doing has earned them a lot of enemies who are now only too happy to use being unrestrained to eliminate them\u201d that is way too close to the core conflict of the magistrates. It also kind of undermined the point that the Eye was supposed to operate in utmost secrecy. I quite like how the relationship to Bal Keraz has changed from \u201che\u2019s basically the only person they can think to report to, and he\u2019s kind of screwing them over\u201d to being somebody courted as an ally because they have mutual interests. I think I get what the intent was in having given them a specific focus on financial auditing (including possibly a reference to the origins of the US secret service), but even within the scope of the Realm\u2019s idea of multiple overlapping authorities it felt a bit redundant. I think that both the All-Seeing Eye and House Iselsi benefits a lot from the downfall and regression of the former being moved <i>way</i> along the timeline, for a number of reasons: playing into the distinctions between the Imperial Service and the Scarlet Dynasty, letting the Eye feel more diverse than just being an organ of a particular Great House, and for that matter letting that House have a more distinct identity than being imperial spies. Between all of that and having no reference at all to Sidereals, the All-Seeing Eye feels like a much stronger autonomous system with higher agency, which I think gives it more character and potential in the long run.<br /> <br /> {happens to range ahead a bit trying to scout out the end of the chapter: sees the \u201cBehind the Eye sidebar}<br /> <br /> Oh goddammit. Fine, leave out, like\u2026 a quarter of that prior paragraph. I stand by the stronger sense of distinction from House Iselsi, as they\u2019ve gone from being the primary composition of the Eye to just the background of several of its handlers (which at least does seem like a sensible place for them to have been posted by the Empress), and the Vendetta certainly adds an interesting plot hook to Iselsi attempting to <i>transform</i> the All-Seeing Eye into their own domain so that its power can be directed towards their enemies (between the idea that their very long exile made them almost synonymous with the Eye and the statement that this was an open secret among the Dynasty, before it felt a lot more like a liability and a contributing factor to why the Houses were trying to bring the Eye down). The presence of Sidereals isn\u2019t absent, but it\u2019s at least downplayed, and the idea that part of what the few Star Chosen intelligencers do is direct cells towards their own agendas at least has an air of sinister mystery to it.<br /> <br /> Moving on! The description of their status in the Time of Tumult gets a good start right out the gate by distinguishing how they\u2019re faring from the severely embattled magistrates. I like the idea that the source of the Eye\u2019s disarray isn\u2019t that it\u2019s being sabotaged procedurally by opponents in the Realm\u2019s system, but a form of uncertainty and inertia in their own mission; that the people in charge are adrift, and the whole group is being sapped of initiative and cohesion. Yes, I think it makes them a lot more distinct and interesting to go from yet another branch of the Imperial Service that is being gutted (which is emphasized as more difficult due to the decentralized structure) into one in which a lack of clear direction from the top, not to mention an operating budget, means that a lot of the cells are breaking away to direct their talents towards their own benefits. I\u2019m\u2026 pretty sure that\u2019s actually true to how a lot of intelligence services break down, whether it be from things like the authorities trying to decommission them or a major change in the guiding ideology, not to mention outright government collapse. So it\u2019s not only very resonant with reality, and some of the essential anxieties of such groups existing in the first place, it makes the All-Seeing Eye a lot scarier within the setting, and a richer source of hooks and complications not only within the Realm\u2019s conflict but throughout the setting; there\u2019s a lot of dramatic potential to mine from the idea from the idea that a circle acting in the Threshold is in opposition to a rogue network of Realm spies commanded by experienced and ruthless Terrestrials trying to carve out a dominion for themselves with covert operations.<br /> <br /> I think enriching the All-Seeing Eye like this is pretty damn great. It really speaks well to the work ethic of the writers, that they took this thing that already held up fairly well and redesigned it significantly to address a few shortcomings, give a stronger sense of the group\u2019s function and identity, and a very compelling place in the setting\u2019s layout and future. I think this might well be one of the strongest cases for the presentation of the Realm as having a lot more fight left in it than it was given in previous Editions, to go from a description that made its intelligence service feel as though it was in dire straits into this powerhouse.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m aware of people in the past who said that they would never buy The Realm because they just viewed it as getting the same information for the third time in a row. I already thought they were being foolish for the projected inclusion of things such as satrapies and the Caul, but this, <i>this</i> is the kind of thing that they ought to be made aware of so that they can understand exactly why they were not giving the developers and writers the credit that they deserved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T15:13:49-07:00","id":1315760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jetstream</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315629#post1315629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Normally I wouldn't, but making a judgment of a game line based upon a single snippet of fiction that doesn't even really qualify as a short story is just... What?</div> </div> </div> </div>That snippet is what's being used to advertise this book, and it (superficially) represents a long standing and deeply resented view (of Deebz being trivial).<br /> <br /> One of my favourite Exalted characters is Lillun: if Onyx Path advertised a new book with a one page story of a young girl getting raped by demon princes, I imagine lots of people would make judgements on the gameline.<br /> <br /> The first bit of fiction people are exposed to in this book is random mortal nobodies making a mockery of the Terrestrial Exalted in their greatest stronghold. That's the first impression the writers chose to go with. Then they threw that first impression out to the world for free, and gated the redeeming part of the story behind a pay-wall. After seventeen years of Terrestrials getting dunked on.<br /> <br /> I was reading The Realm on my phone and literally threw my (not inexpensive) device across the room when I read &quot;Dragon's don't have teeth&quot;. Equitable Remedy is <i>wrong</i>, but I &quot;get it&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T15:28:53-07:00","id":1315762,"title":"","content":"Since this has become a place where we either praise the book or went our rage with the depiction of lack of Dragon Blooded teeth, or say something in between, I am going to chime in and say that I loved it! Like the Dragon Blooded main splat, which I truly loved (and I was never big on them in Second Edition, since the Realm just wasn't as interesting to me then), since it invoked in me for the first time a feeling of dynastic society and that the Great Houses as well as former Shogunate gents in the Patrician classes was interesting indeed. It wasn't really that interesting to me in previous editions. It is now. It just hit home with me. I cannot wait to get my hands on the physical book ! It is simply a collection of wonderful setting material, but if anything, it is a bit short and should give us a 10-20 more pages on existing as well as new named satrapies. It gives only cursory information on Satrapies really, but I understand that word count is an issue. But yes, again, I too loved the book, though I have only had the chance to skim it yet, and have yet to read properly the chapter on the Blessed Isle.I love the feel it gives to the Realm. Seems more appealing to me in this incarnation. I just simply like it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T18:05:04-07:00","id":1315780,"title":"","content":"So, as I\u2019ve said at a couple of points already, I\u2019m interested in seeing the Deliberative, as there seems to be set up for a few alterations made to its place in the Realm (I already know of one redaction in particular).<br /> <br /> The origin is more or less the same, if maybe given a bit more specificity in connection to some instability and criticism in the wake of the failed Scavenger Land invasions (I get a chuckle out of the statement that making a concession for the critics was more practical than boiling them in oil). The function or lack thereof of the Deliberative is retained more than I expected, although I do discern a subtle, if meaningful, change; the description of how the Empress engages in machinations with regards to the Deliberative to give herself a more firm basis for disregarding their suggestions when she doesn\u2019t want them. Its power is still an illusion, but the Empress was less obvious about it. I do think that lends itself to how the Deliberative is rising in the presence of a head of state that won\u2019t deny them; it\u2019s the most overtly political form of power still functioning in the Realm, although still probably quite distinct in being something where the scions of multiple Houses gather together.<br /> <br /> The description of the Deliberative\u2019s buildings sounds attractive, and it\u2019s helpful to consolidate all of the appointments that the body is empowered to make in one place (along with being clear on what is beyond even nominal authority, the power to raise or strike Great Houses standing out particular now that the history of that practice has more emphasis). I think the matter of how long their functional commitment is provides an avenue to dot characters with the title of senator around the Realm as needed, and it\u2019s nice to be given a firmer number of how many Dynasts are committed to that position. I like how the Deliberative\u2019s Guardians indicate that it\u2019s a particularly rowdy variety of parliament; I like their uniforms especially.<br /> <br /> Not much changed to the actual composition of the Greater Chamber, although in the usual spirit we\u2019re at least given a personality to make one of the positions a lot less abstract. I think it also helps to give the body a bit more life by elaborating on how it has internal divisions by House, and particularly with its cliques and factions; I like the way that they\u2019re used as a bridge for the subject of Deliberative concerns over who the successor for the Empress might be. It\u2019s also nice to be reminded of one of the new additions in the race to the top with the Sesus matriarch, particularly how she\u2019s got some cross-factional support. The process for introducing and debating legislation also remains functionally the same as it ever was, if given with a lot more brevity that I personally find more inviting to read. I have always liked how such descriptions make little references to the uncomfortable tedium of lengthy sessions, and the little jeering rituals of legislators. About the same goes for the Lesser Chamber, although I like how it\u2019s Master Tide Lojan is attributed with a particularly lucrative and slightly dubious political legacy (as well as affiliation with a specific Great House), and I\u2019ve always been fond of how the Chamber as a whole prefers throwing its weight around to acquiescing to the Exalted above.<br /> <br /> Yes, the description of how legislation used to be passed or vetoed is the prime spot to indicate that the whole incident of the Empress having once violently purged an especially intransigent Deliberative has been quietly removed. And Fokuf seems to have been afforded the slightest additional degree of dignity, insofar as he\u2019s savvy enough to at least be portrayed as understanding the situation he\u2019s in and just quietly sitting back to enjoy himself while everybody else fights over power. Nothing to say about the senatorial uniform besides the nice touch of personalisation in being able to add sashes to it. The accompanying picture makes a very good show of them, a nice depiction of how such proceedings can be enlivened just by the vibrant appearance of the Dragon Blooded, and it gives a strong indication of the rambunctious debate with how in each other\u2019s face they are. I think it\u2019s clever how it uses a number of little touches to make the place visually reminiscent of popular depictions of the Roman Senate, while still retaining its own distinct look underlying those details.<br /> <br /> Hmm, yes, there didn\u2019t turn out to be much to say on the Deliberative, it\u2019s a direct and to the point aspect of the Realm\u2019s governance. This entry actually does look short, and\u2026 I <i>do</i> always like the stuff to do with money\u2026<br /> <br /> But first, a sidebar on a few hanging points around the Deliberative. I\u2019ve always liked how the whole subject of holding a funeral for the Empress is a distinct source of debate in the Realm, with the supernatural concerns standing alongside the common issue of Dynastic custom, and the way that the possibility of her still living causes a lot of apprehension; the added point that a funeral has long been in the works and would be ruinously expensive is a nice reference to the likes of Qin Shi Huangdi, as well as another basis to be reluctant to actually initiate proceedings (I imagine that the Empress made such arrangements because she wanted to ensure she was memorable in the event that some Lunar or other assassin got lucky). As for Fokuf, I hadn\u2019t considered how his low status would intersect with House Tepet\u2019s particular new standards of honour and drive, but they make sense as something that would leave him an especially diminished figure; that as a basis for him being an especially defeated failure to Exalt adds an effective sympathetic dimension. Heh, still, he wouldn\u2019t be Fokuf if a little bit of extra dignity didn\u2019t still ultimately leave him as a thoroughly undignified person; it\u2019s nice that he\u2019s at least folded into another point about how the peasantry has a capacity to rise in even greater revolt. And I both like how it retains that bit of the Scarlet Throne coming to life and rejecting him when he dared sit on it, and the note of ambiguity on whether or not such a thing might be folklore or metaphor. I kind of prefer the idea that it didn\u2019t, if only for the image of Fokuf having a single moment of feeling a bit pleased with himself and deciding to try the seat out, only to look out over the room from the lofty position and it very quickly dawning on him exactly what he was doing, creating a build-up of nervousness before bolting off of it.<br /> <br /> Now for the money. I like how it starts off with a description of all of the basic structural ways in which the Empress could harness her wealth to exert power over the economy of the Realm. The idea that she could drive commerce through her taxation demands is reminiscent to me of accounts of imperial Chinese encouragement of peasants doing things like raising silkworms to have the necessary silver, and it\u2019s interesting that the process for distributing currency came from paying it directly to her servants and ensuring that others around the Realm would sell them things. I also like how there\u2019s an interplay given to Dynastic incomes with the idea that altering their stipends could influence their prefectural tax policies, not least for how it emphasizes that newly clarified point on how the Houses derive wealth from the Blessed Isle, and the idea that stationing portions of the military could invigorate the economy gives yet another purpose to the activities of the legions in the domestic sphere. Also nice to see the ways in which the Empress\u2019 Purse could use financial engagements to manipulate a variety of cultural factors, which strikes me as another source of inspiration for players of characters in (or looking to build) a similar position.<br /> <br /> It's a reasonable offset to the worst consequences of imperialism to make a few references to the Realm\u2019s military deployments helping to create some environments in which it can thrive more, although those are effectively balanced by references to the Empress employing predatory national finance practices that recall ways in which modern imperialism and its successors have exploited parts of the world that were subjected to colonialism. I also like that idea that one of her easiest methods for curtailing uprisings was to give amnesty on loans. All of this reads like a very careful balancing act, the kind of thing that could have occupied a lot of her time (and both the bureaucracy in general and any personal staff she possessed).<br /> <br /> And it\u2019s all grand to give a summary of how this system, while harmful, was so deeply invested that its dramatic removal is having disastrous cascading effects. I like the idea that Peleps and V\u2019neef competition in the West has reassigned enough naval resources to allow an eruption of piracy the most among these.<br /> <br /> Ah, the salt rate. I\u2019ve always been a fan of it as one of the most prominent instances of tying the setting\u2019s fantastical qualities to mundane resource considerations, especially for invoking old references to the distribution of vital salt. That and the idea that it\u2019s so important that its gods can wield a disproportionate amount of influence is just fun. This is another one that has been solid from its inception, and so requires no adjustment here (and thus no more words than necessary).<br /> <br /> Hmm\u2026 yeah, save the sidebars for the end again. Oh, I don\u2019t know why it took me so many looks to see that last section was saying \u201cFinance in the <u>Time of Tumult</u>\u201d, I kept seeing it as just stating the Realm and thinking it was going to talk about the banking and such. No, yeah, another dedicated subject on how things are being set up for the future is great. So while there was a concession given to how the withdrawal of Realm military power creates dangers for markets, we\u2019re given the other side of the coin in which less capacity to lean on satrapies gives them greater financial autonomy than they\u2019ve ever known, for better or worse (now that some are ready to start conquering neighbours without restraint); it will be interesting to see any follow-up to that in the chapter about satrapies (also nice how there\u2019s a note on satrapies retaining a hint of caution in the event that their overlords return with a vengeance).<br /> <br /> Okay, so the matter of peasants becoming dispossessed <i>is</i> being brought up, but the background is different; it\u2019s not about deliberate Dynast schemes to seize control of peasant lives or lands, it\u2019s an emergent consequence of how uncertainty is causing debts to be called in urgently before they end up quite certainly defaulted. I like how it retains the spirit of the old danger to the lower classes while placing them in a more sophisticated context, as well as the picture it paints of the Realm\u2019s instability and how creditors can even expect to be repaid. With that established, <i>now</i> I feel confident in committing all of the familiar taxation systems and their associated schemes to history. And while we did get the point about how decreasing confident in traditional credit is creating a much higher demand for hard currency, I appreciate a spotlight being shined on the human consequences of this, particularly the manner in which slaves are being worked to the bone to acquire the raw materials. Finally, it\u2019s very clever how it ties all of these concerns into plot hooks for conventional high adventure, with examples of the ways in which the martial prowess or cunning of the Exalted can be harnessed towards numerous financial opportunities, which nicely complements the earlier descriptions of the economic benefits to Realm military patrols. I do particularly like the image of that one about buying businesses in an endangered city and then letting their value increase when the crisis has been resolved, if only for the idea of the way in which it can obligate characters to stick around and invest their energy in a community that might have otherwise been thrown away, as well as the simple mental picture of a given charming representative business increasing in prosperity over time; it\u2019s reminiscent of things that I like in several video games. Still, building up a fleet out of captured prizes is not without its charms.<br /> <br /> The sidebars look simple enough; the credit one is basically an expansion on the core\u2019s point on informal economies by illustrating how that extends to general attitudes towards the greater convenience of financial interests and overall honourable agreements to actually transactions in tangible money; it\u2019s a good reminder that this is the kind of thing that people have always done in one form or another, rather than merely being a modern innovation enabled by computerisation. For the Immaculate perspective on interest, I think it effectively elaborates on the faith\u2019s general outlook for their condemnatory attitudes towards predatory lending to include touches of intrinsic condescension towards the lower classes, a reminder that they\u2019re not necessarily looking out for the best interests of commoners for the right reasons. While the idea that they view accepting a loan with exorbitant rates constituting a hope or expectation of status rising to be able to meet it is a clever way of giving the doctrine of the Perfected Hierarchy some additional dimensions.<br /> <br /> Yes, chapter two has been quite good. Where it looked at things that worked well in the past, it gave them new life with a bit of revised agency or deeper, more distinct character. Where there was something that didn\u2019t work so well or was given insufficient attention, it extracted cruft and replaced it with a greater sense of purpose and activity, and more energetic involvement in the affairs of the Realm. The new take on the All-Seeing Eye does really stand out and has probably the most intriguing future prospects, but I do still have to give the crowning achievement to the write-up of the Ministries for the manner in which they\u2019re invested with greater narrative power, more interesting personalities, and general attention to the question of what they do with an eye towards making it interesting. The chapter does a grand job of taking what might seem to some to be the dull matter of how the empire runs and making it into a viable stage for high adventure.<br /> <br /> If I\u2019m meant to read that credit for Lea at the start as a literal reference to this chapter\u2019s title, then he gets a cookie.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T19:17:08-07:00","id":1315786,"title":"","content":"I really like how this book has gone out of the way to make the Blessed Isle feel large and a little more-... Wild, than before. Before, it felt like it was just a stage for dynastic politics and the upcoming civil war, but now it feels like a place where things actually happen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksi plays Peek-a-boo for keeps. ~ nalak42","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/203-kleptomania","name":"Kleptomania","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar203_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-05-31T23:12:08-07:00","id":1315818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kleptomania</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315786#post1315786\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really like how this book has gone out of the way to make the Blessed Isle feel large and a little more-... Wild, than before. Before, it felt like it was just a stage for dynastic politics and the upcoming civil war, but now it feels like a place where things actually happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>This was a bit with the bandits to me too. I think a big thing is that even if you have thousands of Dragon-Blooded, they can't be everywhere, and a big thing about the game's emphasis so far has beena break down of civil order. Not because Dragon-Blooded are weak, but because the Blessed Isle is enormous and succession crisis cause this.<br /> <br /> I guess to the criticisms above on the fiction, I think the fiction fits in the contex of the Introduction which was right before it. Note the first things read in order isn't the fiction, but the Introduction. Which includes the Edward Said quote, and stuff flat-out stating the Dynasty is mighty, but the looming civil war is fucking it up.<br /> <br /> I guess tome reading into the boasts of a bandit robbing people in a time where bandatry is on the upswing because Exalts aren't omnipotent, is adding more to the implications there than there are, and ignoring context that was established in the few pages right before it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T00:48:11-07:00","id":1315837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315818#post1315818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I guess tome reading into the boasts of a bandit robbing people in a time where bandatry is on the upswing because Exalts aren't omnipotent, is adding more to the implications there than there are, and ignoring context that was established in the few pages right before it.</div> </div> </div> </div>So glad the Lunar domains listed in the free manuscript also all struggle with random mortal bandits making a mockery of them in their own strongholds. Really shows that mortals matter and can challenge Exalts. Remember that story in the corebook about how the Bull's forces can't contend with mortal bandits ramaging over his empire. I found the stories of mortal bandits going graverobbing in Thorns particularly compelling.<br /> <br /> Certainly, it's not like <b>only</b> Terrestrials get advertised as having mortals walk all over them, whilst all other Exalted wield omnipotent power over their domains.<br /> <br /> Oh.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> Again, I don't agree with Equitable Remedy's assessment. Because once you read the full text, it is (reasonably) balanced. But if you are reading the free preview on DriveThru and deciding whether you want to buy this book, then I can see how this could be a turn off.<br /> <br /> I don't have a solution: the writers are going to write what they feel is compelling, DriveThru is going to preview the start of the book, Dragonblooded Dynasts are not the default protagonists. It's just an observation that the main Terrestial domain is publicly showcased by how toothless it is, whilst the Celestials get buffed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T03:25:21-07:00","id":1315847,"title":"","content":"I find it interesting that a lot of people feel that Dragonblood have consistently been depicted as lame and rubbish as stupid. I never really got that. When I started playing, it seemed that the Realm was a giant and powerful empire, and Solars and Lunars scared and on the run. But I wonder if that's because I played pretty much only Dragonblood games for 3-4 years before I played much Solars, and the DBs were much higher xp than the Solars (who were starting characters), and had way more experience and artefacts and so on. Also there were those combo rules that really did in Solars...<br /> I remember once our entire Solar party fought a single optimised Fire Aspect with Five Dragon Style and artefacts, we barely beat him.<br /> <br /> Perhaps if I'd come into the game as someone playing a Solar, and DBs had just been the chumps who occasionally turned up and got beaten, I'd see it differently. Clearly this is something a lot of people feel strongly about.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which includes the Edward Said quote</div> </div> </div> </div>One of my friends pointed out that the Realm starts with a quote from a book that argues that the West a)sees the East as decadent, fanatical, and despotic (and feminine), and b)only sees the East through its own lense.<br /> It then presents a setting where a) a decadent, fanatical and despotic (and matriarchal) Empire of exotic Chinese-looking people who wear Chinese clothes (and occasionally Japanese), do stereotypical exotic Asian stuff like practice kung-fu and spaghetti Buddhism (with some shade about Hinduism thrown in) and who conquer the freedom-loving white people of the North with colonisation and imperialism, in a way that b) actually isn't much based on real Chinese imperialism, but more on European colonialism with a bit of Rome thrown in.<br /> <br /> And of course, this was mostly written by Euro-Americans.<br /> <br /> He felt this was a bad look.<br /> <br /> Not my opinion, but an interesting perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T04:24:54-07:00","id":1315850,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2402_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2402\" data-vbnamecard=\"2402\">The Wizard of Oz</a>, on your friend's opinion, I'd agree I don't think it's too controversial that Sword and Sorcery, with its prime of life in the '30s to '60s, tends to freeze some pre-Said features of views of the our world and history. More than in the case of Tolkienic fantasy which is not much concerned with the world outside NW Europe. (This is all without particularly thinking Said's argument was as strong as many seem to think it was). And that then this is hard to avoid to *some* degree in work which is &quot;of the S&amp;S lineage&quot;, including Exalted). Look at Essos in GoT for another ex.<br /> <br /> That said, I still disagree more with your friend than agree, in specific counterpoints: <br /> <br /> 1) Said's argument was as I understand it not particularly too concerned with the idea that attributed to &quot;the East&quot; was cruelty or fanaticism, which would not be tenable since NW Europe of his time - whose English speaking discourse he's largely criticizing - was at least as much or more critical of Europe of &quot;the Dark Ages&quot; / pre-Enlightenment, and of Africa, and of the native and colonial societies of the Americas (which are all of &quot;the West&quot; or certainly not of &quot;the East&quot; in any case).<br /> <br /> The Realm's matriarchy (which is strongly emphasized in 3e) is surely not connected with being a &quot;feminine&quot; society in the manner that Said argued was the discourse of his time, in the sense of being &quot;sensuous&quot;, &quot;emotional&quot; or &quot;weak&quot;, and if anything subverts and opposes this idea.<br /> <br /> 2) As I understand, Realm people don't particularly look overwhelmingly East Asian, IRC? That would if anything be more like the freedom loving people of the East (who are generally more sophisticated than in the north), and the oppressed people of the Southwest (albeit I think An-Teng is if anything more down for some Said based criticism, and also recapitulates a particular kind of &quot;Orientalism&quot; peculiar to how both the Han Chinese and Western countries view and treat Southeast Asia today). Neither are their clothes particularly, I think, Chinese/Japanese in form as described in text (though the art may not quite bear that out since artists are probably pulling from real world reference stock for their visual imagination).<br /> <br /> 3) Real Chinese imperialism was far too constrained by geography from being anything the Realm could practice. They didn't really go for ocean going ships in the manner of Europeans of the last 500 years. They weren't situated in the middle of a sea surrounded by a huge diversity of rich societies, both threatening and conquerable, like Rome. Rather societies they had little incentive to conquer (the steppe/desert/mountain plateau is hard to hold and too expensive, mainland Southeast Asia is just not worth it). (Though the Blessed Isle internally is not so unlike China. Nor so like it.)<br /> <br /> If we expect that even vaguely Chinese-like people should practice something that culturally resembles Chinese imperialism, regardless of geography, then we're looking at differences in Chinese vs European imperialism as a product of culture, rather than geography and relative circumstances, which seems to me obviously not the right way of looking at it. And it's also a big constraint (even if we did accept that) on the imaginary fictional secondary worlds we can live in to force them to rote conform to history in such details. <br /> <br /> (I'd go further and say it seems almost borderline moronic to imagine some European cultural tendency which led to European style maritime imperialism, rather than business as usual cultural chauvinism and expansionism combining with a different technology and set of geographical imperatives).<br /> <br /> 4) The Realm's hierarchies may throw some shade at Hindu Caste, but I think most would agree Hindu Caste quite frankly deserves that shade, as a longstanding over the last 2000 years and pretty inequitable institution. (And to the degree that cultural understanding and sympathy moderates that shade, that's present in Prasad, where a Hindu-esque Caste and Jati is more literally and directly depicted.)<br /> <br /> 5) To the degree Exalted presents cruel, hierarchical East Asian influenced societies, this is extremely influenced by pop culture Chanbara and Wuxia (Ninja Scroll, Shaw Brothers movies!) where these societies descendants offered critical and bloody views of their own history, in the same manner that Western societies in the cinematic age did of their own past. The Witchfinder General or Solomon Kane or even A Knight's Tale are not too different in spirit with their depiction of the church and ruling class from bloody-handed depictions of cruel samurai and mandarins. <br /> <br /> So this is not just or simply a continuation of some Western view (&quot;the Western gaze&quot; or whatever) from outside, but earnestly influenced by Eastern self depiction and criticism of inequities in their own past societies, when people in those societies had a voice to talk themselves about history, unmediated by Western observation or high social class.<br /> <br /> ---------------------<br /> <br /> (On a side note, to chime into this thread, appreciated reading some of the responses in this thread. Don't have the primary text so don't see much value in chiming in on any points in it though. Trying to avoid having my opinions of tone and presentation too influenced by anyone else's though, until such time as read it, as don't want to end up disliking it because of what some poster or other likes in it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T04:50:18-07:00","id":1315853,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315850#post1315850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) As I understand, Realm people don't particularly look overwhelmingly East Asian, IRC?</div> </div> </div> </div>Way more in this edition than previously. The art in the Realm is very Chinese-presenting (which I actually think is good, because it's way more consistent than before) in terms of architecture, clothing, food, etc, but also the people themselves (to the extent that artists are good at presenting racial differences, which is actually quite hard to do; the Twilight sig is apparently supposed to look like Han Chinese, but his art in the Core book doesn't really look like that to me).<br /> The main ethnicity are called W\u00e0n (rather similar to the Han), though other ethnicities can be found, especially on the coast where they've interbred with people from the Threshold. I can't find a description of them though; I'm sure there is one, but I can't find it. I will say though that most of the people depicted in the art look East Asian, with almost univerally black hair and dark eyes, except for the Dragonblood (who still look East Asian, but with wacky hair colours).<br /> <br /> Certainly, the art looks very much like artists were told to draw people who, for the most part, looked Chinese.<br /> (Except for the satrapy section of course.)<br /> <br /> IIRC, previous editions said people in the Realm were mostly tall, with black curly hair, or it just described them as &quot;Asian-looking&quot; (like Lyta). I don't see any curly black hair in the art in the Realm, it's all straight black.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Anyway, I agree with you (certainly, I think the Realm practicing Imperialism like mainland China rather than nautical Britain or Spain would seem weird), I just thought it was an interesting note to raise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 06-01-2019, 07:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T05:13:11-07:00","id":1315855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315853#post1315853\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Way more in this edition than previously. The art in the Realm is very Chinese-presenting (which I actually think is good, because it's way more consistent than before) in terms of architecture, clothing, food, etc, but also the people themselves (to the extent that artists are good at presenting racial differences, which is actually quite hard to do; the Twilight sig is apparently supposed to look like Han Chinese, but his art in the Core book doesn't really look like that to me).<br /> <br /> The main ethnicity are called W\u00e0n (rather similar to the Han), though other ethnicities can be found, especially on the coast where they've interbred with people from the Threshold. I can't find a description of them though; I'm sure there is one, but I can't find it. I will say though that most of the people depicted in the art look East Asian, with almost univerally black hair and dark eyes, except for the Dragonblood (who still look East Asian, but with wacky hair colours).<br /> <br /> Certainly, the art looks very much like artists were told to draw people who, for the most part, looked Chinese.<br /> (Except for the satrapy section of course.)<br /> <br /> IIRC, previous editions said people in the Realm were mostly tall, with black curly hair, or it just described them as &quot;Asian-looking&quot; (like Lyta). I don't see any curly black hair in the art in the Realm, it's all straight black.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... Maybe to some degree this <i>is</i> edition shift - I don't remember Deled (in his iconic 1e cover), Avaku, Mnemon, etc. looking especially East Asian in previous editions, nor others of the Realm from the art, and the 1e description I work from emphasized characteristics equally compatible with East Asia and the broader Mediterranean region (white skin, tall stature, full lips), and &quot;strong noses are typical&quot;. But this is subjective anyway, esp. in fairly stylized art so I'm not confident on that.<br /> <br /> I think there's a bit of &quot;damned if you, damned if you don't&quot; about how East Asian Realmfolk are in the art - if they're not then authors could get castigated for cultural appropriation and whitewashing (martial arts, &quot;spaghetti Buddhism&quot;), and if so then authors are associating people of this appearance with the fairly brutal central hegemon culture. You could perhaps opt out of this by soft pedaling the Realm's practices, but I don't think that's what anyone wants to see. Or maybe there's a way out of that I can't see, which is entirely possible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315853#post1315853\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, I agree with you (certainly, I think the Realm practicing Imperialism like mainland China rather than nautical Britain or Spain would seem weird), I just thought it was an interesting note to raise.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I think it's a voice and point of view that is worthy of the highlighting in any case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T09:40:10-07:00","id":1315885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315837#post1315837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly, it's not like <b>only</b> Terrestrials get advertised as having mortals walk all over them, whilst all other Exalted wield omnipotent power over their domains.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, for what it's worth, that isn't actually true. The Lunar book is full of domains that have serious problems with banditry. The smaller domains don't because they usually <i>are </i>the bandits. The bigger domains though, they've got issues. The Empire of the Bear actually has so many bandits that their army is stretched thin trying to deal with it, and the leadership knows about this problem and is stumped as how to fix it, with their proposed solutions risking only making it worse.<br /> <br /> They also have a story where a powerful Lunar gets caught out by Shikari and chased down through their own city, after foolishly considering standing and fighting, only to be shown in multiple painful ways why that's not a good idea. I think it sends a good message there, just as the bandit fiction in the Realm does. Everyone needs to be careful because your ability to channel essence better than somebody else doesn't mean you can ignore them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T09:59:24-07:00","id":1315888,"title":"","content":"This next chapter fiction constitutes an interesting snapshot of a lot of what I\u2019ve been praising about references to the legions over the past couple of chapters; how the Blessed Isle is a bit wilder and more dangerous, and using soldiers to address the subject of brigandage. One thing that I like is the way that peasant informants are referred to as being hard to leverage for such a military officer, as their lifestyles don\u2019t lend much to being paid in money or official favours, as it both calls attention to some diversity in lifestyles and the manner in which that can derail even powerful individuals. I also think it\u2019s a good depiction of an unExalted Dynast\u2019s perspective on the Dragon Blooded, between denying oneself too much apprehension about not having inherited the power and having ideas of what they can do of mixed accuracy. And while it\u2019s a compelling story with a sympathetic protagonist, it conveys what is likely to be a significant issue in the Time of Tumult, how the reassignment of the legions among the Great Houses is resulting in more than a few inexperienced officers appointed by political expedience over veteran troops. The accompanying art is sharply detailed (especially nice are the clear distinctions between armour types between ranks), and I particularly like how it appears that their arms aren\u2019t a direct reference to any one real world style, but a mix of influences from different regions and times.<br /> <br /> So, chapter three, The Might of the Realm. The idea of this subject getting a whole chapter to itself is quite exciting; I\u2019m hoping that this will consolidate a lot of official and semi-official information on the composition and operation of Realm military forces, as well as answer a few of my specific questions.<br /> <br /> Okay, introduction with the basic thesis of the Imperial Legions as the great armies of the Realm directed against internal and external enemies that have been broken up into lackeys of the Great Houses\u2026 history of being remnant Shogunate forces and various conscripts and militias converted into full legions with a loyalty focused on the throne rather than devolved to various lower interests. I\u2019m glad to see it stated outright and up front that the majority of officers were outcastes, both for how that clarifies the legions as a force distinct from the Great Houses and frees up a lot more Dynasts fro filling that particular slot, to provide more plausibility to larger numbers appearing elsewhere. Much of the rest is standard information: the fluctuating numbers of legions, the process of cutting off their funding and distributing them among the Houses, the purge of unaligned officers. Something that is new is finally giving the legions a form of centralised command in the form of these nine Crown Marshals, so that\u2019s the first of my lingering questions resolved; the absence of a staff intermediary between the Empress and the generals always struck me as an awkward gap, especially in light of a system where preventing generals from possessing too much power in opposition to the Throne would seem to be a major concern. So I\u2019m glad that a little something has been added to the backstory to fill that space, as well as providing a few additional interesting figures to bring into the civil war.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s nice to get a little sidebar putting together the information on exactly how many legions each House has gotten, to save the need to go back and forth between their descriptions in What Fire Has Wrought. I think it\u2019s amusing that some of the Houses have taken a stopgap measure for low loyalty and morale in the form of pouring a lot of money into their legions, particularly for how that can tie into extracting more wealth from a different sector. I think they\u2019re making much better use of the \u201cdisbanded\u201d/rogue legions here than they have in the past; adding another outcaste general abroad with an agenda on top of a reminder of Saloy Hin is all well and good, but the real standout is the idea that many troops have disappeared into less accessible portions of the Blessed Isle, to give the dangers of banditry quite a few more teeth. The notion of the Realm\u2019s elites being concerned that a rogue legion might have pulled itself together in secret within their own borders is quite fun, although I particularly like the idea of an interchange of troops between legions in the form of prefects remanding captive deserters to the forces of their own House, rather than back towards the House they might have fled from.<br /> <br /> Right, we\u2019re given some nice context for why exactly the legions of the three military Houses were still never quite up to the quality of the Imperial ones, something I find necessary in the face of altering the military history of the Scarlet Dynasty such that all of the Houses no longer had their own troops. It also helps to round out the presentation of the original House legions in What Fire Has Wrought, where isolation from the greater military context made it a bit uncertain just how powerful they were and how much they were employed for active warfare. It also lends itself to understanding the function of the Imperial Legions themselves, with the idea of a greater frequency to doing battle abroad, particularly against the forces of Lunars (nicely complementing Fangs at the Gate). It\u2019s also very useful to describe the existence and format of paramilitary forces in this structure, especially to free the legions of all stripes from satrapy garrison duties. Given the modification to satrapies, I like how it can still add in a bit of Imperial appointments for the sake of checking local power in the form of garrison commanders coming from Houses other than the ones that run the satrapy, and I think it adds a nice bit of tension for those commanders to recruit even more troops from the local populace who can be reliably loyal to themselves. I take the final form of House soldiers to often be something like largely ceremonial personal and household guards, in addition to those small, specialized forces that could be trained in a House\u2019s distinct style while remaining too small to engage in the kinds of operations that proper legions could (excepting how the navies of Peleps and V\u2019neef include marines). I\u2019m guessing that the value of those Sesus and Iselsi commandos was being able to have forces for projecting their clandestine power in addition to Exalted scions, while Mnemon siege engineers sound like they would have been professionals to be hired out to particular legions as needed. I think all of this is good for covering necessary expansions on the Realm\u2019s military, adding a bit of diversity, and placing the new facts of traditional Great House forces into context. I find it to be a richer viewpoint than before when it was all Imperial Legions, plus all of the Houses had legions that would be hardly referenced and sucked, except for three of them.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, what does this sidebar have to say about Tepet\u2026 oooh, there\u2019s some very good information here. Updating the background of the scheming that undermined the Tepet campaign against the icewalkers looks solid, bringing up the point of this being the latest occurrence of the Empress handling a House that was growing too strong, and making good use of the addition of an Imperial military staff in the exact form of the sabotage. Whatever my feelings in the past about the idea that winning against the Tepet legions inflicted severe damage on Kaneko\u2019s forces, I find the description of it here to be compelling; I can buy into the whole thing more when it\u2019s conveyed in terms of a determined Tepet force making a lot of progress even without proper support and that Yurgen was pressed into giving battle. And sure, even a circle of Solars would be taking a lot of risks when locked into close quarters with so many Dragon Blooded. Eh, it helps the mandate of improving the credibility of the Realm to make a significant military victory of prominent Solars less absolute, and if realistic military considerations don\u2019t go out entirely in the face of Exalted powers, they at least get some recalibration. The major thing here is to convert Tepet\u2019s policy and business of training up regional military forces into more of a leg for it to stand on in the coming conflict. I like the way that whole thing diversified militaries that could be found in the Threshold a bit, and it\u2019s interesting to consider how Tepet\u2019s lofty reputation could return dividends for it at a point when their peers would write them off. Plus, I\u2019m always a fan of military campaigns consisting of big, diverse forces from multiple geographic areas marching under a common banner. Heh, get them in line with Tepet quickly enough to make them a lot more attractive to V\u2019neef again, and there would be the makings of quite a tidy full-scale military going there; maybe even options for imperial establishment outside of the Blessed Isle for them to set themselves up with (which would certainly give V\u2019neef an option for survival besides becoming empress). Good job there.<br /> <br /> Right, the long-touted war tourism; it\u2019s not quite what I expected from the name (more of a practice of Dynasts as private individuals involving themselves in Threshold conflicts for fun and profit) and isn\u2019t quite consistent with the real word use of the term, but the important thing is that it\u2019s a basis for making Realm military activity in the Threshold more frequent and dynamic, and a source of more consistent outrages to fuel, say, the motives of Lunar Exalted. I like how it references the capacity for colonial operations to function at a loss as the basis for doing this, both in acknowledging the potential for imperialism to be unprofitable and as a logical drive for such lower-scale militarism. My question about how House militaries going so far as full-blown conquest is addressed on the point of how authority over such territories was assigned by the Empress, which puts me in mind of interesting situations in which a House might end up winning more than it expected or wanted; I\u2019m thinking of how during the Jin-Song wars, the Chinese Song ended up withdrawing a lot of forces in the face of Jin defeat, leaving their enemies in the awkward position of needing to annex large territories filled with a sizable hostile populace, that stretched their capacity to administer or defend (I\u2019m seeing something like Cathak defeating a neighbour more thoroughly than they expected, resulting in a new satrapy going to enrich a rival House).<br /> <br /> I also find that it adds another interesting layer to the differences between House militaries and the true legions; that in the Threshold, the involvement of the greater power of the latter would be perceived as a severe and terrifying escalation. I like the choice to use this matter of ongoing House military activity as the place to introduce the military policies of the Empress. As for those policies themselves, I appreciate the way that it has priorities that enable the setting to have been home to some more tense international relations and elaborate systems of arguing in war\u2019s favour despite the overwhelming power of the Realm; you can have scenarios in which smaller, but decently powerful neighbours were or are locked in a tense balance between asserting themselves and presenting a difficult nut for the Realm to crack, and needing to not come across as too dangerous as to let the Realm claim itself provoked. That also adds a nice background tension for Houses past and present, engaging in schemes intended to make a neighbour commit sufficient force so that they could hand the Empress a justification to annex on a platter, but not overreaching in a manner that left the House needing to call in help or clean up their messes. I would imagine that the Realm has never functionally lost a satrapy, but occasions in which provoking a neighbour into conquest or liberation was severe enough that the legions needed to go in and\u2026 <i>remind</i> a satrapy of whom its overlords were (owing to having no local establishment to do so) in addition to dealing with the other kingdom that forced the necessity, I can see that as having been the very downfall of a House.<br /> <br /> I think that at a time like this it\u2019s useful to provide a reminder that <b>big</b> wars can take place in very <i>small</i> areas. Look at the map of Third Edition Creation; if you\u2019ll look at the area around the Lap, you should see a small dark green splotch of trees just above it. I believe that\u2019s more or less the size of the Levant, which means that in terms of the military capabilities common to the Second Age, you could have a series of military conflicts with all of the intensity of the Crusades in there. See that island just to the left of An-Teng? I think that\u2019s about the size of the Low Countries, which has been home to numerous major conflicts, not least being the Eighty Years War. Err, the Duchy of Burgundy was pretty small and was a focus of significant conflicts from neighbours in France, Germany and Switzerland for centuries. You get my point, even on the areas of the map closer to the Inland Sea you can fit a lot of places where a lot of this intrigue and very lengthy and invested military campaigns could have occurred while still only advancing the Realm\u2019s control over the map by inches at a time. <br /> <br /> Aaaand I think that\u2019s a good place to leave it for the moment, right before it starts getting into the intricacies of legion structure. It\u2019s good looking stuff so far, in how it\u2019s expanded the backstory and added some good directions for the future in play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T10:21:31-07:00","id":1315891,"title":"","content":"Bandits existing does not equal bandits walking all over them.<br /> <br /> Also there were no Dragonblooded in the fiction being beaten down by bandits. Those were mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/746-kunoichi","name":"Kunoichi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar746_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:34:15-07:00","id":1315896,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315885#post1315885\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, for what it's worth, that isn't actually true. The Lunar book is full of domains that have serious problems with banditry. The smaller domains don't because they usually <i>are </i>the bandits. The bigger domains though, they've got issues. The Empire of the Bear actually has so many bandits that their army is stretched thin trying to deal with it, and the leadership knows about this problem and is stumped as how to fix it, with their proposed solutions risking only making it worse.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is one line about The Empire of the Bear struggling with bandits, and it's in with war on foriegn powers (&quot;However, an ongoing rise in insurrection, banditry, and foreign raids \u2014 stimulated by Iscomay\u2019s high taxes and oppressive traditions \u2014 has overstretched the army.&quot;). They also haven't had a Lunar backing them for over 500 years. That's not even remotely the same as previewing the Lunar book with a story on how they struggle with bandits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They also have a story where a powerful Lunar gets caught out by Shikari and chased down through their own city, after foolishly considering standing and fighting, only to be shown in multiple painful ways why that's not a good idea. I think it sends a good message there, just as the bandit fiction in the Realm does. Everyone needs to be careful because your ability to channel essence better than somebody else doesn't mean you can ignore them. </div> </div> </div>If you're talking about Seven Devil Clever, there's an Immaculate DB in that fight. And she wins. And Nexus isn't by any meaningful stretch a Lunar city.<br /> <br /> (And at the end of the fight she immediately starts hunting the Immaculates in the city with her private army of *sigh* mortal bandits. After saying that she didn't need to because of the alliances she'd made with the city's gods.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 06-01-2019, 02:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:41:46-07:00","id":1315898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315837#post1315837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So glad the Lunar domains listed in the free manuscript also all struggle with random mortal bandits making a mockery of them in their own strongholds. Really shows that mortals matter and can challenge Exalts. Remember that story in the corebook about how the Bull's forces can't contend with mortal bandits ramaging over his empire. I found the stories of mortal bandits going graverobbing in Thorns particularly compelling.<br /> <br /> Certainly, it's not like <b>only</b> Terrestrials get advertised as having mortals walk all over them, whilst all other Exalted wield omnipotent power over their domains.<br /> <br /> Oh.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> Again, I don't agree with Equitable Remedy's assessment. Because once you read the full text, it is (reasonably) balanced. But if you are reading the free preview on DriveThru and deciding whether you want to buy this book, then I can see how this could be a turn off.<br /> <br /> I don't have a solution: the writers are going to write what they feel is compelling, DriveThru is going to preview the start of the book, Dragonblooded Dynasts are not the default protagonists. It's just an observation that the main Terrestial domain is publicly showcased by how toothless it is, whilst the Celestials get buffed.</div> </div> </div> </div>With respect, which Exalts are these that are omnipotent?<br /> <br /> Solars actively have a kill order on them barring you spending all of your merits and BP on building a state strong enough to <i>maybe </i>survive you not having to flee for being what you are. The Lunar book is full of examples of the various hazards in establishing their domain, to say nothing of the fact that (as seen in the Empire of the Bear) they can very easily lose control of them and that the mortals are either actively or passively subversive of their plans (RAKSI is seen having trouble reigning in her ministers' impulses and she is probably the most autocratic). Heck, the Empire of the Bear is a case of a Lunar domain growing far more powerful after the Lunar left and then the Lunar trying to walk back in to claim it (and not finding it easy to grab). Sidereals (barring a complete rewrite) have more than a few gods that are powerful enough and motivated enough to crush the life out of you if you irk them. Even Deathknights have to be wary of especially powerful and cranky ghosts, its one thing to ignore a peasant's spirit but it is another thing entirely to cross the local six thousand year old hermit lunar ghost.<br /> <br /> I also really hesitate to say that you can ignore mortals that easily, the Realm puts an extreme effort into building social limits, but the fact of the matter is that eventually any exalt will have a streak of bad luck or simply pick too many fights and get overwhelmed by mere mortals. Exalts that forget that tend to be short-lived.<br /> <br /> For the record, I do agree that Exalt's should feel powerful but it has never been the case that any Splat is omnipotent anywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11320,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","user":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","date":". 06-01-2019, 02:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:47:01-07:00","id":1315899,"title":"","content":"There's a major gulf between &quot;the Dragon-Blooded can't deal with bandits&quot; and &quot;the Dragon-Blooded <i>could </i>deal with bandits but they don't because there's a mother of all succession crises going on&quot;. One arrogant bandit claiming Dragon-Blooded are toothless doesn't make it true, especially given the heavy implication at the end. This is really just twisting the text to arrive at the preconceived notion that the writers have it out for Dragon-Blooded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","name":"Morty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:49:24-07:00","id":1315901,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315899#post1315899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a major gulf between &quot;the Dragon-Blooded can't deal with bandits&quot; and &quot;the Dragon-Blooded <i>could </i>deal with bandits but they don't because there's a mother of all succession crises going on&quot;. One arrogant bandit claiming Dragon-Blooded are toothless doesn't make it true, especially given the heavy implication at the end. This is really just twisting the text to arrive at the preconceived notion that the writers have it out for Dragon-Blooded.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, are bandits not universally known for their masterful grasp of national politics, risk-assessment skills and humility?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:55:12-07:00","id":1315902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315901#post1315901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, are bandits not universally known for their masterful grasp of national politics, risk-assessment skills and humility?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not to mention all those times they're used by writers to deliver objective assessments of the setting and its inhabitants.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","name":"Morty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T11:58:41-07:00","id":1315904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315902#post1315902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not to mention all those times they're used by writers to deliver objective assessments of the setting and its inhabitants.</div> </div> </div> </div>They are indeed our most reliable source on the present state of things, it is likely why they are renowned for their high quality of education and honesty. They are also well-known community people, have you heard of their high-quality insurance plans?<br /> <br /> It is a relief in these troubled times that one can simply purchase protection!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016