do. <br /> <br /> Except the Dragonblooded fiction, which is about the Bull in the North destroying your legions, cowtowing to your mortal niece and bandits openly mocking you whilst killing your subjects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 06-02-2019, 03:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T13:06:09-07:00","id":1315916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315913#post1315913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> If you want examples if omnipotent Exalts, look at the chapter fiction for any of the other splats. Volfer walks unarmed, unarmoured and nude into the middle of a Realm camp and kills everyone. Seven Devils Clever (the example cited above) takes on a Wyld Hunt with two Dragonblooded and their mortal servants and wins before hunting Immactulates for sport.<br /> <br /> The chapter fiction showcases what that splat can do.<br /> <br /> Except the Dragonblooded fiction, which is about the Bull in the North destroying your legions, cowtowing to your mortal niece and bandits openly mocking you whilst killing your subjects.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a unique reading of events.<br /> <br /> The over all Tepet performance has dramatically shifted in interpretation, with them killing two of the Bull's circle and literally disarming a third. That Arada did this almost single-handedly in the midst of a titanic clustercluck induced more by the Realm backstabbing Tepet than the Bull himself is doubly impressive!<br /> <br /> Volfer is indeed known for his magnificent risk-assessment and complete inability to make poor-choices, clearly not the sort of man dumb enough to charge a Warstrider, except that time that he very clearly did that and got punted for his trouble.<br /> <br /> Two Dragon-Blooded (who we know literally nothing about while we know that SDC is certainly a clever and tactical fighter) can only be generously called a proper Wild Hunt and again, young Exalts are not known for being mental giants (in the sense of common sense, blame the Fever) and she is pretty clearly being cocky.<br /> <br /> You are also making quite of being selective about your citations, given that you are ignoring the fiction of Solar being dragged broken and impaled with suppression-pin to the feet of the Empress. You are also ignoring the rest of the chapter fictions in the Realm tripping over themselves to show how enormous and mighty the literal world empire is.<br /> <br /> If you want to expand on this, you can also see the Lunar Book constantly reminding you that DBs are dangerous and that the Realm has and WILL wreck anyone that is too much trouble. All of those cool stories of what the ancient Lunars did typically ended with them being killed by DBs.<br /> <br /> This is all without mentioning Ragara's new Glorious Solar Soul-Slurpee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11320,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","user":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","date":". 06-01-2019, 04:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T13:27:54-07:00","id":1315927,"title":"","content":"Volfer doesn't walk into a camp and kill everyone. He walks into a teahouse, beats up a few mortal soldiers, then a Dragon-Blooded walks in and we don't know what happens next. It's not even selective, it's just plain incorrect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":186,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","user":"Morty","date":". 06-01-2019, 04:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","name":"Morty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T13:52:07-07:00","id":1315935,"title":"","content":"So... moving on.<br /> <br /> I've finished the book now, so thought I'd do a bit of a review (though not as detailed as Isator's).<br /> <br /> Chapter 1:<br /> The history is much better than 2nd ed, not in that it's been radically changed, but in that it's more of... well, a history. There are developments, changes, new things that happen, etc. 2nd ed's history tended to be &quot;some stuff happened, then we'll hardly talk about anything for about 600 years, then there's a ton of stuff.&quot; It feels more dynamic this time.<br /> The stuff on life generally (entertainment, clothes, etc) was not that interesting to read, but if you are doing a game in the Realm, very useful. (I'm not... so it's not.) Though some times the games, etc, seemed a little random.<br /> The House stuff was okay, basically just expanding a bit on what was in What Fire Has Wrought. Nothing radically surprising.<br /> <br /> Chapter 2+3:<br /> Like the entertainment and clothing, I found this chapter on the ministries, etc, to be not that interesting to read, but it probably is quite useful for those playing a Dynastic game. Stuff that was a little undefined in 1st ed, though not too bad, but sorely lacking from 2nd ed.<br /> I generally noted that it all, kind of, hangs together better than in 2nd ed, especially in terms of things like the Realm's class system. Sometimes with 2nd ed you felt like writers hadn't read the game in detail, and had forgotten what another writer wrote; I don't get that impression with 3rd ed. It feels much more consistent.<br /> <br /> Some of the stuff is new, but there's also a fair bit that was in 2nd ed, but all over the place, or not in much detail. For example, most of the stuff on the Legions was in 1st and 2nd ed, but not in a convenient place together. So it's useful for just looking up stuff like &quot;how many DBs are in a Legion&quot; and &quot;how many auxiliaries do they have&quot; and indeed &quot;how many Legions even are there?&quot; More of a game supplement for practical play than an evocative setting document.<br /> <br /> Chapter 4: Again, this wasn't much radically new, but a lot of things from 1st and 2nd ed put together and written well and consistently. I was glad to see the return of the Immaculate Order's quests for lost Immaculate texts, which was in 1st ed.<br /> I am still disappointed by the lack of quotes from the Immaculate Texts. Especially as the Fajad satrapy section includes a sidebar of quotes from the Abhari religion.<br /> If you read, say, the FFG 40K rpgs, they're chock-full of random quotes from Imperial Saints, at the beginning of chapters, in sidebars, as sub-headings, etc. And Vampire had the Book of Nod and Ericyes Fragments; it's not like WW/OP has never done quotes from in-game religious texts. I think it would just really help roleplaying Dragonblood, when you can throw scripture at each other or at Anathema. We always enjoyed quoting Imperial scriptures when playing Dark Heresy. Or, indeed, bible verses when playing Vampire: The Dark Ages.<br /> Oh well. I do remember Vance and Minton saying that, one day, they'd like to do a Cults and Gods book, maybe they can put some in there.<br /> <br /> Chapter 5:<br /> I was pleasantly surprised by this chapter. The Blessed Isle is still the most boring part of Creation, but it's <i>way</i> more interesting than in 2nd ed. There's actually stuff to do beyond DB vs DB intrigue. Not just bandits, but monsters, ancient relics, ancient ghosts, and even a few Raksha. I thought the way they presented the Realm as being on top of the ruins of the First Age's capital, with the dangers from ancient tombs, etc, that brings, was a really good idea.<br /> The cities also now have some actual character. And the art depicts them nicely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T13:57:40-07:00","id":1315937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315916#post1315916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is a unique reading of events. *snip*</div> </div> </div> </div>Needless to say, I disagree. If you want to make a thread discussing this please do, but I don't want to derail this one further.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T14:18:06-07:00","id":1315940,"title":"","content":"<br /> Chapter 6:<br /> When I looked at the bookmarks for this chapter, I wasn't very excited, because it's mostly stuff we've seen before, with only two entirely new places. But I actually found it more interesting than expected.<br /> I'm going to split this into seperate parts for each Satrapy.<br /> <br /> Fajad:<br /> The Needle does not look at all like what I expected, but it's still cool. I thought this satrapy's description was nice in explaining what stuff is going on around the Satrapy, talking about its links to the West, and generally doing a good job of showing how it's evolved and changed over history. I also liked that it talked about how relatively recent the Realm's conquest was, and how it's affected the satrapy (badly).<br /> <br /> Like Chapter 1, I think there's a big difference between 2nd and 3rd ed in how it depicts history; in 2nd ed, it seemed like it was all about the First Age, the Usurpation, then some irrelevant stuff happened, then there was the Contagion, then some more irrelevant stuff happened no-one cares about, then it's the 8th century, and then the Time of Tumult. Whereas this edition makes it seem like Exalted has a modern history, not just an ancient history that makes you wonder how so many illiterate people care so much about events 2000 years ago, and not stuff that happened 200 years ago.<br /> <br /> The Northern Colonies: Interesting to see a)a nautical empire actually making colonies, and b)cultures that trace themselves from the Shogunate (which, after all, you'd assume the Dragonblood see as a Golden Age, yet 2nd ed barely talked about it). Neither Cherak or Pneuma are riveting, but I think you could use them quite well in a Northern game actually, with the cadet houses. I've always had a soft spot for House Ferem, tbh. Anyway, they have given Cherak some new stuff, with it being the remnant of a great Empire, and a number of cadet houses in the area rather than just Ferem, which I think gives more plot ideas.<br /> <br /> Greyfalls: This rather dull 2nd ed satrapy, enlivened only by its interesting ethnic groups, has had a nice revamp, with a really cool piece of art. They've thought more about the hassle of actually getting troops and stuff there. If you want to do &quot;Dynastic party in the Scavenger Lands&quot; it now works quite well as a homebase, I think.<br /> <br /> Prasad: We've seen Prasad in the Core book, so I thought this'd be quite dull, but I quite liked this section. A nice description of an Empire of dragonblood that's very different from the Realm, and how it affects local geopolitics. There's a lot going on in 2-3 pages. It'd definitely encourage me to do a game where Prasad is the evil empire the PCs must fight.<br /> <br /> The Lap: Basically the same as before, just written better, and with more stuff going on nearby. Which is the case with a lot of 2nd ed places in 3rd ed.<br /> <br /> Zhaojun: This one I wasn't that into, because it's basically a collection of Immaculate-believing Chinese-esque city-states ruled by... the Chinese-esque Realm. I found that a bit odd actually. I guess the purpose of this is that if you want to use an area of Chinese-themed lands but you don't want to use the Realm and all that comes with it, you can use Zhaojun.<br /> I did like that it was not just one satrapy, but a collection.<br /> I would like to know something about their relations with the quite different city-states of the Cinder Coast, the ones talked about in the Lunars book.<br /> <br /> Wu-Jian: Other than constantly wondering how they feed everyone, I liked Wu-Jian way more than I expected. For a ragged city of thieves and n'er'do-wells, I'd choose Wu Jian over Nexus any day (but then, I hate Nexus, and banned all PCs from coming from there). It has martial arts societies who are also criminal gangs (because obviously), nearby eelfolk, wicked gods and Raksha, Dragonblood aristocrats hogging the land, etc.<br /> Not sure what the Selachii Raptors are though. Could have done with a quick explanation (are they spirits? Raptorfolk? Pirates? It's very unclear).<br /> <br /> Utahi: I didn't find Utahi amazing, but I thought it was very good as an example of a Western Satrapy, because it discusses the situation in the area, geopolitics, history, etc. So it gives context for western satrapies generally.<br /> <br /> The Caul: I'm not sure I'd want to run a whole game there, but it's a very interesting location, with some very original stuff, and written very mythically. For players who love combat, I think that it works excellently for a party of Dragonblood; you can be from all kinds of backgrounds, and ignore the civil war, and just get on with fighting Lunars. Not my cup of tea these days, but for some players it's great. And for others, a visit to the Caul could be interesting, in a game primarily set in the South-West or the bottom of the Western archipelago.<br /> <br /> <br /> Overall, I felt the satrapies weren't quite as exciting as the Lunar dominions (which I thought were great), but they were still well-written and they all make good locations for games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T14:23:17-07:00","id":1315942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315913#post1315913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> If you want examples if omnipotent Exalts, look at the chapter fiction for any of the other splats. Volfer walks unarmed, unarmoured and nude into the middle of a Realm camp and kills everyone. Seven Devils Clever (the example cited above) takes on a Wyld Hunt with two Dragonblooded and their mortal servants and wins before hunting Immactulates for sport.<br /> <br /> The chapter fiction showcases what that splat can do.<br /> <br /> Except the Dragonblooded fiction, which is about the Bull in the North destroying your legions, cowtowing to your mortal niece and bandits openly mocking you whilst killing your subjects.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Technically, this splat is about the general state of things in The Realm, <i>not</i> the Dragon-Blooded. The Actual Dragon-Blooded chapter fiction in WFHW starts with a newly formed hearth of young DBs defeating a Lunar and a Solar and dragging the Solar back to the Scarlet Empress in chains. The Realm is a dying giant crawling with maggots, perfect for the type of character described in WFHW to go about adventuring in trying to save it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T15:10:46-07:00","id":1315950,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315913#post1315913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q\" target=\"_blank\"> https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=j1l7N-WLa3Q </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> If you want examples if omnipotent Exalts, look at the chapter fiction for any of the other splats. Volfer walks unarmed, unarmoured and nude into the middle of a Realm camp and kills everyone. Seven Devils Clever (the example cited above) takes on a Wyld Hunt with two Dragonblooded and their mortal servants and wins before hunting Immactulates for sport.<br /> <br /> The chapter fiction showcases what that splat can do. <br /> <br /> Except the Dragonblooded fiction, which is about the Bull in the North destroying your legions, cowtowing to your mortal niece and bandits openly mocking you whilst killing your subjects.</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn\u2019t a splat book, it\u2019s a setting book. The splat book is What Fire Hath Wrought. The job for a setting book is to provide setting hooks for player characters to engage with, not examples of npcs doing the job for them.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m just surprised hear someone* complain about 3E\u2019s direction with the Dragon-Blooded when they\u2019re more powerful relative to other Exalted than they have ever been presented in either previous edition.<br /> <br /> * other than people who are really into 19th century eugenics and breeding camps","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 06-01-2019, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T17:15:21-07:00","id":1315970,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315940#post1315940\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For a ragged city of thieves and n'er'do-wells, I'd choose Wu Jian over Nexus any day (but then, I hate Nexus, and banned all PCs from coming from there).</div> </div> </div> </div>A wise and just proscription. Nexus is really just a poor man's Lankhmar, and Exalted can do better (and it does).<br /> <br /> What I like most about Wu Jian is that it's a self-contained slice of kung fu flick first, and the gate to the West second. You've got rowdy fight boys, drunkards, narrow alleys and rickety rope bridges, and technically an important hub for several sea routes down below, but that's mostly a good reason for characters (martial artists) from across Creations to gather here. It's the perfect place to do something like <i>The Man With the Iron Fists</i>.<br /> <br /> I'd say it's a shame that it didn't get a picture, but I'm not particularly fond of the art in this book. Besides, there's already a shot of Wu Jian in the corebook. <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://images-wixmp-ed30a86b8c4ca887773594c2.wixmp.com/f/b503fa00-e372-42b6-8afe-fd9dd7fbd07e/da2s29p-8ca1967b-3557-436f-8c2b-02a558da74f1.jpg/v1/fill/w_1600,h_832,q_75,strp/the_slums_of_wu_jian_by_franklinchan_da2s29p-fullview.jpg?token=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..zVq6XOxrbhguPM0kMxTFZPukk6h-gKh9OKi3OAwYuVE\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T17:31:48-07:00","id":1315972,"title":"","content":"I made Nexus reskinned Braavos (expy-Venice in a Song of Ice and Fire/Game of Thrones) to make it more interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T18:15:56-07:00","id":1315977,"title":"","content":"The basic composition of the legions is the same as it ever was. Apparently that includes the point of how their missile troops are all put together in the status of low value skirmishers; they don\u2019t have the specific line of being held in lower esteem than the baggage train slaves, but still at half pay and expendable. Scott Taylor once commented on this as indicating a failing in Realm doctrine arising from having not really fought serious wars over the centuries, which <i>could</i> clash with their greater military activity\u2026 buuuut Rome apparently got by without a very strong emphasis on archers, so I wonder a bit at how accurate his sentiments were. I suppose it comes down to whether or not anybody is supposed to have the heavy firepower of 14th century Welsh longbowmen.<br /> <br /> A focus on supplementing with auxiliaries seems to be new. I think the composition of the leadership, with how officers have seconds and staffs was often an idea held behind the scenes that hardly ever made its way into actual text, so it\u2019s nice to have it fully laid out here. I\u2019ve always liked how the game is something that brings attention to how, practically speaking, a legion will operate below full strength; in addition to everything it says here about losses due to leave or casualties, one thing I\u2019ve learned from my reading is that an army on the march will often have troops who simply fall behind due to needing to stay and guard something like a supply wagon that\u2019s being repaired, or being behind schedule in rousing the troops from billets.<br /> <br /> I like how the backgrounds given for some of the legion nicknames are used as a way to not only characterize them, but further describe Creation to us, and giving an overall picture of typical legion engagements, and reading about the standards (which have the basic features for pride and practicality) led me to learning what the word chryselephantine means; I wouldn\u2019t have considered before how Immaculate aniconism would work with a standard, but limiting them to variations on dragon figures makes a lot of sense.<br /> <br /> Good to get that breakdown of how the legions go about recruitment, and the general process of advancement within a legion; I can imagine that campaigning abroad is one of the most steady sources of a continuous stream of immigrants to the Blessed Isle. I find it curious that the usual disqualifications can be disregarded if sufficiently bribed, but I suppose it\u2019s a plausible basis for letting legions include the occasional misfits, and a lot of officers could probably do with the money. The matter of how the officers of individual legions have responsibility for recruitment, rather than there being a dedicated government agency for it, makes sense, but I still like it being stated outright; I also find it interesting how this is folded into informal assessments that will be made by high ranking prefectural officials. The idea of Dynasts who try to make a gesture of entering the legions as a recruit not being taken seriously has existed before, but I don\u2019t think that it was ever quite stated in terms of them not actually being bound. And I like this idea that foreign Exalted who might see the legions as a means of getting to the Realm (and its attendant privileges even for those of their kind from humbler origins) being required to first command among the auxiliaries and then spend some time as a regular soldier; I wonder if any established Dragon Blooded who immigrated in that matter hold a grudge towards their officers. My one mild criticism of this part would be how it references the matter of soldiers taking an oath, but doesn\u2019t feel like it fully introduces it.<br /> <br /> For weaponry, I was wondering if they would go with what was apparently an old idea in the game\u2019s development, where line soldiers employed axes instead of swords, but it would appear not. It gives a good picture of how the legionnaires are gotten into fighting shape with daily hard exercise, and I quite like the description of the way that individual training in the use of the weapons is a secondary concern to getting the soldiers to effectively fight in close, flexible formations.<br /> <br /> The picture there has a few things to commend it: I find the generally muted colour scheme colour scheme to both emphasize the grind of such warfare and make the more vibrant colours of the fantastical elements stand out more. The Exalted all stand out well; it\u2019s a good look for all of the five Dragon Blooded to have varying differences from the standard military uniforms to set them apart (the one with the helmet being the closest to the footsoldiers to the point that it took me a couple of looks to recognise them), and the Lunar looks very imposing without her proportions being too exaggerated, and I like her fur clothing, braided hair and expression. The Artifact weapons being rendered in this style has a very interesting effect; they\u2019re very large in the usual manner, but they feels very natural to me. It\u2019s a kind of effect of them not feeling out of place until I\u2019m suddenly struck with how large or stylized some of them are; the blue weapon carried by the hooded Terrestrial works especially in that regard. And I just like the principle of showing a clash between the Realm and a Lunar.<br /> <br /> The sidebar putting us in the shoes of a legionnaire beginning their daily routine is a good way to begin providing a picture of the lifestyle of such a soldier that occupies the majority of their time in lieu of fighting; I especially like the concern given for being camped somewhere that doing laundry will be possible. For the section as a whole, I like having a place to look back at for reminders of these kinds of routines and the hard living, especially the very thorough consideration given to the source and quality of food since those are pivotal to the functioning of an army. I would think it would mostly be useful for giving an image of what the home base would be like for player characters who are in the field with a marching army (as well as their disposition for those spying or attacking them from without). I appreciate being provided with the circumstances in which legionnaires can actually spend their pay, and it\u2019s interesting how the gender mix of their composition is addressed by the availability of maiden tea for the event of fraternization. On that whole subject of payment, it\u2019s good how an illustration is provided for the resources that can be brought to bear by a national entity versus the resources of a private entity with even the means of a Great House. I like the added tension to the matter of how Houses that are falling behind with armies that are used to prompt payment are beginning to look on their new troops with concern, and the way that the restraint of the legions might play off of a decreasing esteem in the Threshold and stronger motivations for opponents. The punishments for infractions sound appropriately brutal.<br /> <br /> All right then, the matter of introducing the concept of an oath is forgiven for the sidebar containing the actual wording. I really like how this incorporates elements of Immaculate theology into the terms for how soldiers conceive of their place and bind themselves to punishments for failing to uphold it; the idea that a sworn legionnaire should regard a treacherous fellow as Anathema is a particularly nice touch. Also like seeing a reference to the ideal of the Upright Soldier being in there.<br /> <br /> Now, retirement benefits strike me as often being one of the stronger historical incentives for joining an army, so let\u2019s see what they get. Let\u2019s see\u2026 it would seem that a lot of the benefits are indirect; there\u2019s no outright pension, so soldiers who manage to live out their full term (which seems to have a reasonable enough survival rate, given the time required) seem to be dependent on effectively saving their salaries, and maybe working to stand out from the ranks so they can get things such as promotions, bonuses from the pillage and commendations to the powerful on the Blessed Isle. Still, the description would seem to imply that even for the lowest ranked soldiers, pay is generous enough that one can save reasonably while still living comfortably in the barracks, and the opportunities for promotion and better pay seem reasonable enough. Oh, on my first read I missed how it referred to the existences of officers who\u2019ll opt to elect to provide additional bonuses on honourable discharge, so that is probably not infrequent. I think it\u2019s interesting how decoration for gallantry can come in a form as ornate as diadems, and the most interesting thing to me would be how it can serve as a means of the commoners earning the attention of elites in the Realm, creates a particular sense that the legions in deployment might be a place where the usual social strata are loosened a little bit. It\u2019s different, but I\u2019d say that it works with how the Realm\u2019s land distribution works, and it has benefits enough for the soldier who is prudent and diligent. <br /> <br /> Now, for the officers. It gets some very compelling information out immediately with the point of how the usual severe discipline along the chain of command is becoming more lax as the structure of legions becomes reorganised to suit House appointments who are less expedient to punish. I\u2019m very glad to have it finally stated outright that officer ranks above scalelord are purchased as a commission. That plays off in a particularly distinct way with the matter of how a majority of Exalted officers used to be Found Eggs, whose commissions are paid for by the Empress; where in real life the point of commissions was to limit military command to the aristocratic class, on the general principle of ensuring only those with independent means would join so that they wouldn\u2019t treat the military as an option to enrich themselves, here it reads to me as more of a form of discouragement for the Dynastic Dragon Blooded, an emphasis on how there are more lucrative opportunities elsewhere and leaving more space open for the outcastes, while tying those with imperial sponsorship more closely in a sense of obligation. The matter of how there are explicitly 40 to 60 Dragon Blooded, and how this includes seconds-in-command, suggests to me that as a practical matter the Exalted officers are concentrated towards the top (generally no lower than winglord), it\u2019s just that the top has been made a bit heavier. I\u2019m okay with that, on the whole; I\u2019m mostly just happy that we get clear statements, and that it helps make the legions sound powerful. The benefits that can be granted at retirement sound like a lot of interesting backstories that can be given to outcaste functionaries. On the other side of things, I like the statement that there are mortal officers who were capable of rising even as high as dragonlord. It\u2019s all effectively punctuated with the way that the legions being appropriated by the Houses are disrupting usual systems of appointment and promotions.<br /> <br /> Heh, the matter of how Exalted contributions strengthen the legions is emphasized with the statement that they typically lead from the front, and I like the reminder that doing so puts them at very high risk whatever their power. If the description of the life of soldiers develops a backdrop, I\u2019d say that the duties ascribed to junior officers conveys how the likely position of player characters interacts with that backdrop, while the socialising of the more senior officers is not only a thing that such players can rise to, but an idea for the kinds and motives of characters that can appear in such social scenes from another character\u2019s perspective. It\u2019s cute that such a position can bring on an especially strong series of marriage proposals, and it adds to the sense of the legions as a means of advancement for officers from peasant backgrounds to also receive such (if probably hardly ever so highly placed). The kind of leave that can be taken by Dynastic officers further helps ensure that the legions don\u2019t absorb so many of them, and I like the second-officer system ensuring that such a thing doesn\u2019t leave legions distinctly understaffed.<br /> <br /> Right, let\u2019s look at some of these sidebars. The format of encampment sounds fairly standard, though I like the hint given for how the evolution of such fortifications served as the seed for establishing a lot of settlements around the Realm. I like the way that the sidebar on the morning routine of an Exalted officer establishes things such as their more ornate, personalised uniform, the practical elements of command, and not necessarily eating much better than the ranks (although they\u2019ll at least be cleaner and better groomed). And the advice on how to run battles with legions is about how I would do call it; that you don\u2019t run the player characters (and their armies) as clashing with one or more legions all at once, but break down the engagements to a closer level (with more manageable numbers of Exalted) to focus on combat at a more surgical level, which can be strung together to influence the shape of a larger battle or campaign. I think such a thing allows greater warfare to retain significance to the game while not creating circumstances in which it elevates beyond the scope of the usual adventuring into an outright wargame, and I\u2019d say that even a single dragon of medium or heavy troops commanded by Dragon Blooded and accompanied by a battle group of missile troops/cavalry and an auxiliary can be quite formidable even to Celestial Exalted.<br /> <br /> That seems to about cover the core fighting forces of Realm legions, so I\u2019ll cut off there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T18:28:56-07:00","id":1315979,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s interesting trying to conceptually balance how this edition plays up the territorial strongholds of the Houses which obviously evokes feudal Europe, and is probably happening in the line this edition in-part because of the pop-culture influence of Game of Thrones and A Song of Ice and Fire...<br /> <br /> ... with the centralized authority of a bureaucratic state that previous editions put more focus on.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a reason Imperial China and Bourboun France worked to centralize the nobility together - it was in order to empower the apparatus of the state directly under the central authority of the Emperor/King.<br /> <br /> I guess the way to read this is that the Dynasty has moved in that direction, but it\u2019s snapping back to a \u201cWesteros\u201d feudal model in the Empress\u2019 absence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T18:51:25-07:00","id":1315983,"title":"","content":"\u200bI'm intrigued by Isator's reading of the book, but I'm still working my way through <i>Scion: Hero</i>, and I've made a promise to myself not to read an Exalted book and a Scion book simultaneously.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315913#post1315913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Seven Devils Clever (the example cited above)</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, the example cited was Seven Devil Clever.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 06-01-2019, 09:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-01T20:04:35-07:00","id":1315996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315979#post1315979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It\u2019s interesting trying to conceptually balance how this edition plays up the territorial strongholds of the Houses which obviously evokes feudal Europe, and is probably happening in the line this edition in-part because of the pop-culture influence of Game of Thrones and A Song of Ice and Fire...<br /> <br /> ... with the centralized authority of a bureaucratic state that previous editions put more focus on.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a reason Imperial China and Bourboun France worked to centralize the nobility together - it was in order to empower the apparatus of the state directly under the central authority of the Emperor/King.<br /> <br /> I guess the way to read this is that the Dynasty has moved in that direction, but it\u2019s snapping back to a \u201cWesteros\u201d feudal model in the Empress\u2019 absence.</div> </div> </div> </div>One thing I like about this take is that it really shows you how unique and clever an individual the Empress was (in addition to being understandably egotistic). Everything in the realm is built from the ground up to have no legitimacy without her, the apparatus of state has no basis or even central authority to function with without her for a prolonged period.<br /> <br /> But the interesting thing is that we very clearly see that she was right to build the state that way, the Legions did have points of authority built in that might have survived her (the Marshals), and they were the very first thing to get (sometimes literally) decapitated by the Houses.<br /> <br /> The Realm book has actually brought something into my mind though, how could an Exalted society actually function properly without her methods? There are few if any enforcement mechanisms (social or physical) that could actually work on them and Essence Fever makes younger Exalts something that, at most charitable, is perpetually unstable.<br /> <br /> You can't even make the argument that pure freedom is a good thing in Exalts, since unlike we mortals, they can fabricate doomsday weapons and Workings that turn continents to metaphors without the requisite infrastructure and intermediaries that prevents someone with access to control of atomic weapons from drawing a happy face on the moon with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11320-gigaton-falcon-emu","name":"Gigaton-Falcon-Emu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"786"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T00:39:01-07:00","id":1316028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315996#post1315996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Realm book has actually brought something into my mind though, how could an Exalted society actually function properly without her methods? There are few if any enforcement mechanisms (social or physical) that could actually work on them and Essence Fever makes younger Exalts something that, at most charitable, is perpetually unstable.<br /> <b><br /> You can't even make the argument that pure freedom is a good thing in Exalts</b>, since unlike we mortals, they can fabricate doomsday weapons and Workings that turn continents to metaphors without the requisite infrastructure and intermediaries that prevents someone with access to control of atomic weapons from drawing a happy face on the moon with them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hold my beer.<br /> <br /> What you're saying is that it's ok for the Empress to oppress, murder and enslave innocent people because if she didn't maybe someone else would be worse.<br /> <br /> That's like trying to justify the Nazi regime because German Communist Terrorists existed and might have been worse. (Or the Patriot Act.)<br /> <br /> IRL humans can build atomic weapons. But a civilised people doesn't enslave innocent people just-in-case a third party builds a dirty bomb. That's nutso.<br /> <br /> Education and mutual respect should be the foundation of mortal society (in game and in real life). People should be taught that working together and helping others helps yourself, and that other people have inherent rights and value. Just as you or I don't go around victimising innocent people, we need to all understand that attacking people who haven't done anything wrong is unacceptable. If someone is Exalted, they don't become a ravening monster -- they're still a person, with all their memories and moral compass. But they have a will to act. Let them defend the innocent and murderously hunt down the guilty. If they go rogue, let those Exalts dedicated to justice (Wyld) Hunt the criminals down. You don't need a crazy redhead with her finger on The Button to convince people that murder is wrong.<br /> <br /> And, honestly, it's not like Big Red has done a good job. The Queen of Fangs has been happily munching babies for the last seven hundred years. The Sids muck with reality on a quantum level every Tuesday. There are clans of Deeb assassins. Random nobodies find First Age treasures and wreck unknown havoc. And her disappearance is what sets the stage for the PCs to make the world a better place -- not some central authority, but champions working together.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315983#post1315983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, the example cited was Seven Devil Clever.</div> </div> </div> </div>A typo. Happily amended. Thank you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315942#post1315942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Technically, this splat is about the general state of things in The Realm, <i>not</i> the Dragon-Blooded. The Actual Dragon-Blooded chapter fiction in WFHW starts with a newly formed hearth of young DBs defeating a Lunar and a Solar and dragging the Solar back to the Scarlet Empress in chains.</div> </div> </div> </div>It actually starts with those Anathema having brutally destroyed a Wyld Hunt including Immaculates and causing so many deaths that it hurt the hand of one of the survivors to write the all the condolance letters. And the Solar later escapes. (And Lunars have feign death charms, but let's not go there.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315950#post1315950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This isn\u2019t a splat book, it\u2019s a setting book. The splat book is What Fire Hath Wrought.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good job 2/3 of the examples I cited were from WFHW.<br /> <br /> We don't have Direction books yet to compare the fiction with. We'll see if the tone is the same. *shrug*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 06-02-2019, 03:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T02:28:01-07:00","id":1316041,"title":"","content":"On the Legion section you were discussing Isator, on skirmishers, I noticed that the medium and heavy infantry are now generally experienced skirmishers. Ie you become a skirmisher, then get promoted to the infantry. I think that's new for 3rd ed, and makes sense to me in terms of why the skirmishers are badly paid.<br /> <br /> In terms of Rome, while it's not my area, I believe the Romans used to use more slingers and javeliners, which is why they didn't use a ton of archers.<br /> Also, of course, their own tactics were very effective against archery, and there was a fair few civil wars where they fought themselves.<br /> <br /> I thought the second-in-commands for each position (so you've got your Dragonlord and deputy Dragonlord, Winglords and deputy Winglords, etc) was new for 3rd ed, but my friend claimed it was somewhere in 1st ed, it's just that this stuff was all over the place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ... with the centralized authority of a bureaucratic state that previous editions put more focus on.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a reason Imperial China and Bourboun France worked to centralize the nobility together - it was in order to empower the apparatus of the state directly under the central authority of the Emperor/King.<br /> <br /> I guess the way to read this is that the Dynasty has moved in that direction, but it\u2019s snapping back to a \u201cWesteros\u201d feudal model in the Empress\u2019 absence. </div> </div> </div>True, but even China had to frequently set up Dukes to run outlying regions, because it was so difficult to run everything from the centre.<br /> <br /> Of course, Dragonblood tend towards an aristocratic model where powerful families rule far-flung regions, because of their blood potency.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Realm book has actually brought something into my mind though, how could an Exalted society actually function properly without her methods? There are few if any enforcement mechanisms (social or physical) that could actually work on them and Essence Fever makes younger Exalts something that, at most charitable, is perpetually unstable.<br /> <br /> You can't even make the argument that pure freedom is a good thing in Exalts, since unlike we mortals, they can fabricate doomsday weapons and Workings that turn continents to metaphors without the requisite infrastructure and intermediaries that prevents someone with access to control of atomic weapons from drawing a happy face on the moon with them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's really the same as for mortals.<br /> No society on earth gives humans true freedom; everywhere has laws, police, soldiers, etc. In some, those are instruments of tyrrany, in failed states they're instruments of warlords, and in the most advanced states they do important things like reducing murder, rape, theft, etc.<br /> <br /> Exalts are like super-societies; the solution to supervillains is superheroes. Exalted criminals require Exalted police.<br /> <br /> The Empress didn't really build her society entirely to benefit the people. That's not why she created competing power bloc dynasties and turned them against each other. I don't mean the Empress didn't think about the ordinary people at all; that's why there's the Magistrates, Dragon-Handlers, and indeed 90% of the teachings of the Immaculate Order (the other 10% are to benefit the Dragonblood, of course). She created competing power blocs so they'd fight each other and not try and remove her from her position on the top of the pile.<br /> <br /> In doing so, she's created an insidious culture that encourages Dragonblood to see themselves as more moral and spiritually pure than mortals (rather than just more skilled), to be ruthlessly competitive, and solve their problems via assassination, war, etc. And to see themselves as part of one small group in society rather than the whole; most Dynasts are far more loyal to House X than to the Scarlet Dynasty or Dragonblood Host.<br /> <br /> All of this has primed them for war as soon as she's gone.<br /> <br /> (Which all makes for a good setting of course!)<br /> <br /> Though I can, of course, very much imagine that many people in the Realm see her methods as the only effective ones.<br /> <br /> <hr />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T02:30:59-07:00","id":1316042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316028#post1316028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, honestly, it's not like Big Red has done a good job. The Queen of Fangs has been happily munching babies for the last seven hundred years. The Sids muck with reality on a quantum level every Tuesday. There are clans of Deeb assassins. Random nobodies find First Age treasures and wreck unknown havoc. And her disappearance is what sets the stage for the PCs to make the world a better place -- not some central authority, but champions working together.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I agree with your overall point, the Sidereals mucking with reality on a spiritual level is generally a good thing, and she knows it.<br /> In such a complex system, bugs are inevitable, so you've got to keep patching.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T03:55:50-07:00","id":1316050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316041#post1316041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, Dragonblood tend towards an aristocratic model where powerful families rule far-flung regions, because of their blood potency.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I think the Realm is constrained to this quite unusual model of a long term stability of relatively cohesive blocs of competing aristocrats combined with a strong centre, because of the degree to which the central power is constrained to replacing aristocrats with loyal appointees (the Empress is not like the Unconquered Sun in this regard, where he can turn out personally loyal appointees to facilitate his authority rather than bother with independent family power bases). So this unusual model of trying to balance power between blocs of aristocratic families emerges, rather than just trying to smoosh them.<br /> <br /> (Which is great because it leans into much of what the Grabowski found both interesting and worth of criticism about Chinese government over time, as well as the sort of Dune/Rokugan-esque model of powerful houses and clans, which is appealingly gameable and fairly rich for intrigue).<br /> <br /> The Empress is also building this in the consciousness of how the Shogunate worked, with a *lot* more dependence on a central authority.<br /> <br /> On history, I was going to note earlier, but thought off topic, a lot of the Realm's ideology is constrained or enabled by the degree to which they're putting together an idealised version of previous Realm society (Shogunate and more hazily, Old Realm) which really were world empires. That's something where Realm imperial does owe more to China than nautical imperial powers like Rome, Britain, Spain that expanded over the seas; the ideology has a bit more of &quot;The empire long divided, must unite&quot;, and restoration of the legitimate power, and protecting the fringe against the outer barbarians (Fair Folk, unruly spirits and so on). More so than Rome's &quot;We must conquer these expansive Mediterranean and Central European states/tribe like Carthage and the Gauls before the conquer us&quot;, or Spain/Britain's conquering of Americas/Asia in order to capture more tax revenues in order to remain in theory more secured as a nation against French demographic power / &quot;Barbary&quot; / the &quot;Turk&quot; etc, without much thought of deriving legitimacy from an earlier predecessor that spanned those regions. (Realm collaborators probably argue that they're helping to reassemble the old empire, rather than that they're subjugating their nation).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gigaton-Falcon-Emu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315996#post1315996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can't even make the argument that pure freedom is a good thing in Exalts, since unlike we mortals, they can fabricate doomsday weapons and Workings that turn continents to metaphors without the requisite infrastructure and intermediaries that prevents someone with access to control of atomic weapons from drawing a happy face on the moon with them.</div> </div> </div> </div>A go at an argument to the contrary: Some possible upsides to Exalted individual freedom though; <br /> <br /> - More individualized Exalted actors may be less likely to lead to high stakes wars than situations where all Exalted are under large blocs. That might reduce the frequency of seriously apocalyptic events, and at least distribute risk.<br /> - More individualized Exalted actors won't struggle against authorities for their freedom<br /> - More individualized Exalted actors are more free to follow passions and interests, and that could have significant upsides. Free Exalted might get you a terrible Working you might not expect, but also a highly positive one. We don't have an equal but opposed (creative rather than destructive) nuclear device example, but the Exalted do.<br /> <br /> The big problems with nuclear proliferation in our world seems like the long term damage to the biosphere and the chances that a lone actor rogue state can tip the world into a full blown apocalypse, due to organisation of huge quantities of those weapons into armed camps and the possibility that they can precipitate a nuclear winter.<br /> <br /> If we had a world where small states could nuke each other, but with no consequences beyond that (no huge organisation of the world into two armed camps, no nuclear winter or biosphere collapse), then nuclear proliferation would be a very different question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T11:46:19-07:00","id":1316129,"title":"","content":"Its interesting that the Shogunate was ostensibly highly centralized on paper, but in practice it was extremely feudal and decentralized, while the Realm under the Scarlet Empress was on paper administered by the Deliberative and the Great Houses but in truth the Empress held all the power. Now that Her Redness is gone, the fiction has become fact and the Realm is rapidly going the way of the Shogunate","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-02T22:02:43-07:00","id":1316195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315896#post1315896\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is one line about The Empire of the Bear struggling with bandits, and it's in with war on foriegn powers (&quot;However, an ongoing rise in insurrection, banditry, and foreign raids \u2014 stimulated by Iscomay\u2019s high taxes and oppressive traditions \u2014 has overstretched the army.&quot;). They also haven't had a Lunar backing them for over 500 years. That's not even remotely the same as previewing the Lunar book with a story on how they struggle with bandits.</div> </div> </div> </div>No it's not quite the same thing, but it also has a section about how a Lunar made herself the Jesus of her own religion, and has now returned home to find her second coming met with calls of &quot;Oh, no, no, you don't understand the Bible, allow us to instruct you.&quot; If Lunars were the invincible omnipotent god beings she'd come back, bust open a cold keg of beer and instantly take the helm of her society again with no problems, instead of being vexed by her moon-touched children following her own religion.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315896#post1315896\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you're talking about Seven Devil Clever, there's an Immaculate DB in that fight. And she wins. And Nexus isn't by any meaningful stretch a Lunar city.<br /> <br /> (And at the end of the fight she immediately starts hunting the Immaculates in the city with her private army of *sigh* mortal bandits. After saying that she didn't need to because of the alliances she'd made with the city's gods.)</div> </div> </div> </div>She absolutely does not win, and at no stage in that encounter did she have the upper hand even once. She arrogantly waggles a fin at her hunter for kicks because she thinks she's invisible, gets caught out and proven wrong, decides staying and fighting is the right choice, until the monk proves her wrong <i>again</i> by filling her lungs with water, instantly turning her rage into fear for her life.<br /> <br /> Then she bolted, taking a nasty wound because Dragonblooded archers react fast enough and with the accuracy to snap an arrow into a shapeshifting leaping fox, and fled to her little fox hole in the city where she didn't dare make a noise for an entire hour, because one peep, one whispered word risked bringing the might of the Dragonblooded into her home, where she and her allies would be killed. The only thing that even barely saves it from being a total farce of a Lunar fiction is that sentiment of &quot;Next time, it'll be different.&quot; Even in second edition the Dragonblooded never got shat on that bad in their own chapter comics.<br /> <br /> &quot;The gods blessings would strengthen her gang, assuming the ruffians survived.&quot; is a line that, to me, implies that many of those bandits are going to be incinerated in the anima banners of the terrestrial exalted.<br /> <br /> <br /> Meanwhile in What Fire Has Wrought we have connected chapter fiction where a young group of Dragonblooded go 5v2 against celestial exalted, with enough power to spare that they actually take one hostage. That alone is crazy impressive, especially since to capture one they had to say to themselves &quot;No, no I COULD go all out and make this a lethal attack, but I don't need to, we've won this now, I'll dial it back a touch so that I can have a nice gift for the Empress.&quot; and then proceed to take one of those supposedly invincible exalts and reduce them to a drooling pathetic mess on the floor before their queen.<br /> <br /> From then on we have an uncouth Sesus jerk getting schooled by somebody who's basically a the Dynastic equivalent of a Dungeons and Dragons adventurer, who's job description includes dangerous tomb exploration and monster slaying. So basically a badass. Then one about how crushing the responsibilities of the Dynasty can be, and how to be a punk and fight the system.<br /> <br /> Then onto a story including a line about how the Realm pulling forces back has caused an explosion of banditry. Then a mercenary goes on to to triple her usual rate because you're a damn fool to go messing around in Realm territory, even without their legion there.<br /> <br /> On from there to a story about how Tepet Arada is tired of telling people about how he killed Jochim, a Solar who he bested in personal combat before taking his head back to the Isle. That's old news, he's going to tell you about the OTHER powerful Solar that he killed in personal combat. After he kills one of Arada's heathmates but we need to make Solars feel threatening somehow otherwise they're not credible antagonists for Dynasts. We just have the 2e problem in reverse, where people used to go &quot;Yeah, they're an enemy, but they're just DBs.&quot; now we'd have &quot;Yeah, they're solars, but we outnumber them two to one.&quot; and that's kind of shitty even if Solars weren't also a PC splat with a clear and purposeful power disparity.<br /> <br /> Then onto a story pretty much exemplifying the importance of patricians in this edition, mortals matter sometimes, but even they want as many Dragonblooded on their side as they can.<br /> <br /> THEN onto one where a Solar assassin of the Night caste fails hilariously at killing Mnemon. Mnemon is just sitting there casually contemplating the mess that is Jiara as one might contemplate a messy house and how best to clean it. Offhanded remarks about her arrival to the city causing rebels and Solars to flee before her and the towers to crumble beneath her legions. Quickly she fends off an assassination attempt by a lone Solar, fool that she was to think Mnemon would be such easy prey, and then the House Matriarch immediately turns around and starts hunting Novia down.<br /> <br /> After that a story about a DB monk ruining some god's day, taking not a single hit himself but pummeling the god into submission, and eventually obedience.<br /> <br /> Then a story about how an outcaste took hold of a kingdom by her own hand, and how the crown lays upon a troubled brow Conan style. Also about how shitty it is now that the Solars are back, which, yeah, duh. They've returned to destroy the world, and only Wyld Hunts, like they used to be, can stop them.<br /> <br /> Next story continues that, adding on a bit of survivor's guilt, and the reuniting of the old hearth, which flows right into the final one about her and her DB buddies going out to kick ass and take names. Specifically hunting down a Solar.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I understand how tempting it can be to fall back into that old way of thinking, like in 2e where by the end new Exalted characters measured their badassitude by making casual comments about killing four dragonblooded, offscreen because that'd be such a boring and one-sided battle. That just isn't where we're at anymore though. In the Realm book the only DBs we see in chapter fiction are an abbot who deftly manipulates a trio of peasant leaders, a trio of Dragonblooded who not only toy around with nation sized areas for fun and profit, but are doing so again in the south, and a no-nonesense magistrate who's going to take them down. Those are all pretty badass characters.<br /> <br /> I would also urge anyone reading this not to take it too far in extreme the other way. Dragonblooded are powerful, but they're not invincible. If you're playing a game with Solar PCs you aren't 100% going to end up foaming spittle all over the Empress's nice new marble floors. Just understand that if you're not careful then that's a strong possibility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T04:47:18-07:00","id":1316224,"title":"","content":"I like the Realm Legions sidebar because it's very close to something I've been advising people to do since 2nd edition. Good to finally see that in a book.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T05:07:17-07:00","id":1316225,"title":"","content":"Really drives home how dire House Tepet's stakes are. Especially since the two houses responsible for its downfall have the most legions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T06:12:27-07:00","id":1316235,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316225#post1316225\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really drives home how dire House Tepet's stakes are. Especially since the two houses responsible for its downfall have the most legions.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Scarlet Empress is lacking legions for the moment <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I think I read a text box (don't remember the page) in the Realm as to how the Empress personally ordered the Tepet legions hindered (although I got to admit that I thought it strange that she would do so in a crusade against Anathema, as I had assumed war against Anathemas was outside politics for her like that).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T07:50:35-07:00","id":1316248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magnus K</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316235#post1316235\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Scarlet Empress is lacking legions for the moment <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I think I read a text box (don't remember the page) in the Realm as to how the Empress personally ordered the Tepet legions hindered (although I got to admit that I thought it strange that she would do so in a crusade against Anathema, as I had assumed war against Anathemas was outside politics for her like that).</div> </div> </div> </div>My guess looking at it there are a couple reasons that it kind of makes sense:<br /> - Tepet actually got along well with Cathak until this happened, to my understanding. This is not a really good state of affairs to have two houses with like, eight or nine personal legions between them, being so damned chummy. This was a good opportunity to subborn that.<br /> <br /> - She thought that even if it hurt them, it was at the time of implementation two Anthema, whcih would probably still be killed in the process as that's how this has gone down before. Nont six, which is what Tepet actually hit when they went in. Hypothetically if Her Redness were still around, she'd of had other priorities on that area.<br /> <br /> A lot of it is kind of based around 1) Not having a succession crisis afterwards and 2) Not having Anthema running rampent. Which well, she wasn't around to compensate for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":353,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","user":"Blaque","date":". 06-04-2019, 04:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T08:18:55-07:00","id":1316251,"title":"","content":"You make good points there, Blaque. Of course, we are both speculating on motives, but your points seem logical enough.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T08:20:29-07:00","id":1316252,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magnus K</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316251#post1316251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You make good points there, Blaque.</div> </div> </div> </div>He usually does.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T08:54:31-07:00","id":1316258,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316252#post1316252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">He usually does.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, well I am new to the forum. I have read it now and then a few times over the years, and also made an account last year, but I only now started to post so, I don't know anyone really. But so far I have read many interesting posts from interesting people, no doubt about that !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T17:27:04-07:00","id":1316333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316050#post1316050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> A go at an argument to the contrary: Some possible upsides to Exalted individual freedom though; <br /> <br /> - More individualized Exalted actors may be less likely to lead to high stakes wars than situations where all Exalted are under large blocs. That might reduce the frequency of seriously apocalyptic events, and at least distribute risk.<br /> - More individualized Exalted actors won't struggle against authorities for their freedom<br /> - More individualized Exalted actors are more free to follow passions and interests, and that could have significant upsides. Free Exalted might get you a terrible Working you might not expect, but also a highly positive one. We don't have an equal but opposed (creative rather than destructive) nuclear device example, but the Exalted do.<br /> <br /> The big problems with nuclear proliferation in our world seems like the long term damage to the biosphere and the chances that a lone actor rogue state can tip the world into a full blown apocalypse, due to organisation of huge quantities of those weapons into armed camps and the possibility that they can precipitate a nuclear winter.<br /> <br /> If we had a world where small states could nuke each other, but with no consequences beyond that (no huge organisation of the world into two armed camps, no nuclear winter or biosphere collapse), then nuclear proliferation would be a very different question.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, so for the first few points, I\u2019m not sure if the intention is that wars would be better if they were smaller? Like, individually, the loss of life is lower but I don\u2019t think you could reasonably say that Jibei raiding the smaller powers to its west is ok just because they\u2019re not as big as the Realm. That\u2019s kind of the whole deal of the hundred kingdoms is dozens of small and constant wars, and it\u2019s pretty solidly Not a Good thing.<br /> <br /> Distributing Risk isn\u2019t really a safety measure when the risk is that a sorcerer could summon armies of demons, or an Eclipse could utterly collapse the regional structure of supply lines. We also know that Exalts generally Don\u2019t remain as individual actors even if they can, because their goals and ideas are rarely constrained to one part of the world, or won\u2019t remain that way for long. Yurgen Kaneko didn\u2019t want to rule his tribe fairly and reasonably and leave everyone else alone, and even if he did, the Realm Itself is a response to a need for a unified force of Exalts. It\u2019s not unreasonable for Exalts to band together, often it\u2019s necessary. It\u2019s also not unreasonable for an Exalt to want to curb or limit another exalt\u2019s behaviour, often it\u2019s Why they Exalted.<br /> <br /> And just... on the last point. The biggest problems with nuclear destruction in our world *Include* fallout and the potential for instigating events. But the base premise of mass murder is still, y\u2019know, right up there on the list of concerns. Smaller powers can do that without the Radiation and retaliatory missile strikes by way of firebombing, gas attacks, and starvation. And they do.<br /> Furthermore, the division of the globe between two enormous factions started before nuclear weapons and continued for a long time before the principle of Mutually Assured Destruction was solidly established.<br /> <br /> I really think you\u2019ve misconstrued the nature of nuclear weapons and their place in global conflict.<br /> Fallout and retaliation are good reasons to oppose nuclear weapons, but the purpose and function of the device itself is the wholesale destruction of a civilian population. Insofar as a nuke might have a military target, it is far more effective as a nullifier on the Home Front. That\u2019s what it does, regardless of radiation. It\u2019s what it did the only time it was deployed in war, and it\u2019s been the threatened use from that point onwards. The idea that radiation-free nuclear devastation absent from NATO or the USSR would\u2019ve been, what, manageable? Small Scale? It doesn\u2019t bear out. Any attack of that scale on a civilian population is devestating and irreversible. Repeated and opposed use would pretty quickly leave nothing salvageable of the parties involved.<br /> It makes proliferation a different question, that I will grant. But just because it doesn\u2019t blanket the earth in nuclear winter, it doesn\u2019t make it any less apocalyptic.<br /> <br /> Sorry I know this is way OT for the thread, but I kinda had to respond.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1247,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1247-maseiken","user":"Maseiken","date":". 06-03-2019, 08:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1247-maseiken","name":"Maseiken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1247_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T18:33:17-07:00","id":1316348,"title":"","content":"Okay, had some other things to attend to, so now (after a fairly straightforward picture of officers engaged in high society that lends itself a bit of flash with a distinct regal colour scheme) the question of support staff, which I think would be useful for rounding out the cast and considerations of a chronicle concerned with campaigning.<br /> <br /> I particularly like being given a description of what the field staff of a legion\u2019s general consists of, as it helps to provide the picture of what high level command duties are in between actual battles, and generally characterising what running an army on the march would be like. The layout of how an army communicates in battle seems not only useful for characterisation, but it complements the advice about running combat in play to have an idea that a focused battle group could be sending and receiving messages to the background as part of shaping the story of how a larger battle is going. Engineers are straightforward, while the idea of a uniform distribution of sorcerers adds to the sterling reputation of the legions in terms of their added destructive power and any such magic as might aid communications and mobility. I like how the first duty of attached Immaculate monks is tending to the spiritual needs of the troops, even if interacting with local gods and rooting out heresy are the more overtly dramatic ones; I find the idea of the cults that might develop in legions to both flesh them out, and provide inspirations for how some of them might develop having gone rogue. The baggage train gives a good breakdown on the makeup and significance of the supplies and maintenance personnel that would follow alongside a legion (and apparently it is still going with the idea that the slaves who manage such are treated better than the skirmishers, if only in the context of good treatment as a general strategy to prevent disruption of the baggage), and I think the high rank of the quartermaster would make for some interesting personality scenes for command staff. It\u2019s a slight pity that camp followers are discouraged, since historically such people lend an interesting quality to marching armies being followed by what are virtually mobile cities (often of less than reputable people), but it makes sense for the quality ascribed to the legions, and I would say there\u2019s still interesting potential to reap out of the arrival of a sanctioned merchant and their wares and services; maybe makes it more of a special treat for the soldiers.<br /> <br /> Okay, sidebar on Artifacts. It would seem that, in keeping with their more personal approach, they didn\u2019t go with an idea that legions or the empire as a whole would maintain arsenals of the regular ones to assign as needed, so it falls down to what one can get from the private stocks of one\u2019s House (even if being in the legions means that such a grant is nigh permanent) and special gifts from highly placed superiors. I think the last line of that paragraph gives the concept its strongest story and character potential, both in the idea that a legion\u2019s officers might acquire a patchwork of weapons acquired from its various battles (or sacking cities or sacred places), hire services as needed, and especially of officers occasionally making their own weapons. I think the last lends itself to both characters wielding daiklaves or similar that are very strongly matched to them and iconic of some of their harshest challenges or greatest achievements, or acquiring a weapon with such a backstory. The part about First Age weapons seems to mostly come down to a plot hook, but a very nifty one.<br /> <br /> Auxiliaries probably constitute the most intriguing development of both the Realm\u2019s military doctrine and makeup and their relationships with the satrapies. Somewhere I had an impression that the Realm\u2019s source for cavalry was being modified, but it would appear that it remains the province of auxiliary troops (with the curious addition of the Marukani of all people, despite their traditional depiction as allies of Lookshy; it makes me wonder if that society will place greater emphasis on the self-determination of given homesteads and communities, and thus more flexibility in who they serve with). The main difference that I see here from previous depictions is the manner in which it is used to add places to the setting and give distinct origins a more unique character when fighting alongside the legions, as well as the premise that waging war against a power that might be on an equal footing to a force commanded by several dozen Dragon Blooded leads them to swelling their numbers as large as possible. I would suppose that outright conscription is the most severely intrusive practice that the Realm generally employs in satrapies, and could even see such a thing as significantly transforming a nation\u2019s attitude towards them (especially given how they\u2019re employed in battle).<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve wondered about the precise meaning behind the use of the term \u201cjanissaries\u201d since it was introduced attached to Medo, so I can see sense in the premise that it\u2019s modified a bit to the context of the Realm\u2019s imperialism by way of serving as a tribute of professional soldiers (which could possibly also be a source of superior archers to Realm skirmishers in theatres where they might be seen as necessary). I like this possibility that a martial satrapy might end up as one with significant cultural influences not as a direct result of Realm colonialism, but of deployments of their people in distant lands.<br /> <br /> I vaguely recall the martial orders being described in Fangs at the Gate\u2019s section on the Caul, but don\u2019t remember them being directly described as they are here. Obviously they have a lot in common with Christian military orders, especially the association with what is virtually a crusader state and capacity to reorganise as charitable organisations (not to mention the centralised sanction, with the Scarlet Empress in place of a direct religious authority), while I\u2019d say that the general Immaculate aesthetic and the idea of forming in times of significant external danger or internal civil disorder synthesizes in a lot of the Japanese sohei. It\u2019s an interesting idea for activities that might be undertaken by far flung monks, and could conceivably be a more visible presence of the Realm\u2019s influence than even the satrap. The example groups all have something to recommend them: the Wardens strike an image distinct from how one might typically regard a martial order or arm of the Realm with specialisation on guerrilla tactics, the Sword-Saints are an interesting premise for an element of the war for the Caul whose arrogance and infighting is probably doing more harm than good for their cause (another reference to the Crusades), but the Dragonguard are the major standout. They\u2019ve got a nice sense of transition over time, of feeling and recovering from a major disaster, they stand apart from most Immaculate branches by proselytizing through direct conquest, they effectively adapt the core premise to present something that can rule as a sovereign power while still technically following their mandate, and they\u2019re a good example of the kinds of consequences that can result from Exalted intervention that takes on a life of its own.<br /> <br /> Since the presence of the Dynasts in the Imperial Legions has been downplayed, I find it makes sense for mercenaries to constitute a significant way that they would historically assert themselves militarily in the Threshold. I like that for providing an avenue in backstory for Dynasts to be intertwined in the kinds of intriguing disaster that can have often developed from dependence on mercenaries, such as the history of warfare between Italian city-states, and the manner in which mercenary contracts and rapine perpetuated the Thirty Years War; I could see it as the background to particular sources of outrage directed at the Realm, and involved in the downfall of a House or two. That they\u2019re being drawn to the Blessed Isle itself bodes especially poorly for the prospect of civil war, adding to the conflict between House the possibility of large companies that, where not unreliable and untrustworthy, find themselves free to roam the Isle aligned to nobody. It seems like a particularly effective means for other Exalted to get to the heartlands of the Realm unnoticed, hiding amidst a group that a Dynast or other official has obligingly provided with a plausible excuse for arriving, and thereafter disappearing within the more diverse and turbulent environment that those people have created. I\u2019m amused by the image of a Dragon Blood who comes to the horrified realisation that they provided Anathema with access to the Realm\u2019s shores, and gives pursuit to try and correct the mistake before it\u2019s realised by their peers.<br /> <br /> The first word on the typical strategies and tactics is the kind of thing that I\u2019ve supposed in the past; that while not unsophisticated, the Realm\u2019s military thinking is inclined towards the conservative, although it reads to me here as incentivized a bit by where one can place accountability (although I would have to imagine that a certain status quo to their dominance and the kind of power they bring to bear limits a perceived need to modify over time). The idea of an emphasis on speed and manoeuvrability is also something I\u2019ve been developing over reading this chapter, in combination with the familiar format of the legions and scope of the world they\u2019re against. In essence, the Realm strikes me as a power in which focusing their forces around a core of Exalted commanders lends itself to many situations in which their armies are outnumbered by the enemy; the impression I get of their response to that is a preference for using high speed campaigning around enemy territories to disrupt supply lines, population centres and strongholds, leaving an enemy army that will be tired and weak that can either be engaged while poorly deployed to defeat in detail or else lure against a defensively arranged formation that waits for an opportunity to surge against a weak spot in enemy lines with a wedge of well-equipped, highly disciplined troops tipped with a concentration of Exalted power, rather than trying to match a lot of enemies in lines against lines. I would find that to be consistent with the text provided for the Thousand Correct Actions that was provided in Second Edition (which I\u2019ve always presumed was drawing on notes compiled by the game\u2019s original developers).<br /> <br /> While we\u2019re not given a direct outline of what constitutes innovative tactics for some generals to employ, I\u2019d say that we\u2019ve been given a strong enough picture of the makeup of a legion to be able to infer some possibilities. I would imagine things such as tactics that place a much stronger emphasis on archers or cavalry are among them, perhaps trying to integrate a style particular to a given janissary corp into the wider tactics of the legion. Still, the idea that the success of such things can end with them becoming incorporated into the military canon makes me thing that they might more typically take the form of using the traditional legion composition and deploying it in novel and experimental ways that carry noteworthy risks; I\u2019m thinking of something such as Hannibal\u2019s unorthodox formation at Cannae, and a battle plan dependent on having a weak centre deliberately give ground without breaking to be able to draw superior Roman numbers into a lethal encirclement. And some might plain depend on the application of powerful or unusual magics, or manipulations of the environment; I can see the odd circumstance in which a Realm officer finds that the way to deal with the enemy is to trigger an avalanche down upon them (Dragon Blooded can certainly possess some very powerful and destructive magic in their own right, but I feel as though Realm tactical doctrine would discourage treating them as the primary cog in a battle plan because their distribution is not uniform and access to them can be disrupted). In any case I can suppose such tactics being drawn into the library for consideration, while still not given the greatest emphasis or first choice when regarded as unnecessary. Like, you\u2019re going to remember when a legion defeated an enemy force with a heavy concentration of archers, but that\u2019s something to fall back on when circumstances seem to demand it, not something to build an army towards.<br /> <br /> Still, a lot of that may be more academic, or a consideration for circumstances in which veteran outcastes come to the fore, with the idea that less well-drilled and experienced Dragon Blooded are being given positions of command in the new House legions. On that note, I like the number of possible axes given for the origins of particular favoured tactics among the legions, even going beyond their own organisation and the military Houses; Mnemons might have been a minority among legionary commanders, but it makes sense that they would bring the outlook and expertise of their backgrounds to a preference for fortification. I particularly like the idea given of a tactical tradition passed along a single family line deriving from the applications of a personal heirloom Artifact.<br /> <br /> The basic outline that the sidebar on intelligence gives for how legions gather strategic information is fine, and the continued significance given to the All-Seeing Eye in the form of general data on the composition and objectives of enemy forces to provide to commanders that is now being withdrawn is a nice emphasis on their new sense of power and purpose. But the biggest thing that I take from that paragraph is the focus on how House Sesus stands out in the current environment. What Fire Has Wrought outlined Cathak as the greatest military power owing to its number of legions and pre-existing strong military tradition, Tepet\u2019s outlook has been improved by pointing to foreign militaries of high quality that could be willing to ally with them, and now we have a light shone on Sesus as the one that have the most superior intelligence gathering capabilities at a time where that component of other forces has been compromised. None of these things quite render the military Houses as the only contenders, but it does draw attention to how their having a stronger and deeper background in warfare makes them major complications and obstacles. It all effectively sets a stage for the Realm teetering on the brink of a kind of conflict that they\u2019ve never before known.<br /> <br /> And I\u2019ll finish off for now with the descriptions of the specific and somewhat idiosyncratic forces. First is the Vermillion Legion, which within the overall picture currently given for the Realm\u2019s military I can only assume is a group that at some point in its history displayed a particular willingness to recruit from social quarters of low repute, found itself treated as an appropriate dumping ground for Dynasts of poor quality, and somewhere along the line decided to lean into that image in things like the willingness to employ more unorthodox or unsavoury tactics (where it wasn\u2019t forced upon it by the kinds of assignments it was given and recruits or officers it found a hard time ranging beyond). It\u2019s probably the pulpiest element of the Realm, and its features are so iconic that I find it to have made it across Editions almost entirely intact (the main loss seems to be for a strong emphasis on Dynasts being posted to the legion with the specific intent of them being killed); while I think it has just enough to be woven into the general milieu of the Realm, I would think those qualities ought to be embraced for the sake of a force that has a very grimy texture to it and an endearing sense of proud iconoclasm in a society that is so concerned with conformity. Mind, it\u2019s ironic to consider how, out of all the legions, this might have been the one in which the highest proportion of officers were actually Dynasts throughout its history; although I can imagine that more than a few outcastes who graduated from Pasiap\u2019s Stair with honour and began their careers in more savoury positions ended up here due to machinations, undue experimentation or bad luck. Such a thing would probably have been a strong basis for the legion\u2019s core emphasis on merit, competence and internal camaraderie, even if it\u2019s probably accumulated a lot of Dynasts who internalised and perpetuated such.<br /> <br /> Now, the Legion of Silence of the Scarlet Empress. The first thing that I notice is that there\u2019s no reference to them being eunuchs whatsoever. I had had a feeling that element would be removed; there had been criticisms at times that, magic or no, such a thing actually lends itself to severely diminished physical prowess. In retrospect, it also made them a bit gender uniform in a manner that felt somewhat out of place. In any case, I\u2019d say the contribution it made to them was questionable. The second thing that I see is how they\u2019re explicitly made immensely strong if not outright superhuman with the use of sorcery; I remember a period in which some develop thoughts were to attribute their prowess to the highest quality of training, but I would ultimately think that doesn\u2019t quite make them stand out enough. The current approach to Charms precludes their enhancements from being attributed to a specially developed branch of those, but the current approach to sorcery rather obviates the need. I\u2019ve always liked how they would be assigned duties of escorting Dynasts on important missions at the direction of the Empress, since I\u2019ve never really like the premise of the very highest quality soldiers being confined to bodyguard duties for a monarch who exists at the heart of fortifications that would seem to give them little to do on a regular basis (looking at you, Adeptus Custodes), although with the current approach to Lunars one might comfortably attribute the Legion with making a major contribution to the Scarlet Empress still being alive, in the face of impressive opposition no less. I find this overall reference to the Legion of Silence to lend them a bit more agency, with the focus on them as continuing at their posts, answerable to nobody, to lend an effectively sinister air, and the emphasis on their inscrutable internal communications carrying the suggestion that they\u2019re up to something. I even find the description given of their upbringing (and a little bit of how their commanders received orders directly from the Empress) to humanise them a bit; that one is both in just giving some focus to the image of them as children and the circumstances that make them what they are, and creating a background of interactions with other people. Their little sample write-up looks reasonably impressive, although I appreciate the added notion that they\u2019re generally mysterious and Storytellers have a freedom to imbue them with initial capabilities as needed or desired; it gives me an impression that this write-up is the image that most would get seeing them at rest, with the unnerving potential or more beneath. Of the Merits, I like how the Imperial Devotion one limits its availability by way of the elegant solution of a very particular background from which such an extreme could arise.<br /> <br /> Last is a sidebar on militias, rounding out both the military and administrative make-up of the Blessed Isle by taking the form of minor forces recruited under prefects and governors to provide the most basic municipal protection duties. I would find the majority of this description to be useful as finishing off the picture of what Realm life is like, providing identity to who might turn up at some problem situations, and a bit of background for some people associated with the legions. The most forward facing quality of them is how they figure into the prospect of civil war, finally addressing the idea of extending beyond the familiar scope of Realm legions and into the image of great masses of peasant conscripts set on the march to plunder neighbouring prefectures or provide home defence that might be ineffectual against even House paramilitaries, let alone true legions. It\u2019s the foundation for a very brutal image of the human cost that such warfare might have (or at least the assets, should one\u2019s priorities rest on that side).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-03T20:49:17-07:00","id":1316361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I\u2019ve never really like the premise of the very highest quality soldiers being confined to bodyguard duties for a monarch who exists at the heart of fortifications that would seem to give them little to do on a regular basis (looking at you, Adeptus Custodes), </div> </div> </div>Wait, so does that mean that after the Empress disappeared, the Legion of Silence could have quietly gone insane and become semi-nude hedonistic bodybuilders like the Pillarstodes?!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://youtu.be/I9n6iMeIW8A\" data-code=\"I9n6iMeIW8A\" data-provider=\"youtube_share\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/I9n6iMeIW8A/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> Joking aside, I'm honestly surprised that I never noticed the similarities b/w the Legion of Silence and the Custodes","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-03-2019, 11:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-04T01:14:42-07:00","id":1316395,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1247-maseiken\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1247_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1247\" data-vbnamecard=\"1247\">Maseiken</a>, I'll also try to avoid the OT aspect as much as poss, while hopefully responding to your overarching point rather than necessarily everything you've raised (may lose some nuances). I'm not so much responding to the question of whether many smaller wars are &quot;better&quot; than a few larger ones , but whether largely distributed, unaffiliated risks and lots of benefits to individual Exalted having personal freedom would precipitate the rise of political orders to try and control all of the Exalted in the manner of the systems that I believed Gigaton seemed to be talking about. It's a question of the process of political evolution towards a particular kind of government question, mainly.<br /> <br /> So on that I'm generally of the opinion that individual Exalted being able to do these things would probably give rise to local responses (finding local counters), rather than commitment to a big overarching system (which would be more likely to arise from alliances between Exalted growing really big, if at all). Yurgen Kaneko probably inspires a local response in the North, which then collapses or inspires another response, etc. rather than catalyzes a world dominant system to try and manage all the Exalted (in the manner that the Scarlet Empress seems to aspire to manage all the Dragonblooded).<br /> <br /> Re; nuclear proliferation, I agree in a world absent NATO and the USSR where there was deeply fragmented proliferation among many actors and then much use probably would have been apocalyptic, not so much under dispute; I'm disputing whether a real means of restraint of nuclear proliferation would even arise in such a world with many actors, rather than two big ones concerned with a big conflict in which each had huge stockpiles and clearly led to MAD. Political evolution question again.<br /> <br /> Also noting that such an outcome wouldn't necessarily hold in Exalted as there are a lot of high quality gods and Exalted working able to fix lands that have been struck by devastating magic etc. Though this probably would not stop civilization being pushed to limits a number of times.<br /> <br /> *although many small wars are certainly more recoverable from than a total onslaught all at once which is part of that question - there's some argument that more fragmented state systems exhibit some more robusticity than more centralised, unified ones, in terms of growth tending to be more robust to any given disaster .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-04T05:32:19-07:00","id":1316417,"title":"","content":"In regards to the Bull's local counters, I imagine that Yurgen got a big local hype boost after the Battle of Futile Blood. Before, he was just an Anathema warlord from a culture of nomadic barbarians. Now he's <i>The Bull of the North</i>, who threw back the Realm and broke the legendary Tepet Legions. A lot of northerners aren't going to care that his victory was pyrrhic, they're going to sign on with this guy for looting rights and a few of the disgruntled satrapies are going to be wondering if things might be better under Yurgen's boot than under the Realm's. The closest regional hard counters would be Medo, the Haslanti League, and maybe Iscomay. The more powerful players(Lunars and The Lover) would probably try to subvert the Bull and bring him to their side rather than killing him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T04:46:31-07:00","id":1316601,"title":"","content":"I think Iscomay is too far away, though it's a bit difficult to be sure as it's not 100% clear where the Bull's forces and empire are (though I guess his forces do have a lot of nomads, so they could move around a lot).<br /> <br /> Medo, interestingly, is a Tepet Satrapy, which could very much affect their response to him.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T05:48:47-07:00","id":1316611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1315847#post1315847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find it interesting that a lot of people feel that Dragonblood have consistently been depicted as lame and rubbish as stupid. I never really got that. When I started playing, it seemed that the Realm was a giant and powerful empire, and Solars and Lunars scared and on the run. But I wonder if that's because I played pretty much only Dragonblood games for 3-4 years before I played much Solars, and the DBs were much higher xp than the Solars (who were starting characters), and had way more experience and artefacts and so on. Also there were those combo rules that really did in Solars...<br /> I remember once our entire Solar party fought a single optimised Fire Aspect with Five Dragon Style and artefacts, we barely beat him.</div> </div> </div> </div>The main problem with Dragon-Blooded was that they weren't allowed perfect defences in their native charmset in a system that had become increasingly reliant upon them, to the point where there were spells in Terrestrial Circle Sorcery/Shadowlands Circle Necromancy that they could barely defend against.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T06:09:36-07:00","id":1316614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316601#post1316601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Iscomay is too far away, though it's a bit difficult to be sure as it's not 100% clear where the Bull's forces and empire are (though I guess his forces do have a lot of nomads, so they could move around a lot).<br /> <br /> Medo, interestingly, is a Tepet Satrapy, which could very much affect their response to him.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I really wish that they gave a general outline of the Bull's empire's borders. <br /> <br /> Medo is looking like a good place for House Tepet to flee to if the Roseblack doesn't get the results they're looking for. The Medoans respect them and they're on an expansionist war footing and looking for firepower. Transporting Dynasts to Medo is way less troublesome than transporting Medoan cavalry to the Blessed Isle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T06:15:46-07:00","id":1316616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316614#post1316614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I really wish that they gave a general outline of the Bull's empire's borders.<br /> <br /> Medo is looking like a good place for House Tepet to flee to if the Roseblack doesn't get the results they're looking for. The Medoans respect them and they're on an expansionist war footing and looking for firepower. Transporting Dynasts to Medo is way less troublesome than transporting Medoan cavalry to the Blessed Isle.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Tepet Sidebar leads me to an...interesting idea for a game, of a Tepet Dynast traveling the old Tepet Satrapies and calling upon her families reputation to build a colitation-nation out of these disparate warrior peoples. Maybe not enough to retake the Scarlet Throne, but enough to be a new Shogunate-Successor nation (I like a Civil War that ends with many different successor states--Peleps in the West, the Prasadi breaking off in the east, etc)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T06:26:42-07:00","id":1316618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316616#post1316616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The Tepet Sidebar leads me to an...interesting idea for a game, of a Tepet Dynast traveling the old Tepet Satrapies and calling upon her families reputation to build a colitation-nation out of these disparate warrior peoples. Maybe not enough to retake the Scarlet Throne, but enough to be a new Shogunate-Successor nation (I like a Civil War that ends with many different successor states--Peleps in the West, the Prasadi breaking off in the east, etc)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I always saw that as the more likely outcome of the Realm Civil War(as opposed to a new Empress taking the throne), its becoming really prominent this edition now that differences between the houses have become more prononced. My best guess is the events of the Warring States period and the Sengoku Era play out on the Blessed Isle. The Houses fragment the Realm into smaller states and the Immaculate Order splinters into countless sects and theocratic dominions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T06:28:49-07:00","id":1316619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316611#post1316611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main problem with Dragon-Blooded was that they weren't allowed perfect defences in their native charmset in a system that had become increasingly reliant upon them, to the point where there were spells in Terrestrial Circle Sorcery/Shadowlands Circle Necromancy that they could barely defend against.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think when we started neither we nor the ST were mechanically savvy enough to be wielding the flurry-of-grand-goremaul that totally smashed everything. And of the 7 PCs, I think 4 had power armour (and another was a Water Monk, so had a really good defence anyway).<br /> <br /> So actually, when we started the combats worked quite well, as our power armour's soak stopped anything being too lethal, and the Solars we fought tended to be armed with regular daiklaives, not massive double-handed weapons.<br /> <br /> Whereas later when we did other DB games where we didn't wear power armour, every now and then someone would be horribly be killed by a goremaul (another PC basically killed mine by accident with a Grand Goremaul) or Direlance+Five Dragon Force Blow. But it was only like, once a year. Not all the time.<br /> <br /> And in the Solar game we did, I played a Twilight for about 7 years with no perfect defence, but the ST tended not to throw very dangerous foes at us.<br /> <br /> All-in-all it made a much more satisfying combat experience than later on once we'd twigged to how to break the combat system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really wish that they gave a general outline of the Bull's empire's borders.</div> </div> </div> </div>As it's a nomadic empire, it shouldn't be too distinct, but some kind of indication would be good.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T09:26:31-07:00","id":1316651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316619#post1316619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, I think when we started neither we nor the ST were mechanically savvy enough to be wielding the flurry-of-grand-goremaul that totally smashed everything. And of the 7 PCs, I think 4 had power armour (and another was a Water Monk, so had a really good defence anyway).<br /> <br /> So actually, when we started the combats worked quite well, as our power armour's soak stopped anything being too lethal, and the Solars we fought tended to be armed with regular daiklaives, not massive double-handed weapons.<br /> <br /> Whereas later when we did other DB games where we didn't wear power armour, every now and then someone would be horribly be killed by a goremaul (another PC basically killed mine by accident with a Grand Goremaul) or Direlance+Five Dragon Force Blow. But it was only like, once a year. Not all the time.<br /> <br /> And in the Solar game we did, I played a Twilight for about 7 years with no perfect defence, but the ST tended not to throw very dangerous foes at us.<br /> <br /> All-in-all it made a much more satisfying combat experience than later on once we'd twigged to how to break the combat system.<br /> <br /> <br /> As it's a nomadic empire, it shouldn't be too distinct, but some kind of indication would be good.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes an accident that\u2019s totally what it was and then the Satrap issued a general Pardon .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12796-beast-of-bitter-oblivion","name":"Beast of Bitter Oblivion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12796_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-05T09:30:08-07:00","id":1316652,"title":"","content":"The Satrap who killed my bureaucrat? Yes, he did pardon himself, but I don't know how well that'd stand up when the dead Sesus's family investigated.<br /> <br /> Still, I'm secure in the knowledge that satrap was later tricked into signing his own death warrant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-06T03:16:02-07:00","id":1316790,"title":"","content":"Those of you who have followed Onyx Path products throughout the years, do you have any approximate guess as to how long it will be before I can order the physical copy of this wonderful book ? I really have no clue as to how long this pre-release process usually takes. Like many Game Masters, I do loath having to read PDF's (although this book is not the worst having as a PDF, since it does not contain mechanics), and I am very much looking forward to be able to buy the real book so I can bring this beauty to my table <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24253,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","user":"Magnus K","date":". 06-06-2019, 06:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24253-magnus-k","name":"Magnus K","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-06T04:27:37-07:00","id":1316798,"title":"","content":"Month or two, probably.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/746-kunoichi","name":"Kunoichi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar746_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-06T17:28:13-07:00","id":1316913,"title":"","content":"So the Imperial Navy is something that I found What Fire Has Wrought to effectively clarify and consolidate exactly what its place in the Realm\u2019s military structure was and the precise nature of House Peleps\u2019 authority over it, right down to the fun detail that the ruling council of the House does double duty as the Admiralty. Now that gets expanded upon with the command hierarchy organised beneath that board, as well as the composition and duties of the Navy; the Earth Fleet absorbing the old duty of the Merchant Fleet in dealing with pirates and smugglers, and the other four being concerned not merely with projecting imperial force in the form of dealing with lesser fleets and ferrying the legions, but the unique concerns of the setting of needing to deal with supernatural opposition from the relatively conventional (ala naval forces commanded by Lunars) to the particularly unusual such as dangerous sea monsters.<br /> <br /> On the point of fleet composition, one major departure from prior depictions such as in Savage Seas is that the differing priorities of the fleets means that they have varied ship layouts, rather than repeating \u201c30% triremes, 40% biremes, 5% tenders and the rest are small craft for marines\u201d six times without any alteration. Even bigger than that is how the Water Fleet is finally directly attributed with a concern for blue water craft so that it can actually <i>make</i> the trips to the Western satrapies (in addition to the Air Flate having such ships so that it can range at least as far north as the white sea); in fairness, the Water Fleet of prior Editions existed in a context in which the Realm had dominated in the West for a lot longer and so its duties could plausibly have consisted if coastal patrols of those islands, but it was still a bit of an oversight (especially if they were supposed to be dealing with pirates).<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I prefer the ranks given to the highest commanders of each fleet being grand admiral rather than fleet admiral, just because I think it sounds cooler (and it recalls fond memories of Skies of Arcadia). For those beneath them, I appreciate the direct statement about how the Exalted fill the ranks of the admirals (and what their duties are), particularly the point with how family connections to Peleps (and sometimes significant talent) ensure that other scions might achieve the ranks, and that otherwise the officers of the conceivably still vast numbers of ships are almost entirely mortal. The idea that a peasant who has the skill and fortune to be appointed captain of a ship is promoted in status of the Realm as well is especially interesting; it implies to me that there\u2019s a unique prestige attached to the Navy, both in affording its commissions the power to raise a person like that and the sense that it would be unacceptable for one to have the rank of captain and be anything less than a patrician.<br /> <br /> Sorcerers would obviously have uniquely valued capabilities for ships, while it\u2019s fun that the typical distance maintained from them complements the characteristic superstition of sailors to create an especially lonely position; I suppose that the navy must compensate such volunteers particularly well. It\u2019s likewise fun for the sailors of the navy to find the prospect of offending maritime spirits terrifying enough that, whenever a monk isn\u2019t attached who can keep an eye over their shoulder, they\u2019ll forego Immaculate propriety altogether in favour of propitiating such spirits (as well as outright worshipping an Immaculate Dragon, which I imagine many monks would find even more offensive).<br /> <br /> I quite like how the hard delineation between navy and legions, in combination with the fact that the heart of the Realm is an island and thus 100% dependent on sailing, creates an environment of mutual disdain between them. Particularly the combination of how legionnaires don\u2019t like the sense of powerlessness inherent to being stocked into the holds of a ship and entirely reliant on the sailors to get them safely to another shore, with how such powerlessness (and any other compromising positions) being the only thing sailors tend to see of the legionnaires kind of undermines their mistake and cultivates an unimpressive image.<br /> <br /> And then the Water Fleet\u2019s reconfiguration in keeping with the West now being presented as an exciting new frontier is capitalised by the point that Peleps is investing even more heavily in making it seaworthy for the sake of their expansionist ambitions in that Direction. This dynamic future and being given a strong emphasis in the greater naval makeup is in particular contrast to how First Edition portrayed the Water Fleet, as the most hard pressed owing to the Time of Tumult rendering its theatre of operations the least tenable to the point of it being described as shrinking drastically.<br /> <br /> As for the Merchant Fleet, I find something particularly different in the <i>feel</i> of it when it\u2019s directly addressed as having previously been the sixth fleet of the Imperial Navy; it makes the transition feel like a stronger characteristic of it. That\u2019s something that leads nicely into the point that the Fleet has not yet thoroughly come under the control of V\u2019neef; they got rid of the Peleps, but retain enough veterans (on whom they are probably quite dependent) who hold some older loyalties to leave the new power compromised. I had imagined that this rendition of the Merchant Fleet would be more concerned on providing the cargo vessels, but it would appear that it still consists of warships, just applying them to escort and pirate hunting purposes (I\u2019m especially amused by the idea that they engage in entrapment to round out their income with prizes); nevertheless, I would like to imagine that the Fleet operates a few gigantic treasure ships in the event that a Great House wants to lay claim to some sizable treasure as tribute. I like the way that V\u2019neef\u2019s ambitions in the West amount to leaving more of the tribute vulnerable (which creates the interesting scenario that even satrapies that are trying to keep their payments going are coming up short), and the idea that they\u2019re still opposing any efforts of rival Houses to develop fleets sufficient to protect their tribute seems like a cunning long-term strategy, a means of leveraging their limited but highly valuable power into survival across a civil war (especially the point that all they have to do is withhold services rather than proactively attack).<br /> <br /> Heh, with the fact that the ships owned by the other Houses include the occasional ship to protect cargo vessels, I wonder if any Dynasts have ever chanced their arm at trying to pass off tribute as regular commercial shipping in order to cut Peleps or V\u2019neef out of their cut. I\u2019d also wonder if their paramilitaries might have better relations with the privately owned ships that ferry them than the Imperial forces do with one another. I\u2019m guessing that Cathak still has ships to serve that purpose for their legions in the way that Sesus and Tepet do (or did), just not as many merchant or similar ships to supplement them. It\u2019s a good look to address the matter of how Peleps becoming an enemy of the other Houses might roundly transform the Scarlet Dynasty\u2019s relationship with the sea; facing the prospect of losing the familiar methods of transporting the legions, needing to appropriate merchant shipping into impromptu war vessels (always a good time, and successful more often than you\u2019d think), and particularly that idea that the outbreak of war could cut the Blessed Isle off from the Threshold (definitely militarily, possibly totally if they lack the forces to keep the sea lanes safe). It\u2019s an intriguing premise just for the planning sessions and developments of military theory that it might entail for the characters.<br /> <br /> To double back a bit, I mostly like that picture of a ship at sea; the scarlet sails seem like a fitting way for Imperial ships to proclaim themselves, the sun in the distance looks very good, and I like the look of the officer\u2019s uniform, kind of a fusion of 18th century naval and what I think old Korean might have looked like. I do wonder if the number of masts is fitting to something that I suppose is a galley, but that might be nitpicking\u2026 if it was meant to be a trireme, it should technically have two more rows of oars. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> As for the sidebars, I\u2019m surprised at how strong the navy comes across; I wouldn\u2019t have expected the marines to have such high traits (although that might be offset a bit by the lower likelihood of being commanded by the Exalted), and the blue-water vessels look particularly heavily armed; perhaps not quite enough to capture Age of Sail gunships exchanging broadsides, but as close as you\u2019ll get in typical Exalted. I also like how the battle group traits account for different sizes and quality of other personnel that will be found upon the different classes of ship. For the treasures, I would find it to be expected that they could acquire a decent number of items seized from enemy ships, but what\u2019s interesting is how their access to First Age weapons differs from that of the legions; the ancient ships might not be in the best condition, but at least Peleps had access to them, which affords them a distinct means of retaining control for themselves in the absence of the Empress. It probably helped that such power had somewhat less range than warstriders would have (although the dangers inherent to using them to blockade the Blessed Isle or rampage through the West\u2026).<br /> <br /> I think the section on the Realm\u2019s maritime power does a good job of making their activities an engaging focus for stories, and it comes across as quite strong in the things that are made dependent on it. I would have found it in the past to be a bit of a static element of the setting, and not the most vivid part of the Realm in particular, but the points on how it is or could evolve through the Time of Tumult give it more presence.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-07T13:51:27-07:00","id":1317055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1316619#post1316619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, I think when we started neither we nor the ST were mechanically savvy enough to be wielding the flurry-of-grand-goremaul that totally smashed everything. And of the 7 PCs, I think 4 had power armour (and another was a Water Monk, so had a really good defence anyway).</div> </div> </div> </div>At that level it is pretty difficult to notice a significant problem unique to Dragon-Blooded, since comparisons to the Solar play experience are offset by the quantity of unexpected attacks in the Abyssal arsenal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-09T16:06:47-07:00","id":1317436,"title":"","content":"All right, just a short bit of the chapter left. I\u2019ll catch up soon I\u2019m sure, seeing a decent window.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> With the number of times that the book has referred to making war with the Lunars and the larger context of presenting them as the major opponents of the Realm, it makes a lot of sense to have a dedicated analysis of such warfare from the perspective of the Realm. I like how there\u2019s a common broad strategy in which Lunars can effectively build up extremely motivated forces to hit more directly from the front, while the Exalted themselves can employ their varied and subtle talents to soften up the legions from the side and back, making them more susceptible. I feel as though this section in particular is being written with a certain in-character bias; that the Realm would delegitimise the motives of Lunar dominion inhabitants by attributing their acts to religious fervour generated by Exalted, rather than grievances from the rapine of the empire. I see something similar in their perspective of the kinds of powers and other advantages that the Lunars and those behind them can bring to bear.<br /> <br /> I think there\u2019s a good sense of escalation to the processes used in opposing Lunars, from the relatively basic of being heavily drilled to use good anti-infiltration tactics, to having good fortification tactics to mitigate empowered armies, up to using their supernatural assets to counter Lunars as best they can directly. I especially like how it places the Dragon Blooded into context of how significant they are in forcing the Silver Pact\u2019s prevailing strategy; that when a full legion or more is massed together, such a large number of those Exalted is enough to give even powerful Lunars paused. I find an interesting balance presented for Lunars in making a major show of force against the Realm, with the idea that such a thing exposes their dwindling arsenals of First Age to attack, but only by way of becoming the target of such, even if the framing is in terms of the Realm\u2019s own reluctance to commit such force. I think it can make some campaigns involving Lunars, in backstory or otherwise, into a series of interlocking dynamics; Lunars trying to make a big enough nuisance of themselves to expose an irreplaceable weapon and then make a risky go at it, and Dragon Blooded commanders needing to decide whether things have gotten bad enough to take that chance yet.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Heh, if there was ever going to be something to demonstrate the Realm\u2019s complacency over the return of the Solars, and sell me on the value of the icewalker forces behind Yurgen having been severely damaged in the act of destroying Tepet, it would be the idea that the Scarlet Dynasty as a whole can view that war from afar and be pleased that it was a victory on their part. I also have a liking for that idea that limitations to information transmission and Dynast priorities and self-absorption mean that they don\u2019t even fully believe that the Solars have returned en masse; I think it gives their own society a lot more credibility when their internal politics don\u2019t exist in the context that a resurgence of Solars is a widely spread theme, as well as setting the stage for scenes of extreme shock at just how big a deal Solars can truly be. The sidebar in particular is very good for presenting this in terms of its value for player characters; I especially like the point of how the wider apathy affords an unoccupied niche in which player Terrestrials can make a name for themselves, while Celestial Exalted ought to consider balancing the window afforded by the lowest backing ever for the Wyld Hunt and not making such a big fuss of themselves too early that the Realm manages to pull together in opposition to them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Finally, having established all of the facets of imperial military power, the game looks forwards to the times developing in its dramatic recent withdrawal. I like how, for a start, it gives rebellious Solars and Mnemon\u2019s reprisal an uncertain position; it\u2019s enough to establish a scenario in which Realm forces can still throw down, but also illustrates the weaknesses of dividing the legions among Houses and still gives the Solars a chance to bounce back. The next two examples of places capitalising on a complete withdrawal of Realm power are nice snapshots of new locations (I have nothing specific to say, but that\u2019s not a sleight on them), but for Medo I particularly like how, given its backstory and the terms in which it was initially described to us (one of the first new locations of the Edition), I have a real fondness for the idea that the lack of Realm oversight is finally granting them an opportunity to resume the empire building that got them into trouble in the first place, with the notion that they\u2019d actually like the survivors of their Tepet overlords to join in being the icing on top; it\u2019s a very distinct international relationship.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The military stuff has been pretty good; much like the bureaucracy, more than knowing its composition I always wanted a strong picture of what it did, both on a day-to-day basis and in the grand scheme of the empire. I think that a fine job of that has been done here, providing not only a framework for devising the actual manoeuvres of the legions and navy, but the significance of how they\u2019re being appropriated and a decent general frame for stories or chronicles focused around involvement in an army on the march.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-09T19:18:15-07:00","id":1317466,"title":"","content":"I for one, really enjoyed the fact that a Lunar is working with Dragon Kings in the military chapter. Blood sacrifice aside, I'm glad to get some more DK mentions in 3e. In hindsight, its a bit of a given that (semi)enlightened DKs and Lunars would be easy allies, they're bound to have territorial overlap after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-16T06:36:36-07:00","id":1318842,"title":"","content":"After a long pause I continue with my analysis of this book.<br /> <br /> The second story continues to deepen the corruption of Realm using the bandits of the first story. Those who should eliminate corruption are now persecuted and killed by the criminals who have much more support among the higher echelons.<br /> The part of the magistrates I liked a lot, it is brief but it transmits all the necessary information. I like how each magistrate is different and they operate in a non-rigid and structured way.<br /> The section of the ministries was great. I knew that Realm's politics were deadly, but I did not remember that the rise in the bureaucracy was based on murder as much as for passing exams. It is a pity that I do not detail more in what the exams consist of, but between the name they receive and the bureaucratic examinations of imperial China I think it is not difficult to be inspired. The description of the different ministries in one place is always useful, as well as the reminder of how the Empress secured the overlapping of ministries and how she created or divided ministries as needed. The bureaucracy is an aspect often overlooked but I think the book does a good job of making it interesting and useful for gaming groups. Some ministries already knew them but others are new, all are very interesting, like the sidebars, especially that of sorcerers.<br /> The situation of the All-Seeing Eye is transmitted quite well and reading the section I came up with some ideas of adventure. Very interesting.<br /> <br /> The deliberative is as I had always imagined full of betrayals and stabbings in the back. Condemned to become literal as the political situation deteriorates. The Regent has not changed much between editions, although at least now he recognizes what situation he is in and everybody hates him, but at the moment it is too useful for him to be killed.<br /> Finally a good explanation of how the economy is affected by the disappearance of the Empress. Giving a foothold to the Guild in the Realm is one of the biggest mistakes the dynasties have committed so far, but that will only show with time.<br /> The story of the next chapter is still a consequence of the first, in what I suppose will be the theme and shows once again the internal decomposition of Realm. This time with such audacious bandits who attack the legionaries and how nepotism negatively affects the legions. I like continuity and I'm curious to see how this story ends. I must say that the mini stories in rpg books I have never liked, but using them to tell a unified story has always seemed the best possible use that can be given to these stories.<br /> The chapter is very interesting, not only speaks of the imperial legions, also speaks of the military forces of the Great Houses, gives more details of the current state of the Tepet House (2 Solars dead, which intuited the story of Arada in the Dragon -Blooded book), and the paramilitary forces of the Kingdom and the garrisons of the Satrapas. It could be said that both sides suffered irreplaceable losses. What is important is that it was shown that Realm can be defeated in an open and clear conflict. At least in its current state, which gives encouragement to each ambitious warlord who reaches the news and things will only get more in their favor as the Realm continues to deteriorate internally.<br /> Like everything else in Realm, it has gotten worse without the Empress.<br /> The detailed treatment of the legions in more detail than ever is very interesting. A typical legion has many more Dragon-Blooded than expected, had always thought more in numbers between 12 and 20. Even if the vast majority are Essence 2, 40 dragon-blooded are extraordinarily dangerous by themselves, much more with support of the legion itself. Suddenly a Legion of Realm is much more threatening than I had ever thought.<br /> The martial orders are new and are a good addition. The Vermillon legion is now the best legion. The legion of silence has always pleased me and two of its merits seem perfectly applicable to PC who wish to have them.<br /> The Navy and the legions despise each other very much, like everyone in theRealm thanks to the Empress. There is not much detail about the fleet, the truth is that I would have liked more detail, especially types of boats used.<br /> Of course the whole defensive system of Realm is collapsing and most ignore the emerging anathemas, many dynasts are going to have a sudden awakening.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25189-dagurasu","name":"Dagurasu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-17T09:47:42-07:00","id":1319069,"title":"","content":"After a few days of highly invested obligations, I think I'm ready to get back on this horse.<br /> <br /> Now, for the fiction opening the Immaculate Order\u2019s chapter. I find it gets off to a good start with the introduction of the varied peasant origins: you get a number of names to get a sense of how the Blessed Isle might designate locations, some agency in the common purpose of far-flung people in seeking ecclesiastic aid, and the interesting practice of such petitioners not only having to provide service in kind, but due so with grace and humility as designated by the Perfected Hierarchy. Indeed, for all of the snippets setting up the relationship between the Order and the peasantry, beginning on this foot bodes well.<br /> <br /> I also like the modified sense of awe in the presence of a Dynastic abbot, things such as being allowed to share tea with somebody of such high station, how she speaks to them in a language of commoners, and that for all of the accommodations they remain nervous. I enjoy the way that each of the representatives are taking different attitudes towards raising their grievances, with some more blunt than others (even if trying to dance around going too far in expressing defiance with regards to the Dynasty), and find the abbot\u2019s initial responses in slight body language to be a fun bit of characterisation; one little touch that I find nice is how the peasants refer to only the Exalted, with no qualifiers, reinforcing the point that the Terrestrials will be the only Exalted they\u2019ve ever known.<br /> <br /> Finally, I find the dynamics belying the abbot Mnemon Amalu\u2019s direct responses to the peasants to be fascinating; the teasing out of what they\u2019re actually trying to seek from her, the manner in which she presents a somewhat fearsome presence while not being so threatening that the petitioners don\u2019t continue to speak their minds (perhaps even enhancing their doing so), and the manner in which she breaks down their grievances, combined with local concerns that they\u2019re not asking about, in a manner that reinforces the state ideology while simultaneously inferring accommodations, in a manner that reinforces the awe of the peasants. I love the way that she\u2019s implying a quid pro quo (especially one that refers to the greater emphasis the Order is being given in this Edition as a secret/cultural police), but one that doesn\u2019t come across to me as cynical or nakedly corrupt; strictly speaking, neither the thing she\u2019s asking for nor what she\u2019s offering in return are bad things for the downtrodden. I think that people could do a lot with that image of the Order having more flexibility in being an authority that people can appeal to, while not losing its core values or character; certainly, such an image would do a lot for maintaining and enriching the credibility of the Order as a sincerely popular social force.<br /> <br /> I like how the accompanying artwork is a more abstract, stylized image, consisting of sharply contrasted shapes and a pyramidal arrangement of characters. I find the design of abbot Amalu to be good in terms of being an older, more natural looking woman who radiates an aura of serenity. And I find the shading to be reminiscent of a 70s Hong Kong martial arts movie, which is quite fitting to the subject matter on several counts.<br /> <br /> The chapter\u2019s introduction covers the basics (I\u2019m fond of the juxtaposition presented between the Order serving its purpose with a combination of patient instruction and effectively beating some people up), and then we\u2019re given an outline of the Philosophy itself. That\u2019s not exactly something new, but I find that it develops new interest in beginning with a series of agrarian analogies for the Perfected Hierarchy; I find the imagery compelling in its own right, and it makes sense as a narrative to communicate the theology in terms resonant with the majority of the Realm\u2019s population. Something that I like in particular is how it references duties to one\u2019s lesser as something that takes place throughout the Hierarchy, as I imagine that there\u2019s value in cultivating the idea among farmers that the basic practical work that they need to do for their livelihood also contributes to the spiritual elevation of lesser beings. Still, for the benefit of loftier viewpoints, I also find the description of the Perfected Hierarchy in more directly theological terms to be useful; I also think it helps to clarify a couple of minor points that might have been a bit vague before, such as the exact perspectives on the Elemental Dragons, ultimate spiritual purpose, and the status of monks.<br /> <br /> Likewise, I think this description of the prayer calendar may be the most direct that the Immaculate approach to worship is; I think that there have been times in the past in which the calendar and the privilege of Dragon Blooded to worship were conflated, so I like being given the direct statement that each god\u2019s prayer day has the mortal population engage in guided worship, while the Exalted are free to pray whenever. I also like the inclusion of how the calendar mostly differs across regions to focus populations on more local gods, which can account for variations in Cult size for different gods involved with the Order. On that note, I like the sidebar describing the manner in which gods who adhere to the Philosophy express that (the direct appearances at their own ceremonies and visibly engaging with the Dragon Blooded), and both of the motives for doing so; that some find the scripture compelling enough to regard it as a fine moral guide for the world is all well and good, but I also think a reminder that some may go above and beyond for the sake of keeping their overlords\u2019 good graces is valuable. Also nice to see Gri-Fel referenced, with the promise of more attention later; I finally managed to get ahold of my Second Edition corebook for the first time in more than a year, and among other things it reminded me of my fondness for that fellow.<br /> <br /> People have often complained at a sense of uniformity in the Realm\u2019s spirituality, so I\u2019m interested to see what is said of heresy in it being brought up this early. For a start, I like how it\u2019s presented as not just a deviation from orthodoxy, but a kind of expression of it; that one is not necessarily malicious, but an example of how life is born in ignorance. Hence, there\u2019s a framework for monks and other pious adherents to treat outsiders with a more considered approach, rather than defaulting to contempt. It strikes a chord with my having recently reread a passage describing contemporary attitudes to so-called early Christian heresies such as Arianism (if not quite yet far enough to have the attitude of \u201cthere are multiple valid interpretations\u201d).<br /> <br /> Anyway, the major heresies concerned with illicit worship and the Anathema. For the first one, I\u2019m liking the way that this is structured; it gets the most obvious form of people worshipping gods out of order or keeping with Immaculate mores out of the way early (although adds some nuances in the form of how satrapies present the challenge of highly ingrained religions, and that there are occasional compromises of syncretism), and then uses the matter as the basis for varieties of heterodoxy within the Philosophy itself. The Pure Way obviously stands out, I like seeing the call back to the Five Insightful Criticisms, and I\u2019m curious about what form heretical ghost veneration could take (possibly some system in which the idea of an ascending hierarchy of souls is leveraged into the idea that those that linger have some worthwhile insight, or are demonstrating a lack of need to reincarnate). The big thing is the heretical worship of the Immaculate Dragons; I think it makes the point that orthodoxy holds that the Dragons are to be emulated rather than worshipped much more strongly here when it details several of the forms and motives that such improper worship can take, rather than merely stating the distinction. Daana\u2019d as a patron for the non-cisgender is grand, although I\u2019m drawn a bit more by her being regarded as a sea goddess to some, and I find it interesting to present a scenario in which even the Dragon Blooded can slip into a heterodox view of a Dragon (in addition to the idea that they\u2019re granted the unique permission of occasionally worshipping the five). I can\u2019t fully recall if the Heresy of the Antitheses came up before, but I\u2019m liking its presentation here of a kind of apocalyptic cult believing in the impending return of the Immaculate Dragons as their opposites; I would think that worshipping such holy devils would entail an enthusiasm for that return out of a perception that the world is in need of such punishment, but the idea of believing in it and praying for their own exclusion does speak to an interesting kind of fatalistic outlook. I suppose you don\u2019t need to hope for the world\u2019s end if you think things are bad enough that it\u2019s impending anyway, and just wish to be spared.<br /> <br /> I like the sidebar addressing the question of what happens when the Dragon Blooded themselves end up as objects of worship, particularly the point about how a desire to not motivate it (either from piety or fear of censure) partially informs the loftiness of the Dynastic attitude. The idea that the Scarlet Empress was an object of secret worship is nothing new, but it\u2019s developed nicely here with some details such as the notion that it\u2019s enabled by veneration of her being naturally strewn through the idioms of the Blessed Isle\u2019s languages, or essentially viewing her as a bodhisattva. It\u2019s interesting to me that the more spiritual Dynasts don\u2019t lend credence to the idea; I suppose that confronting it would create an awkward dilemma concerning whether or not punishing people for acting in a particular manner on Immaculate dogma describing the Empress is appropriate. I can imagine that on any occasion where a monk encountered such a thing directly, they\u2019d sooner rationalise it as just people who are especially earnest in esteeming the Scarlet Empress, rather than make a public show of associating veneration for her with repression. And it looks helpful to lay down a simple little sidebar establishing Immaculate doctrine and general public perception of the other realms of existence; the substance of which is largely familiar, but I find the turn of phrase for the Underworld to adjust my perspective of how they see ghosts from \u201cpeople erroneously holding on to life\u201d to \u201cpeople who fear to engage in their next life\u201d, and the Wyld seems to downplay the sheer scope of it.<br /> <br /> The last I\u2019ll look at for the moment is the part about Anathema heresies. First off, that picture; it seems to me that this would be less of a literal scene and more depicting something in the Immaculate perspective, with how sinister that Solar looks; even the jagged look of the anima strikes me as an exaggeration in keeping with Immaculate prejudices. The armour that he\u2019s wearing is a different look to an Artifact, and I think it has interesting design possibilities for something like that to make the magical material prominent at the outlying components. Of the hunters, I question that Earth Aspect\u2019s choice of attire, but I like her hateful expression and large club.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m rather liking this contrast between the low-level pervasiveness and relative uniformity of heretical worship of divinities with the unique and varied dangers brought with the arrival of every new Anathema; the corebook already established the definition for that term that encompasses all inimical beings, but I find something striking in the focus here on them as sources of new kinds of heterodoxy. That\u2019s nicely complemented by the point on how each Wyld Hunt needs to be equally mutable and adaptive, a line of thinking that feels most strongly informed by their primary focus over the centuries being the Lunar Exalted. I find the emphasis on the motivating factor for Dragon Blooded being strong enough to carry them over vast distances to be decent, and particularly like this notion that part of that motive is to reinforce their egos.<br /> <br /> Heh, I was just earlier thinking about how Ma-Ha-Suchi will have a certain prominence for making access to the Southeast difficult, so I like him being given as an example of the kind of danger of Anathema being allowed to grow into their power. That Wyld Hunts sometimes try to take their targets alive has been in the game before, but never quite seemed to be focused on in descriptions of the activity. Given how it\u2019s a much more prominent element of this Edition, it\u2019s sensible to refer to the matter at this point; I find the description of how and where they\u2019ll lock such Anathema away to be especially sinister, and I like how such prisons have more to them than just the Imperial Manse. I think such an addition to the setting should be quite useful for anybody who ever wanted backstory to include a Lunar having a Tai Lung style prison break. I do wonder about these unkillable Anathema that might be taken so; would that refer to censured beings besides the Exalted, or the occasional Celestial who is too strong for attacking Terrestrials to bring down entirely.<br /> <br /> It would appear to me that there have been some adjustments to Immaculate perspective of Solars and Lunars even beyond the matter of recognising them as Exalted; I had gathered that it was always going to be a subject of deeper academia, but this would seem to say that monks understanding the truth of them is a matter of course. In any case, I find the statements here to do a lot to sell its credibility to any holdouts; the perspective that Solar and Lunar power is inevitably out of keeping with the Perfected Hierarchy, a seeming viewpoint that it can come whether one acted out of order or not and is more of a pitiable thing. I think it maintains consistency while still taking easy outs such as providing the Wyld Hunt with a convincing argument of their innate Exalted-ness off the table, and I\u2019m amused at the statement that monks let the nuances of the distinctions lie for lay Immaculates lest they find the difference too confusing.<br /> <br /> Aaaand to just step ahead a bit, another handy sidebar concerning the subject of the Incarnae. The idea that the Immaculates won\u2019t worship them despite assigning them an extremely high status (which\u2026 may not necessarily be an inaccurate one; the Elemental Dragons are a bit of a black box, after all) because they don\u2019t really provide anything back into the world\u2026 that feels to me like a point of theology that would have originated among their own intellectuals rather than from the Sidereals. I couldn\u2019t really see a motive for the Star Chosen to exclude the Incarnae from the prayer calendar, while it would seem to not be regarded as worth steering them away from when they made their own conclusions about it. It\u2019s probably no skin off the noses of the Incarnae in any case, and I think the situation effectively sets up the likes of the Sun being forgotten in the Realm without needing any direct suppression.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-17T11:15:45-07:00","id":1319087,"title":"","content":"I always wondered if the Elemental Dragons even acknowledge or notice the existence of the Dragon-Blooded in their current state. They made them for the Primodial War, but never interacted with them in any capacity afterwards (save in the fictional accounts of the Immaculate Dragons), even NEET Sol Invictus pays more attention to the Solars than the Elemental Dragons do to Terrestrials. We know they're not wholly quiescent since they protested imprisoning the Kukla(though 3e may do away with even that). I wonder if its a function of their investment in the underpinnings of Creation's essence that they don't involve themselves in temporal affairs or if the Dragons viewed their Chosen as means to an end and stopped paying attention when they fulfilled their purpose in the war.<br /> <br /> As for excluding the Incarnae from the prayer calendar, I can see Sidereals actually encouraging it. Sol and Luna cults tend to correlate with Solar and Lunar activity, so suppressing worship is a good way to prevent Solars and Lunars from drumming up easy support (since the whole, &quot;solars and lunars stole the power of the incarnae&quot; line doesn't really make sense given 3e's view on &quot;Anathema = Exalt doomed to go insane&quot;). The Maidens ignore prayers or randomly answer them in their typical inscrutable capriciousness, so any worship they receive or fail to receive is meaningless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-17-2019, 02:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-17T12:09:07-07:00","id":1319103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1319087#post1319087\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always wondered if the Elemental Dragons even acknowledge or notice the existence of the Dragon-Blooded in their current state. They made them for the Primodial War, but never interacted with them in any capacity afterwards (save in the fictional accounts of the Immaculate Dragons), even NEET Sol Invictus pays more attention to the Solars than the Elemental Dragons do to Terrestrials. We know they're not wholly quiescent since they protested imprisoning the Kukla(though 3e may do away with even that). I wonder if its a function of their investment in the underpinnings of Creation's essence that they don't involve themselves in temporal affairs or if the Dragons viewed their Chosen as means to an end and stopped paying attention when they fulfilled their purpose in the war.<br /> <br /> As for excluding the Incarnae from the prayer calendar, I can see Sidereals actually encouraging it. Sol and Luna cults tend to correlate with Solar and Lunar activity, so suppressing worship is a good way to prevent Solars and Lunars from drumming up easy support (since the whole, &quot;solars and lunars stole the power of the incarnae&quot; line doesn't really make sense given 3e's view on &quot;Anathema = Exalt doomed to go insane&quot;). The Maidens ignore prayers or randomly answer them in their typical inscrutable capriciousness, so any worship they receive or fail to receive is meaningless.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I certainly agree that the Dragons dont pay much attention to their chosen that much, I'm not sure its because they dont necessarily dont care from them. From what the Corebook implies, I think it's safe to say that all the dragons are currently dormant, not just 2 but of them this time. So them not acting is more from that since it definitely mentioned that they used to be more active before, though what exactly being asleep to them might mean is probably something not normal, with how apparently their very being is said to be mixing in with Creation. In 3e in the relationship between the patrons and their Exalted is certainly more closer then before, with all of them taking a personal interest in choosing them, thus making the Chosen actually Chosen. The Dragons are the really the only ones that not though I hesitate to say that Dragon-blooded Exaltation is totally automatic and that it doesnt carry the will of their patrons in some inscrutable way. The Elements after all contain as much emotional connotations as plain physical. And frankly after seeing the details on the Pilgrimage to Caul it certainly is more spiritual then I expected then just biological","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":3742,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","user":"Iceblade44","date":". 06-17-2019, 03:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T07:24:16-07:00","id":1320268,"title":"","content":"Okay, I\u2019ve had a number of other concerns to attend to over the last several days (and indeed, over the entire period since the book was made available), but right now my intention is to have the thing finished by the end of the week.<br /> <br /> So for all that the Immaculate Texts lie at the heart of the Philosophy, and thus have been frequently referenced throughout the history of the game, I feel as though it\u2019s often been a bit vague on what the actual substance of them is and how they convey the tenets of the Philosophy. That\u2019s something I found to be even harder to pin down when it included a premise of Texts coming from the First Age and being continuously added, even if it was a premise I embraced. I find the rendering here to clear up a lot of that confusion, particularly with the idea that the Texts are meant to provide narratives explaining and validating every social role within the Perfected Hierarchy, and I quite like the image of these narratives being supposed statements of the Immaculate Dragons or those who witnessed them. I find these details to provide an excellent framework for imagining some passage that might be quoted by characters in play, as well as ideas for the format of a monk\u2019s preaching; if they should see people seemingly acting in a manner unbecoming of their station, and citing scripture that repudiates their actions. I\u2019m also liking this added dimension to them as promoting the foreign and domestic policies of the Realm (especially as it might pertain to the future governed by a more devout House such as Cathak or Mnemon).<br /> <br /> Yes, I\u2019m getting the impression from the description of how the canon is expanded that for the most part the Immaculate Texts consist of pre-existing First Age manuals, tracts and essays of high quality that are largely just\u2026 arranged in a manner that creates a coherent image and with some modifications to the context or attribution to place them in the mouths of an Immaculate Dragon. Given the range of subjects the Texts cover, I think there\u2019s a lot of potential in the idea of widely varied translations and readings. I\u2019m also interested in the martial arts being contextualized as a valid method for spreading the faith and proving points of interpretation, as well as this forming a basis for duels to resolve doctrinal differences between monks.<br /> <br /> Now, the matter of the Immaculate view of history was a prominent part of the preview given with What Fire Has Wrought, as well as a bit of discourse before that. So the subject of how there are different narratives for the populace at large and the more thoroughly educated monks has already been talked to death; to reiterate my stance, it\u2019s not the first thing that would come to me, but I have no strong issues, and it does close off a few possible lines of contradiction. I still find it to be helped a lot by the point that the Philosophy has been constructed to require little repression to pass for true history, and the most fraudulent elements of it are things that are kind of esoteric mysteries anyway. I also like it being introduced with the subject of the Order being a major concentrated force of historical scholarship, both for characterising the organisation and creating archives that can have useful information, but no small amount of risk.<br /> <br /> Oooh, the organisation section gets off to a very nice start; the contrast between the deep investment in the Realm itself with the missionaries transmitted with minimal support beyond the administrative reach, the image of what common monks do in the Blessed Isle\u2019s minor villages on annual rounds in combination with their more sinister cultural police elements.<br /> <br /> Just from an immediate overview of the organisational structure and accompanying sidebar, I find that a few revisions have been made to the Order\u2019s inner workings. There\u2019s a clearer sense of how it\u2019s distributed around the Blessed Isle, and what it does in its holdings, things that I often found a bit ambiguous before. I rather appreciate this clear hierarchy of temples to missions to Directional missions to the Palace Sublime, the direct layout of the duties and activities of each and how resources and information flows up and down the ladder. I especially like the description of how temples interact with their satellite communities, the manner in which they serve as hubs of peasant spiritual activity and can foment personal and financial relationships with the monks; plus the way that they add one last point of character to the Blessed Isle\u2019s culture, with a form of universal (if religiously managed) basic education. I also find monasteries to be interesting in the ways that they\u2019re distinct from what I understand of most real life forms of monasticism that both fits them within the Order\u2019s theology (they don\u2019t exactly have conceptions of contemplating enlightenment or divinity, or praying for humanity separate from worldly distractions, while the scriptural focus gives them a large body of work to study in isolation), and provides incentives for outsiders to seek them out rather than leave them to their seclusion. The matter of Exalted monks retreating to communities dedicated to study of the Elemental Dragons for the sake of cultivating their Essence is particularly inviting, although I also like all of the little things that might compel a monk to join such a community (especially refuge from enemies).<br /> <br /> And for the last of the distinctions in practices are the itinerant monks who do double duty as providing services for communities that are beyond the reach of temples (whether according to an assigned route or following their own wishes) and being secretive trouble shooters. This is about the same as it ever was, and a nice way to both extend the Order\u2019s presence to smaller and more isolated communities and just add the interesting character of the lone monk who must act on their own initiative and conscience rather than being a functionary of a local temple. I honestly find it a bit cute how it makes a callback to Second Edition formalizing the role of spies and assassins into a dedicated order by making the term \u201ccelestially guided\u201d into a colloquialism or euphemism for those who are assigned those duties, at the same time as I have a preference for it being a more versatile, ad hoc practice whose command structure is more distributed. I also like how the itinerants are ideally suited to the function of census taking for the Order as a whole, adding to its sense of sinister omnipresence.<br /> <br /> Yes, when I was reading about the All-Seeing Eye, it made me think about various fictional depictions of the ur-authoritarian secret police, especially in properties such as Star Trek; the kind who are supposed to know what ever citizen has for breakfast each morning. I was already into the idea that the Immaculate Order had that role more (even if the Eye does have domestic intelligence purposes), and that feeling is sharpened by both having seen the restructured Eye, which is less suited to or interested in such minutiae, and getting a look at these instruments that the Order would have for acquiring such intel. I\u2019m also interested in the idea that the Order might not be so severe with such lapses; that they can become aware that people have fallen into unsanctioned practices, but that their first instinct might be to summon such to the temples to be reprimanded and instructed before going to anything like disappearing them. I\u2019ll see what more the chapter has to say on the subject.<br /> <br /> The Mouth of Peace appears to have received a major overhaul; I do not believe that prior Editions stated that the role was definitively for women, and there\u2019s a much stronger inspiration drawn from the Dalai Lama (although I feel some resonance with Arha from Earthsea, albeit for almost entirely superficial reasons). That\u2019s not just in the basic concept of their spiritual leader being a reincarnation who needs to be identified from a young age (I like how they have Sidereal prognostication to give that some precision, but still need to backpedal in the event that the selection doesn\u2019t even Exalt), but in the manner in which the question of succession/reincarnation has become politicized. Having her be concerned with the possibility that her absence might bring about civil war and trying to find a procedural argument for directly appointing the next in line is both a nice way of incorporating the position\u2019s traditional role of counselling against war and giving the Order its own concern looking forward into a timeline for games. Glancing around a bit, it would also appear that the positions of Paragons of the Immaculate Dragons have also been eliminated, so it seems to drop the dichotomy of the Mouth of Peace\u2019s involvement in political affairs being a necessarily sullying sacrifice and affords her a greater spiritual significance; maybe I was just annoyed at how Second Edition really hammered it in, but I prefer this alternative introduction as more of a figurehead position, in combination with being the Philosophy\u2019s highest representative to secular institutions of power. It would appear that the angle of being virtually a puppet of Chejop Kejak has been dropped altogether, which was honestly something that I had begun developing reservations about, and I think there\u2019s something more compelling in this dynamic in which she had free will but little power to contest the Sidereal\u2019s policies, while the question of succession is leaving her with one last opportunity to make a mark. I also think that it makes for a stronger and more distinct drive than there being tension between the Mouth and her former House over the administrative distribution of the Order.<br /> <br /> Speaking of the absence of the Paragons (an element that I never entirely cared for), we get what appears to be the most significant alteration to and expansion of the Order in the Breaths of the Immaculate Dragons. I\u2019m always into subjects of administrative divisions and clearly stated institutional functions, so I\u2019m all for something like this to both really hammer in what the Order might do on a day to day and larger policy making basis, and to give some texture for its plans and actions that can be engaged with in play. I also like the idea that administration of the Order is distributed along separate axes of location and purpose, particularly for the complexities that this adds to its internal hierarchies. Indeed, something that looks very interesting to me is the sense that, with the removal of the Paragons and the increased emphasis on the Mouth of Peace as a figure of public relations, that the leadership of the Immaculate Order is a bit more decentralized; even if temples have to report upwards, there\u2019s not a strong commanding figure at the top of the pyramid, while it seems like the people who govern and implement the Order\u2019s social projects are more dispersed and flexible (even if they have headquarters to give some coordination). I think it gives a capacity to represent Immaculate organs that are interacted with in play more autonomy and strength, at the same time as there\u2019s a certain expendability to them. It\u2019s a structure that I find very interesting in making the Order feel more like a system that transcends any of its individual members; it\u2019s a bit reminiscent to me of how the book Northern Lights held that the Catholic Church had eliminated the Papacy centuries ago, and was currently governed by a tangle of courts, colleges, and councils.<br /> <br /> As for the Breaths themselves, the first couple of them are a bit reminiscent of the portfolios of the Paragons, but not in any distracting way that prevents me from appreciating what they add to the activities of the Order and the Blessed Isle as a whole. From the Breath of Sextes Jylis, I\u2019m beginning to suspect that a few changes have been made to the process of entering the Order, and I find something interesting in the manner in which they\u2019re presented as internally policing; I also like the idea that one of the Order\u2019s missions is to provide environmental sustainability, since it touches on questions I\u2019ve had concerning things like forestry and depletion in the Realm. The construction focus of the Breath of Pasiap seems the most on the nose, but I find it enriched a bit with the idea that they sometimes contract their services out, and being the ones in charge of the Order\u2019s financial management is a particularly intriguing combination (if construction would end up as one of the largest sources of costs and income); I like that the Order has a priority in such funding of not giving away to the laity precisely how wealthy the organisation as a whole is. Having the Breath of Mela as a dedicated division for combat training makes sense with the higher emphasis being placed on monks employing martial arts for instructive purposes, as well as how some of them engage in crusade (much more than old references to the idea that engaging in military ventures was something far off and theoretical), while I also think it effectively reins some of that sentiment in with the Breath having the smallest staff owing to most monks not wanting to learn much combat technique. When I started reading the Breath of Hesiesh, I was worried that it was going to be kind of dull and non-descript, but that was relieved when the general mandate of \u201cwhatever is not covered by procedure\u201d was supplemented on a focus on handling the weird and the esoteric, and particularly with the commensurate tendency to draw in the more directionless or unusual members of the Order; I just like the idea that the monks who turn up to handle something particularly inauspicious might be rather quirky, or that the other Breaths in crisis might be supplemented with staff they\u2019re not entirely sure they want. And I\u2019d find the Breath of Daana\u2019d being focused on community development and well-being to be a good reminded of how deeply involved the Order is under any circumstances, but think it\u2019s an especially good fit with the idea that they\u2019re liable to be the first to see unacceptable activity and the ones most conflicted about reporting it.<br /> <br /> Between the Breaths and the things like temples and monasteries, I not only find this to be a strong expansion on the Immaculate Order, but another very good example for other kinds of comprehensive institution that can be added to a game, particularly ones that characters might develop in play.<br /> <br /> Last for the time being are subjects concerning the Sidereals. First the sidebar concerned with the Wyld Hunt; I believe that in the past, astrology to locate Anathema was more of the All-Seeing Eye\u2019s remit, but I prefer it being consolidated with the Order since they\u2019re the ones who manage the Hunt and have the Sidereals. I like the way that it makes use of the altered presence of such thaumaturgy in the setting to both make a point about the Order having a more accommodating attitude to the development of such folk magic and being part of a general scheme to provide a plausible excuse for the discoveries that Sidereal magic can yield. I also quite like how it references not only the proper Wyld Hunt but several of these other components of the Order that have been introduced to create an impression that a discovered Anathema has a widely distributed net that can be closed around them; it\u2019s a more complex structure than just finding them with omniscient magic, and I think that it can create for more tense and dramatic sessions and storylines in which one needs to identify and evade a network of observers and informants that will be on the lookout and both work to delay you and transmit your presence up the line. There can be a prelude of activities undertaken before the true and fully furnished Wyld Hunt attempts to descend upon you in overwhelming force. It\u2019s a good perspective on the resources that the Realm was able to mobilize to ensure that its Hunts were the best in the business.<br /> <br /> Finally there\u2019s the focus on the Sidereals themselves. Like many other things brought over, this is a nice, lean description; the focus is on the presence of those Exalted within and their contributions to the Order, rather than giving a separate history lesson on them. I find the emphasis here to be less on the Sidereals as the secret masters of the Order; it feels to me more like they employ their capabilities more to supplement its initiatives and ensure it doesn\u2019t stray from a beneficial line, as well as being the staging ground for Sidereal agents to work towards preserving the Realm as a whole. I\u2019m personally gratified by this being a point in the game\u2019s line in which it can come out and directly state that, until the resurgence of the Solars, the Fellowship was basically united in purpose (I\u2019m guessing that the outliers would largely amount to ronin and those in league with Raken Thulio), and it\u2019s only with recent events that the Gold Faction has become a thing again. I think there\u2019s a subtle adjustment of tone to the matter of Chejop\u2019s dwindling life, not so much impending disaster as a punctuation of the fact that the larger Bronze Faction has been losing some of its biggest players; the line given to followers knowing the history of successfully keeping Solars in check through effective application of force makes me think there\u2019s an eye towards the idea of Sidereals planning around Chejop\u2019s redundancy. And I quite like how it finishes off on the point that a major source of their current difficulty is in fact handling the suddenly very fractious and self-involved Dragon Blooded, since it maintains a focus on the significance of conflicts internal to the Realm and its reigning Exalted and the agency afforded to such. I find the line on Chejop trying to shore things up while preparing to harness the considerable resources of the Immaculate Order (which have just been effectively outlined for us) to be rather exciting; it creates an anticipation for the Sidereals making themselves involved in the conflict within the Realm in a big way, with a massive number of agents and adherents and assets, a powerful force along an axis separate from the usual politics of the Scarlet Dynasty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T12:56:20-07:00","id":1320335,"title":"","content":"Rather than dormant, the dragons could be busy keeping the metaphysical balance of the elements through Creation, and don\u2019t have time for geopolitics and concerns about individual human lives, even their Chosen.<br /> <br /> This option is particularly of use if you want to open the door for environmental cataclysm based on something happening to one or more of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T14:07:16-07:00","id":1320346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320335#post1320335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather than dormant, the dragons could be busy keeping the metaphysical balance of the elements through Creation, and don\u2019t have time for geopolitics and concerns about individual human lives, even their Chosen.<br /> <br /> This option is particularly of use if you want to open the door for environmental cataclysm based on something happening to one or more of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not both<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Seriously though I'm not against having the dragons being major forces in keeping the world stable and that they can do so just by their nature. Even when asleep<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T14:41:21-07:00","id":1320351,"title":"","content":"It's not a game about the Elemental Dragons, it's about the Dragon-Blooded and the other Chosen. Whatever explanation you need to make sure that attention is firmly fixed on the actual protagonists of the game instead of their patrons (like the suggestions provided for the Incarnae) should suffice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Violet Green, Freelancer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23657-violetdreamer","name":"VioletDreamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23657_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"163"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T20:17:14-07:00","id":1320386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>VioletDreamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320351#post1320351\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a game about the Elemental Dragons, it's about the Dragon-Blooded and the other Chosen. Whatever explanation you need to make sure that attention is firmly fixed on the actual protagonists of the game instead of their patrons (like the suggestions provided for the Incarnae) should suffice.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> With the DBs constantly harping about being the Chosen of the Dragons(or by the Dragons), their lack of true interaction with them sort of stings. 2e had enough of DBs being cosmic losers viewed as uncouth pawns or inconsequential vermin by every being of sufficient power. 3e done a good job redeeming that aspect, but i'd still like the Dragons to stop being ciphers. UCS has been locked in his celestial man-cave for centuries and he's still got enough personality to be a complex character, by extension informing and influencing the actions of his Chosen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-23-2019, 11:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T21:50:11-07:00","id":1320403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>VioletDreamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320351#post1320351\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a game about the Elemental Dragons, it's about the Dragon-Blooded and the other Chosen. Whatever explanation you need to make sure that attention is firmly fixed on the actual protagonists of the game instead of their patrons (like the suggestions provided for the Incarnae) should suffice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Might want to tell the Immaculates that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T21:57:50-07:00","id":1320405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320386#post1320386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> With the DBs constantly harping about being the Chosen of the Dragons(or by the Dragons), their lack of true interaction with them sort of stings. 2e had enough of DBs being cosmic losers viewed as uncouth pawns or inconsequential vermin by every being of sufficient power. 3e done a good job redeeming that aspect, but i'd still like the Dragons to stop being ciphers. UCS has been locked in his celestial man-cave for centuries and he's still got enough personality to be a complex character, by extension informing and influencing the actions of his Chosen.</div> </div> </div> </div>My overly-parellel drawing mind would suggest that a) the Dragons and UCS both being mostly distant actors works nicely to have DBs and Solars be foils b) UCS isn't...that fleshed out in 3e lore.Nor do I expect him to be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-23T23:03:34-07:00","id":1320415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320405#post1320405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My overly-parellel drawing mind would suggest that a) the Dragons and UCS both being mostly distant actors works nicely to have DBs and Solars be foils b) UCS isn't...that fleshed out in 3e lore.Nor do I expect him to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm fine with keeping them as distant actors. Making the Exalted the stars of the show. On a side point though I also don't want them to not be parts of the setting that you cant interact with. This isnt for every game, but I'm not against having characters meeting Sol, talking and debate with him, possibly see him in some sort of action depending on where my players and dice falls. This would basically be the same with the Dragons. What i want to do is tell an interesting story, so one thing that I dislike is being presented with a possible path and element a story could have, but then be told that I cant use it. Mind I'm not wanting something on the level of Glories or even QC blocks, I just see the Incarna though as any other characters in the setting like Mnemon. Now Mnemon is far more interactible, having far more compelling motives to interact with the plot, and less chance of overshadowing PCs. That's why she got a QC and more integrated lore. I dont want that for the Incarnae and Dragons, I just want something more for players to potentially interact with, if it ever goes down that way. Or that's my thoughts anyhow. I dont want Incarnae as protags, just story characters would be fine for me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T05:38:27-07:00","id":1320466,"title":"","content":"My biggest problem is that thee Elemental Dragons display no personality outside the traits the Immaculates ascribe to them, which makes their inclusion in the setting seem superfluous. Aside from like three sentences in <i>Compass: Yu-Shan</i>, they exhibit no action outside of their task of shepherding Creation's essence, which makes their participation in the Divine Revolution seem either like their programming concluded the Primordials had to go(like some kind of dystopian computer) or Gaia actually had to give them directions to join the conflict.<br /> <br /> We know what Sol Invictus is, he is the King of Heaven, once horrified and ashamed of his Chosen but has now worked up the courage to finally turn his face back to Creation. He excels at whatever task he is put to, but every flaw of his is a thousand times more dire because the fate of Heaven rests on his shoulders. As with Sol, so to with the Solar's regarding Creation. It humanizes him and makes him seem reachable by the PCs<br /> <br /> Luna is the ever changing god-devil-beast, she is so chaotic and protean that she shouldn't even be able to function, and yet she does.<br /> <br /> The Maidens are stock archetypes that mask a five-faced, ruthless chessmaster.<br /> <br /> The Dragons are a set piece that are never elaborated upon or explained as individuals. Ebby can crush them like gnats apparently. The Dragon-Blooded care about them, but the feeling doesn't seem mutual so the DBs come out looking like chumps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-24-2019, 08:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T05:59:39-07:00","id":1320468,"title":"","content":"Yeah, it would make sense to have short descriptions in, say, a section on the Elemental Poles, as that's where they live. Just a bit of roleplaying advice for their personalities or something. What their court is like. Etc.<br /> <br /> It's not particularly important; no more so than, say, some third circle demons. It's not like visiting the Elemental Poles is something many people do in their games. But it's not something you can't do.<br /> <br /> Saying this, they are avoiding doing much even on the Incarnae this edition, and I can understand why. I don't think it needs to be declared forbidden, the way they have the escape of the Yozis, but nor is it very pressing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T06:08:12-07:00","id":1320469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320386#post1320386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> With the DBs constantly harping about being the Chosen of the Dragons(or by the Dragons), their lack of true interaction with them sort of stings. 2e had enough of DBs being cosmic losers viewed as uncouth pawns or inconsequential vermin by every being of sufficient power. 3e done a good job redeeming that aspect, but i'd still like the Dragons to stop being ciphers. UCS has been locked in his celestial man-cave for centuries and he's still got enough personality to be a complex character, by extension informing and influencing the actions of his Chosen.</div> </div> </div> </div>In terms of their relationship with their Chosen, it's fine; the Dragonblood instead venerate what are basically 5 made-up deities that share the names of the Dragons. But despite being fake, it means a lot to them (and there are ancient elements of more genuine Dragon-worship preserved).<br /> <br /> And, frankly, the Immaculate Philosophy is way more interesting than the Solars' relationship with the Unconquered Sun. <br /> <br /> To me, the point of having less irrelevant Elemental Dragons isn't anything at all about the Dragonblood (unless you want a plot where the PCs discover the Catholic Church has covered up the truth about Jesus... Sorry, the Immaculate Philosophy has covered up the truth about the Dragons).<br /> <br /> It's rather about elemental courts and elemental disasters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T09:01:35-07:00","id":1320506,"title":"","content":"The Dragon's had almost as much about them written in 1e I think as the Sun. I kind of wonder if the Incarna having gotten so much expanded on them in 2e and the Dragon's relegated to the oft ignored 3CD status of that edition is skewing some of this.<br /> <br /> Frankly I kind of think their brief take in GoD was about what was needed. They ran the turning of the seasons, made their Chosen and established the Terrestrial Bureaceacy. Lesser elemebral beings approach them in form because they are the platonic expressions of their element. <br /> <br /> I just don't see what elaborating in beings meant to be distant does when they're well, distant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T11:07:58-07:00","id":1320535,"title":"","content":"I suppose the question is: Do the Dragons actually care about the Dragon-Blooded and are the DB's truly &quot;Chosen&quot; or just a bunch of lucky inbred demigods. I realize the potential for terrestrial exaltation lies in Dragon-Blooded heritage, but do the Dragons actually have any effect on whether or not one of those with the dragon's heritage exalts. Past editions had nearly every other Exalt type looking down on them for receiving exaltation by &quot;mere accident of birth.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T12:54:19-07:00","id":1320560,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320535#post1320535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I suppose the question is: Do the Dragons actually care about the Dragon-Blooded and are the DB's truly &quot;Chosen&quot; or just a bunch of lucky inbred demigods. I realize the potential for terrestrial exaltation lies in Dragon-Blooded heritage, but do the Dragons actually have any effect on whether or not one of those with the dragon's heritage exalts. Past editions had nearly every other Exalt type looking down on them for receiving exaltation by &quot;mere accident of birth.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I would just go for the former and ignore the latter, since its so dismissive that its dumb, which I'm thankful that 3e is going that direction. If I were one to lay down the feelings of the dragons, I would probably portray them as reverent ancestors, which they basically all. The source of Terrestrial Exaltation and patriarchs to all Dragon-blooded lines. The Dragon-Blooded are Chosen, that I would have indisputable. Though what &quot;Chosen&quot; means to the Dragons doesn't mean it would have to be same as the rest, that is recognizing the &quot;Dragon-Blooded&quot; as &quot;family&quot; rather then &quot;worthy&quot; though that might mean the same thing in the eyes of the Dragons. Or thats how I would do it, in any case I just don't want 3e say that apathy is actually interesting in this type of stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T13:12:38-07:00","id":1320564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320506#post1320506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Frankly I kind of think their brief take in GoD was about what was needed. They ran the turning of the seasons, made their Chosen and established the Terrestrial Bureaceacy. Lesser elemebral beings approach them in form because they are the platonic expressions of their element.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Though a problem now is that most of the stuff you listed no longer works, with Elementals no longer being part of the Terrestrial Sphere outside of lesser dragons or the Bureaucracy being in full cooperation with Yu Shan and the Dragons being mentioned no where it that. As well I don't think they no longer have control of the Seasons, I would think Yu Shan would still keep that as a Bureau. Also one thing I didn't like about the GoD Dragons that while they were displayed as mighty, they were kinda portrayed as being under the Incarna or at least only their to serve their interests and Gaia's. Like they had no independence. They didn't set up the Terrestrial Bureaucracy for their own benefit, they did it because Gaia told them to and made it a Bureaucracy so it wouldn't burden the Incarna. Thats literally how it was written in the text, and I'm not so fond in sticking with that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T18:01:36-07:00","id":1320629,"title":"","content":"<br /> Almost everything in the chapter up until now has been concerned with how the Immaculate Order do, and I find it to have accomplished that well, so it makes sense to now apply the same clarity to defining specifically what the Order does. I find it very effective to begin with the matter of how places where the Order holds sway (I like it being non-descript here as a reminder that there\u2019s a bit more reach than just the Blessed Isle) they preside over every major event of a person\u2019s lifetime; naming (implicitly still having that practice in which children can go years without a name), building layouts (very important in small communities to ensure they don\u2019t infringe upon or otherwise disrupt each other), funerals (I find it interesting how the Philosophy doesn\u2019t have a standard funeral rite, and so defers to whatever will be pleasing to the departed soul as defined by local custom). It\u2019s nice to see a point of consistency brought in with the subject of marriage; it often felt weird to me that monks didn\u2019t preside over Dynastic marriage but did for everybody else, so I like how their involvement here now consists of advising on a good time to marry rather than officiating. I think it really sells the ubiquity of the Order to refer to their place in the lives of everybody from the lowliest peasant to the House matriarch, and what those people respectively get from the arrangement. But the most important thing that I\u2019m getting from this is that it\u2019s putting the Order\u2019s role as cultural police front and centre; that it views society as requiring constant vigilance even when seemingly compliant to ensure that the Perfected Hierarchy is adhered to. I like how that point of the group perceiving itself as needing to stand apart in order to fulfil that role places their modelling their entire clergy off of monasticism into context.<br /> <br /> The section on the Order\u2019s political activities reiterates the observation I made about how it presents a more nebulous and decentralized face, which matches nicely with how it\u2019s described as not engaging in games of power and wealth. It\u2019s an interesting contrast to a lot of real life hierarchical religious organisations, in which clerics might seek or need temporal power and sources of obligated income, to have the idea that most of what they have arises from donatives. Mind, in contrast with a lot of real religious organisations that I\u2019m familiar with (primarily Christian), the Order <i>does</i> have a very close and cooperative tie with the centralised government and the monarchy; seeking land and business is probably less necessary when you can rely on the Empress to provide a lot of your money. As for the manner in which it does act politically, I especially like the idea that they publish literature to promote ideas on appropriate social conduct, as well as the way that they form networks of connections as an instrument to deal in rewards or punishments as appropriate for those around them in a more subtle manner. I think that historically, censure from religious authorities can be a bit of a dubious means of leveraging secular authorities, but I can buy that the Order makes itself popular enough among the majority that people will follow suit when monks scorn impious officials. And I\u2019m quite intrigued by the idea that their missionaries and frontier bases take a very severe hand against the surrounding communities when they can; I imagine that the presence of Exalted amongst them helps out a lot in getting away with taking such a subversive role when abroad, in contrast with a lot of similar situations in reality where the missionaries tend to keep their heads down when adrift in a possibly hostile world. I thought before that Immaculate proselytizing constituted the most overt form of Realm cultural enforcement over imperial possessions, and I like the way it\u2019s presented here, that the lack of Immaculate adherence is considered the basis for barbarianism more than forms of dress, language or custom, and thus obligates monks (and maybe other supporters) to oppose and sabotage such improper practice.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m presuming that the picture is of a monk advising officials; I like the colouring and brightness of the whole thing, particularly in the women\u2019s hair, and the framing does a good job of giving the monk a central and imposing presence that is complemented by her softer and more accommodating expression and posture. I will note how she should technically be bald, though.<br /> <br /> Mind, for all of the things that have given the Immaculate Order a softer air, I think it\u2019s good to bring things on point with the way that their ultimate objective is phrased: to <i>force</i> the societies of Creation into adherence with its vision. That conviction, the idea that they employ social engineering techniques to enforce it, attributing every misfortune or unhappiness with deviation from the Perfected Hierarchy\u2026 the biggest thing that I see in this are the sources of contention with non-Realm Immaculates. Not to mention possibilities that might arise should Mnemon have her way. That goal, that methodology, and the focus on the matter of managing information, that\u2019s really bringing the Dai Li elements being added or emphasized in the Order to the forefront, and I am <b>here</b> for it.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s an interesting idea, that the Order would be able to fold its role of censorship into the fact that they provide basic education to so many people; they cultivate the image of themselves as the only credible judges and distributors of permissible knowledge, and all in a form that maintains their public good will. The Order might restrict what literature it provides, but it\u2019s still an organised endeavour towards providing literature to people at large (whom they also work to ensure are literate) in a world where they might otherwise have no such access. It\u2019s a compelling image of a more subtle method by which the Realm maintains control, how the Sidereal provided support of the Order benefitted the power base of the Scarlet Empress, even if it lies atop a system in which they\u2019ll engage in very public reprimands and destruction of unsanctioned material to reinforce their legitimacy and keep people in line. Having been told that the Realm produces literature for widespread consumption, I\u2019d like to know what some of the substance of such might be, and the idea that monks commonly appear as a motif or device for the revelation of truth serves that purpose as well as elaborating on the general perception of the Order; I particularly like the way that this perception constitutes a more benign take on the monk in the rule of observer and inquisitor. However, for all that I\u2019ve been referring to them as secret and/or cultural police, the fact does remain that they\u2019re an independent organisation, and I think that point is effectively made by the fact that the group uses its own discretion in deciding what to do with the information it gathers, whether that be handing it to the authorities, using it as a means of developing its power networks, or keeping a lid on it and trying to bring wayward souls back into upright conduct.<br /> <br /> I can see why the sidebar on the Philosophy\u2019s aniconism would introduce an idea that the Order can employ a more flexible, even variable standard for what is acceptable; even if they ensure more widespread literacy, I can take the point raised by other people in the past that such a strict enforcement makes a lot of basic commerce impractical. If the purpose of aniconism is to discourage idolatry, it makes sense that the monks would preferably exert a judgement on what representative images actually cross that line, and reserve censure for those that do. I still like the idea that the baseline for art and decoration on the Blessed Isle prefers abstraction and geometry, and the phrasing around the permission for depicting Elemental and Immaculate Dragons suggests to me that it\u2019s a more recent allowance, adding to the feeling that the Realm\u2019s society has been an organic thing that differs over time. I neglected to bring it up before, but I do notice how there\u2019s no inclusion whatsoever of a line to the effect of the Realm\u2019s society having been more or less the same across its history, which honestly never really reflected how it was described even back then.<br /> <br /> Now, one of the things I\u2019ve been most intrigued by before now, the Order\u2019s complicated relationship with social upheaval. One thing that I like here is acknowledgement that the Order itself can be a contributor or victim of disorder, offsetting other language that might create an impression of uniform integrity to the monks. I also think it\u2019s good to set things in perspective; the Order might have the novel function of facilitating uprisings, but we\u2019re also given a clear reminder that it is just as if not more often right there alongside the forces suppressing them. I see a little nod to practices of the Medieval Catholic Church in the line about the Order specifically <i>not</i> denying the authority of objectionable secular authorities (part of the point of excommunication of princes was that it was supposed to be a statement that people were no longer subject to them, and were free, even obligated, to overthrow them\u2026 even if that basically never happened), while I find the alternative in their own particular idiom to be quite evocative; the escalating series of censure that starts at monks publicly shunning, that rises through organising protest and arrives at unleashing the martial arts should the authorities try to suppress with force. It <i>is</i> interesting how the Empress, and apparently even the current Great Houses, took a fairly even hand in responding to such risings, with the allowance of some destruction to let off some steam before reprisals with surgical focus on the leadership while the movement at large is allowed to resume their normal lives and maybe even accomplish some things in the form of destroyed tax records and grievances against local officials resulting in their replacement; I find it particularly cute how rebel monks are publicly reprimanded in an inconsequential manner since they probably had actual approval from their superiors (it reminds me of the abbot from 36th Chamber of Shaolin). I will however offer one criticism of this section in that large sections of it do seem to be repeating verbatim things described earlier in the book, especially the account of Seven Rivers and the Unbroken Rushes Rebellion; this looks like it could have done with some tighter editing (maybe the fully released version went and did that?).<br /> <br /> As for the part directly stating and elaborating upon the tenet in the Realm\u2019s Immaculate Philosophy that the Dragon Blooded are the rightful rulers of all Creation\u2026 it divides me a little bit. To be sure, there\u2019s some very interesting stuff in there, at the core of which is the distinct conceit that being Exalted is not merely a signifier of enlightenment through multiple lifetimes of correct action, but a reward for it that entails freedom to a level of privilege and indulgence that no mortal under Immaculate tenets is afforded. Sure, you still apparently get those more stoic Terrestrials who opt to conduct themselves with a bit more austerity, but the idea that the Order upholds that a measure of decadence is the earned right of those incarnated from many cycles of wisdom and adherence\u2026 it\u2019s not just a very peculiar religious idea and the most overt element of the Philosophy being constructed with an eye towards enticing and empowering the Dragon Blooded, but it does put some of House Mnemon into perspective; how their piety is not at odds with them drawing upon a wide range of pleasure and artistic expressions. I can imagine some rather distinct theology reconciling the disparate expectations and allowances of the Philosophy. And it certainly creates an interesting framework for the upbringing of Dynastic children, and how religious tutelage may include preparation for enjoying the grandeur and power that comes with Exaltation.<br /> <br /> My main reservation is about some of the particulars of how it describes the Exalted experience and relationships with mortals, buuttt\u2026 I can reconcile that partially with an idea that this does not constitute a 100% literal description of how things work as much as the general standards that the Order projects (and permits), and partially by getting over it a bit and taking the point that yes, maybe I could have more of an eye to how the powers and resources provided to the Exalted of the Dynasty might encourage them towards loftier goals and a perspective that they\u2019re above the concerns of mortal life. Kind of a \u201ccalling for Diet Coke and one appears in your hand\u201d situation.<br /> <br /> Hmmm\u2026 leave it here for now, and then finish the chapter tomorrow. Maybe get a start on the next one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T18:56:17-07:00","id":1320637,"title":"","content":"I often wonder what Immaculate missionaries are like. Probably well received in areas where the local deities are abusive but less so in other places. Taken as an objective whole, the Immaculate Faith lacks broad spectrum appeal outside of a society already indoctrinated to believe in its precepts(like the Realm and its more servile satrapies). I suspect the Immaculates omit of theology a lot depending on where they're doing missionary work, only to enforce the scriptures more strictly once the populace is cowed by an imperial garrison. The promise of DB privilege in the next life(false though it may be) probably provides incentive for the more cynical aristocratic converts while pragmatic concerns such as regular weather and harvests and the occasional monastic charity probably attract agrarian and urban working class types. Warrior cultures probably have their champions humiliated by immaculate monks and have converts who want to side with the winners.<br /> <br /> With Civil War being the likeliest outcome and Chejak soon to croak, I like the idea of the Immaculate Faith splintering into more organic sects, as opposed to the authoritarian social control mechanism it is now (though I also like the idea of some of those sects being worse than the orthodoxy, give me Immaculate Senpou Temple!) With the Mouth of Peace soon to die, a very chaotic interregnum period is gonna pop up. Lots of competing Mouth of Peace candidates I suspect. More heretical worship of the Dragons, like Sextes Jylis fertility cults and Mela warrior cults (also Antithesis Heretics), is very appealing as a setting aspect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-24-2019, 10:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T20:36:35-07:00","id":1320649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iceblade44</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320564#post1320564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Though a problem now is that most of the stuff you listed no longer works, with Elementals no longer being part of the Terrestrial Sphere outside of lesser dragons or the Bureaucracy being in full cooperation with Yu Shan and the Dragons being mentioned no where it that. As well I don't think they no longer have control of the Seasons, I would think Yu Shan would still keep that as a Bureau. Also one thing I didn't like about the GoD Dragons that while they were displayed as mighty, they were kinda portrayed as being under the Incarna or at least only their to serve their interests and Gaia's. Like they had no independence. They didn't set up the Terrestrial Bureaucracy for their own benefit, they did it because Gaia told them to and made it a Bureaucracy so it wouldn't burden the Incarna. Thats literally how it was written in the text, and I'm not so fond in sticking with that.</div> </div> </div> </div>While there's a lot ot be improved on, I htink that it's notable a lot did change from there too on the depictions of the Incarna and Gaia from there to 3e as well. Gaia is notably just less active in general in Creation, and Autochthon toned down a shitload. I think that there's room to improve on teh Dragons ab it, but I think that htis need for them to have some say or be present much in the Dragon-Blooded stuff, when a lot of their Exaltation is so fundementally not built on the same themes fo the Celestials, seems kind of looking for problems that aren't there, or taking stuff that was expanded elsewhere (again, probablyb adly) and saying that htey need similar expansion, even if the stuff on the Incarna itself is being toned down.<br /> <br /> I dunno, I just htink that a lot of the setting is kind of at this point built on the inscrutibility of the Five Elemental Dragons also. There's a bit of &quot;the setting is wrong&quot; that comes from looking for more that irks me on a level I guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-24T21:24:27-07:00","id":1320655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320649#post1320649\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> While there's a lot ot be improved on, I htink that it's notable a lot did change from there too on the depictions of the Incarna and Gaia from there to 3e as well. Gaia is notably just less active in general in Creation, and Autochthon toned down a shitload. I think that there's room to improve on teh Dragons ab it, but I think that htis need for them to have some say or be present much in the Dragon-Blooded stuff, when a lot of their Exaltation is so fundementally not built on the same themes fo the Celestials, seems kind of looking for problems that aren't there, or taking stuff that was expanded elsewhere (again, probablyb adly) and saying that htey need similar expansion, even if the stuff on the Incarna itself is being toned down.<br /> <br /> I dunno, I just htink that a lot of the setting is kind of at this point built on the inscrutibility of the Five Elemental Dragons also. There's a bit of &quot;the setting is wrong&quot; that comes from looking for more that irks me on a level I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>oh I agree that I wouldn't want their Exaltation to be given more in depth or to be made more like the Celestials. In my case I just forge it that its because the Dragons aren't Gods thus what Exaltation means for them shouldn't be the same, but works a little bit different to fit more with what they are. Like Exaltations shouldn't be like shards as it was in 2e, all the same but with different flavors, but different miracles that express in some way the Patron's divinity. Or something along those lines. Thats how I see it anyway","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-25T13:01:25-07:00","id":1320819,"title":"","content":"Oh dang, I forgot to comment on the sidebar about slavery. Nothing much to it, as slavery is considered to be little different than any other unfortunate incarnation in the Perfected Hierarchy, with the same standards of \u201cmistreatment warrants an aided revolt\u201d. Actually, there are a few old points that I\u2019m surprised aren\u2019t mentioned here, such as Dragon Blooded being considered the only ones properly enlightened for the responsibility of owning people, or the perspective that enslaving barbarians is seen as an efficient means of facilitating their education in the Philosophy.<br /> <br /> I think that in the past descriptions of the Immaculate Order have placed the experience of those within it first, and the activities afterwards. I find this reversal to be more engaging; playing monks or getting into their background might be a thing, but I feel as though the strongest presence of the Order in games is in the execution of their duties, so that\u2019s the more important info. That and I feel as though the old arrangement left the matter of the Order\u2019s conduct a bit truncated, and attention might have been exhausted by the preceding at that point anyway; in this form, I\u2019m already informed about and invested in the Order, and the subject of monks\u2019 lives is just a nice bonus.<br /> <br /> Anyway, the introduction of the subject is good, the matter of how becoming a monk is a hard lifelong commitment of service, and I particularly like that idea that they view those who enter but can\u2019t hack it are still being instructed in the Perfected Hierarchy.<br /> <br /> So, the subject of entry; nice start in listing all of the sources and purposes that people might find in joining up, with a distinct point of hardly being founded on donated children at all; it would appear that even those who are put on a track to be trained towards monkhood from youth are intended to be voluntary, which might be fitting to how the Order has a much heavier workload than might be typical of monastic organisations. Of the backgrounds, I particularly like how the Order offers a refuge for marginalized and outlawed folks, and think the idea that Dragon Blooded might want an environment to cultivate deeper awareness of their Essence is the strongest insight yet into what motivates Dynastic Exalted towards them.<br /> <br /> A rather significant revision is the process of entry itself; where before, all postulants needed to present themselves at the temple in the heart of the Imperial City and be accepted by the Paragon of Sextes Jylis, here the system for admission is distributed at a much more local level. Quite apart from further emphasis on the decentralized structure, the old system never really made much sense; the Order was always presented as having an enormous number of monks, and the Blessed Isle is huge (not to mention restricting of travel for the lower social strata), the idea that they could maintain their numbers with such a concentrated recruitment system was quite ludicrous (although it\u2019s fine for the Palace Sublime to be considered the only place suitable to assess Dynastic postulants). Even distributed, the substance of actually being a postulant remains\u2026 actually, it\u2019s slightly modified, removing the flagellation by way of kneeling from the prior Editions (especially Second) to focus on the point that they\u2019ll be tested by monks with commands and insults before being directly assessed by the monk in charge. Makes sense, the Immaculate Philosophy isn\u2019t exactly big on corporeal mortification. Oh hey, and for the first time we\u2019re actually given a layout of the process of training once postulants advance to acolytes, as opposed to First Edition\u2019s \u201cit happens\u201d and Second\u2019s \u201cyou\u2019ve got to journey from the Imperial City to the Palace Sublime, and now you\u2019re a monk\u201d.<br /> <br /> I mentioned 36th Chamber of Shaolin before, and I\u2019m finding that even more evident in this training process; the system of academic and martial instruction leading up to tiers of seasonal examination that need to be passed in set number before qualifying as a monk. I kind of like the way that washing out of the training at this stage isn\u2019t necessarily a shameful thing, and can perhaps still carry a certain prestige of having made it even that far and having learned a few things before returning home, not to mention the way that the Order can still have uses for those who aren\u2019t cut out to be full monks. Yess, I like the idea that oblates originate from failed acolytes whose services can still be accepted. I\u2019m guessing that the difference between an acolyte and a monk who has not yet achieved the First Coil is that the latter cannot be dismissed for failure of tests, expected to brush themselves off should they be overcome by those extremely severe and elaborate physical, mental and spiritual trials and get back on the horse. I like it, if for nothing else than giving me pictures of grand training montages, and maybe some formative experiences that monk characters can refer back to. Finally, I like the continuation of a greater sense of how the Order is organised towards its missions in the way that those who achieve the First Coil consult with members of the Breath of Sextes Jylis to actually be given an assignment, since it emphasizes how they\u2019re training towards being an active presence in the Realm rather than just being cloistered. I appreciate the levels of the Coils being described with brevity, and a focus on their functional responsibilities.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s a picture, but\u2026 it\u2019s just a repeat of an earlier one showing a peasant uprising. Indeed, that also accompanied the description of historical risings that I\u2019ve found to be repeated exactly here\u2026<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, the details on monastic naming have been slightly modified, but the basic principle remains the same; entry level comes with being given a minor humbling name, and advancement grants the privilege of electing to go back to one\u2019s original name or ask the archimandrite to confer a new ne focused on presenting one\u2019s nature as a monk. I like to imagine the measure of camaraderie and informality in the idea that monks may call one another by diminutives when their full new names are a bit of a mouthful.<br /> <br /> Not much to say about the lifestyle of the monks, since it mostly remains the same as prior Editions (and probably about what one would expect from their presentation). Humble dress and personal styling, vows of celibacy and poverty, and dietary restrictions, the works. There are a few little elaborations that I like, such as noting how beards shouldn\u2019t be styled, pointing out how there are values to one\u2019s family in going childless, that part of the point of building lavish temples is to reflect well on the donations of the community, and the ways in which monks might make up for shortcomings in their appearance and eating habits. One thing that has my imagination is how monks are permitted to possess nicer, more elaborate robes for ceremonial occasions, largely because I happen to have looked at some pictures of Christian monks and priests wearing decorated vestments, so can see some very compelling images of such.<br /> <br /> Huh, so it would appear that the Immaculate Order regards hermits as an acceptable oddity rather than a fully established practice; I take it that some monks, either from their motives for joining in the first place or how they take to particular qualities of the Texts, find themselves unsatisfied with even monastic life, and feel driven towards complete solitude. I suppose that in the eyes of the Order, even if this constitutes a dereliction of their normal duties it\u2019s not <i>technically</i> a violation of their vows, and maybe there are also some other monks who sympathise with their fellows wanting to be removed from distraction for deeper internal contemplation. In any case, it\u2019s cute how such isolated figures become the subjects of rumour and myth among the lay populace, who take the refutations of the Order as just confirming the idea that there\u2019s something strange and mystical to hermitage. I think a large number of pilgrims scouting out in search of those hermits would make for an interesting scene to engage with in some manner.<br /> <br /> Ah yes, that sidebar is clearly complementing the description of the Immaculate work ethic; how despite the expectations of outsiders of secluded contemplation, the activities of the Order are actually highly demanding on their time. I think that makes for another interesting insight into the relationship between the Order and lay populace, how they might work diligently on things like public benefits and ministering, but in a manner intended to be largely invisible. I suppose that some of that is administrative work that many people are liable to take for granted anyway, but that even for their more active involvements they might cultivate an image of effortlessness, encouraging people to not fully think about things such as, say, where all of those pamphlets come from. I can imagine that as not only adding to the mystique of the Immaculate Order, but aiding in their secret policework, which I suppose is an element of their mission not fully recognised by most citizens. It makes me think of a feeback loop in which people are conditioned to not develop awareness that the monks are watching them, such that when a monk publicly reprimands some inadequate commune or individual, that too has the air of an uncannily keen insight. Still, I also like how this whole section emphasizes the essential humanity of even monks, and that even they need vacations sometimes (albeit of a form that adheres to their vows and strictures). From that, I suspect that more than a few hermits consist of monks who took their sabbatical off in the wilderness and just found themselves unwilling to ever go back.<br /> <br /> Yes, personal connections looks like another aspect that is calibrated with a mind towards keeping the monks engaged with and present in society at large for story purposes. I think there are some clever ways in which such an agenda is folded into consistency with the Order\u2019s overall mission, especially with Dragon Blooded monks and the Great Houses and the focus given on their secret police role (hence it being useful to have friends and family to observe and provide information). I get a kick out of how there\u2019s a line on family loyalty being encouraged, but secondary to loyalty to the Order, considering that\u2019s basically the opposite of the line given for monks who come from House Mnemon (which surely provides the most of them from the Dynasty by far). I\u2019m particularly pleased with the emphasis given here for the Order\u2019s view on Sworn Kinships, considering how that was such a powerfully enriched element from What Fire Has Wrought; Hearths with lay Exalted are all well and good for groups in which they need somebody to advocate Immaculate morals and represent the Order\u2019s interests, but I\u2019m personally more interested in the idea of Kinships consisting solely of Exalted monks (something that I\u2019ve never really thought about before). Still, having a monk in a group of players is probably very useful for a Storyteller who wants to provide a group with hooks and the occasional prod.<br /> <br /> I can\u2019t find very much to say about addressing the subject of monks who leave the Order, beyond the general appreciation for the subject being addressed. I do like the way that it entails a kind of reverse shunning, with the family that one might return to very studiously not addressing where one was and fellow monks acting as though you were never one of them; I suppose these standards are also in the interest of preserving the image of the immutability of the Order, since even creating a script for everybody to knowingly act within can shape outlook. Still, I find the idea of a monk who refuses to give up the vestments despite dismissal, and who preserves themselves through success in highly violent duels, to be a very compelling plot hook; I\u2019m picturing something reminiscent of Burn Legend, of an especially vicious looking monk in a stormy wilderness with hardened bloody knuckles.<br /> <br /> And it\u2019s brought to a close with the matter of perspectives on Exalted besides the Anathema by default, which is obviously a good deal more elaborate than ever before.<br /> <br /> First up are the Sidereals. Not much new here, it continues the core\u2019s revised line about how they\u2019ve set themselves up to be seen as servants of Heaven who aid in preserving the Perfected Hierarchy. I think it mostly just consolidates the information on who and how Terrestrials become aware of them. I get what the Bronze Faction is going for by starting to promote interpretations that paint the Gold Faction with the same brush as those they\u2019re starting to work alongside, but it also feels like something that is increasing the risk of them giving themselves away. It\u2019s a nice little hint of them starting to lose their handle on things.<br /> <br /> The Exigents section expands upon what was stated on Immaculate views of the Chosen of small gods in the core book (which, yes, was a thing, despite how many people seemed to miss it); that their existence constitutes improper congress between god and mortal in contravention of the Perfected Hierarchy, that overrides all other considerations, with the added point that (much like sorcerers and thaumaturgists) their capabilities can be valuable enough that they can get a head start on redemption by serving the Order itself. The core book said that the label of Anathema was reserved for those who actively worked against the supremacy of the Dragon Blooded, but the phrasing here seems to suggest that it can be enough to merely not properly acknowledge it. All of that done, we\u2019re once more given some of my beloved examples. The characters given are all solid, a good cross section of various interactions between Exigents and the Order; I like how even the one declared Anathema still regards himself as a devotee, and there\u2019s something particularly intriguing in the idea that respectable gods on the Blessed Isle might produce Exalted who will serve with the Order as a matter of course. And I especially like them as examples of Exigents specifically, with some very interesting gods to draw upon and unusual avenues or applications for their powers. I also like how one of them has a mortal lifespan, particularly for her being in an extremely dangerous and sensitive position; it would certainly be a poor lookout if the Time of Tumult entailed a bunch of forbidden Exigents going free because their jailor passed from old age.<br /> <br /> In reading the God-blooded sidebar, I need to remind myself that the majority of them will have no exceptional powers, and the most significant thing that the Order will be watching for will be continued improper relationships with divine parents. That idea of the Order taking responsibility for a bunch of children that a god accumulated in pursuit of power is also quite intriguing (although I imagine that they\u2019d almost never be kept as a group while they\u2019re being raised).<br /> <br /> Not much left now. Liminals are almost unknown, but inauspicious by default due to being undead, offset only by the observed tendency to drive other ghosts back from whence they came. The Order doesn\u2019t have an intrinsic beef with Getimian Exalted, but here we\u2019re given an interesting point in which their function as an extension of Sidereal interests ends with them directed towards purposes connected to the Star Chosen; still, at least the monks get fancy new meditation out of it. Abyssals can be straightforwardly designated as Anathema by default, and are apparently cause for some fresh speculation among monks as to the exact source of their power, from which I take it that the Deathlords and Neverborn are still a subject of limited awareness (I\u2019m intrigued by the prospect of \u201cbleak majesty of dead suns\u201d). While the scant few Infernal Exalted remain an unknown quantity, associated with the Order solely by virtue of how the Sidereals will see to it that they\u2019re regarded as Anathema once they\u2019ve got a clearer picture of the situation; I\u2019ll admit that the reference to Sidereals pursuing them with dread smacks a bit of some of the Second Edition hype, but under the circumstances I\u2019m willing to look at it less in terms of there being something outstanding about them and more about them being an unnerving and unanticipated new permutation on the Solars.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ll admit, I had my doubts about another book giving a chapter for describing the Immaculate Order, but it\u2019s acquitted itself very well here. The Order feels like a more dynamic and engaging organisation than ever before, and the clearer picture of their structure, resources and personnel makes them look both easier to utilise practically and more inviting to do so. The Breaths and the expanding description of their training period in particular stand out to me. I wasn\u2019t looking to care more about the Order as I was for the Thousand Scales, but the effect is the same nonetheless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-25T20:25:07-07:00","id":1320881,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320629#post1320629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My main reservation is about some of the particulars of how it describes the Exalted experience and relationships with mortals, buuttt\u2026 I can reconcile that partially with an idea that this does not constitute a 100% literal description of how things work as much as the general standards that the Order projects (and permits), and partially by getting over it a bit and taking the point that yes, maybe I could have more of an eye to how the powers and resources provided to the Exalted of the Dynasty might encourage them towards loftier goals and a perspective that they\u2019re above the concerns of mortal life. Kind of a \u201ccalling for Diet Coke and one appears in your hand\u201d situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you expand on what you might need to &quot;get over&quot;? While it's true that no explanation is universal, I'm wondering what scenarios the given explanation created that gave you pause, and why.<br /> <br /> I've been looking forward to your read-through of the of Immaculate Order chapter, and have enjoyed it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320637#post1320637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I often wonder what Immaculate missionaries are like. Probably well received in areas where the local deities are abusive but less so in other places. Taken as an objective whole, the Immaculate Faith lacks broad spectrum appeal outside of a society already indoctrinated to believe in its precepts(like the Realm and its more servile satrapies). I suspect the Immaculates omit of theology a lot depending on where they're doing missionary work, only to enforce the scriptures more strictly once the populace is cowed by an imperial garrison. The promise of DB privilege in the next life(false though it may be) probably provides incentive for the more cynical aristocratic converts while pragmatic concerns such as regular weather and harvests and the occasional monastic charity probably attract agrarian and urban working class types. Warrior cultures probably have their champions humiliated by immaculate monks and have converts who want to side with the winners.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my mind, Immaculate missionaries are as varied as any other monks, but are driven by either the challenge of hard, slow work or the perceived need of the ignorant masses beyond the Order's grasp. They come from every walk of life, and indeed they must, because when one faces the chaos of the outer Threshold, one never knows what skills will come in handy. Monks don't choose their assignments, but the Order prefers not to waste monks on duties they won't pursue diligently while unmonitored.<br /> <br /> As for broad spectrum appeal, there's no wider net than safety, order, and a sense of shared purpose.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Hey, check out my first original RPG, <i>Post-Mortem, </i>here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem</a><br /> <br /> Or read my Exalted novella <i>The Silence of Our Ancestors</i> here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/304805/The-Silence-of-Our-Ancestors-A-DragonBlooded-Novella\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...looded-Novella</a>","edited":{"userid":1961,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","user":"Dex Davican","date":". 06-25-2019, 11:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","name":"Dex Davican","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1961_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-26T04:28:07-07:00","id":1320929,"title":"","content":"I'm almost entirely not reading this thread until I'm done with the book, but the timing of your response caught my eye, so I gave it a look:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dex Davican</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320881#post1320881\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Could you expand on what you might need to &amp;quot;get over&amp;quot;? While it's true that no explanation is universal, I'm wondering what scenarios the given explanation created that gave you pause, and why.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm very particular about how the books present the lifestyle and experience of the Exalted in relation to humanity at large (both how they form relationships with mortal and in the things that they care about). It is for reasons including my general outlook, characterisation that I find most compelling, and having found over the years that discourse that reads and encourages the Exalted to be beyond and unconcerned with mortal considerations to be infuriating at best.<br /> <br /> If you looked at my reading of Fangs, you may recall it as a consistent feature in how I enjoyed several Lunar relations with their Dominions.<br /> <br /> However, my impulse is always to seek reconciliation, and I can see a few avenues towards that here. One is in noting how there is an ideological impulse towards the Dragon Blooded seeing their lives as so distinct from and superior to the surrounding mortals, with the typical aristocratic privilege supplemented by mystical powers and an ideology that ascribes divinity to themselves.<br /> <br /> The getting over it part is in reminding myself as ever that the game is not written towards my exact preferences (not to mention ideas I developed and became attached to in the dry period), and it would be truly absurd for me to expect it to be.<br /> <br /> And truth be told, looking at those paragraphs again, the description of Exalted separation doesn't feel nearly as acute, nearly as offending to even my sensibilities. It almost makes me wonder why I took exception in the first place.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-26T09:09:52-07:00","id":1320971,"title":"","content":"Interesting. I'd intended to highlight the entitlement and alienation from the lower classes that players (who are often sympathetic toward underdogs) might need help wrapping their minds around. I hadn't considered how that section might encourage players to separate their characters from human concerns. It's a good thing to bear in mind, though I'm glad it apparently didn't veer into that territory.<br /> <br /> Thanks for responding.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Hey, check out my first original RPG, <i>Post-Mortem, </i>here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem</a><br /> <br /> Or read my Exalted novella <i>The Silence of Our Ancestors</i> here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/304805/The-Silence-of-Our-Ancestors-A-DragonBlooded-Novella\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...looded-Novella</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","name":"Dex Davican","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1961_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-26T15:13:19-07:00","id":1321048,"title":"","content":"Still working through the book, but the depiction of Patricians in the first chapter really made me want to play (or much more likely have a player in my game play) an Exalted Patrician. I've heard people say that the experience of playing a Dynast seems so foreign or removed from their personal experiences that is makes it difficult or less appealing to play, but The Realm paints the prospect of playing a Patrician Outcaste much more favorably. I really liked the bit about Patrician houses being pledged to the Great Houses and this makes me wish there were more canon Patrician houses.<br /> <br /> Realizing the Chapter Fictions worked best in unison, I skipped ahead and read them all, so I guess minor spoilers-<br /> <br /> <br /> Just wanted to make sure I have the right of it that we see the ground level effects of a Hearth's Plotting<b>\u00ae</b> and Scheming<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u2122, that self-same Hearth, and then the hope of a Magistrate and a Monk being put on their case, yes? Does the Un-Exalted Dynast ending up on the Caul look to dovetail back into the main plot, or is it just a nice side story?<br /> <br /> Also, what exactly is the Abbot asking of the peasants before she intervenes? I guess I'm not enlightened. Basically just asking for more substantial evidence, like what might be provided by the Magistrate?</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksha are my fae-vorite.<br /> <br /> Reincarnation of magnificentmomo.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1542-morosemorgan","name":"MoroseMorgan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1542_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"778"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-26T15:47:45-07:00","id":1321053,"title":"","content":"The Abbot is mostly dancing through a series of formalities where she makes it clear to the peasants that, since they\u2019re peasants and they\u2019re complaining about the behaviour of their betters, they\u2019re technically in the wrong, before threading a careful etiquette needle to communicate that, yes, she wants to help them.<br /> <br /> \u201cPerhaps you can sift through rumors for a truth that will allow us to cure this bandit plague.\u201d<br /> <br /> My read of the interaction is that the important term there is \u201c<u>a</u> truth\u201d. The Order will need a solid doctrine-compliant reason why it\u2019s right to intervene, and the peasants who know the area and have social connections to the local groups the bandits e.g. get their food from and fence their goods through are best positioned to gather that. Essentially, \u201cI want to help, guys, but you need to give me a better argument than this. Find a way in which the bandits or the people backing them are blaspheming, then come back.\u201d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-26T16:17:45-07:00","id":1321055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MoroseMorgan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321048#post1321048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just wanted to make sure I have the right of it that we see the ground level effects of a Hearth's Plotting<b>\u00ae</b> and Scheming[COLOR=#222222][FONT=sans-serif][SIZE=14px]\u2122, that self-same Hearth, and then the hope of a Magistrate and a Monk being put on their case, yes? Does the Un-Exalted Dynast ending up on the Caul look to dovetail back into the main plot, or is it just a nice side story?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not really tied directly into that plot so much as it is a coda for Nellens Tregane.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T04:03:20-07:00","id":1321144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320466#post1320466\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Dragons are a set piece that are never elaborated upon or explained as individuals. <b>Ebby can crush them like gnats apparently.</b> The Dragon-Blooded care about them, but the feeling doesn't seem mutual so the DBs come out looking like chumps.</div> </div> </div> </div>My take away was they could tank the Sword of Creation and the destruction of an Elemental Pole, but that a full powered Primordial who had closed their only weakness would then be able to deliver a coup de gr\u00e2ce. Which is totally not the same thing.<br /> <br /> (That said, the same book said any random Solar could Perfect away the Sword of Creation emerging unscathed for like 8m. So... YMMV... I guess. But I think surviving the Sword is narratively impressive even if the Elemental Dragons weren't built for paranoia combat.)<br /> <br /> But, yeah, I don't have a problem with the Yozi being able to easily defeat 3CDs/Behemoths/Primordial Offspring. The Primordials are pretty much the top dogs in the Exalted setting, and whilst the Yozi are the fallen, defeated remnants of the Primordials they're still more powerful than just about everything else in Creation. They can crush just about anything like gnats. Hell a big part of Malfeas (as a city) is that he can (and will and does) crush anything within him like they were gnats -- and only the physically strongest of all the Yozi is able to resist being squashed like a bug.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 06-27-2019, 07:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T05:30:49-07:00","id":1321151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321144#post1321144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My take away was they could tank the Sword of Creation and the destruction of an Elemental Pole, but that a full powered Primordial who had closed their only weakness would then be able to deliver a coup de gr\u00e2ce. Which is totally not the same thing.<br /> <br /> (That said, the same book said any random Solar could Perfect away the Sword of Creation emerging unscathed for like 8m. So... YMMV... I guess. But I think surviving the Sword is narratively impressive even if the Elemental Dragons weren't built for paranoia combat.)<br /> <br /> But, yeah, I don't have a problem with the Yozi being able to easily defeat 3CDs/Behemoths/Primordial Offspring. The Primordials are pretty much the top dogs in the Exalted setting, and whilst the Yozi are the fallen, defeated remnants of the Primordials they're still more powerful than just about everything else in Creation. They can crush just about anything like gnats. Hell a big part of Malfeas (as a city) is that he can (and will and does) crush anything within him like they were gnats -- and only the physically strongest of all the Yozi is able to resist being squashed like a bug.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Incarnae, specifically Sol Invictus, would be capable of posing a significant threat to the yozis were it not for the primordial geas. Theion explicitly wanted something that could threaten the other Primordials. Up until RoTSE, The Elemental Dragons were merely one step down from the Incarnae and in 2e are subsouls of Gaia(who's noted to be terrifyingly strong for Primordial) meaning they should lack the inherent geas preventing them from attacking primordials.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T06:15:28-07:00","id":1321156,"title":"","content":"I don't think Gaia was supposed to be terrifyingly powerful for a Primordial; she was the youngest, with a much lower number of subsouls. I think she was probably one of the weakest.<br /> <br /> And the Elemental Dragons are weaker than the Incarnae; yes, the strongest incarnae might be a match for a Primordial, maybe, but the Maidens aren't, and the Dragons are weaker than them.<br /> <br /> But I think that a battle of Demon-Dragon vs Element-Dragon should be epic and devastating and cataclysmic. In the process, tens of thousands of humans might be crushed like gnats, but I think it'd be pretty lame if the Ebon Dragon just breathed shadows once and Pasiap fell over dead. Sure, I'd expect the Ebon Dragon to win, but not effortlessly.<br /> <br /> Basically, the scaled-up equivalent of a battle between an Infernal and a Dragonblood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T06:48:13-07:00","id":1321157,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321156#post1321156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think Gaia was supposed to be terrifyingly powerful for a Primordial; she was the youngest, with a much lower number of subsouls. I think she was probably one of the weakest.<br /> <br /> And the Elemental Dragons are weaker than the Incarnae; yes, the strongest incarnae might be a match for a Primordial, maybe, but the Maidens aren't, and the Dragons are weaker than them.<br /> <br /> But I think that a battle of Demon-Dragon vs Element-Dragon should be epic and devastating and cataclysmic. In the process, tens of thousands of humans might be crushed like gnats, but I think it'd be pretty lame if the Ebon Dragon just breathed shadows once and Pasiap fell over dead. Sure, I'd expect the Ebon Dragon to win, but not effortlessly.<br /> <br /> Basically, the scaled-up equivalent of a battle between an Infernal and a Dragonblood.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The thing is, RoTSE describes the Ebon Dragon whacking Pasiap as if he was stepping on an ant. As for Gaia, the deities in Yu-Shan are described a being utterly terrified at the prospect of Gaia's full return, which makes sense given that the moment Oramus and Cythera popped out Luna(i.e. Explicitly Insane Shapeshifting God-Devil Monster), she fell in love with her. Autocthon is the Primordial with the smallest soul pantheon. Gaia provided the very bedrock of Creation and snapped the primordials out of their Zen-Mu depression funk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T06:50:24-07:00","id":1321159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321156#post1321156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think Gaia was supposed to be terrifyingly powerful for a Primordial; she was the youngest, with a much lower number of subsouls. I think she was probably one of the weakest.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have no idea what your basis is for making a claim about her number of sub-souls is. Even when the Dragons were canonically subsouls, nothing ever suggested they were her only ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T07:53:49-07:00","id":1321166,"title":"","content":"I don't think they were ever actually canonically sub-souls. It was just strongly implied.<br /> <br /> Anyway, it was from this section:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scroll of Glorious Divinity (not Glorious Divinininininity)</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The upper limits of the number of component<br /> souls a Yozi might possess are unclear. None of the<br /> imprisoned Primordials have less than a dozen, and the<br /> Yozi Adorjan, the Silent Wind, consists of 20 souls, of<br /> which Jacint is the 18th. Although She Who Lives in<br /> Her Name consists of more than 100,000 flames within<br /> crystal spheres, her 30th sphere contains her fourth soul,<br /> and she holds no more souls than is average for her<br /> brethren. The 23rd soul of the King of the Primordials<br /> was executed when he became Malfeas, and surviving<br /> First Age records indicate that some savants found it<br /> interesting that 23 was also the apparent number of<br /> imprisoned Yozis. Neither of the remaining Primordials<br /> seems to be as well endowed with souls as even the least<br /> of the Yozis. It is possible that Gaia naturally had fewer,<br /> as the youngest Primordial, and that one of the inciting<br /> factors of the mockery heaped upon Autochthon by his<br /> fellow Primordials might have been an abnormally low<br /> number of constituent souls on his part. Of course, it<br /> is also feasible that the two unvanquished Primordials<br /> have merely managed to conceal the existence of their<br /> other components.</div> </div> </div> </div>So she appears at least to have less than twelve, probably significantly less (5 would make sense; Autocthon has 8, so that'd give her less than him. But it's never said that she has 5. Maybe she has more than Autocthon. But certainly it's a lot less than Adorjan or Malfeas, etc).<br /> Though they do say it's possible she's hid them or something.<br /> But it certainly seems that way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 06-27-2019, 10:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T07:56:56-07:00","id":1321168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321157#post1321157\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The thing is, RoTSE describes the Ebon Dragon whacking Pasiap as if he was stepping on an ant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, and it's pretty lame. Where's the apocalyptic battle between two giant dragons?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T09:40:50-07:00","id":1321184,"title":"","content":"On the subject of the Elemental Dragons- I remember reading the CoCD: Yu Shan, when I got to that section about how the first Lesser Elemental Dragon ascended to their ranks, and that the Five Elemental Dragons were so pleased by that that Sextes Jylis demanded that such elementals will no be treated as commoners, my second thought was &quot;wait- they have opinions?!&quot;. My first thought was &quot;wait- THEY CAN TALK?!&quot;<br /> <br /> Like, I knew that the Elemental Dragons were intelligent beings, and that of course they could talk and that each had a true personality and such- but my reaction was still a shock from the fact that they are actually described as being as such. I mean, any personality trait we actually knew about them was in fact what was given for the Immaculate Dragons, which knew were in fact fictitious characters based around historical Solars. Maybe it was just me, but if that reaction of surprise that the Dragons actually do something active in the world and have things like opinions and such showed up in even 10 percent of those who read the books, it is a problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T09:54:33-07:00","id":1321188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321184#post1321184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">which knew were in fact fictitious characters based around historical Solars.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're not based on Solars. Other than that, I agree with your point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T10:18:00-07:00","id":1321193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321188#post1321188\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They're not based on Solars. Other than that, I agree with your point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? I was sure that there was a thing that the stories about the Immaculate Dragons are in fact based on actual historical solars.. I'll have to check why I thought so. Thanks for the correction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T10:29:59-07:00","id":1321195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HamSandLich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321157#post1321157\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing is, RoTSE describes the Ebon Dragon whacking Pasiap as if he was stepping on an ant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, after Pasiap took a direct hit from the Realm Defence Grid in a super-overload explosion that destroyed the entire Imperial Mountain.<br /> <br /> (And AFTER The Unconquered Sun got murdered.)<br /> <br /> Pasiap had literally just taken uncountable damage from the most powerful warmachine in existence. A mortal with a rock could have effortlessly crushed the Unconquered Sun under those circumstances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T10:33:34-07:00","id":1321196,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321193#post1321193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really? I was sure that there was a thing that the stories about the Immaculate Dragons are in fact based on actual historical solars.. I'll have to check why I thought so. Thanks for the correction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of the feats were from the Solar's. Melia taking the place of Brigid for the invention of Sorcery etc..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T10:34:18-07:00","id":1321197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321184#post1321184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the subject of the Elemental Dragons- I remember reading the CoCD: Yu Shan, when I got to that section about how the first Lesser Elemental Dragon ascended to their ranks, and that the Five Elemental Dragons were so pleased by that that Sextes Jylis demanded that such elementals will no be treated as commoners, my second thought was &quot;wait- they have opinions?!&quot;. My first thought was &quot;wait- THEY CAN TALK?!&quot;<br /> <br /> Like, I knew that the Elemental Dragons were intelligent beings, and that of course they could talk and that each had a true personality and such- but my reaction was still a shock from the fact that they are actually described as being as such. I mean, any personality trait we actually knew about them was in fact what was given for the Immaculate Dragons, which knew were in fact fictitious characters based around historical Solars. Maybe it was just me, but if that reaction of surprise that the Dragons actually do something active in the world and have things like opinions and such showed up in even 10 percent of those who read the books, it is a problem.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> And in 1e's Games of Divinity, they're described as thinking the original elementals that appeared post-Primordial War were too weak and stupid to do anything meaningful, making the fact that they speak up for the Lesser Elemental Dragons but apparently don't even take notice of the DBs really jarring. All the other exalts have patrons who interacted with them on some level post-Primordial War, but the DBs come off as being beneath the notice of their own progenitors (which contributed to the &quot;DB = Cosmic Loser&quot; theme that was everywhere in 2e)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24948,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","user":"HamSandLich","date":". 06-27-2019, 01:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24948-hamsandlich","name":"HamSandLich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24948_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"161"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T10:45:24-07:00","id":1321200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321193#post1321193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really? I was sure that there was a thing that the stories about the Immaculate Dragons are in fact based on actual historical solars.. I'll have to check why I thought so. Thanks for the correction.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess JohnDoe is right that in previous editions they claimed Mela invented sorcery (but then Lunars claimed a Lunar discovered sorcery), but most of their feats are either made up, dramatisations of big battles where the Dragons were ascribed feats done by many people (in 1e, for example, one battle where Mela zapped everything with lightning was actually one the DBs won with a giant lightning-zapping airship), or the feats of various different Dragonblood.<br /> <br /> Basically, a lot of sources were drawn together to create them, with a dose of entirely made-up stuff. But it's mostly Dragonblood, not Solars. Actually, after Dragonblood, the next-most source was Sidereals; Sidereals came up with the Immaculate Dragon styles, Sidereals trapped the Solars in the Jade Prison rather than Daana'd trapping them in the Underworld, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T11:06:09-07:00","id":1321201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Vance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321055#post1321055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not really tied directly into that plot so much as it is a coda for Nellens Tregane.</div> </div> </div> </div>The original outline for <i>The Realm</i> had only six chapters, with the Caul included in one of the other chapters. Breaking the Caul out into its own chapter came relatively late in development, at which point we needed to add a new chapter fiction when the chapter fictions for the first six chapters were already written. A coda for a side character seemed the best way to attach this additional chapter fiction to the overall narrative spine.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T12:43:05-07:00","id":1321225,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321166#post1321166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think they were ever actually canonically sub-souls. It was just strongly implied.<br /> <br /> Anyway, it was from this section:<br /> <br /> So she appears at least to have less than twelve, probably significantly less (5 would make sense; Autocthon has 8, so that'd give her less than him. But it's never said that she has 5. Maybe she has more than Autocthon. But certainly it's a lot less than Adorjan or Malfeas, etc).<br /> Though they do say it's possible she's hid them or something.<br /> But it certainly seems that way.</div> </div> </div> </div>This as a note is contradicted in later books. <b>Dreams of the First Age</b> has a passing mention of Gaia having &quot;twenty hidden souls&quot;. Additionally, later books (namely I htink the Ink Monkeys) canonized the Dragons as her souls, but as you note, it was not until much later this actually became the case.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T12:44:27-07:00","id":1321226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321200#post1321200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I guess JohnDoe is right that in previous editions they claimed Mela invented sorcery (but then Lunars claimed a Lunar discovered sorcery), but most of their feats are either made up, dramatisations of big battles where the Dragons were ascribed feats done by many people (in 1e, for example, one battle where Mela zapped everything with lightning was actually one the DBs won with a giant lightning-zapping airship), or the feats of various different Dragonblood.<br /> <br /> Basically, a lot of sources were drawn together to create them, with a dose of entirely made-up stuff. But it's mostly Dragonblood, not Solars. Actually, after Dragonblood, the next-most source was Sidereals; Sidereals came up with the Immaculate Dragon styles, Sidereals trapped the Solars in the Jade Prison rather than Daana'd trapping them in the Underworld, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a bit of a weird one on the Mela and sorcery is that the original <b>Book of 3 Circles</b> attributes it to Hesiesh. It didn't get changed until <b>Savant &amp; Sorcerer</b> I think to force Aspect-alignment or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T12:46:42-07:00","id":1321227,"title":"","content":"On the Immaculate Dragons themselves, they are likely as stated syncretics of dozens of things. The Immaculate Philosophy apparently pre-dates the Usurpation, and it is likely that there were indivdiuals wiht the names of the Five Immaculate Dragons long ago who got a lot of stories tagged onto them over the five millennia of the First Age and the hundreds of nations that rose and fell in that era. Think about how much magical and whatnot was attributed to the Buddha or Jesus just a century or so after they died. Now add an apocalypse or six and you get a lot that's probably hard to say &quot;Hesiesh was really a Solar&quot; as much as he was anything.<br /> <br /> EDIT: So I don't have to make another reply. On the Elemental Dragons, couple things to remember:<br /> <br /> They were only made canonically teh same names as the Immaculates in <b>Compass: Wyld</b>. Before that htey were just the Air Dragon, Earth Dragon, etc.<br /> <br /> I think a big thing about divorcing them from Gaia's soul hierarchy and instead being their own unique fully formed deities is probably for their better. While they maye ach be individually weaker than the Sun, I am on board treating them collectively as a patron on par with any of the Incarna (ie, they together probably are equal to the Maidens). The gods of the five elements tha tmake all the world are cosmic and powerful beings, just that their nature is difuse, hard to pin down and hidden. I also am fine with them not interacting with their Chosen in the way different from the INcarna in part since I'm fine with them just not interacting with anyone unless you go out looking for htem. They don't give visions ot their Chosen since it's not their thing, rather than not caringa bout htem.<br /> <br /> A lot of the whole &quot;A Yozi can beat up any dragon&quot; is bourne pretty much out of 2e views of power, how much &quot;win more' things are at the top of said power, and how much things hsould just be stomped on. Which mind, is fine for late 1e and most of 2e, but since this is a thread about a 3e book about 3e canon which isn't calicified yet, I think i'ts worth keeping things open on what is possible and fitting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":353,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","user":"Blaque","date":". 06-27-2019, 03:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T20:00:46-07:00","id":1321311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321156#post1321156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think Gaia was supposed to be terrifyingly powerful for a Primordial; she was the youngest, with a much lower number of subsouls. I think she was probably one of the weakest.<br /> <br /> And the Elemental Dragons are weaker than the Incarnae; yes, the strongest incarnae might be a match for a Primordial, maybe, but the Maidens aren't, and the Dragons are weaker than them.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>While I do get what your getting by this, to expand this further. That too would be an generalization. One being all Primordials are equal. That the Ebon Dragon is the same as Adorjan or Isidoros is equal to Elloge and etc. I think when you get at a scale as big and vast as the Yozi, Incarnae, and Dragons such realization on whether who strongest or not should not be talked about that much, which I like with 3 not mentioning that. All we know is the Sol is strongest because thats how he is and everyone else is equilivant, including the Dragons. No need to go in depth when talking about these vast beings who is the source of your power. If you want to depict such fights in your games as setting climax then its probably be best to be more individual as you said. The battle between the Water Dragon against like say Kimbery would be very different compared how it would be against Cecelyn and thus that, if any games ever get to such a point anyhow.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321193#post1321193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really? I was sure that there was a thing that the stories about the Immaculate Dragons are in fact based on actual historical solars.. I'll have to check why I thought so. Thanks for the correction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I think it was discussed in <i>The Realm</i>. When you become a proper monk for a period you get told that the Immaculate Dragons are allegories based on the achievements of few mythic dragon-blooded whose legends have been blended together. Thats at least the official position that the Immaculate Order has on them<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-27T20:34:58-07:00","id":1321316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iceblade44</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321311#post1321311\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah I think it was discussed in <i>The Realm</i>. When you become a proper monk for a period you get told that the Immaculate Dragons are allegories based on the achievements of few mythic dragon-blooded whose legends have been blended together. Thats at least the official position that the Immaculate Order has on them</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily when you're a monk for a period. It was just mentioned that the truth of the matter* was only for the particularly studious and persistent while still faithful and were inquisitive enough that the explanations were needed. Most others don't have the interest or attention needed to force it to be known that the Immaculate Dragons are compilations, the Order being founded in the Shogunate (though with older teachings and such), and that Lunars and Solars are exalts who are just unfortunate enough to have power that will invariably drive them insane and to become dangers to everything.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> * Well as they understand it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3400,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","user":"nalak42","date":". 06-28-2019, 09:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-28T11:54:15-07:00","id":1321414,"title":"","content":"Thanks for affirming my understanding <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar229_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"229\" data-vbnamecard=\"229\">Stephen Lea Sheppard</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar527_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"527\" data-vbnamecard=\"527\">Robert Vance</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1120_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1120\" data-vbnamecard=\"1120\">Eric Minton</a>. I dig it.<br /> <br /> I'm really enjoying this book. I tried and failed many times to read through and internalize Compass Blessed Isle in 2e, but it was almost like homework, while I am really enjoying The Realm and for the first time in my Exalted tenure would like to play on the Blessed Isle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksha are my fae-vorite.<br /> <br /> Reincarnation of magnificentmomo.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1542-morosemorgan","name":"MoroseMorgan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1542_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"778"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T12:31:19-07:00","id":1321530,"title":"","content":"I love the new lore on the nature of the Mouth of Peace, though i\u2019m not sure how I feel about the current one being near death from old age and afraid of the chaos her death will unleash - because it feels redundant with Chejop.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m leaning instead toward the current Mouth being 15 years old and having just Exalted to everyone\u2019s relief. So she\u2019s positioned to be used as a tool for power by someone else, but she\u2019s still an Exalted herself, even if she\u2019s only 15, and those who underestimate her, and think her exploitable as a prop could find themselves unable to hold her reigns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T13:25:09-07:00","id":1321535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321530#post1321530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love the new lore on the nature of the Mouth of Peace, though i\u2019m not sure how I feel about the current one being near death from old age and afraid of the chaos her death will unleash - because it feels redundant with Chejop.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m leaning instead toward the current Mouth being 15 years old and having just Exalted to everyone\u2019s relief. So she\u2019s positioned to be used as a tool for power by someone else, but she\u2019s still an Exalted herself, even if she\u2019s only 15, and those who underestimate her, and think her exploitable as a prop could find themselves unable to hold her reigns.</div> </div> </div> </div>As it happens, I agree with you about 50%. Which is why I wrote the Mouth of Peace so that she could gracefully or tragically exit the stage and allow you to play the new one if that worked better for your game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Hey, check out my first original RPG, <i>Post-Mortem, </i>here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem</a><br /> <br /> Or read my Exalted novella <i>The Silence of Our Ancestors</i> here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/304805/The-Silence-of-Our-Ancestors-A-DragonBlooded-Novella\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...looded-Novella</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","name":"Dex Davican","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1961_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T13:45:09-07:00","id":1321536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dex Davican</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321535#post1321535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As it happens, I agree with you about 50%. Which is why I wrote the Mouth of Peace so that she could gracefully or tragically exit the stage and allow you to play the new one if that worked better for your game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh because she\u2019s trying to choose a successor rather than deal with the reincarnation cycle meaning at least 13 years or so until Exaltation of the next one and then all her adolescent training?<br /> <br /> Of course can\u2019t imagine a lot of the Order is going to accept a Mouth who objectively cannot be the reincarnation of the one soul who his believed to have been every Mouth until this point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T13:49:40-07:00","id":1321539,"title":"","content":"I mean, it could be a nice setup for a future Dalai Lama-Panchen Lama plotline. Where part of her putting the younger one in place is with a goal of having her replacement be sought-out/found more orderly in this upcoming time of crisis. (Ie, having someone setup with enough authority to pick hte next Mouth of Peace independent of whatever regime is there after the war.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T14:35:59-07:00","id":1321545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321536#post1321536\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh because she\u2019s trying to choose a successor rather than deal with the reincarnation cycle meaning at least 13 years or so until Exaltation of the next one and then all her adolescent training?<br /> <br /> Of course can\u2019t imagine a lot of the Order is going to accept a Mouth who objectively cannot be the reincarnation of the one soul who his believed to have been every Mouth until this point.</div> </div> </div> </div>A lot probably won't. Some really excellently-edited newfound First Age scripture, the support of several Great House scions, and some light historical revisions will help, if the new Mouth of Peace can get all those things. If the Realm survives the Time of Tumult, in a mortal generation or two only a few thousand people in Creation will even remember the change personally. And every monk is taught--though not all fully accept--that the continuation and improvement of the perfected hierarchy is more important than any single person or objective historical truth. The custodians of society bear a crushing weight.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Hey, check out my first original RPG, <i>Post-Mortem, </i>here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem</a><br /> <br /> Or read my Exalted novella <i>The Silence of Our Ancestors</i> here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/304805/The-Silence-of-Our-Ancestors-A-DragonBlooded-Novella\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...looded-Novella</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","name":"Dex Davican","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1961_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T17:29:30-07:00","id":1321563,"title":"","content":"They could actually set up a cyclical thing - there are two souls. One Mouth of Peace trains their successor, and then that one upon assuming the position finds the reincarnation of their old master and trains them as their successor in-waiting, and so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T19:27:30-07:00","id":1321568,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321530#post1321530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love the new lore on the nature of the Mouth of Peace, though i\u2019m not sure how I feel about the current one being near death from old age and afraid of the chaos her death will unleash - because it feels redundant with Chejop.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m leaning instead toward the current Mouth being 15 years old and having just Exalted to everyone\u2019s relief. So she\u2019s positioned to be used as a tool for power by someone else, but she\u2019s still an Exalted herself, even if she\u2019s only 15, and those who underestimate her, and think her exploitable as a prop could find themselves unable to hold her reigns.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC it was 2e that played up the whole dying really soon. Even if they are still keeping it Chejop Kejak and the Mouth of Peace occupy different spheres, the Realm probably wouldn't notice if Kejak died. While Yu-Shan wouldn't really care if the Mouth of Peace died.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-06-29T20:01:59-07:00","id":1321572,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321568#post1321568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> IIRC it was 2e that played up the whole dying really soon. Even if they are still keeping it Chejop Kejak and the Mouth of Peace occupy different spheres, the Realm probably wouldn't notice if Kejak died. While Yu-Shan wouldn't really care if the Mouth of Peace died.</div> </div> </div> </div>When Chejob dies is also something edition-based. 2e really pushed the &quot;He's gonna die in like a year&quot;, even making the current timeline set him up to being 4,999 years old.<br /> <br /> The fiction for him in the 1e corebook by contrast even notes that he would die in &quot;a century or three&quot;. I think &quot;I'm in my twilight, but I have time to think on it&quot; and &quot;Oh shit I'm going to die any time now&quot; are pretty different. It's just that the latter at some point became the assumed with Chejob.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-02T01:15:01-07:00","id":1321854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1321563#post1321563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They could actually set up a cyclical thing - there are two souls. One Mouth of Peace trains their successor, and then that one upon assuming the position finds the reincarnation of their old master and trains them as their successor in-waiting, and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is going be what I am going to have the dying Mouth of Peace attempt to establish with choosing a successor. And the Bronze faction will run with the idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-11T03:09:07-07:00","id":1323636,"title":"","content":"I'll be back to reviewing shortly enough, but for the moment I feel prompted to say something I initially withheld.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dex Davican</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1320971#post1320971\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting. I'd intended to highlight the entitlement and alienation from the lower classes that players (who are often sympathetic toward underdogs) might need help wrapping their minds around.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've seen so many accounts of people who evidently need help wrapping their minds around the opposite. People who seem to think that casually levelling atrocities on bystanders is the default, if not a creative and competitive exercise.<br /> <br /> Then there's shit like this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I remember when my 3e game I ran met a mortal artist who painted an equivalent to the Sistine Chapel [except with the Dragons], they decided he had to secretly be a sidereal or some other exalt type, because clearly no mortal would be able to do something that well done. They then decided that this gracious host was clearly trying to kill them, and... murdered an NPC, entirely because they were convinced it had to be an exalt in disguise. <br /> <br /> It was just a mortal. I had a player [the one who decided to join battle and ambush the artist] get very mad at me for 'misleading them' by making a Mortal actually capable of something worthy of awe and wonder. I never once said that anything about the art seemed magical, when the twilight tried to us AESS she saw nothing magical anywhere around them, and they were still so convinced that just because I claimed it was 'some of the most beautiful art you've ever seen' it had to be something non-mortal. More than that, they assumed it was something non-Dragonblooded too. </div> </div> </div>Something about reading that now shook me a lot more than the first time...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-11T07:52:01-07:00","id":1323682,"title":"","content":"To be fair, I wonder how much of that is the fault of the setting/player expectations and how much is on the ST.<br /> <br /> Many STs, lacking the language skills and experience to speak otherwise, use &quot;the most x you've ever seen&quot; as shorthand for magical.<br /> <br /> If you routinely describe Exalted deeds as &quot;the most x you've ever seen&quot; because you're trying not to say &quot;this is magic yo!&quot; then you can't act shocked when the players suss what you're trying to convey to them.<br /> <br /> And yes, the idea that mortals can't do anything sucks, and it's good that 3E is moving away from that.<br /> <br /> But if a Solar artist is rolling 10+ successes and a mortal artist is rolling","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-17T12:38:20-07:00","id":1325360,"title":"","content":"Excited, Realm is now shipping hardcover. <br /> Unexcited, shipping cost is more than the book cost. Holy crap. If only colour e-readers were a capable thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/977-major","name":"Major","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar977_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"326"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-17T13:15:02-07:00","id":1325377,"title":"","content":"Neat, purchased!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2610-lycaniz","name":"lycaniz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2610_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-01T11:31:28-07:00","id":1328762,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1323636#post1323636\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll be back to reviewing shortly enough</div> </div> </div> </div>Bumping this in hopes of persuading Isator to continue. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-03T17:30:54-07:00","id":1329199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Minton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1314066-the-realm?p=1328762#post1328762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bumping this in hopes of persuading Isator to continue. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I <i>mean</i>, it's just, ehhhh...<br /> <br /> I should be finished with Sekiro: Shadows Die Twice in the next few days, that should relieve my attention for this again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T14:51:50-07:00","id":1332737,"title":"","content":"Okay. Okay. Okay.<br /> <br /> My hard copy of What Fire Has Wrought arrived today, and it judges me; I can feel Kingfisher Swift's gaze deconstructing me from that place on the cover where she would be if it wasn't for it being a special edition. It will not do.<br /> <br /> So with that, and... demand from one of the book's developers, which I suppose qualifies as popular...<br /> <br /> Have to get a start now, so that there's a bit of motion to get those cogs going.<br /> <br /> But I'm a bit tired and still kind of lazy, so for now I'm just going to post up something that I wrote two months ago and left because I wanted it to be longer before putting it in. I think I can use it as a springboard.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Well, my plans to be finished before the end of last week are scuppered. They probably weren\u2019t realistic in any event, what with some of the remaining chapters being filled with new setting information, and being abroad to visit the parents\u2026 eh, I had no time. See what I can do from here.<br /> <br /> For now, a new chapter, starting with a repeat of the fine Blessed Isle map, and a fiction whose opening places a lot of emphasis on the sheer age of human habitation of the Blessed Isle, a scope of history that defies even the Exalted character inhabiting the locale. It\u2019s funny, the moment before reading this I just happened to be perusing the Lore Charms, so it\u2019s fitting to get this nice instance of a character feeling his way into the depths of a subject\u2019s history through Essence.<br /> <br /> It's nice to see another Hearth show up, with a very distinct character from the one in What Fire Has Wrought, and a purpose rooted in the development of the civil war. I\u2019m often fond of the idea that some Dragon Blooded might just casually levitate, so I like seeing it here, and how it\u2019s an in-character basis for a character recognising the development of another\u2019s Essence. I was enamoured with their scheming from the start (I often like the premise of characters sending in troops dressed in a deceptive manner to sabotage enemies while leaving limited traces, whether it\u2019s Dune or Blackadder), but what I particularly liked was the dawning comprehension of why these particular Exalts are directing their efforts towards House Nellens, what with Sesus objecting on principle, Ragara disliking their financial independence, and Ledaal having an eye towards shoring up their own holdings by annexing their Nellens neighbours. What Fire Has Wrought focused a lot on alliances at the level of entire Great Houses, but it\u2019s interesting to think that the lead up to civil war might be enhanced and complicated by a number of smaller scale alliances on the level of Kinships taking their own initiative.<br /> <br /> Heh, on a second read, I find the touch of how they\u2019re toasting their own success by drinking wine from Juche to be particularly dickish and enjoyable.<br /> <br /> The scene makes for a nice art piece of the trio sharing their drink while a tapestry of Creation hangs in the background, it\u2019s a good conveyance of the idea that for some Dynasts the Time of Tumult represents an opportunity to start feeding off of a vulnerable Creation (a nice match to the irony of the fiction\u2019s final line). I like the character designs particularly; their clothing really accentuates that thing of Dynast fashions being impeccably made by not too ostentatious (Ragara Daja\u2019s patterned black cloak in particular), they all look appropriately distinctive and mildly eccentric in their colouring (the braided green hair on Ledaal Marek is quite becoming, and it being complemented by his clothing really brings out the golden tint in his eyes), and I especially like how there\u2019s a somewhat older cast to their features, which in context gives me an impression that Dragon Blooded in the prime of life don\u2019t quite look eternally young as comfortably settling into a certain feel of agelessness. The piece has been done in that style prominent in this Edition that tends towards realism, but it\u2019s managed to effectively reconcile that with the more outlandish and stylized qualities that one would expect in Terrestrial Exalted especially; there are a bunch of elements in each character\u2019s design that are understated on their own, but they make one another pop in combination, so you\u2019ve got somebody with a realistically proportioned face with clothing that looks as though it could exist, who still projects a feeling of being a magical anime weirdo. I like it a lot, and these might just supersede the designs of the signature Hearth in being my favourite realisations of Dragon Blooded.<br /> <br /> So then, chapter five, The Blessed Isle. I know that the book\u2019s title addresses the fact that it\u2019s covering the Realm as a whole, including the satrapies in the next chapter, but it still feels a little bit odd to see those words be a chapter title like that. Anyway, that introduction covering the manner in which the Isle constitutes an economic and spiritual heart to the world is nice, particularly the emphasis on how the old magics of the Exalted contribute to the land\u2019s bounty, but it\u2019s the third paragraph there that is the particular standout. The subject of the Blessed Isle\u2019s ethnic makeup, both in terms of the apparent uniformity against its great size and the matter of how its original composition would logically have been a bit cosmopolitan, has felt like an overlooked topic to me for years, so I\u2019m very glad to see it addressed directly. It\u2019s nice to see the predominant ethnic group analogous to Han Chinese finally being given a name, and the explanation of their prominence amounting to \u201cthey happen to have been the biggest group following the Contagion\u201d is effectively straightforward. I can read the line on the Wan being alongside other autochthonous groups in two ways; either that, much like reality, having settled in a place so many millenia ago (and under circumstances that might not have entailed displacing a pre-existing people) means that for all intents and purposes they functioned as the various indigenous groups of the Blessed Isle (albeit by now either heavily integrated into the majority or remaining as small pockets), or else that they\u2026 literally sprung out of the ground by grace of the Elemental Pole of Earth. Either technically works. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> In any case, it\u2019s nice to have a few lines by which the appearances and practices of the Isle\u2019s inhabitants might differ outside of the Scarlet Dynasty, and I especially like the added characterisation to the slightly more liberal coastal regions as home to various kinds of immigrant. And I take the last line to mean that the official policy of the Realm as that existing as its citizens effectively supersedes all previous divisions and affiliations, for good and ill (although I would imagine that some groups might experience some local prejudices and discriminations).<br /> <br /> Following that intro description of the spiritual significance of the Isle, I like being given a sidebar going into a bit more detail on the subject of local sorcerous prodigies. That there was magic to be found on the Blessed Isle was never exactly absent from the writing, but I feel as though there might not have been a consistent line on how widely it was to be found, so I think it\u2019s good to provide a basic standard. In the interests of maintaining the image of the Age of Sorrows and keeping the Blessed Isle within it, I\u2019m also glad that the standard amounts to such constructs and sorceries still existing, but largely coming in the form of precious local phenomena while the reigning Exalted lack the means to expand upon them; it adds to the Isle\u2019s diversity, can create points of contention between areas and something to include in fights over prefectural appointments, and can give a lot of prominence to otherwise out of the way locales in a manner that I like (I enjoy fiction in which minor villages have something to them that make them a focus of significant fame and interest while still being limited in the capacity to grow). I also find the matter of how not all of these powers are stable, particularly with the return of the Solars, to effectively add another source of native conflict. Of the examples, the idea of a prefecture possessing immortal orchards seems as though it would have massive economic implications, but the one that stands out to me the most is the deadly dream afflicting Bright Obelisk (if only because I find it reminiscent of a neat bit of obscure magic referenced in Jonathan Strange and Mr. Norrell).<br /> <br /> Hmm, setting examples in Fangs were pretty long, and there are {glance at the table of contents} about 24 of these. But they\u2019re {quick glimpse ahead} not super long, only a couple of pages each\u2026 I think that I can manage a decent number of these at a time, get through this chapter quickly enough.<br /> <br /> Okay, first up is Endless Prefecture, which begins by introducing the point that the highest areas of the Imperial Mountain are not under any prefectural jurisdiction, but a few are located around lower points of the slope, with our highly placed subject being inhabited only by Immaculates and occasionally visited by pilgrims and Dynasts in pursuit of knowledge or fun. I\u2019ve always enjoyed the premise of the Mountain as being a spot for perilous spiritual journeys, so I like the path that might be followed for such being given a name and charming details such as the finely adorned temples and the manner in which they support themselves and those who are travelling upwards; I get a nice image of colourful banners fluttering in the wind of the exposed mountainside. I find it amusing how the uppermost temple is given a name such as Heaven\u2019s End when it\u2019s described as not actually being that far up the mountain, and serving as a threshold against the onset of dangerous beings and powers that might descend from the peaks gives a bit more activity to a spot on the map that might have previously been impressively sized but a bit dull.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m liking the manner in which the topography of the Mountain is conveyed to us through descriptions of the landscapes that surround the pilgrim path; the impression I\u2019m getting is that the Imperial Mountain is a lot more craggy and riddled with depressions more amenable to flora and fauna than my previously imagined enormous cone of sheer rock. The presence of ancient mystical ruins in these places sounds both like an incentive for characters to visit in the first place and providing them with a more immediate return for their efforts. I think this holds up with the sidebar addressing the Mountain directly; I\u2019m not quite sure of the mathematics that would make it visible so from the coasts, but the impression I\u2019m getting is of a mountain that is distinctly big, possibly bigger than any on Earth, but not hundreds of miles big. I like it being made the subject of a lot of myth unverified by the text, and find an interesting tension to the idea that mining from it directly was traditionally forbidden but coming to the verge of being enacted by Dragon Blooded in need of materials. One thing that seems particularly different from prior Editions is the subject of the Mountain\u2019s peak and the city of Meru. As I recall from prior Editions, Meru was said to have been situated along the Mountain\u2019s slopes, while the peak itself was bare and home to things like the Well of Souls and some very powerful Earth demesne. Honestly, Meru covering the sides of the Mountain never quite sat well with me, both in terms of the sheer scale it implied and the awkward question of how visible or accessible the ruins were to the people of the Isle. I prefer if being placed at the peak for a number of reasons; being a lot more out of reach and probably on a much smaller scale is one of them, as is the simple image of having an elite settlement so greatly elevated over the rest of the world (something that particularly goes with the implication that its residence was a much more exclusive matter). Heck, it even provides a salve against the Mountain not being ridiculously high; that was something I liked for the premise that there was a vantage point from which one could see almost all of Creation sprawled out around one, and in this setup that could just be done with the idea that the city of Meru includes a <i>really</i> high and rather narrow tower; somewhere between a feature of Indian mythology and a sky elevator.<br /> <br /> I think the spirit of Grabowski\u2019s statement of the Imperial Mountain constituting a challenge for the Exalted mountaineer is still present, but the presence of deadly magics, unruly spirits and twisted monsters makes approaching such a bit more invested than it just being very high up (even if it retains the difficulties and dangers of low temperatures and limited air).<br /> <br /> Last of all is Pasiap\u2019s Shadow, which is using the premise of the deeper crags and cavities of the Mountain as a means of providing ghosts with a refuge on the otherwise inhospitable Blessed Isle. What I like here is how such inaccessibility is turned against the dead inhabitants in a way that leaves them distinct and interesting, introverted in the extreme. And I find it a particularly good touch that the place is made inaccessible, but not unknown, and I find the incentives given for people (even mortal) to seek it out to be credible, and a good means of not letting these strange ghosts and the nice descriptions of the daunting and perilous caverns that they inhabit go to waste. <br /> <br /> For the moment, I will finish up with Dejis Prefecture, home of my dearly beloved revised House Mnemon.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> (I only got to an ending of Sekiro a few days before the World Science Fiction Convention started, I was out for twelve hours a day for five days straight, I didn't have time!) <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}